\begin{abstract}
Motivated by recent experimental signatures of fractional Chern insulators in 2D quantum materials, we investigate the realization of $\mathbb Z_3$ parafermion zero modes (PZMs) on edges of spin-polarized $\nu=2/3$ fractional quantum Hall states which are proximitized by superconductors.  Unlike the spin-unpolarized Halperin state, the polarized $\nu=2/3$ edge contains counterpropagating electron and fractional quasihole modes, giving rise to a plethora of competing tunneling and pairing processes. We focus on the vicinity of the fixed point where these modes reorganize into decoupled charge and neutral sectors, with the neutral sector describing an emergent dipole degree of freedom. Using bosonization and renormalization group analysis, we lay out the parameter space in which the interedge dipole interactions gap the neutral sector to stabilize one dominant tunneling and one dominant pairing channel resulting in a double sine-Gordon model in the charge sector which supports $\mathbb{Z}_3$ PZMs at domain walls. We discuss both clean and disordered systems. In clean systems this mechanism requires a certain degree of control of the interaction strength and range. In contrast, disorder can provide a more generic route: 
While short-ranged, edge-uncorrelated disorder  
suppresses the simultaneous pinning required for stable parafermion modes, 
when the disorder on the two edges is sufficiently correlated, as expected for smooth disorder potentials, the parameter regime hosting PZMs increases.

\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The realization of topologically ordered phases and the associated detection of exotic anyonic quantum particles remains a major focus of modern condensed matter physics \cite{NakamuraManfra2020, NakamuraManfra2023, SureshManfra2025, GuerreroSuarezManfra2025, WerkmeisterKim2025,
Kim2024,
Kim2026,
Samuelson2026,Bartolomei2020,
Glidic2023,
Ruelle2023,
Lee2023,
Kundu2023,
Ghosh2025}. Beyond its inherent scientific value, this endeavor is fueled by the dream of an intrinsically robust topological quantum computer. In this framework, quantum information is encoded in anyonic fusion channels whilst quantum braids are the physical processes underlying quantum gates. \cite{NayakDasSarma2008, Kitaev2003} A famous example includes Majorana-based topological quantum computation \cite{Alicea2012, Microscoft2025, Microsoft2026},  corresponding to Ising topological order. In this case, however, the set of quantum gates is too restricted to enable universal gate operations. As a possible remedy, parafermionic generalizations of Majorana zero modes to fractionalized systems with richer $\mathbb{Z}_n$ degrees of freedom have been put forward \cite{FradkinKadanoff1980, ZamolodchikovFateev1985, Fendley2014, MongFendley2014}. In suitable coupled architectures, they can provide a route toward Fibonacci anyons -- a platform for universal topological quantum computation~\cite{Mong2014, Vaezi2014a,AliceaFendley2016}. 

Parafermion zero modes (PZMs) have been proposed to emerge at interfaces between differently gapped regions of fractional quantum Hall (FQH) edges ~\cite{LindnerStern2012, ClarkeAliceaShtengel2013,Cheng2012}. The topic has received substantial theoretical attention and has been extensively studied \cite{Mong2014, Vaezi2013, Vaezi2014a, ClarkeAliceaShtengel2014, CobaneraOrtiz2014, SantosHughes2017,LiangLyanda-Geller2019,Schmidt2020, LiCheng2015, EbisuOreg2017,CalzonaSchmidt2018, SnizhkolGefen2018a, SnizhkoGefen2018b, GroenendijkCalzonaSchmidt2019,NielsenBurrello2022,CaoFradkin2024, YoshitomeGomes2025, ZhouLyanda-Geller2026, BollmannKoenig2026, CaoFradkin2026}.  
A major experimental challenge is the simultaneous realization of a robust fractional topological phase and superconductivity \cite{GuelKim2022, SchillerOreg2023, HuWangLian2024,KurilovichGlazman2023, ZhaoFinkelstein2023,UdayAndo2024, TamLian2026}. Conventional FQH states require strong magnetic fields, which complicate their integration with superconductors (SCs). Fractional Chern insulators (FCIs) provide an attractive alternative: they realize fractionalized topological order in partially filled Chern bands and can occur without an externally applied magnetic field~\cite{RegnaultBernevig2011, NeupertMudry2011, LiuBergholz2024}. Their recent observation in 
2D quantum materials
\cite{Cai2023,ZengShan2023,ParkXu2023,XuLi2023,JiShen2024,RedekopYoung2024,LuJu2024,ChenWang2024,LuJu2025,DingXu2025}, therefore, opens new possibilities for realizing parafermions in experimentally accessible platforms.

