    \label{fig:experiment}
\end{figure}

Twisted bilayer $\mathrm{MoTe}_2$ (tMoTe$_2$) is particularly promising in this respect. Robust fractional states have been observed at filling $\nu=2/3$, while superconductivity has recently been reported to appear within the same moir\'e Chern band, slightly doping away from the fractional regime~\cite{XuLi2023}. This reignited the theoretical study of anyon-driven superconductivity  \cite{Laughlin1988, ChenLaughlin1989, FetterHannaLaughlin1989, FanLi2025,ShiSenthil2025,DivicVishwanath2025,HanWangVishwanath2025,NosovHanKhalaf2026,PichlerVishwanath2026,Goldman2026,MehtaGoldman2026,SeoYoung2026} and at the same time raises the possibility of realizing an FCI--SC heterostructure within a single material. A schematic setup inspired by similar previous studies \cite{LeeKim2017, GuelKim2022, JiaWu2024, HanJu2025, XuLi2025} is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:experiment}. Top and bottom gates tune the sample into the spin-polarized $\nu=2/3$ FCI, while a narrow finger gate locally changes the filling and induces superconducting or insulating regions. Fractional superconducting and insulating regions can thus, in principle, be defined electrostatically without an additional material interface.

With the prospect of such a single-material realization, we here theoretically delineate the parameter space in which $\mathbb Z_3$ parafermion modes may be realized based on 1D heterostructures of SCs and the edge states of spin-polarized $\nu = 2/3$ phases. Our study includes an in-depth analysis of interaction and disorder effects. We find that for clean samples, interaction effects need to be 
externally controlled
to stabilize quantum phases with parafermion edge states. In contrast, for disordered interfaces, the
tuning can be avoided. 
If the disorder potential is long ranged (e.g. Coulomb impurities), the parafermionic phase is stable over a large portion of the phase diagram. However, if the disorder potential is very short ranged and at the same time the tunneling interface is smooth, the phases including parafermions are suppressed.

This manuscript is structured as follows. As a technical motivation, we start in Sec.~\ref{sec:recap} by giving a brief review of how PZMs can appear in FQH--SC heterostructures as a low-energy theory of a double sine-Gordon model. Thereby, we identify the unresolved question of $\mathbb Z_3$ PZMs in a $\nu=2/3$ spin-polarized FQH state.
In Sec.~\ref{sec:microscopicModel}, we introduce the model and define all relevant objects needed to derive the effective low-energy theory. In Sec.~\ref{sec:cleanCase}, we first demonstrate our approach in a fine-tuned case in which the edge reorganizes into two counterpropagating decoupled modes, one neutral and one carrying charge $2e/3$. Subsequently, in Sec.~\ref{sec:dirtyCase}, we discuss the more realistic case of correlated and uncorrelated edge disorder for multiple disorder scenarios and demonstrate a $\mathbb Z_3$ PZM phase without the necessity of fine-tuning. Finally, in Sec.~\ref{sec:Conclusion}, we summarize and discuss our results.
\section{Parafermions in FQH--SC heterostructures}
\label{sec:recap}

We briefly review how fractional quantum Hall -- superconductor (FQH-SC) heterostructures can act as a platform to realize parafermions. In the following, we follow the strategy laid out in Ref.~\cite{ClarkeAliceaShtengel2013}. Throughout the manuscript, we use units $\hbar = k_B = 1$ in which both the reduced Planck constant and Boltzmann's constant are set to unity.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{RecapFigure.png}
    \caption{\textbf{a)} Schematic of a FQH-SC heterostructure. A trench in an FQH liquid is subsequently filled with a SC ($\Delta_L$), an insulator ($t$), and a SC ($\Delta_R$). At the interfaces, parafermions (red stars) emerge. \textbf{b)} Potential landscape due to tunneling and pairing. The interfaces create potential wells in which bound states can be trapped. The zero-energy states (red stars) have parafermionic statistics.}
    \label{fig:RecapFigure}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Fractional quantum Hall edge states}


Let two fractional quantum Hall edges at filling factor $\nu=1/k$ be separated by a thin trench. The trench is filled with a SC 
(thinner than its coherence length) and regions of insulating material that allow for electron pairing, i.e., crossed Andreev reflection (CAR) and tunneling, respectively. These materials form interfaces at $x_R$ and $x_L$ as shown Fig.~\ref{fig:RecapFigure}.

On the FQH edges, the two edge modes (``top'' and ``bottom'') can be described by two counterpropagating bosonic modes with the usual commutation relations
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
        [\phi_t(x), \phi_t(x')] & = \frac{i\pi}{k}\text{sign}(x-x'), \\
        [\phi_b(x), \phi_b(x')] & = -\frac{i\pi}{k}\text{sign}(x-x'), \\
        [\phi_t(x), \phi_b(x')] & = -\frac{i\pi}{k},
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
whose dynamics in the absence of CAR or tunneling are determined by the quadratic Hamiltonian 
\begin{equation}
    H_0 = \sum_{\tau=t,b} v_\tau \int \dd x  (\partial_x\phi_\tau)^2.
\end{equation}
Note that the non-vanishing commutator between top and bottom edge modes presumes that these modes are connected at $x \to +\infty$ and avoids the introduction of Klein factors~\cite{GuyonSafi2002}.

A trench of SC or insulator separates the top and bottom edge modes, see Fig.~\ref{fig:RecapFigure}a).
Only electrons can cross the trench, which can be represented in terms of vertex operators of top and bottom modes,
\begin{equation}
    \psi_\tau \sim e^{ik\phi_\tau}, \quad \tau = t,b.
\end{equation}
The corresponding density operators are $\rho_{t/b} = \pm\frac{1}{2\pi} \partial_x\phi_{t/b}$. It is useful to introduce the non-chiral fields
\begin{equation}
    \Phi = \frac{1}{2}(\phi_t +\phi_b)\quad\text{and}\quad\Theta=\frac{1}{2}(\phi_t - \phi_b),
\end{equation}
where $\Phi$ is the phase canonically conjugate to the total density $\rho=\frac{1}{\pi}\partial_x\Theta$. Their mutual commutation relation reads
\begin{equation}
    [\Phi(x), \Theta(x')]=i\frac{\pi}{k}\theta(x-x'),
    \label{eq:commutationRelationPhiTheta}
\end{equation}
with $\theta(x-x')$ being the Heaviside step function.

Bosonizing the tunneling and pairing terms, 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
     H & = - \int  \dd x t(x)  \psi_t^\dagger\psi_b +  \int\dd x \Delta(x) \psi_t \psi_b  + \text{H.c.} \\
    & \sim \int  \dd x t(x) \cos(2k\Theta) - \int\dd x \Delta(x)\cos(2k\Phi),
    \label{eq:reviewBosonizedTunnelingPairing}
\end{split}
\end{equation}
eventually yields a Hamiltonian that 
resembles a double sine-Gordon model \cite{Lecheminat2002}. 
We will assume regions with topologically distinct gaps corresponding to regions with predominant tunneling and predominant pairing forming an interface. For theoretical simplicity,
the support of the functions $t(x)$ and $\Delta(x)$ is  assumed to have zero overlap as displayed in Fig.~\ref{fig:RecapFigure}b).


\subsection{Semiclassical minima and parafermionic bound states}
\label{sec:MinimaAndDerivationOfParafermions}

According to Hamiltonian \eqref{eq:reviewBosonizedTunnelingPairing}, the fields will be pinned to the values $\Theta = \frac{\pi}{k}\hat m-\frac{\pi}{2 k}$ and $\Phi = \frac{\pi}{k}\hat n_{L,R}$ in the tunneling and left/right pairing region, respectively. Here, $\hat m$ and $\hat n_{L,R}$ are integer-valued operators whose eigenvalues depend on the particular minimum in which the fields $\Theta$ and $\Phi$ are pinned. They inherit the commutation relation 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    [\hat n_R, \hat m ] &=i\frac{k}{\pi}, \\
    [\hat n_L, \hat m] & = 0
\end{align}
\label{eq:nmCommutation}
\end{subequations}
directly from Eq.~\eqref{eq:commutationRelationPhiTheta}.

As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:RecapFigure}a), the interfaces form potential wells capable of trapping bound states. These states must satisfy the boundary conditions imposed by the pinned $\Theta$ and $\Phi$ fields in the tunneling- and pairing-dominated phases, respectively. At the interface, localized zero-energy modes can be constructed. For temperatures below the energy gap to the first excited states, the projected zero-energy states, indicated by the red stars in Fig.~\ref{fig:RecapFigure}, can then, up to phase factors, be expressed as
\begin{align}
\begin{split}
    \alpha_{tR} \sim e^{i\frac{\pi}{k}(\hat n_R + \hat m)}, \quad
    \alpha_{tL} \sim e^{i\frac{\pi}{k}(\hat n_L +\hat m)},  \\
    \alpha_{bR} \sim e^{i\frac{\pi}{k}(\hat n_R - \hat m)}, \quad 
    \alpha_{bL} \sim e^{i\frac{\pi}{k}(\hat n_L -\hat m)}.
\end{split}
\end{align}
Note that the two pairs $\{\alpha_{tR},\alpha_{tL}\}$ and $\{\alpha_{bR},\alpha_{bL}\}$ of parafermions are not independent, as they only differ by a phase shift. To indicate that the Hilbert space is only spanned by one of the two operator sets, the stars representing $\{\alpha_{bR},\alpha_{bL}\}$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:RecapFigure}a) are empty.


Together with the commutation relation \eqref{eq:nmCommutation} one can show that the zero modes $\alpha_{\tau i}$ fulfill the $\mathbb Z_{2k}$ parafermion algebra
\begin{equation}
    (\alpha_{\tau i})^{2k}=1,\quad \alpha_{\tau L}\alpha_{\tau R} =\alpha_{\tau R}\alpha_{\tau L}e^{-i\frac{\pi}{k}}. 
\end{equation}
In this sense, we define a realization of parafermions in the given FQH–SC heterostructure as the existence of a low-energy theory that pins the dual fields $\Theta$ and $\Phi$ in distinct spatial regions separated by an interface.

We conclude this section with a comment on the interface between the one-dimensional SC and the FQ(A)H insulator (i.e., replacing the region labelled $t$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:RecapFigure}a) by the FQ(A)H insulator such that the edge states form a U-turn hugging the green $\Delta$-regions. It turns out that this situation can be obtained from our setup in the strong tunneling limit. Note that, effectively, strong tunneling can also be reached for weak bare tunneling amplitudes, if the residual interactions turn the coupling constant $t$ to be relevant. From the perspective of the quantum Hall state~\cite{Haldane1994}, in the infrared, the junction has become transparent to transporting fractional charges~\cite{KaneFisher1992}. We refer to this phenomenon as ``healing'' of the trench.
As a corollary of relevance for the physics discussed in this paper, the PZMs are theoretically expected also at the end of a superconducting finger sticking into the FQ(A)H liquid -- as experimentally realized in Ref.~\cite{GuelKim2022} -- provided that at least $\Delta$ is a relevant perturbation.


\subsection{Parafermions beyond Laughlin states}

While the previous derivation, using Laughlin states, seems to only realize $\mathbb Z_{2k}$ parafermions, it has also been shown that a similar construction can be carried out in the presence of more complex states. For instance, the FQH states with filling fractions $\nu=2/3$ can realize a double sine-Gordon model with three-fold periodicity (i.e., replacing $``2k"$ by $``3"$ in the trigonometric functions of Eq.~\eqref{eq:reviewBosonizedTunnelingPairing}) and thus $\mathbb Z_3$ parafermions. There are multiple different realizations of the FQH states with filling fraction $\nu=2/3$ that realize the same topological order but differ significantly in their physical implications at the edge theory. The (spin-unpolarized) $\nu=2/3$ Halperin 112 state \cite{Halperin1983} describes two coupled 
quantum Hall fluids with opposite spin polarizations (see Fig.~\ref{fig:FQHStates}a), which are often considered SU(2) symmetric \cite{Mong2014, Vaezi2014a}.  
Another $\nu=2/3$ state, relevant to spin-polarized Landau levels and non-degenerate Chern bands and believed to be realized in twisted MoTe$_2$ or rhombohedral graphene, is the particle–hole conjugate of the $\nu=1/3$ Laughlin state. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:FQHStates}, this state can be understood as a $\nu=1/3$ Laughlin liquid of holes in an otherwise filled Landau level or Chern band \cite{Laughlin1983, Girvin1984}. Its edge hosts two counterpropagating modes, an integer mode supporting electron excitations of charge $-e$ and a fractional mode supporting quasiparticle excitations of charge $e/3$, which are separated by the distance $r$, where $e>0$ is the elementary charge. The latter state can occur spin-polarized or spin-unpolarized \cite{XieGuoZhang1989, EisenesteinWest1990}. However, since the FCI state realized in twisted MoTe$_2$ as well as rhombohedral multilayer graphene is most likely spin-polarized \cite{WangXiao2024, DongSenthil2024}, we focus in this work only on the polarized case. 

To understand this in more detail, it is worthwhile to revisit the construction of the electron from FQH edges via vertex operators. Let a general FQH edge host $\boldsymbol{\phi} =(\phi_{k_1},\phi_{k_2},\dots,\phi_{k_N})$ edge bosons. Here, the $k_j$ are elements of some index set. 
If all fields have a unit compactification radius, a generic quasiparticle is defined by the integer-valued vector $\v l$ via $V_{\v l }\sim e^{i\v l\cdot\boldsymbol{\phi}}$. For $V_{\v l}$ being an electron, it must create a quasiparticle of charge $-e$, and have a statistical angle $\theta=n\pi$, with $n$ being odd, such that the electronic vertex operators anti-commute. These constraints still allow infinitely many $\v l_j$ that fulfill these criteria. Thus, the physical electron can be constructed from a superposition of all these possible electron operators, that is \cite{WenBook2007}
\begin{equation}
    \psi = \sum_{j}c_j e^{\v l_{j}\cdot \boldsymbol \phi},
    \label{eq:electronOperatorGeneral}
\end{equation}
where the coefficients $c_j$ are determined by microscopic details. While all $V_{\v l_j}$ have the same physical charge and fermionic exchange statistics, their scaling dimensions are in general different. Thus, if one is only interested in the low-energy theory, one may only keep the most relevant operators in the sum \eqref{eq:electronOperatorGeneral}.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{FQHState.png}
    \caption{a) Two quantum Hall liquids at filling $\nu=1/3$ with opposite spin polarization are coupled to form an SU(2)-symmetric $\nu=2/3$ Halperin (112) state. 
    b) Spin polarized charge conjugate $\nu=2/3$ state. The edge consists of two counterpropagating edge modes.}
    \label{fig:FQHStates}
\end{figure} 

In the Halperin state, since the edge theory is symmetric under flipping the spin, one can construct two equally relevant fermionic operators. One corresponds to a spin-up and the other to a spin-down electron. Considering spin-conserving tunneling and singlet pairing, bosonization results in a double sine-Gordon model of the shape in Eq.~\eqref{eq:reviewBosonizedTunnelingPairing} \cite{Mong2014, Vaezi2014a}. 

The situation becomes more complicated once one considers the spin-polarized state. This is because there is no underlying spin-SU(2) symmetry. For instance, perturbing around the decoupled electron $\phi_{\tau1}$ and $e/3$-quasi-hole $\phi_{\tau1/3}$ edge theory, the two most relevant electronic vertex operators are 
\begin{equation}
    \psi_{\tau1} \sim e^{i\phi_{\tau1}}\quad\text{and}\quad \psi_{\tau1/3} \sim e^{i3\phi_{\tau1/3}},
\end{equation}
where $\Delta_1 =\frac{1}{2}$ and $\Delta_{1/3}=\frac{3}{2}$ are the scaling dimensions of the respective operators in the absence of residual interactions. Thus, for small interaction around the fixed point of decoupled electron and quasi-hole edge modes, the largest low-energy contribution comes from the electron edge modes, which would yield Majorana fermions at the interface, but no parafermions. On the other hand, if the interactions are strong such that $\Delta_{1/3}<\Delta_1$, 
only the $1/3$ mode would contribute, and the interface traps $\mathbb Z_6$ parafermions. This illustrates 
that only a non-trivial combination of several electron operators can actually give rise to $\mathbb Z_3$ parafermions in analogy to the Halperin state. 
However, it is not a priori clear which tunneling and pairing channels of these multiple 
electron operators will contribute most and must be handled simultaneously. 

In the remainder of the paper, we resolve these conceptual barriers by considering the vicinity of a fixed point at which the electron and quasihole modes reorganize into a charge mode with charge $-2e/3$ and a neutral mode, and scrutinize the parameter regime hosting PZMs. At this \textit{Kane-Fisher-Polchinski} (KFP)~\cite{KaneFisherPolchinski1994} fixed point, the two modes are decoupled and do not interact with each other. Here, this fixed point can be reached by two means: For clean samples, interactions need to be fine-tuned along a line of fixed points (for the stability of PZMs, exact fine-tuning is not necessary, see Sec.~\ref{sec:cleanCase} below). Alternatively, the decoupled fixed point is stabilized (i.e., it is an attractive fixed point) in the presence of
disorder-induced quasiparticle backscattering. The attractive 
KFP fixed point in the presence of disorder was originally proposed to explain the experimentally observed robust two-terminal conductance of $G=\frac{2}{3}\frac{e^2}{h}$, in contrast to an expected non-universal conductance $G$ for a clean system of
interacting electron and quasihole modes. 

Starting from the vicinity of this fixed point, we derive a Hamiltonian of the shape of Eq.~\eqref{eq:reviewBosonizedTunnelingPairing} as the appropriate low-energy theory and demonstrate that, through the construction outlined above, $\mathbb Z_3$ parafermions can be trapped at the interface between tunneling and superconducting regions. We first consider the clean but fine-tuned case to demonstrate the mechanism and then discuss the more generic case of disordered edges and how the disorder influences the arguments laid out in Sec.~\ref{sec:MinimaAndDerivationOfParafermions}. 
Our results reveal a sizable region of parameter space in which PZMs are stabilized.


\section{Microscopic Model}
\label{sec:microscopicModel}

\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ExperimentUVModel.png}
    \caption{\textbf{a)} Schematic of the interface of a tunneling (t) and pairing ($\Delta$) region coupled to two $\nu=2/3$ spin-polarized FQH states. The top and bottom edges consist of two counterpropagating edge modes, one carrying charge $-e$ (blue) and one carrying charge $e/3$ (red). At each edge, the modes are separated by a distance $r$. The two edges themselves are separated by the distance $d$. \textbf{d)} Internal structure of the neutral dipole moments (purple arrow). An ``up" dipole consists of three quasiholes and one electron. A ``down" dipole is an electron hole together with three charge $e/3$ quasiparticles.}
    \label{fig:UVModel}
\end{figure}

In this section we recapitulate the general edge structure of the $\nu=2/3$ spin polarized FQH state and derive a microscopic model that describes the heterostructure as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:UVModel}. Thereby, we discuss all the important objects such as electron operators represented as vertex operators of the bosonic edge modes and an emergent $\mathfrak{su}(2)_1$ algebra that describes physical charge dipole moments. 


\subsection{Ultraviolet Edge Theory}

The canonical starting point for the edge theory of a spin-polarized $\nu=\frac{2}{3}$ fractional quantum Hall state consists of two counterpropagating bosonic edge modes: an electronic mode $\phi_1$ carrying charge $-e$ and a fractional quasihole mode $\phi_{1/3}$ carrying charge $e/3$ \cite{FradkinBook2013, WenBook2007} with the commutation relations 
($p,q \in \{1,1/3\}$)
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    [\phi_{t,p}(x),\phi_{t,q}(x')]
    &=
    i\pi(K^{-1})_{pq}\,\text{sign}(x-x'),
    \label{eq:CommutatorTop}\\
    [\phi_{b,p}(x),\phi_{b,q}(x')]
    &=
    -i\pi(K^{-1})_{pq}\,\text{sign}(x-x'),
    \label{eq:CommutatorBottom}\\
    [\phi_{t,p}(x),\phi_{b,q}(x')]
    &=
    -i\pi(K^{-1})_{pq}.
    \label{eq:CommutatorMixed}
\end{align}
dictated by the ${K}$ matrix
\begin{align}
    K = \begin{pmatrix}
        1 & 0\\
        0 & -3
    \end{pmatrix}.
    \label{eq:bareKmatrix}
\end{align}
\end{subequations}

We consider the setup shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:UVModel}b, where the electronic and quasi-hole modes are separated by a distance $r$. The top $(t)$ and bottom $(b)$ edges, separated by a distance $d$, are coupled through a tunneling-dominated region for $x<0$ and a CAR-dominated region for $x>0$, forming an interface at $x=0$.

The microscopic Hamiltonian
reads
\begin{subequations}
\begin{equation}
    H = H_\text{kin}+ 
    H_\text{int} + H_t + H_\Delta + H_\text{dis}, \label{eq:fullHamiltonian}
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align}
    H_\text{kin} & = \sum_{\tau,p}v^{(0)}_p \int \dd x (\partial_x\phi_{\tau p})^2, \\
    H_\text{int} & = \sum_{\tau\tau'= t,b}\int \text{d}x\text{d}x'\text{d}y\text{d}{y'} \Big [U_{\tau\tau'}(x,x',y,y') \notag \\
    &\times \rho_{\tau}(x,y)\rho_{\tau'}(x', y') \Big ], 
    \label{eq:HintInUV}\\
    H_t & = -\int_{-\infty}^0 \dd x t  (\psi^\dagger_t\psi_b + \text{H.c.}), \\
    H_\Delta &  = \int_{0}^\infty\dd x \Delta  (\psi_t \psi_b + \text{H.c.}), \\
    H_\text{dis} & = \frac{1}{a}\sum_{\tau=t,b}\int\dd x \left(\xi_\tau(x) e^{i(\phi_{\tau1}+3\phi_{\tau1/3})}+\text{H.c.}\right).
    \label{eq:DisorderedHamiltonian}
\end{align}
\label{eq:UVFullHamiltonian}
\end{subequations}
Here, $a$ is a short-distance cut-off.
In the subsequent sections, we elaborate in detail on each term of this Hamiltonian.

The first term contains the kinetic energy of the bosonic modes with bare velocity $v^{(0)}_p$ predominantly determined by the confining edge potential.

All inter- and intra-edge interactions are encoded in the interaction kernel $U(x,x',y,y')$, which includes electrostatics and all other possible microscopic interaction channels of the charge densities $\rho_\tau(x,y)$. In particular, these density interactions renormalize the mode velocities. In the following, we write $v_{p} = v^{(0)}_p +\delta v_{p,\text{int}}$, where $\delta v_{p,\text{int}}$ contains all {intra-edge interaction} corrections due to Eq.~\eqref{eq:HintInUV}.  

The terms $H_t$ and $H_\Delta$ correspond, respectively, to tunneling and pairing between the two edges, and are written in terms of the physical electron operators.
The physical electron operators $\psi_\tau=\sum_{\{j\}}c_{\tau j}\psi_{\tau j}$  consist of a sum of infinitely many electronic vertex operators \cite{WenBook2007}. The sum may be truncated to only keep the (most) RG-relevant operators, see Sec.~\ref{sec:Elop} for details. 

In addition, we include quenched disorder-induced backscattering, $H_{\rm dis}$, which couples counterpropagating edge modes on each side of the trench through charge-conserving tunneling. Disorder also generates forward scattering, which acts as a spatially varying chemical potential. This contribution can be eliminated from the quadratic Hamiltonian by a position-dependent shift of the bosonic fields.  Random interedge backscattering and/or crossed Andreev reflection will also be considered and will result in an $x$-dependence of $t, \Delta$. The scattering amplitude of intra-edge backscattering is described by the zero-mean Gaussian random variable $\xi_\tau(x)$ with local correlations. In the following, we will discuss three cases: (i) the clean case, where $\xi_\tau=0$, (ii) the case of correlated disorder (e.g., Coulomb impurities) where $\xi_t(x) = \xi_b^*(x)$  and
\begin{align}
\label{eq:DisorderPotential}
    \overline{\xi_\tau^*(x)\xi_{\tau}(x')}
    &=
    W\delta(x-x'),
\end{align} 
as well as (iii)  the case of uncorrelated disorder (e.g., short-range impurities),
where $\xi_t(x), \xi_b^*(x)$ are unrelated {but Eq.~\eqref{eq:DisorderPotential} still holds}. Here, the overline indicates a disorder average, and $W$ is the disorder strength. 

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{mechanisim.png}
    \caption{Schematic of the multi-step renormalization group flow and the hierarchy of energy scales. In the presence of disorder-induced backscattering, at $T_\text{KFP}$, the edge reorganizes into a decoupled charge-$2e/3$ and neutral modes. In a second RG step, inter-edge interactions gap out the neutral channel at $T_\sigma$. For edge-correlated disorder, tunneling and pairing channels persist the disorder average and gap out the charged modes, and parafermion zero-energy states emerge. }
    \label{fig:Mechanisim}
