\subsection{The KFP fixed point in the absence of tunneling and CAR}

Near the KFP fixed point  the edge modes decouple into a neutral $\phi_{\tau\sigma}$ and a charge-$2/3$ $\phi_{\tau\rho}$ modes (either due to fine-tuned interaction $U_{\tau\tau'}$ or driven by disorder)~\cite{KaneFisherPolchinski1994}. Thus, at the temperature scale $T_{\text{KFP}}$, all intra-edge interaction between the $\rho$ and $\sigma$ channels are effectively zero and disorder purely couples to the neutral channel, see Eq.~(\ref{eq:KFPbasis}). This first RG step is depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:Mechanisim} where the system flows from the UV theory a) to the KFP fixed point b). For fine-tuning in the clean case, $T_\text{KFP}=D_\text{UV}$, where $D_\text{UV}$ is the UV energy scale: 
the edge modes are decoupled throughout all energy scales. 
Far from the fine-tuned point, disorder-induced backscattering is needed to decouple the edges into a charged and neutral mode at a temperature scale $T_\text{KFP}=D_\text{UV}\left(\frac{W}{v_\sigma D_\text{UV}}\right)^{1/(3-2\Delta_W)}$ at which the disorder strength reaches strong coupling, with $v_\sigma$ the velocity of neutral modes (see below). Furthermore, $\Delta_W$ is the scaling dimension of the disorder term Eq.~\eqref{eq:DisorderedHamiltonian}. 

\subsubsection{Generalities}

At the KFP fixed point, the decoupled edge modes in terms of the UV fields are
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:KFPbasis}
\begin{align}
    \phi_{\tau,\rho}
    &=
    \sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}
    \left(
        \phi_{\tau,1}
        +
        \phi_{\tau,1/3}
    \right),
    \\
    \phi_{\tau,\sigma}
    &=
    \sqrt{\frac{1}{2}}
    \left(
        \phi_{\tau,1}
        +
        3\phi_{\tau,1/3}
    \right).
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
with the corresponding $K$ matrix
\begin{equation}
    K_{\text{KFP}}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix}
        1 & 0\\
        0 & -1
    \end{pmatrix}.
    \label{eq:KFPmatix}
\end{equation}
The commutation relations are identical to Eqs.~\eqref{eq:CommutatorTop}--\eqref{eq:CommutatorMixed}, with $K$ replaced by $K_{\text{KFP}}$ and $p,q\in\{\rho,\sigma\}$.  

These new fields may be interpreted as bound states of the underlying microscopic quasiparticles, namely electrons and quasiholes. In this sense, the charged $2e/3$ mode corresponds to an electron combined with one quasihole, while the neutral mode corresponds to one electron combined with three quasiholes. Thus, although the neutral modes are overall charge neutral, they retain an internal structure due to the microscopic spatial separation of the $\phi_{\tau1}$ and $\phi_{\tau1/3}$ fields. This gives rise to a dipole moment~\cite{BidMahalu2010} $\mathbf p=e\mathbf r,$
which points perpendicular to the propagation direction of the edge states (see Fig.~\ref{fig:UVModel}b). We therefore think of the dipole moment as a pseudospin with up and down states. This interpretation is further supported by the fact that the neutral sector exhibits an emergent 
$\mathfrak{su}(2)_1$ current algebra spanned by operators~\cite{KaneFisherPolchinski1994,ProtopopovGefenMirlin2017}
\begin{equation}
    J^z_{t/b}
    =
    \pm\frac{1}{2\sqrt{2}\pi}
    \partial_x\phi_{t/b,\sigma},
    \qquad
    J_\tau^\pm
    =
    \frac{1}{2\pi a}
    e^{\pm i\sqrt{2}\phi_{\tau\sigma}}.
    \label{eq:bosonicFieldRepOfCurrents}
\end{equation}
We also relate creation and annihilation operators of dipole moments to $x,y$ components via
$J_\tau^\pm=J_\tau^x\pm iJ_\tau^y$
in the usual way. 


\subsubsection{Hamiltonian near KFP fixed point}

Relegating hopping and CAR terms to Sec.~\ref{sec:microscopicTheoryOfTheHEterostructure}, it is convenient to express the Hamiltonian~\eqref{eq:UVFullHamiltonian} near the KFP fixed point using charge and neutral modes. The disorder contribution is

\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:FullHamKFPBasis}
\begin{equation}
    H_{\rm dis}
    =
    \frac{1}{a}
    \sum_{\tau=t,b}
    \int\dd x\,
    \left[
        \xi_\tau(x)J_\tau^+
        +
        \xi_\tau^*(x)J_\tau^-
    \right]. \label{eq:DisNearKFP}
\end{equation}
We next discuss the interaction contribution on a phenomenological level. There are interaction terms 
\begin{equation}
    H_\text{kin} + H_{\rm int} = H_\rho + H_\sigma + H_{\rho\sigma}
    \label{eq:Hkin+Hint}
\end{equation}
of three types: First, there are density-density interactions $H_\rho$, which contain intra-edge components absorbed into the mode velocity $v_\rho$ (we assume mirror symmetry with respect to reflections along the edge so that both edges are equivalent) and inter-edge components $g_\rho$. Second, there are dipole-dipole interactions $H_{\sigma} = H^{(0)}_{\sigma} + H_{\sigma}^{tb}$ -- in the most general case, there are 21 different terms connecting any of the three currents of either edge to another current. However, in the vicinity of the KFP fixed point, each edge is SU(2) symmetric so that there is only a single intra-edge component $v_\sigma$ while interedge interactions (denoted $\lambda_i$) may be assumed to be pseudo-spin diagonal. Finally, density-dipole interactions $H_{\rm \rho \sigma}$ vanish by definition at the KFP fixed point. In the vicinity of the KFP fixed point, the interactions described above are marginal and, therefore, serve as natural perturbations to two decoupled edges. Among all operators constructed from four-body interactions between fractional quasiparticles on the edges that do not transfer fractional charge, these are the most relevant interactions. All contributions to Hamiltonian \eqref{eq:Hkin+Hint} can be explicitly written as

\begin{align}
    H_\rho
    &=
    \frac{3}{2}
    \frac{u_\rho}{2\pi}
    \int\dd x\,
    \left[
        \frac{1}{K_\rho}
        (\partial_x\Theta_\rho)^2
        +
        K_\rho
        (\partial_x\Phi_\rho)^2
    \right], \label{eq:Hrho}
    \\
    H^{(0)}_\sigma
    &=
    \frac{2\pi v_\sigma}{3}
    \sum_{\substack{\tau = t,b, \\ i=x,y,z}}
    \int\dd x\,
    J_\tau^iJ_\tau^i, \\
    H_{\sigma}^{tb}&=
    2\pi v_\sigma
    \sum_{i=x,y,z}
    \int\dd x\, 
    \lambda_{i}
    J_t^iJ_b^i, \label{eq:Hsigma} \\
    H_{\rho \sigma} & = \sum_{\substack{\tau, \tau' = t,b, \\ i=x,y,z}} \int \dd x \tilde{g}_{\tau \tau'}^i \partial_x \phi_{\rho,\tau} J_{\tau'}^i. \label{eq:rhosigmaIA}
\end{align}
\label{eq:FullInterTrenchInteraction}
\end{subequations}
The charge-density interaction $g_\rho$ is absorbed into the renormalized velocity $u_\rho = v_\rho\sqrt{1-4\pi^2(g_\rho/v_\rho)^2}$ and the charge Luttinger parameter $ K_\rho = \sqrt{\frac{1-2\pi g_\rho/v_\rho}{1+2\pi g_\rho/v_\rho}}$. The constants $v_\sigma$ and $v_\rho$ denote, respectively, the mode velocities of the neutral and $2e/3$ charge mode at the KFP fixed point at which $\tilde g_{\tau, \tau'}^i$ vanishes by definition \cite{KaneFisherPolchinski1994}. 
The precise values of the couplings $g_\rho$ and $\lambda_i$ depend on the interaction kernel $U$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:UVFullHamiltonian}.
In the following, $v_\sigma/a$ will serve as the reference energy scale. 

The fields $\Phi_p$ and $\Theta_p$ are nonchiral superpositions of the chiral top and bottom fields:
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:NonChiral}
\begin{align}
    \Phi_\sigma
    &=
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
    (\phi_{t\sigma}+\phi_{b\sigma}),
    &
    \Theta_\sigma
    &=
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
    (\phi_{t\sigma}-\phi_{b\sigma}),
    \\
    \Phi_\rho
    &=
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}
    (\phi_{t\rho}+\phi_{b\rho}),
    &
    \Theta_\rho
    &=
    \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}}
    (\phi_{t\rho}-\phi_{b\rho}).
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
with the corresponding commutations relations 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    [\Phi_\sigma(x), \Theta_\sigma(x')] & = -i2\pi \theta(x-x'),\\
    [\Phi_\rho(x),\Theta_\rho(x')] &  =i\frac{2\pi}{3}\theta(x-x'). 
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Analogously to the charge sector, the neutral part of the Hamiltonian can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        H_\sigma
        &=
        \frac{u_\sigma}{4\pi}
        \int\dd x\,
        \left[
            \frac{1}{K_\sigma}
            (\partial_x\Theta_\sigma)^2
            +
            K_\sigma
            (\partial_x\Phi_\sigma)^2
        \right]
        \\
        &-
        \frac{v_\sigma}{2\pi a^2}
        \int\dd x\,
        \left[
            \lambda_b\cos(2\Theta_\sigma)
            +
            \lambda_p\cos(2\Phi_\sigma)
        \right].
    \end{split}
    \label{eq:sigmaAbelianBosonized}
\end{equation}
Here,
$u_\sigma=v_\sigma\sqrt{1-(\lambda_z/2)^2}$ and $K_\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{1-\lambda_z/2}{1+\lambda_z/2}}$
are the renormalized neutral-mode velocity and Luttinger parameter, respectively. We further define the couplings
\begin{equation}
    \lambda_b
    =
    \frac{\lambda_x+\lambda_y}{2},
    \qquad
    \lambda_p
    =
    \frac{\lambda_x-\lambda_y}{2}. \label{eq:lambdabp}
\end{equation}
Their physical interpretation corresponds to backscattering ($\lambda_b$) and pairing ($\lambda_p$) of the dipole moments \cite{VaeyrynenGefen2022}. Since $\lambda_b$ and $\lambda_p$ couple the $J^\pm_\tau$ operators, we will refer to them 
as exchange interactions in terms of the pseudo-spin. Accordingly, $\lambda_z$ describes the dipole density interactions. 

\subsubsection{Dirty case}
\label{sec:DirtyReview}


To deal with the potentially non-zero disorder, we follow KFP~\cite{KaneFisherPolchinski1994} in that the term $H_\xi$ can be interpreted as a random magnetic field acting in the $x$-$y$ plane of the dipole moment. A suitable local SU(2)
gauge transformation 
can absorb the disorder coupling into the definition of the current operators $J_i$. 

To this end, we briefly review the transformation behavior of the $\mathfrak{su}(2)_1$ fields in the adjoint and fundamental representations.
Under local SO(3) transformations, the current algebra transforms as \cite{ProtopopovGefenMirlin2017, VaeyrynenGefen2022}
\begin{equation}
    J_{t/b}^{i} = S_{t/b}^{ij}\widetilde J_{t/b}^{j} \pm h^i_{t/b},\quad h^i_{\tau}=\frac{1}{8\pi}\epsilon_{ijk}\left[S_\tau\partial_x S_\tau^{\mathrm T}\right]_{jk},
    \label{eq:STrafo}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    S_\tau(x)
    =
    \mathcal T_x
    \exp\left(
        \int_{- \infty}^{x}dx'\,
        M_\tau(x')
    \right), \quad S_\tau (- \infty)=\mathbf 1.
\end{equation}
Here, $\mathcal T_x$ denotes path ordering, and
\begin{equation}
    M_\tau^{ij}(x)
    =
    4\pi\epsilon_{ijk}h_\tau^k.
\end{equation}
The matrix $M_\tau$ can further be expanded as
\begin{equation}
    M_\tau
    =
    \sum_{i=x,y,z}
    m_\tau^iL_i,
    \qquad
    m_\tau^i
    =
    4\pi h_\tau^i, \label{eq:littlem}
\end{equation}
where $L_i$ are the generators of SO(3) (adjoint representation). The current operators $\widetilde J_{\tau}^i$ have the same representation in terms of bosonic fields as in \eqref{eq:bosonicFieldRepOfCurrents} but with the transformed bosonic fields $\tilde \phi_{\tau\sigma}$.

The $\mathfrak{su}(2)_1$ current algebra also has a spinor representation given by the vertex operators 
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf z_\tau = \begin{pmatrix}
        z_{\tau+} \\
        z_{\tau -}
    \end{pmatrix},\quad\text{where}\quad z_{\tau\sigma} = e^{\pm i\phi_{\tau\sigma}/\sqrt{2}}.
    \label{eq:SpinorRep}
\end{equation}
The spinor $\mathbf z_\tau$ transforms according to the fundamental representation of SU(2) corresponding to the orthogonal rotation $S_\tau(x)$, i.e.
\begin{equation}
   \mathbf z_\tau = U_{\tau}(x) \tilde{\mathbf z}_\tau,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
    U_\tau = \mathcal T_x \exp\left(-\frac{i}{2}\int_{- \infty}^x\dd{x'} m_\tau ^i \sigma_i\right)
\end{equation}
with $\sigma_i$ being the Pauli matrices in $\pm$ space and $m_\tau^i$ is defined in Eq.~\eqref{eq:littlem}.

We return to the disorder potential $\xi_\tau(x)$ entering Eq.~\eqref{eq:DisNearKFP} which may be absorbed into ``comoving'' fields by choosing \cite{VaeyrynenGefen2022}
\begin{equation}
    h_\tau^i =
    \begin{cases}
        s_\tau\dfrac{\frac{2}{3}\xi_\tau^i -\lambda_i\xi_{\bar\tau}^i} {v_\sigma\left(\lambda_i^2-(2/3)^2\right)}, & \text{for } i=x,y, \\[6pt] 0, & \text{for } i=z,
    \end{cases}
    \label{eq:hValuesForGeneralDisorder}
\end{equation}
where $\bar\tau$ denotes the edge opposite to $\tau$. 

The matrix $U_\tau(x)\in\text{SU(2)}$, or equivalently its adjoint representation $S_\tau(x)\in \text{SO}(3)$, may be viewed as the endpoint of a random walk on the corresponding group which is induced from a random walk $h_\tau^i(x)$ in the tangent space by means of the path-ordered exponential map. 
The spatial coordinate parametrizes the walk with boundary condition $U_\tau(x)\vert_{x \rightarrow - \infty}=\mathbf 1$. For short-range disorder, the probability measure $\mathcal P(U_\tau) \mathcal D{U_\tau}$, which inherits the white-noise lack of spatial correlations on distances larger than the disorder length scale $l_\text{dis} \sim {v_\sigma^2}/{W}$, approaches the normalized Haar measure $\mathcal D {U_\tau}$,  
that is $\mathcal P(U_\tau) \mathcal D {U_\tau}{\simeq}\mathcal D{U_\tau}$, see App. \ref{AppSec:disorderGaugeTrafo} for details.



\subsection{Tunneling and CAR near the KFP Fixed Point}
\label{sec:microscopicTheoryOfTheHEterostructure}

Having rewritten all interaction terms in a basis convenient near
the KFP fixed point, we now turn to tunneling and pairing terms in the Hamiltonian \eqref{eq:UVFullHamiltonian}. 


\subsubsection{Electron Operator}
\label{sec:Elop}

At the KFP fixed point, each edge possesses two most relevant electron operators,
\begin{equation}
    \psi_{\tau\pm}
    =
    \frac{\kappa_\pm}{\sqrt{2\pi a}}
    e^{
        i\sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}\phi_{\tau\rho}
        \pm
        i\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}}\phi_{\tau\sigma}
    },
    \label{eq:PMfermionDefinition}
\end{equation}
with identical scaling dimensions $\Delta_\pm=1$. The auxiliary Majorana fermions $\kappa_\pm$ ensure the correct anticommutation relations, and the physical Hilbert space is fixed by the constraint $i\kappa_+\kappa_-=1$. In the original field basis, the operator $\psi_{\tau-}\sim e^{i\phi_{\tau1}}$ corresponds to an original electron, while the $\psi_{\tau+}$ electron operator is the combination of 2 electrons and 3 quasi-holes. 

In the important case when the KFP fixed point is disorder stabilized, it is a crucial observation that the quoted most relevant electron operators presented in Eq.~\eqref{eq:PMfermionDefinition} are not written in the laboratory frame but rather in the comoving frame associated to Eq.~\eqref{eq:hValuesForGeneralDisorder}. Specifically, it is convenient to use Eq.~\eqref{eq:SpinorRep} and collect the two electron operators into the spinor $\Psi_{\tau} = (\psi_{\tau+}, \psi_{\tau-})$ that can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}
    \Psi_\tau = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi a}}e^{i\sqrt{\frac{3}{2}}\phi_{\tau\rho}} D_\kappa \mathbf z_\tau,\text{ where } D_\kappa = \begin{pmatrix}
        \kappa_+ & 0 \\ 0 & \kappa_-
    \end{pmatrix}. \label{eq:SpinorElectron}
\end{equation}
This demonstrates that a rotation $S_\tau(x)$ in the current algebra also causes an according rotation of the electron spinor $\Psi\sim \mathbf z$, that is
\begin{equation}
    \Psi_\tau = \hat{ U}_\tau \tilde{\Psi}_\tau,\text{ with } \hat{U}_\tau = D_\kappa U_\tau D_\kappa. \label{eq:PsiSpinor}
\end{equation}
The physical electron entering Eq.~\eqref{eq:UVFullHamiltonian} is then given by the superposition
\begin{equation}
    \psi_\tau
    =
    c_{\tau+}\psi_{\tau+}
    +
    c_{\tau-}\psi_{\tau-},
    \label{eq:electronOperator}
\end{equation}
where the coefficients satisfy
$|c_{\tau+}|^2+|c_{\tau-}|^2=1$
and are determined by the microscopic details of the edges. Note that generically, these coefficients can also have a spatial dependence.

\subsubsection{Pairing and Tunneling terms}

In the following, we express the tunneling ($H_t$) and pairing ($H_\Delta$) Hamiltonians at the KFP fixed point in terms of the fermionic operators $\psi_{\tau\pm}$.  
At the KFP fixed point without inter-edge interactions, the electron operator is given by Eq.~\eqref{eq:electronOperator} and has scaling dimension $\Delta_e=1$. Thus, pairing and tunneling are marginal perturbations around the KFP fixed point.


Using the spinor representation~\eqref{eq:SpinorElectron}
one can write hopping and CAR terms of Eq.~\eqref{eq:UVFullHamiltonian} as
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    H_t & 
   =
    -\int \dd x \Psi_t^\dagger\mathcal T\Psi_b+\text{H.c.}, \label{eq:SpinorHopt}\\
   H_\Delta &=
 \int d x   \Psi_t^{\mathrm T}\mathcal D\Psi_b + \text{H.c.},
\end{align}\label{eq:SpinorHopDelta}
\end{subequations}
in terms of
the tunneling matrix
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal T
    =
    \sum_{\alpha=0,x,y,z}
    t_\alpha\sigma_\alpha,
\end{equation}
and the matrix-valued proximity induced order parameter 
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal D
    =
    (d_0+\mathbf d\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma})
    i\sigma_y.
\end{equation}
 The singlet pairing amplitude $d_0$ is chosen to be real, while the triplet pairing amplitudes $d_i$, with $i\in\{x,y,z\}$, are complex numbers with phases $\delta_i$. These amplitudes are collected into a vector
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf d
    =
    (d_x,d_y,d_z).
\end{equation}
For the disordered case, it is worthwhile to highlight that Eq.~\eqref{eq:SpinorHopDelta} is written in the lab frame and that both $\mathcal T$ and $\mathcal D$ may in the most generic case be spatially dependent. Their spatial dependence absorbs the spatial dependence of $t,\Delta$ discussed above Eq.~\eqref{eq:DisorderPotential} and of $c_{\tau,\pm}$ discussed below Eq.~\eqref{eq:electronOperator}. 
The tunneling strength $t$ and pairing amplitude $\Delta$ are determined by the overlap of the exponentially localized top and bottom electron states.
They can be expected to be exponentially small in the trench width $d$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
    t,\Delta\sim e^{-d/\zeta_{\text{eff}}},
    \label{eq:tDeltaSmall}
\end{equation}
where $\zeta_\text{eff}$ is the effective localization length scale describing the wave-function envelope perpendicular to the edge.

Using Eqs.~\eqref{eq:NonChiral} and \eqref{eq:PMfermionDefinition} to  individually bosonize each channel (see App.~\ref{secApp:Bosonization}), yields 
\begin{widetext}
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
        H_t
        &=
         \int_{-\infty}^{0}\dd x\,\frac{2t_0}{\pi a}
        \cos(\Theta_\sigma)
        \sin(3\Theta_\rho)
        +
        \frac{2}{\pi a}
        \int_{-\infty}^{0}\dd x\,
        \left[
            t_z\sin(\Theta_\sigma)
            -
            t_x\sin(\Phi_\sigma)
            -
            t_y\cos(\Phi_\sigma)
        \right]
        \cos(3\Theta_\rho),
    \label{eq:BosonizedHt}\\
        H_\Delta
        &= 
        -\int_{0}^{\infty}\dd x\, \frac{2d_0}{\pi a}
        \cos(\Theta_\sigma)
        \sin(3\Phi_\rho)-
        \int_{0}^{\infty}\dd x\,
        \frac{2|d_x|}{\pi a}
        \sin(\Phi_\sigma)
        \cos(3\Phi_\rho+\delta_x)
        \notag \\
        &+
        \int_{0}^{\infty}\dd x\,
        \frac{2|d_y|}{\pi a}
        \cos(\Phi_\sigma)
        \cos(3\Phi_\rho+\delta_y)

