\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

A central problem in four-dimensional $\mathcal N=4$ super-Yang--Mills theory is to identify its $1/16$-BPS spectrum at strong coupling.
Through the AdS/CFT correspondence, this spectrum includes the microstates of supersymmetric black holes in $AdS_5$ \cite{Maldacena:1997re,Witten:1998qj,Gutowski:2004ez,Kunduri:2006ek}.
For $SU(N)$ at large $N$, these black holes have an entropy of order $N^2$, requiring exponentially many states that cannot be accounted for by a gas of supergravitons.

A well-understood part of this spectrum consists of graviton operators, which belong to short gauge-theory multiplets and match precisely with the Kaluza--Klein multiplets of supergravity on $S^5$ \cite{Witten:1998qj}.
The challenge is to identify the additional states needed to account for black hole entropy.
A natural tool is the superconformal index, a protected signed count of BPS states \cite{Kinney:2005ej,Romelsberger:2005eg}.
However, its early large $N$ evaluation matches the supergraviton index, so that it appears to not detect any additional contributions corresponding to black hole microstates \cite{Kinney:2005ej}.

A complementary approach constructs BPS operators directly through the cohomology of a nilpotent supercharge $Q$ at leading nontrivial order in weak coupling \cite{Grant:2008sk}.
In this language, BPS operators are described by $Q$-closed operators modulo $Q$-exact ones.
The subsequent analysis in \cite{Chang:2013fba} constructs explicit representatives of graviton cohomology classes and shows that their products span the cohomology in the infinite $N$ limit, where trace relations trivialize.
Additional classes must therefore arise through finite-rank trace relations, anticipating the mechanism now termed fortuity \cite{Chang:2024zqi}.
Explicit examples nevertheless remained elusive for some time.

To connect this weak-coupling construction to black hole microstates at strong coupling, one must determine which states remain BPS as the coupling increases.
The cohomology of the classical, or tree-level, supercharge, denoted below by $Q_0$, describes the states that remain BPS in the one-loop dilatation spectrum.
A non-renormalization conjecture asserts that this spectrum receives no further corrections \cite{Kinney:2005ej,Grant:2008sk,Chang:2013fba}, and was reinforced more recently in \cite{Chang:2022mjp}.
That argument, however, assumes that the supercharge continues to act on composite operators by the graded Leibniz rule.
Quantum contractions generate additional terms that invalidate this assumption \cite{Budzik:2023xbr,Budzik:2025zvu} and can lift classical cohomology classes, as recent results demonstrate \cite{Chang:2025mqp,Choi:2025bhi} and as we discuss below.

The index, instead, is protected independently of this conjecture.
Its earlier agreement with the supergraviton index does not exclude black hole contributions, which could be obscured by boson--fermion cancellations.
Following an observation in \cite{Hosseini:2017mds}, the evaluation of the index at complex chemical potentials does turn out to exhibit the required growth at large $N$ and reproduces the entropy of supersymmetric $AdS_5$ black holes \cite{Cabo-Bizet:2018ehj,Choi:2018hmj,Benini:2018ywd}.
Further analyses identify this growth directly in index coefficients, including at finite $N$ \cite{Murthy:2020scj,Agarwal:2020zwm}.

Subsequent to these advances, the operator approach yielded the first classical $SU(2)$ cohomology classes beyond multigravitons in \cite{Chang:2022mjp}.
Explicit representatives, infinite families of such non-gravitons, higher rank examples and graviton dressings were discovered soon after in \cite{Choi:2022caq,Choi:2023znd,Choi:2023vdm,deMelloKoch:2024pcs}.
The role of trace relations is captured by the distinction between \emph{monotone} and \emph{fortuitous} classes, defined relative to a family of CFTs, obtained in the $\mathcal{N}=4$ case by varying the rank of the gauge group \cite{Chang:2024zqi}.
Namely, a monotone class admits a representative whose $Q$-closedness does not rely on finite-rank trace relations and therefore remains closed throughout the family.
On the other hand, a fortuitous class has no such representative, and its closedness relies on trace relations that necessarily hold only at particular ranks.
The proposed connection between fortuitous states and black hole microstates has inspired further studies of their dynamics and analogous phenomena in other models.\footnote{Related work constructs the weak-coupling BPS operator associated with the first black hole cohomology, studies the rank dependence of its anomalous dimension and the spectrum of nearby non-BPS states \cite{Chang:2023decoding,Budzik:2023vtr}. Complementary approaches organize finite-rank state spaces through trace relations and gauge invariants \cite{Lee:2023iil,deMelloKoch:2025ngs,deMelloKoch:2025cec,deMelloKoch:2025qeq,deMelloKoch:2025rkw,deMelloKoch:2025eqt}, while \cite{Chen:2024BPSChaos,Chen:2026vml,Belin:2026ssc} study BPS dynamics and chaos. Other settings include relevant deformations of $\mathcal N=4$ SYM \cite{Choi:2025pqr}, the conifold \cite{Choi:2026faq}, supersymmetric SYK \cite{Chang:2024lxt,Chryssanthacopoulos:2026mni}, D1--D5 \cite{Chang:2025rqy,Hughes:2025car,Chang:2025wgo,Belin:2026vfd,Giusto:2026rpl}, ABJM \cite{Belin:2025ABJM,Behan:2025ABJM,Behan:2026ABJM}, and the ABJ vector model with a higher-spin dual \cite{Kim:2025vup}.}

As alluded to above, recent results for the $S$-dual $SO(7)$ and $Sp(6)$ theories invalidate the non-renormalization conjecture.
A comparison of truncated indices has exhibited an additional fortuitous class in the $SO(7)$ BMN sector, absent in the corresponding $Sp(6)$ sector \cite{Gadde:2025yoa}.
A subsequent comparison of the full classical cohomologies shows that the mismatch also includes a monotone class \cite{Chang:2025mqp}.
The latter is non-Coulomb, namely, it vanishes when all letters are restricted to the Cartan subalgebra of the gauge symmetry.
Although the two classes cancel in the protected index, the mismatch is incompatible with coupling independence of the classical $Q_0$-cohomology if $S$-duality holds.
And indeed, shortly thereafter, the additional pair was shown to lift at next-to-leading order through the generalized Konishi anomaly \cite{Choi:2025bhi}.
It follows that even a multigraviton class, albeit of non-Coulomb type, can be removed from the quantum cohomology.

Building on the holomorphic-twist approach to quantum supercharges \cite{Budzik:2023xbr}, the analysis in \cite{Budzik:2025zvu} determines the complete first quantum correction $Q_1$ in the $\mathcal{N}=4$ theory, including its action on derivative letters.
Nilpotence makes $[Q_1]$ a differential on $Q_0$-cohomology, although it need not obey a Leibniz rule.
Its non-zero images describe pairs of classical classes removed at this order.\footnote{We use ``one-loop lifting'' for lifting by the first quantum correction to the supercharge, distinct from the one-loop dilatation spectrum described by classical $Q_0$-cohomology.}
Such liftings also occur among higher fortuitous classes in the $SU(2)$ BMN sector, while the lightest class and several graviton dressings of it and its descendants remain unlifted \cite{Kim:2026rnx}.
These developments raise the question of which classical classes can participate in quantum lifting.

In this paper, we investigate the role of non-Coulomb classes in these pairings.
Our starting point is a simple family of monotones built from the gaugino bilinear,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Sn-intro}
    \mathcal S=\Tr(\lambda_+\lambda_-),
    \qquad [\mathcal S^m]\neq0,
    \qquad r<m<h,
\end{equation}
where $r$ and $h\equiv h^\vee$ are the rank and dual Coxeter number of the gauge algebra.
The non-vanishing of these powers in $Q_0$-cohomology follows from classical chiral-ring results \cite{Cachazo:2002ry,Witten:2003ye,Etingof:2003dd,Etingof:2003fy}.
On the Cartan, however, $\mathcal S$ is a sum of only $r$ nilpotent bilinears, so $\mathcal S^m$ vanishes there for $m>r$.
The powers in \eqref{eq:Sn-intro} therefore form a tower of non-Coulomb monotones in $SO(N)$ theories with $N\geq7$, as also observed in \cite{Choi:2025pqr}.
Note that the tower is empty for the special unitary and symplectic families, where $h-1=r$.

We study these classes using field restrictions, following the idea of the Cartan-based non-$Q$-exactness argument in \cite{Witten:2003ye}.
Instead of restricting to the Cartan, we retain a small set of mutually commuting root generators for $\lambda_+$ and the corresponding negative-root generators for $\lambda_-$.\footnote{SvL thanks Chi-Ming Chang for sharing this idea with him.}
This extends the method to the non-Coulomb powers in \eqref{eq:Sn-intro}.
Comparing the restricted powers of $\mathcal S$ with the similarly restricted $Q_0$-exact operators establishes their non-exactness and gives a concrete picture of these classes beyond the Cartan.
We give explicit examples for $\mathfrak{so}_7$, $\mathfrak{so}_8$ and $\mathfrak{so}_9$, and report results through $\mathfrak{so}_{12}$.
Related restrictions also enter constructions of black hole cohomologies \cite{deMelloKoch:2024pcs}.


For $SO(7)$, we show that $\mathcal S^4$ is a superconformal descendant of the non-Coulomb monotone identified in \cite{Chang:2025mqp,Choi:2025bhi}, which we call the Chang--Lin--Choi--Lee (CLCL) monotone.
Its known lifting therefore implies that $\mathcal S^4$ lifts as well.
We independently construct a fortuitous source $F_4$ and verify
\begin{equation}\label{eq:intro-quantum-pairing}
    [Q_1][F_4]=c[\mathcal S^4]\,,
    \qquad c\neq0\,.
\end{equation}
The construction uses an explicit $\mathfrak{so}_7$ trace relation given in Appendix~\ref{app:trace-relations}, following the approach suggested in \cite{Choi:2023vdm,Chang:2024zqi}.
Our method extends to higher powers and ranks, and lifting the first operator in the non-Coulomb tower implies the lifting of the entire tower.

Finally, we establish two general restrictions on quantum pairings in the multi-trace sector of the classical gauge-group families, including operators with derivatives.
First, we show that every monotone class is annihilated by $[Q_1]$, and second, that every $[Q_1]$ image has vanishing Cartan restriction.
Consequently, every non-zero one-loop pairing has a fortuitous source and a non-Coulomb target.
This answers the question raised in \cite{Choi:2025bhi}: two monotones cannot pair, while a fortuitous source may pair with either a non-Coulomb monotone or another fortuitous class with vanishing Cartan restriction.
The fact that a non-Coulomb monotone can only lift through $Q_1$-exactness excludes the lifting of non-Coulomb BMN monotones, including those recently discovered for $SU(3)$ and $SU(4)$ \cite{Kim:2026bhc}.
Instead, a Coulomb monotone is both $[Q_1]$-closed and non-exact, and therefore survives at this order.
For these classes, we establish at finite rank the protection expected from the large-$N$ graviton picture.

A key step to obtain the second result is to show that any $Q_0$-closed operator $F$ in this sector with vanishing Cartan restriction satisfies, at fixed rank,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:intro-discriminant-dressing}
    \Delta^kF=Q_0H,
\end{equation}
for sufficiently large $k$ and some multi-trace $H$, where $\Delta$ is the discriminant of a scalar.
Repeated dressing by $\Delta$ therefore eventually annihilates any non-Coulomb class, whether monotone or fortuitous, in classical cohomology, providing a counterpart to the partial no-hair results for specific graviton dressings in \cite{Choi:2023znd}.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:rev-class-coh} reviews classical cohomology and fixes conventions, and Section~\ref{sec:index} presents the refined-index evidence.
Section~\ref{sec:nC-from-S} studies the gaugino powers and their restrictions.
Section~\ref{sec:quantum-lift} constructs the $SO(7)$ pairing and explains the higher-rank strategy, while Section~\ref{sec:gen-patterns} establishes the general restrictions on quantum lifting.
The appendices collect Lie algebra conventions, supporting chiral-ring arguments, explicit trace identities and details of the quantum pairing.



\section{Classical \texorpdfstring{$Q$}{Q}-cohomologies}\label{sec:rev-class-coh}

In this section, we fix the conventions for the classical supercharge and its cohomology.
We follow the conventions of Chang and Yin \cite{Chang:2013fba}, with the classical supercharge $Q\equiv Q^4_-$.
The letters that enter the $1/16$-BPS complex of $Q$ are denoted by
\begin{equation}
    \phi^n\equiv \Phi^{4n}\,,\qquad
    \psi_n\equiv-i\Psi_{n+}\,,\qquad
    \lambda_{\dot\alpha}\equiv\bar\Psi^4_{\dot\alpha}\,,\qquad
    f\equiv-iF_{++}\,,\qquad
    D_{\dot\alpha}\equiv D_{+\dot\alpha}\,.
\end{equation}
Each of these letters saturates the BPS bound $E=2J_L^3+q_1+q_2+q_3$ of $Q$, with the fields and their covariant derivatives taking values in the complexified gauge algebra $\mathfrak g$. 
Their corresponding free-theory quantum numbers are collected in Table~\ref{tab:n=4-letters} and include the scaling dimension $E$; the Cartan generators $J_L^3$ and $J_R^3$ of the Lorentz group $SU(2)_L\times SU(2)_R$, acting on undotted and dotted spinor indices, respectively; and the Cartan generators $q_1,q_2,q_3$ of the $SO(6)_R$ R-symmetry, which rotate the three complex scalars so that $\phi^m$ has $q_n=\delta_{mn}$.

