At tree level, the letters obey
\begin{equation}\label{eq:derivativesantisymm}
    [D_{\dot\alpha},D_{\dot\beta}]
    =\epsilon_{\dot\alpha\dot\beta}f\,,\qquad
    D_{\dot\alpha}\lambda^{\dot\alpha}=[\phi^n,\psi_n]\,,
\end{equation}
where, as in \cite{Chang:2013fba}, the Yang--Mills coupling is absorbed into the fields, so that the classical supercharge below is independent of $g_{\rm YM}$ and its loop corrections are organized in powers of $g_{\rm YM}^2$.
The classical supercharge acts on the undifferentiated letters as
\begin{equation}\label{Eq:SCalgebra}
    [Q,\phi^m]=0\,,\qquad
    \{Q,\lambda_{\dot\alpha}\}=0\,,\qquad
    \{Q,\psi_m\}=-i\epsilon_{mnp}[\phi^n,\phi^p]\,,\qquad
    [Q,f]=i[\phi^n,\psi_n]\,,
\end{equation}
and on a covariant derivative as
\begin{equation}
    [Q,D_{\dot\alpha}\xi]
    =-i[\lambda_{\dot\alpha},\xi]+D_{\dot\alpha}Q\xi\,,
\end{equation}
with graded brackets when $\xi$ is fermionic. 
By \eqref{eq:derivativesantisymm}, antisymmetric combinations of derivative letters can be expressed in terms of existing letters, so when constructing independent letters, it suffices to only retain symmetrized derivatives, with the gaugino index included in the symmetrization whenever the derivatives act on $\lambda_{\dot{\alpha}}$. 
We can introduce the auxiliary commuting variables $z^{\dot\alpha}$, which allow us to collect the fields and their symmetrized covariant derivatives into the generating fields \cite{Grant:2008sk,Chang:2013fba} 
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
        \phi^m(z)&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n!}
        (z^{\dot\alpha}D_{\dot\alpha})^n\phi^m\,,
        &\psi_m(z)&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n!}
        (z^{\dot\alpha}D_{\dot\alpha})^n\psi_m\,,\\
        \lambda(z)&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{(n+1)!}
        (z^{\dot\alpha}D_{\dot\alpha})^n
        (z^{\dot\beta}\lambda_{\dot\beta})\,,
        &f(z)&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n!}
        (z^{\dot\alpha}D_{\dot\alpha})^nf\,.
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
These generating fields are unconstrained, except for the condition $\lambda(0)=0$, and their transformations under $Q$ are given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:superalgebra-gener-fields}
    \begin{aligned}
    &[Q,\phi^m(z)]=-i[\lambda(z),\phi^m(z)]\,,\\
    &[Q,f(z)]=-i[\lambda(z),f(z)]+i[\phi^n(z),\psi_n(z)]\,,\\
    &\{Q,\psi_m(z)\}=-i\{\lambda(z),\psi_m(z)\}
    -i\epsilon_{mnp}[\phi^n(z),\phi^p(z)]\,,\\
    &\{Q,\lambda(z)\}=-i\lambda(z)^2\,.
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The action of $Q$ on each of the derivative letters can be determined by expanding each of these expressions in powers of $z$. In the above, the generating field $\lambda(z)$ should not be confused with either physical gaugino component, which we denote as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lambda-pm-definition}
    \lambda_+\equiv\lambda_{\dot +}\,,\qquad
    \lambda_-\equiv\lambda_{\dot -}
\end{equation}
throughout the rest of the paper.

We can further organize the generating fields into a single superfield by introducing three auxiliary Grassmann variables $\theta_{m}$ \cite{Chang:2013fba}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:chang-yin-superfield}
    \Psi(z,\theta)=-i\left[
    \lambda(z)+2\theta_n\phi^n(z)
    +\epsilon^{mnp}\theta_m\theta_n\psi_p(z)
    +4\theta_1\theta_2\theta_3f(z)\right]\,.
\end{equation}
By collectively writing the auxiliary variables as $\mathcal Z=(z,\theta)$, we can express $Q$-transformation and constraint in the compact form
\begin{equation}
    \{Q,\Psi(\mathcal Z)\}=\Psi(\mathcal Z)^2\,,\qquad
    \Psi(0)=0\,.
\end{equation}
For the classical gauge algebras considered below, the gauge-invariant operators can be constructed from traces of products of $z$- and $\theta$-derivatives of $\Psi$, evaluated at the origin $\mathcal{Z} = 0$. However, for the gauge algebra $\mathfrak{g}=\mathfrak{so}_{2r}$, these trace invariants must also be supplemented by Pfaffian-type contractions. 

\subsubsection*{Graviton representatives and monotone classes}

With $\partial_\pm\equiv\partial/\partial z^{\dot\pm}$ and $\partial_i\equiv\partial/\partial\theta_i$, the single-trace graviton representatives of \cite{Chang:2013fba} can be written as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:single-trace-graviton-representatives}
    \mathcal G=\left.
    \partial_+^{a_+}\partial_-^{a_-}\prod_{i=1}^3\partial_i^{b_i}\,
    \Tr\operatorname{Sym}_{\mathrm{gr}}\!\left[
    (\partial_+\Psi)^{k_+}(\partial_-\Psi)^{k_-}
    \prod_{i=1}^3(\partial_i\Psi)^{l_i}
    \right]\right|_{\mathcal Z=0}\,,
\end{equation}
where $a_\pm,l_i\geq0$ and $b_i,k_\pm\in\{0,1\}$ and $\operatorname{Sym}_{\mathrm{gr}}$ symmetrizes the factors inside the trace with a minus sign for each exchange of two fermionic factors.
These expressions are $Q$-closed prior to imposing (rank-dependent) trace relations.

A $Q$-cohomology class is represented by a $Q$-closed operator modulo $Q$-exact terms, and hence is non-trivial when its representative is not $Q$-exact.
The result we need is that, before imposing trace relations at finite rank, every single-trace $Q$-cohomology class is a linear combination of these graviton classes, and every multi-trace class is a linear combination of their products \cite{Chang:2013fba}.\footnote{These statements are established in mathematical terms in Proposition~5 and equation~(12) of \cite{Chang:2026bil} respectively for $\mathfrak{g}=\mathfrak{gl}_{\infty}$. In particular, it identifies the full infinite-rank cohomology with the multi-particle Fock space built from the single-trace classes, with the appropriate bosonic and fermionic statistics. See also remark 3 for the analogous statements in the orthogonal and symplectic cases.}
When the infinite-rank limit is taken at fixed charges, no finite-rank trace relations enter, and the graviton description of the classical multi-trace cohomology is complete.

As a brief aside, for orthogonal and symplectic algebras, transpose identities for the Lie algebra generators (see, \textit{e.g.}, \eqref{eq:so(n)-bil-form} and \eqref{eq:sp(n)-bil-form}) identify a trace with its reversed word, up to signs.
On the (anti-)symmetrized traces in \eqref{eq:single-trace-graviton-representatives}, reversing a word of $L$ letters gives the factor $(-1)^L$, so only even $L$ single-traces survive.
The even-length graviton generators and their products therefore give the corresponding description in the orthogonal and symplectic multi-trace sectors \cite{Gadde:2025yoa}.
This statement does not include the additional Pfaffian-type invariants of $\mathfrak{so}_{2r}$.

Following \cite{Chang:2024zqi}, a non-zero finite-rank class in the multi-trace sector is called monotone if it admits a representative that is $Q$-closed before rank-dependent trace relations are imposed.
Monotone classes are consequently the non-zero finite-rank images of the multi-graviton classes just described, since trace relations can make some of these classes dependent or $Q$-exact.
The classes that have no such representative are called fortuitous and their closedness relies on trace relations.
They are proposed in \cite{Chang:2024zqi} as the operators dual to black-hole microstates.

It is convenient to grade gauge-invariant operators by the total numbers of auxiliary $z$- and $\theta$-derivatives in their superfield expressions \eqref{eq:chang-yin-superfield},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mu-grading}
    \mu=(n_+,n_-,n_1,n_2,n_3) \, ,
\end{equation}
where $n_\pm$ count the $z^{\dot\pm}$ and $n_m$ the $\theta_m$ derivatives of $\Psi$.
Under this grading, each gaugino $\lambda_\pm$ carries a single $z^{\dot\pm}$ derivative, the scalar $\phi^m$ the single $\theta_m$ derivative, the fermion $\psi_m$ the complementary pair $\theta_n\theta_p$ of derivatives, and $f$ all three $\theta_{1,2,3}$ derivatives, while a covariant derivative $D_{\dot\alpha}$ adds one $z^{\dot\alpha}$ derivative to the letter it acts on.
For words built from the undifferentiated gauginos $\lambda_\pm$ alone, $n_\pm$ count their respective multiplicities, which we will refer to as their bidegree in Section~\ref{sec:nC-from-S}.

Two features of this grading will be used in the following.
First, $Q$ preserves $\mu$ while raising the number of letters by one, so that each $\mu$-sector is itself a $Q$-complex, graded by letter number.
Second, every letter carries at least one derivative of $\Psi$, which implies that the letter number is bounded by the total grading,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:letter-bound}
    L\leq\ |\mu|\ \equiv\ n_++n_-+n_1+n_2+n_3 \, ,
\end{equation}
with equality precisely when every letter carries a single derivative, which happens when a word is built using just the $\lambda_\pm$ and $\phi^m$ letters.
It follows that a fixed $\mu$-sector is a finite complex whose top piece, at $L=|\mu|$, is annihilated by $Q$.



\subsubsection*{Letter-based truncations}

Let $\mathcal C_{\mathcal N=4}$ denote the full Chang--Yin complex, with $\mathcal C_\lambda\subset\mathcal C_{\mathcal N=4}$ the subcomplex of words built from $\lambda(z)$ alone, \textit{i.e.}, from the gauginos and their covariant derivatives.
Since $\phi^m$, $\psi_m$ and $f$ each carry at least one $\theta$-derivative, $\mathcal C_\lambda$ is the sum of the $\mu$-sectors with $n_1=n_2=n_3=0$.
As $Q$ preserves $\mu$, $\mathcal C_\lambda$ is a direct summand of $\mathcal C_{\mathcal N=4}$ as a complex.
In particular, a non-trivial pure-gaugino class in the $Q$-cohomology of $\mathcal C_\lambda$ cannot become exact by allowing other $\mathcal N=4$ letters, since any $Q$-preimage would have to lie in the same $\mu$-sector.

The truncation to $\mathcal C_\lambda$ is an example of the letter-based truncations considered in \cite{Gadde:2025yoa}.
The best-known one is the BMN truncation \cite{Choi:2023znd,Choi:2023vdm}, that discards $\lambda_{\dot\alpha}$ and $D_{\dot\alpha}$ and keeps $\phi^m$, $\psi_m$ and $f$, and which by the same argument consists of the $\mu$-sectors with $n_+=n_-=0$.
In terms of the superfield, the BMN and pure-gaugino truncations restrict $\Psi(\mathcal Z)$ to $z=0$ and to $\theta=0$, respectively, and are complementary in this sense.
Their indices are the specializations $p=q=0$ and $y_1=y_2=y_3=0$ of the classical refined index defined in Section~\ref{sec:index}.
In \cite{Gadde:2025yoa}, a mismatch between the BMN indices of the S-dual $SO(7)$ and $Sp(6)$ theories was used to detect non-graviton cohomologies, and in Section~\ref{sec:index} we compare the pure-gaugino indices of $SO(2r+1)$ and $Sp(2r)$ in the same spirit.


\section{Index evidence for non-Coulomb classes}\label{sec:index}


We compare the classical refined indices of the $S$-dual $SO(2r+1)$ ($B_r$) and $Sp(2r)$ ($C_r$) theories in the pure-gaugino truncation $\mathcal C_\lambda$, which retains the gauginos and their covariant derivatives. 
Their Coulomb branch cohomologies, defined as the images of classical cohomology under Cartan restriction, agree \cite{Gadde:2025yoa,Chang:2025mqp}. 
These contributions therefore cancel in the index difference, which detects charge sectors containing non-Coulomb classes in at least one theory, where non-Coulomb refers to the vanishing of such classes under Cartan restriction \cite{Chang:2025mqp,Choi:2025bhi}.

Using the grading $\mu$ of Section~\ref{sec:rev-class-coh}, we can define the classical refined index \cite{Chang:2013fba}
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal I^{\mathrm{cl}}_G(y_1,y_2,y_3;p,q)
 =\operatorname{Tr}_{H_Q(\mathcal C_{\mathcal N=4})}
 (-1)^F p^{n_+}q^{n_-}y_1^{n_1}y_2^{n_2}y_3^{n_3}.
 \label{eq:gidx-refined}
\end{equation}
Here, $Q$ denotes the classical interacting supercharge.\footnote{Its cohomology describes the states that remain BPS under the one-loop dilatation operator.
Accordingly, this cohomology is also called one-loop cohomology \cite{Gadde:2025yoa}, and the corresponding refined index is called the one-loop index in \cite{Gaikwad:2025ugk}.
Our terminology refers instead to the order of the supercharge used to define the cohomology.}
The single-letter index, with the adjoint gauge character suppressed, is
\begin{equation}
 i(y_1,y_2,y_3;p,q)=1-\frac{(1-y_1)(1-y_2)(1-y_3)}{(1-p)(1-q)}.
 \label{eq:gidx-letter}
\end{equation}
Since $Q$ preserves the $\mu$-grading, the classical refined index can be computed on the free BPS spectrum, without determining the classical cohomology explicitly.
Further quantum corrections to the supercharge need not preserve the full $\mu$-grading, so this five-fugacity index is not protected at the quantum level.
The fully protected superconformal index can be recovered by imposing $y_1y_2y_3=pq$.

By setting $y_1=y_2=y_3=0$, we can select the pure-gaugino truncation $\mathcal C_\lambda$ introduced in Section~\ref{sec:rev-class-coh}.
Its single letter index is
\begin{equation}
 i_\lambda(p,q)=\frac{-p-q+pq}{(1-p)(1-q)}\,,
 \label{eq:gidx-gaugino-letter}
\end{equation}
where the free gaugino equation of motion accounts for the positive $pq$ term.
We write $\mathcal I_G(p,q)=\mathcal I^{\mathrm{cl}}_G(0,0,0;p,q)$ and $\mathcal I_G(t)=\mathcal I_G(t,t)$, so $t$ counts $n_++n_-$. 
As usual, we project onto gauge invariant states by integrating the multi-letter index over the gauge group with the Haar measure \cite{Kinney:2005ej}
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal I_G(p,q)=\int_G d\mu(g)\,
 \exp\!\left[\sum_{\ell\geq1}\frac{i_\lambda(p^\ell,q^\ell)}{\ell}
 \chi_{\mathrm{adj}}(g^\ell)\right]\,,
 \label{eq:gidx-haar}
\end{equation}
where the adjoint character is given by
\begin{equation}
  \chi_{\mathrm{adj}}(z)=r+\sum_{\alpha\in\Phi}z^\alpha\,,
  \qquad z^\alpha=\prod_{k=1}^r z_k^{c_k}\,,
  \qquad \alpha=\sum_{k=1}^r c_ke_k\,.
  \label{eq:gidx-character}
\end{equation}
Here, $\Phi$ is the root system of $\mathfrak{g}$ and $c_k$ are the coordinates in the root systems listed in \eqref{eq:classical-roots}
The constant term $r$ counts the Cartan generators. 
In order to express the integrand in product form, we introduce the notation
\begin{equation}
 (u;p,q)_\infty=\prod_{m,n\geq0}(1-up^m q^n)\,,
 \qquad (u;p)_\infty=\prod_{m\geq0}(1-up^m)\,.
 \label{eq:gidx-products}
\end{equation}
with $|p|,|q| < 1$, which enables us to express the integral as
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal I_G(p,q)
 =\frac{\bigl[(p;p)_\infty(q;q)_\infty(pq;p,q)_\infty\bigr]^r}{|W|}
 \oint_{\mathbb T^r}\prod_{k=1}^r\frac{dz_k}{2\pi i z_k}
 \prod_{\alpha\in\Phi}(z^\alpha;p,q)_\infty\,,
 \label{eq:gidx-contour}
\end{equation}
where each $z_k$ is integrated around the unit circle, and $|W|=2^r r!$ is the order of the Weyl group, which is the same for $B_r$ and $C_r$.
The factors with $m=n=0$ in the double products contain the Vandermonde determinants associated to the Haar measure.

We evaluate \eqref{eq:gidx-contour} as a power series after setting $p=q=t$.
Denoting the $SO(2r+1)$ and $Sp(2r)$ indices by $\mathcal I_{B_r}$ and $\mathcal I_{C_r}$, we set $\Delta_r=\mathcal I_{B_r}-\mathcal I_{C_r}$.
At rank three, the two expansions first differ at order $t^8$:\footnote{This discrepancy does not conflict with $S$-duality, since the refined index of classical cohomology is not generally protected against further quantum corrections.
The full protected superconformal index requires $y_1y_2y_3=pq$, which is not satisfied by the pure-gaugino specialization at non-zero $p,q$. 
A further specialization $p=0$ or $q=0$ \emph{is} fully protected and was recently studied for $U(N)$ gauge theory in \cite{Eleftheriou:2026yct}.}
\begin{align}
 \mathcal I_{SO(7)}(t)
 &=1+t^2+2t^3+4t^4+6t^5+12t^6+18t^7+33t^8
 \nonumber\\
 &\quad+52t^9+87t^{10}+136t^{11}+225t^{12}+348t^{13}+O(t^{14})\,,
 \label{eq:gidx-so7-series}\\
 \mathcal I_{Sp(6)}(t)
 &=1+t^2+2t^3+4t^4+6t^5+12t^6+18t^7+32t^8
 \nonumber\\
 &\quad+50t^9+84t^{10}+132t^{11}+217t^{12}+332t^{13}+O(t^{14})\,,
 \label{eq:gidx-sp6-series}
\end{align}
so that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gidx-delta3-series}
     \Delta_3(t)=t^8+2t^9+3t^{10}+4t^{11}+8t^{12}+16t^{13}+O(t^{14})\,.
\end{equation}
The first discrepancies are summarized for ranks $1\leq r\leq6$ in Table~\ref{tab:gidx-thresholds}.
\begin{table}[!ht]
 \centering
 \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.18}
 \begin{tabular}{c c c }
 \hline
 $r$ & Groups & First discrepancy \\
 \hline
 1 & $SO(3),\ Sp(2)$   & none \\
 2 & $SO(5),\ Sp(4)$   & none  \\
 3 & $SO(7),\ Sp(6)$   & $t^8$    \\
 4 & $SO(9),\ Sp(8)$   & $t^{10}$  \\
 5 & $SO(11),\ Sp(10)$ & $t^{12}$\\
