For each of the tested ranks $3\leq r\leq6$ we find
\begin{equation}
 \Delta_r(t)=t^{2r+2}+2t^{2r+3}+O(t^{2r+4}).
 \label{eq:gidx-threshold-pattern}
\end{equation}
However, we have not established this pattern for general $r$.
The observed discrepancy has a simple cohomological interpretation.
The gaugino bilinear $\mathcal S=\Tr(\lambda_+\lambda_-)$ has weight $pq$, or $t^2$ on the diagonal.
As we will review in detail in Section~\ref{sec:nC-from-S}, its powers satisfy $\mathcal S^h=0$ and $\mathcal S^{h-1}\neq0$ in classical $Q$-cohomology, where $h$ is the dual Coxeter number.
Since $h(B_r)=2r-1$ and $h(C_r)=r+1$, the power $\mathcal S^{r+1}$ represents a non-zero class for $SO(2r+1)$ but is trivial in cohomology for $Sp(2r)$ when $r\geq3$.
On the rank-$r$ Cartan, however, $\mathcal S$ is a sum of only $r$ independent gaugino pairs, so $\mathcal S^{r+1}$ vanishes under restriction.
The surviving $SO(2r+1)$ class is therefore non-Coulomb, as recently noted in \cite{Choi:2025pqr} as well.
The weight $t^{2r+2}$ of $\mathcal S^{r+1}$ coincides with the first discrepancy in the computed indices.
The next coefficient is consistent with its two first derivatives $D_{\dot\pm}\mathcal S^{r+1}$, each of weight $t^{2r+3}$.
At order $t^{2r+4}$, the coefficient of $\Delta_r$ is $3$ for $r=3$ and $4$ for $4\leq r\leq6$.
These values are consistent with the three second derivatives of $\mathcal S^{r+1}$ and, for $r\geq4$, the additional class
$\mathcal S^{r+2}$.
The latter is non-trivial in the $SO(2r+1)$ cohomology precisely when $r\geq4$.

These matches do not determine all the contributing classes, since bosonic and fermionic contributions can cancel in the index. 
For the same reason, agreement below the first discrepancy does not establish agreement of the cohomologies. 
In the following section, we establish the relevant non-Coulomb classes directly.


