\section{Non-Coulomb classes from the gaugino bilinear}\label{sec:nC-from-S}

In this section, we explain and execute a strategy to determine the classical $Q$-cohomology classes built from the gaugino components $\lambda_+$ and $\lambda_-$ for a general classical gauge algebra.

As explained in Section~\ref{sec:rev-class-coh}, the gauginos and their covariant derivatives form a subcomplex of the full classical $Q$-complex.
Expanding \eqref{eq:superalgebra-gener-fields} to lowest order in $z$, one finds
\begin{equation}
    \{Q,\lambda_{\dot\alpha}\}=0\,,\qquad
    \{Q,D_{\dot\alpha}\lambda_{\dot\beta}\}
    \propto\{\lambda_{\dot\alpha},\lambda_{\dot\beta}\}\,.
\end{equation}
Without derivatives, the $Q$-closed operators are therefore the gauge-invariant functions of the Grassmann-odd, $\mathfrak g$-valued matrices $\lambda_+$ and $\lambda_-$, while the $Q$-exact ones form the invariant part $I^{\mathfrak g}$ of the ideal $I$ generated by the components of
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gaugino-anticommutator-ideal}
    \{\lambda_+,\lambda_+\}\,,\qquad
    \{\lambda_-,\lambda_-\}\,,\qquad
    \{\lambda_+,\lambda_-\}\,.
\end{equation}

The CDSW conjecture \cite{Cachazo:2002ry} states that this classical chiral ring is generated by the gaugino bilinear $\mathcal{S}=\Tr(\lambda_+\lambda_-)$, with the nilpotence relation
\begin{equation}\label{eq:cdsw-conj}
    \mathcal{S}^{h-1}\neq0\mod I^{\mathfrak{g}}\,,\qquad
    \mathcal{S}^{h}=0\mod I^{\mathfrak{g}}\,,
\end{equation}
where $h\equiv h^\vee$ is the dual Coxeter number of $\mathfrak g$.
Witten gave a particularly simple proof of $\mathcal{S}^{h-1}\neq0$ for $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ and $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$ and a proof of $\mathcal{S}^{h}=0$ for all classical Lie algebras \cite{Witten:2003ye}, while his proof of $\mathcal{S}^{h-1}\neq0$ for $\mathfrak{so}_n$ is less direct and uses instanton calculations.
Etingof and Kac developed a uniform algebraic approach and proved the $G_2$ case \cite{Etingof:2003dd}, while Etingof subsequently obtained a purely algebraic proof for all classical Lie algebras \cite{Etingof:2003fy}.

Every power $\mathcal S^m$ is $Q$-closed without the use of trace relations, and by \eqref{eq:cdsw-conj} the powers with $m<h$ are non-trivial monotone classes in the terminology of \cite{Chang:2024zqi}.
On the Cartan subalgebra, $\mathcal S$ reduces to a sum of $r$ nilpotent terms, with $r$ the rank of $\mathfrak g$, so that $\mathcal S^m$ vanishes there for $m>r$.
The powers with $r<m<h$ thus form a tower of non-Coulomb monotones which is empty for $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ and $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$, where $h-1=r$, but not for $\mathfrak{so}_n$ with $n\geq7$.
This was recently observed in \cite{Choi:2025pqr} as well.

We develop a restriction method for establishing the non-triviality of $\mathcal S^{h-1}$, and with it of the whole tower, by placing $\lambda_+$ and $\lambda_-$ on a few basis elements of $\mathfrak g$.
The support is chosen such that $\mathcal S^{h-1}$ survives while the restricted invariant ideal becomes simple enough to test non-exactness.
The Cartan restriction recovers Witten's non-vanishing argument for $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ and $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$.
We work out non-Coulomb restrictions explicitly for $\mathfrak{so}_7$, $\mathfrak{so}_8$ and $\mathfrak{so}_9$, and indicate the generalization to higher rank.



\subsection{The gaugino chiral ring}\label{ssec:gaugino-chiral-ring}

Let $\mathfrak g$ be a complex simple Lie algebra of rank $r$ and dimension $d$.
We work in the normalized Cartan--Weyl basis $T_a=(H_i,E_\alpha)$ of Appendix~\ref{app:cw-basis}, with $i=1,\dots,r$ and $\alpha$ running over the roots $\Phi$ of $\mathfrak g$, in which the invariant form is the identity on the Cartan subalgebra and the dual basis for $\mathfrak g^*$ is $H^i=H_i$ and $E^\alpha=E_{-\alpha}$ such that  $\langle T_a,T^b\rangle=\delta_a{}^b$.
We expand
\begin{equation}
    \lambda_+=x^aT_a\,,\qquad
    \lambda_-=y_aT^a\,,
\end{equation}
with Grassmann-odd coefficients $x^a$ and $y_a$, $a=1,\ldots,d$.
Let $R$ be the algebra of polynomials in the $x^a$ and $y_a$, that is, the exterior algebra on $\mathfrak g\oplus\mathfrak g^*$, and let $I\subset R$ be the ideal generated by the components of \eqref{eq:gaugino-anticommutator-ideal}.
The classical chiral ring is then the invariant quotient \cite{Witten:2003ye}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:invariant-ring-quotient}
    (R/I)^{\mathfrak g}\simeq R^{\mathfrak g}/I^{\mathfrak g}\,,\qquad I^{\mathfrak g}=I\cap R^{\mathfrak g}\,.
\end{equation}
We write $R_{(p,q)}$ and $I_{(p,q)}$ for the parts of bidegree $(p,q)$ in the numbers of $x$ and $y$ factors, which is the grading \eqref{eq:mu-grading} of Section~\ref{sec:rev-class-coh} restricted to pure-gaugino operators.
The three generators in \eqref{eq:gaugino-anticommutator-ideal} are copies of the adjoint in bidegrees $(2,0)$, $(0,2)$ and $(1,1)$.

In the following multi-trace description, we specialize to the classical Lie algebras.
For $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ and $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$, gauge invariants of $\lambda_\pm$ are spanned by multi-traces, where traces of single letters vanish.
For $\mathfrak{so}_n$, multi-traces span the $O(n)$-invariant sector.
For testing the non-exactness of $\mathcal S^p$, it is enough to work in this multi-trace sector.\footnote{For $\mathfrak{so}_{2r}$, the additional single-epsilon, or Pfaffian-type, invariants are odd under an orientation-reversing $O(n)$ transformation, whereas $\mathcal S^p$ is even.
Since $Q$ commutes with this transformation, any $Q$-exact expression for $\mathcal S^p$ can be replaced by its even part.
Products with an even number of epsilon tensors reduce to multi-traces by $\epsilon_{i_1\cdots i_{2r}}\epsilon_{j_1\cdots j_{2r}}=\det(\delta_{i_a j_b})$ \cite{Witten:2003ye}.
Therefore, in the $O(n)$ even sector, there are no additional exact expressions beyond the multi-trace sector.}
Two kinds of single traces lie in the ideal.
A trace in which two $\lambda_+$ or two $\lambda_-$ are adjacent contains $\lambda_\pm^2=\tfrac12\{\lambda_\pm,\lambda_\pm\}$, and the remaining, alternating traces satisfy
\begin{equation}\label{eq:alternating-traces}
    \mathcal T_m\equiv\Tr\bigl((\lambda_+\lambda_-)^m\bigr)
    =\Tr\bigl(\{\lambda_+,\lambda_-\}(\lambda_+\lambda_-)^{m-1}\bigr)\mod\lambda_+^2\,,
    \qquad m>1\,,
\end{equation}
while $\mathcal T_1=\mathcal S$.
Hence every multi-trace other than a power of $\mathcal S$ lies in $I^{\mathfrak g}$, and these traces span (the multi-trace part of) the invariant ideal.


It follows that the quotient in \eqref{eq:invariant-ring-quotient} is spanned by the powers $\mathcal S^p$ in the multi-trace sector.
Their non-triviality depends on whether finite-rank trace relations express them as combinations of these other multi-traces.
Therefore, the non-trivial part of the CDSW conjecture lies in the nilpotence order $h$, especially when it exceeds the rank, as it does for $\mathfrak{so}_n$.



\subsection{Support restrictions}\label{ssec:supp-restr}

For subsets $A$ and $B$ of the basis labels, the support restriction $\rho_{A,B}$ keeps only the coefficients $x^a$ with $a\in A$ and $y_b$ with $b\in B$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:support-restriction-fields}
    \rho_{A,B}:\qquad
    \lambda_+\longmapsto\sum_{a\in A}x^aT_a\,,
    \qquad
    \lambda_-\longmapsto\sum_{b\in B}y_bT^b\,.
\end{equation}
Note that we allow different restrictions of different letters, so that the restriction should not be viewed as a restriction at the level of the Lie algebra.
Since we have the elementary implication that $X\in I^{\mathfrak g}$ implies $\rho_{A,B}(X)\in\rho_{A,B}(I^{\mathfrak g})$, we also have 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:restriction-contrapositive}
    \rho_{A,B}(X)\notin\rho_{A,B}\bigl(I^{\mathfrak g}\bigr)
    \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
    X\notin I^{\mathfrak g}\,.
\end{equation}
A well-chosen, and possibly non-uniform, restriction can thus demonstrate that an operator is non-trivial in $Q$-cohomology, and this will be our key tool in the following.

For powers of $\mathcal S$ it is natural to keep paired variables, $\rho_J\equiv\rho_{J,J}$ for a single set of labels $J$, which we call a support,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:paired-support}
    \lambda_+\big|_J=\sum_{a\in J}x^aT_a\,,\qquad
    \lambda_-\big|_J=\sum_{a\in J}y_aT^a\,.
\end{equation}
We always take $J$ to consist of positive roots, so that $\lambda_-$ takes values in the corresponding negative roots, and the restriction matches the pairing in $\mathcal S$.
Writing $\Tr(XY)=c_{\mathrm{tr}}\langle X,Y\rangle$ for the relation between the trace and the normalized invariant form, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:S-paired-support}
    \mathcal{S}\big|_J=c_{\mathrm{tr}}\sum_{a\in J}x^a y_a\,,
    \qquad
    \mathcal S^p\big|_J=c_{\mathrm{tr}}^p p!\sum_{\substack{J'\subset J\\|J'|=p}}\ \prod_{a\in J'}x^ay_a\,.
\end{equation}
In particular, $\mathcal S^{h-1}$ survives only on supports with at least $h-1$ elements.
On a minimal support, with $|J|=h-1$, it is proportional to the top form of $J$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:minimal-support-monomial}
    \mathcal{S}^{h-1}\big|_J\propto\prod_{a\in J}x^a y_a\,.
\end{equation}
In this case, we note that the restriction test
\begin{equation}\label{eq:restriction-test}
    \rho_J(\mathcal{S}^{h-1})
    \notin
    \rho_J\bigl(I^{\mathfrak g}_{(h-1,h-1)}\bigr)
    \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
    \mathcal{S}^{h-1}\notin I^{\mathfrak g} \,,
\end{equation}
only succeeds when the restricted ideal vanishes in this bidegree.

When no single minimal support achieves this, several can be combined.
For a family of minimal supports
\begin{equation}\label{eq:chosen-subsets}
    \mathcal F=\{J_1,\dots,J_s\}\,,\qquad |J_1|=\dots=|J_s|=h-1\,,
\end{equation}
let $J$ be their union and $\pi_{\mathcal F}$ the projection onto the top forms of $J_1,\dots,J_s$, so that $\pi_{\mathcal F}\rho_J(X)=\rho_{J_1}(X)+\dots+\rho_{J_s}(X)$ in bidegree $(h-1,h-1)$.
In analogy with \eqref{eq:restriction-contrapositive}, we again have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:projected-restriction-test}
    \pi_{\mathcal F}\rho_J(\mathcal{S}^{h-1})
    \notin
    \pi_{\mathcal F}\rho_J\bigl(I^{\mathfrak g}_{(h-1,h-1)}\bigr)\quad \Longrightarrow\quad \mathcal S^{h-1}\notin I^{\mathfrak g}\,.
\end{equation}
We note that not all $h-1$-cardinality subsets of the union $J$ are always necessary to check \eqref{eq:projected-restriction-test}, hence our definition of $\pi_\mathcal{F}$.

The simplest support is the Cartan, or Coulomb, restriction $J_C=\{1,\ldots,r\}$, which places $\lambda_\pm$ in the Cartan subalgebra.
All commutators then vanish, so that the entire ideal restricts to zero, while $\mathcal S^r|_{J_C}\propto x^1\cdots x^ry_1\cdots y_r\neq0$.
For $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ and $\mathfrak{sp}_{2n}$, where $h-1=r$, this recovers Witten's simple proof of the non-vanishing statement $\mathcal S^{h-1}\neq0$ in the CDSW conjecture.


\subsection{Simplifying the \texorpdfstring{$Q$}{Q}-exact ideal}\label{ssec:strong-comm-vanish-ideal}

The restricted ideal simplifies on supports whose generators commute.
We call $J$ a commuting support if
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lower-lower-comm}
    [T_a,T_b]=0\,,\qquad a,b\in J\,,
\end{equation}
which in the Cartan--Weyl basis implies $[T^a,T^b]=0$, but not $[T_a,T^b]=0$, since $T^b=T_{-b}$ for root generators.
On such a support, $\lambda_+^2$ and $\lambda_-^2$ restrict to zero, and with them every multi-trace containing a non-alternating trace.
By Section~\ref{ssec:gaugino-chiral-ring}, the restricted multi-trace part of $I_{(p,p)}^{\mathfrak g}$ is then spanned by the restrictions of the alternating multi-traces
\begin{equation}\label{eq:alternating-multitraces}
    \mathcal T_\nu=\mathcal T_{\nu_1}\cdots\mathcal T_{\nu_s}\,,\qquad
    \nu=(\nu_1,\dots,\nu_s)\,,
\end{equation}
with $\nu$ a partition of $p$ other than $(1,\ldots,1)$, which corresponds to $\mathcal S^p$.
We will call their span the residual ideal at bidegree $(p,p)$.

The residual ideal vanishes altogether if one also imposes the lower--upper condition
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lower-upper-comm}
    [T_a,T^b]=0\,,\qquad a\neq b\in J\,,
\end{equation}
in which case we call $J$ strongly commuting.
For root generators the two conditions state that $\alpha\pm\beta\notin\Phi$ for all distinct $\alpha,\beta\in J$, which is the definition of \emph{strongly orthogonal} roots. 
