\subsection{Discussion}
\label{subsec-intro-discussion}
We now briefly discuss the relation of the main objects and results of this paper with other subjects.

\paragraph{Apparent singularities as classical limits of degenerate fields}
Opers with apparent singularities arise in the classical limit of conformal field theory \cite[Section~3]{Te17}.
Consider Virasoro conformal blocks on the Riemann sphere with primary fields at marked points $p_1, \dots, p_N$, degenerate fields $V_{-1/2b}$ at points $x_1, \dots, x_d$, and one further degenerate field $V_{-b/2}$ at a variable point $y$.
The null vectors of the degenerate fields imply that the conformal block satisfies second-order Belavin--Polyakov--Zamolodchikov (BPZ) equations in $y$ and in each $x_r$ \cite{BPZ}.
In the classical limit $b \rightarrow 0$, with the ansatz
$e^{-S/b^2}\chi(y)$ for conformal blocks, the BPZ equation in $y$ 
degenerates into an oper equation for $\chi$ with simple apparent singularities at $x_1, \dots, x_d$.

The accessory parameters are the derivatives $\nu_r = -\partial_{x_r} S$ of the classical conformal block $S$, and the BPZ equations at the $x_r$ become exactly the constraints that make the $x_r$ apparent singularities.
Furthermore, the residues $\partial_{p_s} S$ at the marked points are the Hamiltonians of the isomonodromic deformation \cite{Te17}.
For higher rank, opers with apparent singularities and the conditions on their accessory parameters are studied in \cite{DM07, DDX26}, and one may expect them to arise in the classical limit of $\mathfrak{sl}_n$ Toda theories.

\paragraph{Collisions as fusion}
The outcome of the collision of two insertions of degenerate fields 
is described by the fusion rule in conformal field theories.
The degenerate field $V_{-1/2b}$ is labelled by the spin-$\frac12$ representation of $\mathfrak{sl}_2$, 
and its fusion with itself follows the Clebsch--Gordan rule 
\[ \frac12 \otimes \frac12 = 0 \oplus 1\]  
\cite{BPZ}.
The two channels are the vacuum representation, which corresponds to inserting no field at all, and the next degenerate representation $V_{-1/b}$, labelled by spin $1$, whose classical limit is a double apparent singularity.

Our results on the annihilation of two apparent singularities (\Cref{prop-A}) and on the formation of a double apparent singularity (\Cref{prop-double-as}) can be seen as the classical limit of this fusion rule, in the vacuum and the spin-$1$ channel respectively.

\paragraph{Neighborhoods of strata}
\Cref{prop-A,thm-B,thm-C} suggest a local picture of $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$ near a smaller stratum.
Let $\mathcal{D}$ be as in \Cref{prop-A}, with $d-2$ simple apparent singularities $x_3, \dots, x_d$, and suppose that it represents a flat connection in the stratum labelled by $d-2$.
We expect that every flat connection close to it can be represented by a branched projective structure whose apparent singularities consist of $d-2$ points close to $x_3, \dots, x_d$, together with pairs of nearby points, each pair joined by twin paths bounding a small bubble.
The family $\{\mathcal{D}_u\}_u$ of \Cref{prop-A} is of this form, with the single pair $x_\pm(u)$ (\Cref{thm-C}).

On the other hand, if the complex structure is allowed to vary, the pair can be removed: 
by \Cref{thm-C}, for small $u \neq 0$, $\mathcal{D}_u$ is obtained by bubbling a branched projective structure $\sigma_u$ on $C'_u$ 
with two fewer apparent singularities and the same monodromy.
Hence, for even $d < 2g-2$, the monodromy of $\mathcal{D}_u$ needs $d$ apparent singularities on $C$ by \Cref{thm-B}, but at most $d-2$ on $C'_u$.
This suggests describing a neighborhood of a stratum in terms of the space of branched projective structures with $d-2$ apparent singularities over Teichm\"uller space, 
which one may call a \emph{universal Bia\l ynicki-Birula stratum}. 
See \Cref{fig-debubbling} for an illustration.


\paragraph{Discontinuity of tangent spaces at smaller strata}
Let $[E_0, \nabla_0]$ lie in the stratum labelled by $d-2$, where $d < 2g-2$, let $L_0 \hookrightarrow E_0$ be its maximal subbundle, and for $x_0 \in C$ let $L_0' = L_0(-x_0)$.
Simpson proved the closure relation by deforming $(E_0, \nabla_0)$, together with the injection $L_0' \rightarrow L_0 \hookrightarrow E_0$ vanishing at $x_0$, 
into a family $(E_\tau, \nabla_\tau)$ with line subbundles $L_\tau \hookrightarrow E_\tau$ of degree $\deg L_0'$, which lies in the larger stratum labelled by $d$ for $\tau \neq 0$ \cite[Theorem~7.6]{simpson-iterated}, see also \cite[Proposition~2.2]{Huang26}.
In terms of apparent singularities, this is the mechanism of \Cref{prop-A,thm-B}: 
the section induced by $L_0' \rightarrow E_0$ has zero divisor $D_{L_0}(\nabla_0) + 2x_0$, 
and if $x_0$ is not a point of $D_{L_0}(\nabla_0)$, then for $\tau \neq 0$ the double point $2x_0$ splits into two simple apparent singularities, which collide at $x_0$ as $\tau \rightarrow 0$.

For $d \geq 4$, Huang chooses $x_0$ to be a point of $D_{L_0}(\nabla_0)$, so that three apparent singularities collide at $x_0$ \cite{Huang26}.
He shows that along this family that limits to $[E_0, \nabla_0]$, the tangent spaces along the Lagrangian leaves associated to Hodge bundles display a discontinuity at the collision limit \cite[Theorem~1.1]{Huang26}.
Hence these Lagrangian leaves do not form a $C^1$ foliation of the stable locus of $\mathcal{M}_{dR}$, which answers the foliation part of Simpson's question \cite[Question~7.4]{simpson-iterated} negatively; the closedness part holds in rank $2$ \cite{DS24}.
Our results suggest an intuitive picture of this collision (\Cref{fig-three-collision}): 
two simple apparent singularities, joined by twin paths bounding a small bubble, collide with a third apparent singularity, which is itself joined by twin paths to another apparent singularity.

