\section{Introduction}

Quantum information theory provides a powerful toolbox for the investigation of many-body systems and quantum circuits. Quantum resources~\cite{chitambar2019quantumresourcetheories} are now routinely adopted as diagnostics of a wide range of phenomena, driven by the successful application of entanglement~\cite{amico2008entanglemententropy} in the study of many-body effects such as scrambling~\cite{nahum2017quantumentanglementgrowth,keyserlingk2018operatorhydrodynamics,fischer2013randomquantumcircuits}, topological order~\cite{kitaev2006topologicalentanglement,levin2006detectingtopologicalorder}, and quantum phase transitions~\cite{calabrese2004entanglemententropy,laflorencie2016quantumentanglementincondensed}. In recent years, non-stabilizerness (or \textit{magic})~\cite{bravyi2005universalquantumcomputation,bravyi2012magicstatedistillation,veitch2014resourcetheoryofstabilizer,howard2017applicationofaresourcetheory} has rapidly emerged as a key complementary probe, largely thanks to the introduction of computable measures such as the stabilizer R\'enyi entropy~\cite{leone2022stabilizerrenyientropy,haug2023stabilizerentropiesmonotones,leone2024stabilizerentropiesaremonotones} and of efficient numerical estimators for many-body states~\cite{haug2023quantifyingnonstabilizerness,lami2023nonstabilizernessviaperfectsampling,tarabunga2024nonstabilizernessviamps}. This has stimulated intense research on its dynamical behavior in random circuits and chaotic systems~\cite{turkeshi2025magicspreading,niroula2024phasetransitioninmagic,szombathy2025asymptoticallyindependent,paviglianiti2025estimatingnonstabilizerness,russomanno2025nonstabilizernessintheunitary,falcao2025nonstabilizernessdynamics,paviglianiti2026truecostfactoringlinking}, in monitored dynamics~\cite{bejan2024dynamicalmagictransitions,fux2024entanglementmagicseparation,tirrito2025magicphasetransitionsmonitored}, as well as on its connection with chaos~\cite{leone2021quantumchaosis} and quantum criticality~\cite{tarabunga2023manybodymagic,haug2023quantifyingnonstabilizerness}. Fermionic non-Gaussianity is currently undergoing a parallel development~\cite{sierant2026fermionicmagicresources,collura2026nonstabilizernessof,aditya2026growthandspreading}. These advances address the fundamental question of how different quantum resources contribute to making many-body quantum physics complex~\cite{leone2021quantumchaosis,paviglianiti2026emergenceofgenericentanglement}. These resources also carry an operational meaning linked to the cost of classical simulation: entanglement limits the performance of matrix-product state (MPS) techniques~\cite{vidal2003efficientclassicalsimulation,schollwock2011thedensitymatrixrenormalizationgroup}, whereas magic controls the computational overhead of stabilizer expansion methods~\cite{gottesman1998heisenbergrepresentation,aaronson2004improvedsimulation,bravyi2016improvedclassicalsimulation,bravyi2019simulationofquantum,seddon2021quantifyingquantumspeedups,bittel2026operationalinterpretation}.

The resource perspective has lately gained significant interest in the study of many-body open quantum systems, especially driven by the discovery of measurement-induced phase transitions~\cite{li2018quantumzenoeffect,skinner2019measurementinduced,gullans2020dynamicalpurification,turkeshi2021measurementinducedentanglement,jian2020measurementinducedcriticality,paviglianiti2023multipartiteentanglement,agrawal2022entanglementandchargesharpening}. In this context, quantum complexity measures are evaluated at the level of individual realizations of the monitoring protocol, i.e., of quantum trajectories~\cite{dalibard1992wavefunctionapproach,dum1992montecarlosimulation,carmichael1993opensystemsapproach,wiseman1996quantumtrajectories,plenio1998quantumjumpapproach,daley2014quantumtrajectories}, and their ensemble averages exhibit critical behavior invisible in the averaged density matrix~\cite{fischer2013randomquantumcircuits,potter2022entanglementdynamicshybrid}. Understanding how such processes impact quantum complexity contributes to delineating a boundary between quantum and classical regimes: sufficiently strong noise can counteract the scrambling of the unitary dynamics~\cite{noh2020efficientclassicalsimulation,aharonov2023polynomialtimeclassicalalgorithm}, thus potentially making the evolution of trajectories efficiently simulable with classical techniques~\cite{zihan2023efficientsaqmpling,dowling2026noiseinducedsimulabilitytransitionoperator}.

Motivated by these ideas, recent works have explored how to reduce entanglement in the trajectories of open quantum systems in order to enhance the efficiency of MPS simulations. A given dissipative channel admits an infinite number of unravelings, which correspond to ensembles of stochastic realizations that average to a common density matrix. Since quantum complexity measures depend on individual pure states rather than on the ensemble's density matrix, their averages differ across unravelings and can be minimized by an appropriate choice~\cite{vovk2022entanglementoptimaltrajectories,kolodrubetz2023optimalityoflindbladunfolding,chen2024optimizedtrajectoryunraveling,vovk2024quantumtrajectoryentanglement,daraban2025nonunitaritymaximizing,cichy2026classicalsimulationofnoisy}, a freedom that has also been exploited to extend the reach of classical sampling algorithms for noisy circuits~\cite{zihan2023efficientsaqmpling}. However, it has also been pointed out that a lower average entanglement does not automatically translate into a lower simulation cost, as the speed of statistical convergence may differ across unravelings~\cite{sander2026computationalregimesmatrixproductstatebasedquantum}. These efforts have mostly targeted entanglement, because MPS methods are the most established computational framework for many-body systems, and only recently has the unraveling been optimized for simulations combining Clifford circuits and MPS~\cite{fioroni2026noiseinduced}. The question of how magic and other resources behave across different unravelings, as well as how this impacts the efficiency of other classical simulation algorithms, stands open. It is a particularly natural question for stabilizer-based methods, because the canonical unraveling of a Pauli noise channel consists of Pauli operators, which are Clifford: in a stabilizer simulation, such noise is free~\cite{gottesman1998heisenbergrepresentation,aaronson2004improvedsimulation,gidney2021stim}, but for the same reason it cannot remove any of the non-stabilizerness generated by the ideal circuit.

In this work, we address this gap by investigating non-stabilizerness in different unravelings of dissipative dynamics. We consider one-dimensional Haar-random and $T$-doped random Clifford circuits perturbed by local dephasing noise, and we compare the trajectory-averaged stabilizer R\'enyi entropy across the full two-parameter family of minimal unravelings of the channel, as well as the nonminimal unraveling in terms of projective measurements. We show that the ``canonical'' unraveling is sub-optimal with respect to the resource: in Haar-random circuits, the representation of the noise process in terms of Pauli jumps is unable to change non-stabilizerness at all, and trajectories retain the magic of Haar-random states. As our main result, we demonstrate that much better choices can be made, even in an agnostic way that does not rely on information on the current state to adapt the unraveling dynamically. Contrary to the intuition suggested by the literature on monitored circuits, the optimal unraveling in the high-magic regime is not the one in terms of projective measurements, but rather corresponds to a generalized measurement; the projective unraveling prevails only when magic is scarce, as in weakly $T$-doped Clifford circuits. We quantify the magic reduction by showing that, while the scaling remains extensive with the system size, its prefactor is lowered by the optimal protocol. Finally, we observe that while the average resource can be reduced, its distribution over trajectories becomes very broad, and a considerable number of typical trajectories retain near-maximal magic. Stabilizer-expansion methods are thus limited by these high-magic trajectories rather than by the mean. These are, however, sampled considerably less often than in the canonical unraveling, which indicates a more efficient trajectory-based simulation, even though magic remains extensive.

The rest of the manuscript is structured as follows. Section~\ref{sec:theory} defines the noisy random circuit setup under study, and presents the theoretical tools and numerical methods: Sec.~\ref{sec:noise} introduces the noise channels and the parameterization of their unravelings, Sec.~\ref{sec:trajectories} covers the quantum-trajectory formalism, while Sec.~\ref{sec:magic} recalls the definition of the stabilizer R\'enyi entropy and the algorithm used to evaluate it exactly. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents our numerical results for the magic dynamics, its dependence on the unraveling parameters in Haar-random and $T$-doped Clifford circuits, its scaling with system size, and its distribution over trajectories. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} summarizes our findings and discusses the implications of these results from the perspectives of resource theory and stabilizer-based classical simulation, also outlining open directions.

\section{Theory and methods}
\label{sec:theory}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{rqc.pdf}
