  initialized in $\ket{0}$. The vertical dashed lines delimit complete
  brickwork layers, each formed by two sublayers acting on complementary
  sets of nearest-neighbor bonds. (a) Haar-random circuit, in which each
  green block is an independently sampled two-qubit Haar unitary.
  (b) $T$-doped Clifford circuit, in which each blue block is an
  independently sampled two-qubit Clifford and a $T$ gate is applied
  independently to each participating qubit with probability $q$. Red
  circles denote the local single-qubit noise channel applied after each
  ideal gate block.}
  \label{fig1}
\end{figure}


We consider a system of $n$ qubits, with Hilbert space of dimension $d=2^n$, whose state is described by a density operator $\rho$. The system evolves under a noisy quantum circuit, in which every ideal two-qubit gate is followed by the independent action of a single-qubit noise channel on each of the two qubits involved in the gate. We consider noisy random quantum circuits with the one-dimensional brickwork architecture depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig1}: a layer consists of two consecutive sublayers of two-qubit gates, one acting on each of the two complementary sets of disjoint nearest-neighbor bonds. Within this circuit geometry, we focus on two classes of gates, illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig1}(a) and (b). In Haar-random circuits, every two-qubit gate is drawn independently from the Haar measure on $\mathrm{U}(4)$~\cite{mezzadri2007howtogenerate}; in the absence of noise, these circuits generate magic rapidly within the first few layers~\cite{turkeshi2025magicspreading}. In $T$-doped Clifford circuits~\cite{zhou2020singletgate,leone2021quantumchaosis,haferkamp2023efficientunitarydesigns}, this rate can instead be tuned. Each two-qubit gate is a uniformly random Clifford unitary~\cite{koenig2014howtoefficientlyselect,bravyi2021hadamardfreecircuits}, and each of the two qubits then receives a $T$ gate, $T=\mathrm{diag}(1,e^{i\pi/4})$, with probability $q$. The doping probability $q$ thus controls how fast magic is generated: at $q=0$ the circuits are purely Clifford and generate no magic, while larger values of $q$ inject magic faster, although even at $q=1$ the circuits remain distinct from Haar-random ones.

All qubits are initialized in the stabilizer product state
$\ket{0}^{\otimes n}$, i.e., $\rho_0=(\ket{0}\!\bra{0})^{\otimes n}$,
so that any non-stabilizerness present at later times is generated by
the circuit. Denoting by $\Lambda_\ell$ the completely positive,
trace-preserving map that describes the $\ell$-th layer, gates and
noise included, the state after $N_L$ layers is
\begin{equation}
    \rho
    =
    \Lambda_{N_L}\circ\cdots\circ\Lambda_{2}\circ\Lambda_{1}(\rho_0).
    \label{eq:rho_evolution}
\end{equation}
In the absence of noise, $\rho$ remains pure at all times. In the
presence of noise, $\rho$ is a mixed state, and it can be represented
as a statistical mixture of pure states in infinitely many ways \cite{wiseman_inequivalence_2001,wiseman1996quantumtrajectories,wiseman2010quantummeasurementandcontrol}. The
central theme of this work is that each such representation can be \textit{unraveled} into a different ensemble of pure-state quantum
trajectories, and that a nonlinear property of the trajectories, such
as their non-stabilizerness, depends on the unraveling although $\rho$
does not. 


\subsection{Noise models and unravelings}
\label{sec:noise}

We describe noise by local, Markovian quantum channels~\cite{breuer2002theoryofopenquantumsystems}. A channel
$\mathcal{E}$ acting on a single-qubit density matrix admits a Kraus
representation
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{E}(\rho)
    =
    \sum_{\mu=0}^{r-1}
    K_{\mu}\rho K_{\mu}^{\dagger},
    \qquad
    \sum_{\mu=0}^{r-1}
    K_{\mu}^{\dagger}K_{\mu}=I ,
    \label{eq:kraus_representation}
\end{equation}
where the second relation ensures trace preservation. Here and below, $I$
denotes the identity operator on one qubit, and $\rho$ in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:kraus_representation} may equally denote the reduced
state of the qubit or the full $n$-qubit state, on which $K_\mu$ acts
as $K_\mu\otimes I^{\otimes(n-1)}$. As stated above, the channel acts
independently on both qubits of a gate immediately after the gate
as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig1}.

The Kraus representation of a channel is not unique. Given a set of $r$
Kraus operators $\{K_\mu\}$, let $V$ be an $s\times r$ isometry, with $V^\dagger V=I_r$.
The operators
\begin{equation}
    \widetilde K_a
    =
    \sum_{\mu=0}^{r-1}V_{a\mu}K_\mu,
    \qquad a=0,\ldots,s-1,
    \label{eq:kraus_isometry}
\end{equation}
define the same channel, since
\begin{equation}
    \sum_{a=0}^{s-1}
    \widetilde K_a\rho\widetilde K_a^\dagger
    =
    \sum_{\mu=0}^{r-1}
    K_\mu\rho K_\mu^\dagger .
\end{equation}
For $s=r$, the transformation is unitary and preserves the number of
Kraus operators. A rectangular isometry with $s>r$ instead produces a
nonminimal representation with a larger number of outcomes. While all
such representations give identical density-matrix dynamics, they
generally define different ensembles of quantum trajectories. We refer
to each choice of Kraus representation as an unraveling of the
channel~\cite{carmichael1993opensystemsapproach,gisin1992quantumstatediffusion,wiseman1996quantumtrajectories,wiseman2010quantummeasurementandcontrol}.

In the main text we focus on the dephasing, or phase-flip, channel;
the analogous constructions for depolarizing and amplitude-damping
noise, together with the corresponding numerical results, are given in
Appendix~\ref{app:noise}. The dephasing channel reads
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{D}_p(\rho)
    =
    (1-p)\rho+pZ\rho Z ,
    \label{eq:dephasing_channel}
\end{equation}
whose canonical Kraus operators are
\begin{equation}
    K_0=\sqrt{1-p}\,I,
    \qquad
    K_1=\sqrt{p}\,Z .
    \label{eq:canonical_dephasing}
\end{equation}
The off-diagonal entries of $\rho$ are multiplied by $1-2p$.
We therefore focus on $0\leq p\leq 1/2$, for which increasing $p$
corresponds to increasing dephasing strength, with complete dephasing
at $p=1/2$.

Up to phases multiplying the individual Kraus operators, any minimal
unraveling of Eq.~\eqref{eq:dephasing_channel} can be
parameterized by two angles. We use
\begin{equation}
    U(\theta,\phi)
    =
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \cos\theta & -\sin\theta\\
        \sin\theta &  \cos\theta
    \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        e^{i\phi} & 0\\
        0 & e^{-i\phi}
    \end{pmatrix},
    \label{eq:unraveling_unitary}
\end{equation}
where one may take $0\leq\theta\leq\pi/2$ and
$0\leq\phi<\pi$. The transformed Kraus operators are defined by
\begin{align}
    K_0^{(\theta,\phi)}
    &=
    e^{i\phi}\sqrt{1-p}\cos\theta\,I
    -
    e^{-i\phi}\sqrt{p}\sin\theta\,Z,
    \nonumber\\
    K_1^{(\theta,\phi)}
    &=
    e^{i\phi}\sqrt{1-p}\sin\theta\,I
    +
    e^{-i\phi}\sqrt{p}\cos\theta\,Z .
    \label{eq:dephasing_unraveling_explicit}
\end{align}
The canonical unraveling is recovered at $\theta=0$, since the
remaining phases do not affect either the outcome probabilities or the
conditional pure states. Note that both Kraus operators of the
canonical unraveling are proportional to Clifford unitaries ($I$ and
$Z$). Any nonlinear function of the state that is invariant under
Clifford operations, and in particular the stabilizer R\'enyi entropy
introduced below, is therefore unaffected by the noise events of the
canonical unraveling: in this representation, dephasing noise can
neither create nor destroy magic along a trajectory. For $\theta\neq
0$, instead, the Kraus operators are non-unitary and generally
non-Clifford, and the noise events do modify the magic of the state.


For comparison, we also consider an unraveling associated with a
projective measurement on the $Z$ eigenbasis. Introducing the projectors onto the eigenstates of $Z$,
\begin{equation}
    P_\pm=\frac{I\pm Z}{2},
\end{equation}
and applying a $3\times2$ isometry, as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:kraus_isometry}, to the canonical Kraus operators, we can rewrite the channel as
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}_p(\rho) = (1-2p)\rho + 2p\left(P_+\rho P_+ + P_-\rho P_-\right),
\label{eq:dephasing_measurement_decomposition}
\end{equation}
which corresponds to the three Kraus operators
\begin{equation}
K_{\mathrm{id}}=\sqrt{1-2p}\,I, \quad K_+=\sqrt{2p}\,P_+, \quad K_-=\sqrt{2p}\,P_- .
\label{eq:projdeph}
\end{equation}
In this unraveling, with probability $1-2p$ the state is left unchanged; otherwise, the qubit is measured in the computational basis and projected onto $\ket{0}$ or $\ket{1}$. The noise thus acts as a measurement in the computational basis, performed at random with probability $2p$. Since the probability $1-2p$ of the identity outcome must be nonnegative, this representation is valid only for $0\leq p\leq 1/2$, the range already considered above. At $p=1/2$, the identity outcome disappears and every noise event becomes a measurement.


Within the minimal family of Eq.~\eqref{eq:dephasing_unraveling_explicit}, a distinguished member is obtained at $\phi=0$ and
\begin{equation}
    \theta^\ast=\arctan\sqrt{\frac{p}{1-p}}.
    \label{eq:thetastar}
\end{equation}
Inserting this value in Eq.~\eqref{eq:dephasing_unraveling_explicit} and using $I=P_++P_-$, $Z=P_+-P_-$, one finds
\begin{equation}
    K_0^{\ast}=(1-p)I-pZ=I-2p\,P_+,
    \quad
    K_1^{\ast}=2\sqrt{p(1-p)}\,P_+ .
    \label{eq:thetastar_kraus}
\end{equation}
This is a quantum-jump unraveling: the ``click'' operator $K_1^\ast$ is proportional to the projector onto the $+1$ eigenstate of $Z$ and occurs with probability $2p(1-p)(1+\langle Z\rangle)$, whereas the ``no-click'' operator $K_0^\ast$ is a weak measurement of strength $2p$ that leaves the $P_-$ component untouched and slightly suppresses the $P_+$ component. Comparing Eq.~\eqref{eq:thetastar_kraus} with Eq.~\eqref{eq:projdeph}, one finds
\begin{equation}
    K_0^\ast=\sqrt{1-2p}\,K_{\mathrm{id}}+\sqrt{2p}\,K_-,
    \qquad
    K_1^\ast=\sqrt{2(1-p)}\,K_+ .
\end{equation}
The $\theta^\ast$ unraveling thus corresponds to the projective one with the identity and $P_-$ outcomes merged coherently into a single Kraus operator. For a given state, this merging changes nonlinear quantities such as $M_2$ at first order in $p$. In Appendix~\ref{app:thetastar}, however, we show that in Haar-random circuits these first-order differences cancel on average, so that the trajectory-averaged $M_2$ of the two unravelings differs only at order $p^2$. We will see below that the $\theta^\ast$ unraveling yields a strongly reduced magic with respect to the canonical one, but that it is not the optimal choice within the family of Eq.~\eqref{eq:dephasing_unraveling_explicit}.

\subsection{Quantum trajectories}
\label{sec:trajectories}

Consider a system in a pure state $\ket{\psi}$. As discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:noise}, the action of a noise channel on this state can be written through its Kraus decomposition, Eq.~\eqref{eq:kraus_representation},
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{E}\big(\ket{\psi}\!\bra{\psi}\big) = \sum_{\mu=0}^{r-1} K_\mu\ket{\psi}\!\bra{\psi}K_\mu^\dagger .
    \label{eq:channel_on_pure_state}
\end{equation}
Each term in this sum is proportional to a pure state. Defining the outcome probabilities and the corresponding normalized states,
\begin{equation}
    \pi_\mu=\bra{\psi}K_\mu^\dagger K_\mu\ket{\psi}, \qquad \ket{\psi_\mu}=\frac{K_\mu\ket{\psi}}{\sqrt{\pi_\mu}} ,
\end{equation}
Eq.~\eqref{eq:channel_on_pure_state} becomes
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{E}\big(\ket{\psi}\!\bra{\psi}\big) = \sum_{\mu=0}^{r-1} \pi_\mu\ket{\psi_\mu}\!\bra{\psi_\mu} = \mathbb{E}_{\mu}\big[\ket{\psi_\mu}\!\bra{\psi_\mu}\big],
    \label{eq:channel_trajectory_decomposition}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbb{E}_{\mu}$ denotes the average over the random outcome $\mu$, drawn with probability $\pi_\mu$. The output of the channel is thus a statistical mixture of pure states. This observation underlies the quantum-trajectory method~\cite{dalibard1992wavefunctionapproach,dum1992montecarlosimulation,carmichael1993opensystemsapproach,plenio1998quantumjumpapproach,daley2014quantumtrajectories}, in which the channel is replaced by a stochastic update: $K_\mu$ is applied with probability $\pi_\mu$, and the state is then renormalized. By Eq.~\eqref{eq:channel_trajectory_decomposition}, this update reproduces the channel exactly on average. Repeating it at every noise event generates a pure-state trajectory $\ket{\psi_\gamma}$, labeled by the sequence of outcomes $\gamma=(\mu_1,\mu_2,\ldots)$.


In a Monte Carlo calculation with $N_t$ trajectories, this average is estimated as
\begin{equation}
    \rho
    \simeq
    \frac{1}{N_t}
    \sum_{\gamma=1}^{N_t}
    \ket{\psi_\gamma}\!\bra{\psi_\gamma},
    \label{eq:trajectory_monte_carlo}
\end{equation}
with statistical fluctuations that decrease asymptotically as
$1/\sqrt{N_t}$.

Different unravelings of the same channel reproduce the same density
matrix, and therefore the same
expectation values for observables linear in $\rho$. They nevertheless
produce different probability distributions over pure states.
Consequently, the trajectory average of a nonlinear pure-state
quantity $\mathcal{Q}$,
\begin{equation}
    \overline{\mathcal{Q}}_{\{K_\mu\}}
    =
    \mathbb{E}_{\gamma}
    \left[
        \mathcal{Q}\!\left(\ket{\psi_\gamma}\right)
    \right],
    \label{eq:nonlinear_trajectory_average}
\end{equation}
generally depends on the chosen Kraus representation. This dependence
is central to the present work. While the mixed-state evolution is
unraveling-independent, the magic carried by the individual
trajectories is not. 


\subsection{Stabilizer R\'enyi entropy}
\label{sec:magic}

To quantify magic of a pure $n$-qubit state $|\psi\rangle$, we
use the second stabilizer R\'enyi entropy
(SRE)~\cite{leone2022stabilizerrenyientropy}, defined as
\begin{equation}
    M_2(|\psi\rangle)
    =
    -\ln\left[
        \frac{1}{d}
        \sum_{P\in\mathcal{P}_n}
        \left|
        \langle\psi|P|\psi\rangle
        \right|^4
    \right],
    \label{eq:M2_definition}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{P}_n=\{I,X,Y,Z\}^{\otimes n}$ is the set of $4^n$ Pauli strings. 
The SRE thus depends on the fourth moment of the distribution of Pauli expectation values. It vanishes if and only if $\ket{\psi}$ is a stabilizer state, is invariant under Clifford unitaries, which makes it a convenient measure of the non-Clifford resources contained in a state~\cite{leone2022stabilizerrenyientropy,haug2023stabilizerentropiesmonotones,leone2024stabilizerentropiesaremonotones}. For Haar-random states
the SRE concentrates around the value~\cite{leone2022stabilizerrenyientropy,turkeshi2025paulispectrum}
\begin{equation}
    \MH
    =
    \ln\!\left(\frac{d+3}{4}\right)
    \simeq (n-2)\ln 2 ,
    \label{eq:haar_magic}
\end{equation}
which is the maximal amount of magic that a typical state can host up
to an $\mathcal{O}(1)$ correction, and which grows extensively with
$n$. We shall use $\MH$ as the reference value against which the
magic of noisy trajectories is compared. 

Evaluating Eq.~\eqref{eq:M2_definition} directly, however, is computationally demanding. It requires all $4^n$ Pauli expectation values and,
for a generic state vector, has an overall computational complexity
of order $\mathcal{O}(8^n)$~\cite{Huang2026XORFWHT}. To reduce this cost, we evaluate Eq.~\eqref{eq:M2_definition} exactly using the XOR–fast Walsh–Hadamard transform (XOR–FWHT) method~\cite{Huang2026XORFWHT}. 

The total computational cost of evaluating $M_2$ with this method scales as $\mathcal{O}(n4^n)$. This represents a substantial improvement over the $\mathcal{O}(8^n)$ scaling of the direct evaluation while remaining deterministic and exact. Nevertheless, the cost is still exponential in the number of qubits, so exact evaluations of $M_2$ remain restricted to comparatively small or medium system sizes. Since $M_2$ must be evaluated for a large number of trajectories, at every circuit layer and over a fine grid of unraveling parameters, we restrict ourselves to system sizes for which its exact evaluation remains affordable ($n\leq12$). Stochastic estimators of the SRE, such as perfect Pauli sampling of matrix product states~\cite{lami2023nonstabilizernessviaperfectsampling,haug2023quantifyingnonstabilizerness,tarabunga2024nonstabilizernessviamps}, could extend the analysis to larger systems in regimes of moderate entanglement, at the price of an additional statistical error.


\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

We present here numerical results characterizing magic for different unravelings. 
Unless otherwise stated, the results shown in this section refer to $n=8$
qubits and to dephasing noise unraveled according to
Eq.~\eqref{eq:dephasing_unraveling_explicit} or to the projective
unraveling of Eq.~\eqref{eq:projdeph}. A scaling analysis up to $n=12$
is presented in Sec.~\ref{sec:scaling}. We denote by $\Mavg$ the SRE averaged over $N_t$ trajectories for each of $N_c$ independent circuit realizations, and we mostly report it normalized by the Haar-random value $\MH$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:haar_magic}, i.e., as $\Mavg/\MH$.
Statistical error bars are smaller than the symbol size in all SRE-averaged quantities presented.

