\subsection{Magic dynamics}
\label{sec:dynamics}

Figs.~\ref{fig2} and \ref{fig3} show the trajectory-averaged SRE
evaluated after each layer of the circuit, for the Haar-random and
$T$-doped Clifford ensembles respectively. The results are plotted for the canonical unraveling at $\theta=0$, and for the quantum-jump unraveling at $\theta=\theta^\ast$. In all cases, magic grows
monotonically with the number of layers and saturates to a limiting
value that depends on the noise strength $p$ and on the unraveling. For
the canonical unraveling, this limiting value is the
Haar-random one, $\Mavg=\MH$, irrespective of $p$, as the one-qubit Clifford Kraus operators leave both the Haar-random and Clifford-random ensembles unchanged~\cite{turkeshi2025magicspreading}. For the
quantum-jump unraveling at $\theta=\theta^\ast$, instead, the
saturation value decreases as $p$ increases. This suppression is a result of the Kraus operators being non-unitary. 


In Haar-random circuits, magic is generated rapidly and saturates after a number of layers of the order of the
system size~\cite{turkeshi2025magicspreading}. In what follows, we take $N_L=2n$ layers for Haar-random circuits, well beyond saturation. In $T$-doped Clifford circuits, the growth rate of magic is instead controlled by the doping probability $q$ (Fig.~\ref{fig3}) and can become very small: at $q=0.05$, the canonical unraveling has not yet saturated after $32$ layers for $n=8$. For these circuits, we therefore consider deeper circuits of at least $N_L=4n$ layers, and $N_L=48$ at the lowest doping $q=0.05$, ensuring that saturation is reached. Since $\Mavg$ has then reached its saturation value, we characterize each unraveling by its value at the end of the circuit.



\subsection{Haar-random circuits: dependence on the unraveling}
\label{sec:haar}


Figure~\ref{fig4} shows the trajectory-averaged magic at the end of noisy Haar-random circuits as a function of the unraveling parameters $\theta$ and $\phi$, for three values of the dephasing strength $p$. Two features stand out. First, for any $\theta$, the magic is minimal at $\phi=0$ and increases monotonically with $\phi$ up to $\phi=\pi/4$, where it reaches the Haar value. At this angle, both Kraus operators are proportional to unitary phase gates, which are absorbed by the subsequent Haar-random gates, so the trajectories remain Haar-distributed. Second, at $\phi=0$, the magic depends non-monotonically on $\theta$: it starts from the Haar value at $\theta=0$, decreases steeply, reaches a minimum at a finite angle $\theta_{\min}$, and then increases again towards $\theta=\pi/4$. Larger values of $\theta$ need not be considered: the replacement $\theta\to\pi/2-\theta$ maps the Kraus pair $(K_0,K_1)$ onto $(K_1,K_0)$ up to the sign of the $Z$ term, i.e., onto the mirror unraveling obtained by conjugation with the bit flip $X$. Since the circuit ensembles are invariant under a bit flip on the noisy qubit (see Appendix~\ref{app:thetastar}) and $M_2$ is invariant under Clifford operations, the average magic is symmetric about $\theta=\pi/4$. Similarly, $\phi$ and $\phi+\pi/2$ are related by a sign flip of the $Z$ term and yield the same average magic, while $\phi$ and $-\phi$ are related by complex conjugation, which also leaves $M_2$ and the Haar ensemble invariant. It is therefore sufficient to consider $0\leq\phi\leq\pi/4$.


The role of the phase $\phi$ can be understood from the structure of the Kraus operators, which are diagonal in the computational basis. Defining
\begin{equation}
    a_\pm=\sqrt{1-p}\cos\theta\pm\sqrt{p}\sin\theta ,
\end{equation}
$K_0^{(\theta,\phi)}$ can be factored, up to a global phase, as
\begin{equation}
    K_0^{(\theta,\phi)}
    = 
    \mathrm{diag}\!\left(1,e^{-i\alpha}\right)\,
    \mathrm{diag}\!\left(A_+,A_-\right),
    \label{eq:K0_phase_decomposition}
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
    A_\pm &= \sqrt{a_\pm^2\sin^2\phi+a_\mp^2\cos^2\phi}, \\
    \alpha &= \arctan\!\left(\frac{a_+}{a_-}\tan\phi\right)-\arctan\!\left(\frac{a_-}{a_+}\tan\phi\right).
    \end{aligned}
    \label{eq:K0_phase_parameters}
\end{equation}
An analogous form holds for $K_1^{(\theta,\phi)}$. The second factor in Eq.~\eqref{eq:K0_phase_decomposition} is a positive diagonal operator, i.e., a weak measurement of $Z$, while the first factor is a $Z$ rotation by the angle $\alpha$, which is a non-Clifford operation unless $\alpha$ is a multiple of $\pi/2$. For small $\phi$, expanding to first order gives, up to a global phase,
\begin{equation}
    K_0^{(\theta,\phi)}=
    \mathrm{diag}\!\left(1,e^{-i\alpha}\right)\,
    K_0^{(\theta,0)}+\mathcal{O}(\phi^2),
\end{equation}
with $\alpha\simeq{(a_+^2-a_-^2)}/{(a_+a_-)}\,\phi$.
Hence, the Kraus operator acts as its $\phi=0$ counterpart followed by a small non-Clifford $Z$ rotation, which injects magic into the state. At $\phi=\pi/4$, where $M_2$ is numerically observed to be maximal, one instead finds $A_+=A_-$, so that
\begin{equation}
    K_0^{(\theta,\pi/4)}\propto
    \mathrm{diag}\!\left(1,e^{-i\alpha}\right)
\end{equation}
is a pure phase gate. In this case, the unraveling is unitary: it does not condition the state on any measurement outcome, but simply dresses the circuit with additional random non-Clifford rotations, which leave the magic at the Haar value. These limits show that choosing $\phi>0$ generally injects magic, making $\phi=0$ the most relevant case. The bottom row of Fig.~\ref{fig4} therefore shows the cuts at $\phi=0$, where the Kraus operators describe a two-outcome weak measurement of $Z$. On the same plots, we mark the angle $\theta^\ast$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:thetastar} and the magic obtained with the projective unraveling of Eq.~\eqref{eq:projdeph}.

