Three observations follow. First, the magic at $\theta^\ast$ coincides
with that of the projective unraveling within the statistical error,
for all values of $p$. This is the near coincidence anticipated in
Sec.~\ref{sec:noise} and explained in Appendix~\ref{app:thetastar}:
for a single state the two unravelings yield SREs that differ at first
order in $p$, but the difference is odd under a bit flip on the noisy
qubit, and it therefore averages to zero over the bit-flip-symmetric
ensemble of states generated by the random circuit. Second, the
minimum of the magic is attained at an angle $\theta_{\min}>\theta^\ast$, at
which neither Kraus operator is a projector: both branches are weak
measurements of $Z$, of different strengths. Third, and most
importantly, the minimal magic obtained within the unitary family is
\emph{smaller} than that obtained with the projective unraveling. This
result runs against the intuition that projective measurements, which
map the measured qubit onto a stabilizer state and remove its
contribution to the magic, should be the most effective way of
unraveling noise if the goal is to minimize non-stabilizerness, and
against the widespread use of projective measurements as the paradigm
of magic-destroying operations in the literature on monitored
circuits~\cite{bejan2024dynamicalmagictransitions,fux2024entanglementmagicseparation,tirrito2025magicphasetransitionsmonitored,russomanno2025nonstabilizernessintheunitary}. A similar conclusion was reached for entanglement in Ref.~\cite{zihan2023efficientsaqmpling}, where weak measurements were found to be the most disentangling unraveling of unital channels. It also shows that there is room for a non-trivial optimization of the unraveling even in the state-agnostic setting considered here, where the same Kraus operators are used at every noise event regardless of the current state of the trajectory.

