\subsection{$T$-doped random Clifford circuits}
\label{sec:cliffordT}

Haar-random circuits generate magic rapidly~\cite{turkeshi2025magicspreading}, driving the state to near-maximal magic, and in this sense represent a worst-case scenario. Circuits of practical interest, such as those implementing quantum algorithms on fault-tolerant hardware, are instead naturally expressed in the Clifford$+T$ gate set. Their magic is controlled by the number of $T$ gates and is generally far from maximal~\cite{bravyi2016improvedclassicalsimulation,bravyi2019simulationofquantum,paviglianiti2026truecostfactoringlinking}. Random $T$-doped Clifford circuits~\cite{zhou2020singletgate,haferkamp2023efficientunitarydesigns,leone2021quantumchaosis} provide a minimal model for this situation, in which the rate of magic generation is tuned by the doping probability $q$. They are therefore well suited to assess whether the conclusions drawn for Haar-random circuits carry over to circuits of more direct relevance for the simulability of quantum algorithms.

Figure~\ref{fig6} shows the trajectory-averaged magic at the end of noisy $T$-doped Clifford circuits as a function of $\theta$ and $\phi$, for increasing doping $q$ at $p=0.025$. As in the Haar case, the magic is minimal at $\phi=0$ and, along this line, reaches its lowest value at an angle $\theta_{\min}$ slightly larger than $\theta^\ast$, owing to the structure of the Kraus operators discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:haar}. The reduction of magic at $\theta_{\min}$ is largest at low doping and decreases as $q$ increases, approaching the Haar behavior, although the $T$-doped ensemble does not converge to the Haar ensemble as $q\to1$.
Whether the projective unraveling performs better or worse than the minimal family depends on the doping. At intermediate and high doping (Figs.~\ref{fig6}(e) and~\ref{fig6}(f)), the projective magic is close to that at $\theta^\ast$ and slightly above the minimum of the family, as for Haar-random circuits. At low doping (Fig.~\ref{fig6}(d)), it is lower than that of any unraveling of the family. This can be understood from the Clifford limit $q=0$, in which projective measurements keep every trajectory a stabilizer state with zero magic, whereas the non-Clifford no-click operator $K_0^\ast$ of the $\theta^\ast$ unraveling generates magic (Appendix~\ref{app:thetastar}). By continuity in $q$, this advantage persists at low doping. When magic is scarce, the best unraveling among those considered is therefore not part of the minimal family.


Figure~\ref{fig5b} shows the dependence on $p$ of the optimal unraveling and of the minimal magic in $T$-doped Clifford circuits at fixed $q=0.1$. In contrast with the Haar case, the optimal angle $\theta_{\min}(p)$ follows $\theta^\ast(p)$ closely over the whole range of $p$, and the magic at $\theta^\ast$ is practically indistinguishable from the minimal one. The minimal magic decreases rapidly at small $p$, faster than in the Haar case, and then flattens to a plateau for $p\gtrsim0.1$. This behavior can be understood qualitatively as the outcome of a competition between the injection of magic by the $T$ gates, at a rate set by $q$, and its removal by the measurement-like noise events, at a rate set by $p$.

