Having established that a suitable unraveling reduces the average
magic of the trajectories, we now ask how this reduction scales with
the system size. Fig.~\ref{fig7} shows $\Mavg$ at the end of noisy
Haar-random circuits for the optimal unraveling, as a function of $n$
between $6$ and $12$ and for several values of $p$, together with the
Haar-random value $\MH$, which coincides with the result of the
canonical unraveling. For all $p$, the minimal magic grows linearly
with $n$ within our numerical accuracy: the optimal unraveling does not
change the extensive character of the non-stabilizerness of the
trajectories. 

While the accessible system sizes are too small to exclude subleading
corrections, the data indicate that the unraveling optimization
achieves a constant-factor reduction of the magic density, and not a
change of its scaling. A typical trajectory of the optimally unraveled dynamics thus still carries an amount of magic proportional to the system size, so that its simulation by stabilizer-expansion methods remains exponentially costly in $n$. Whether the smaller magic density also lowers the exponential growth rate of the average cost depends on the full distribution of magic over trajectories, which we analyze next.


