\section{Introduction}
The information carried by an optical signal is limited by quantum fluctuations, environmental noise, and the energy available to the transmitter. Bosonic Gaussian channels describe these effects for a broad range of optical and microwave links~\cite{CavesDrummond1994,HolevoWerner2001,Weedbrook2012}. Their classical capacity asks for the largest communication rate achievable with vanishing decoding error, allowing arbitrary quantum signal states and collective measurements at the receiver~\cite{Holevo1998,SchumacherWestmoreland1997}. Even for a channel acting on a single mode, this is a many-mode optimisation: entanglement between successive inputs might increase the information transmitted per use. As Hastings famously showed by resolving in the negative the additivity conjectures in quantum information theory, such an advantage is possible for general quantum channels~\cite{Hastings2009}.

For phase-insensitive bosonic Gaussian channels, Gaussian minimum-output-entropy theorems resolve this difficulty and establish the optimality of coherent states with Gaussian-distributed random displacements encoding the message~\cite{Giovannetti2004,GGCH2014,GHG2015,MGH2014,Holevo2015RMS}. Phase-sensitive noise, however, introduces an additional trade-off: indeed, squeezing the signal to match the noise minimises its output entropy, but consumes photons that could otherwise carry a message.
As it turns out, this trade-off identifies a precise threshold in the available energy: above, both requirements can be met; below, the optimal Gaussian ensemble is forced to use a different squeezing and random displacements in only one quadrature~\cite{Schafer2013,Karpov2013,Schafer2016}. Correlated-noise models encounter the same obstruction in their phase-sensitive normal modes~\cite{Pilyavets2008,LPM2009,Schafer2009,Schafer2011,PLM2012,Caruso2014}.

Ji~\cite{Ji2026} recently established Gaussian optimality of the energy-constrained single-use Holevo information, using the affine entanglement inequality originally introduced by Adesso in~\cite[Theorem~1]{Adesso2026}. Here we resolve the optimisation over correlated inputs by applying the newly established additivity result in version 3 of that work~\cite[Theorem~2]{Adesso2026}. In particular, we solve the classical-capacity problem for \emph{every} single-mode bosonic Gaussian channel at every input energy: independently prepared Gaussian signals attain the unrestricted capacity, and neither non-Gaussian inputs nor correlations across channel uses can increase the rate.

The key idea is to use a lower bound on the local entropy of a (multimode) bipartite state in terms of appropriately chosen second moments, originally found in~\cite{Adesso2026} and here reported in Proposition~\ref{epr_prop}. By exploiting it, we derive Theorem~\ref{entropy_thm}, which gives an output-entropy lower bound for parallel uses of Gaussian channels in terms of the second moments of the input. This also determines the optimal allocation of a shared energy budget across different links. For passively diagonalisable correlated noise, the same result identifies the optimal signals in the normal-mode basis. For nondegenerate channels at positive energy, the same analysis also implies that the capacity-achieving average output is unique and quantitatively constrains the average output of any near-optimal ensemble.

The capacity marks the largest rate at which information can be transmitted reliably, but it does not by itself say what happens when one attempts to transmit above this rate. 
Under the same mean-energy constraint, we determine exactly how much the transmission rate can increase when a nonzero average decoding error is allowed. The transmitter can devote more energy to a chosen fraction of the messages while keeping the mean energy fixed, accepting errors on the remainder. By contrast, under the stronger requirement that every codeword obey a hard total photon-number cutoff, this possibility is excluded and the probability of correct decoding decreases exponentially at every rate above capacity. We establish both results at every input energy and extend them to the correlated-noise model considered here. These conclusions hold even when the transmitter is allowed arbitrary, possibly non-Gaussian and entangled signals, and the receiver arbitrary collective measurements.


The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. In
Section~\ref{channels_sec} we introduce the channel model and the
Gaussian single-use optimisation. Section~\ref{entropy_sec}
establishes the sharp output-entropy bound, which is then used in
Section~\ref{capacity_sec} to derive the exact classical capacity and
optimal energy allocation. Section~\ref{memory_sec} extends these
results to channels with correlated Gaussian noise.
Section~\ref{strong_converse_sec} first characterises the average outputs of optimal and near-optimal ensembles, then determines the exact rate--error trade-off under the mean-energy constraint and establishes a complementary exponential strong converse under a hard photon-number cutoff. We conclude in Section~\ref{discussion_sec}
with a discussion of the results and open questions. Technical
proofs and quantitative converse bounds are collected in the
appendices.



\section{Channels and Gaussian Holevo information}\label{channels_sec}

We consider unassisted classical communication without feedback over bosonic Gaussian channels, with a constraint on the mean input photon number. We set $\hbar = 1$ and write $x,p$ for the quadrature operators of a mode, so that $[x,p] = i\id$ and its annihilation operator is $a = (x + ip)/\sqrt{2}$. For a state $\rho$, let $R \coloneqq (x,p)^{\mathsf{T}}$ denote the quadrature vector, let
$d \coloneqq \Tr[\rho R]$ be its vector of first moments, with components
$d_j = \Tr[\rho R_j]$, and let $V$ be its covariance matrix, with entries
\bb
V_{jk} \coloneqq \Tr\Big[\rho\big\{R_j - d_j\id,R_k - d_k\id\big\}\Big] \, .
\label{covariance}
\ee
where $\{B,C\} \coloneqq BC + CB$. Thus the vacuum has covariance matrix $V = \id$~\cite{AdessoRagyLee2014,BUCCO}; our covariance matrices are twice those used in~\cite{Adesso2026}. The mean photon number is
\bb
\Tr[\rho a^\dag a] = \frac{\Tr[V] + 2|d|^2 - 2}{4} \, .
\label{energy}
\ee

For a channel $\Phi$ acting on $m$ input modes, let
$H_m \coloneqq \sum_{j = 1}^m a_j^\dag a_j$. An ensemble
$(p_i,\rho_i)_i$ with average $\bar\rho \coloneqq \sum_i p_i\rho_i$
has Holevo information~\cite{Holevo1998,SchumacherWestmoreland1997}
\bb
\chi\big(\Phi;(p_i,\rho_i)_i\big)
\coloneqq S\big(\Phi(\bar\rho)\big)
- \sum_i p_i S\big(\Phi(\rho_i)\big) \, ,
\label{ensemble_holevo}
\ee
where $S(\rho) \coloneqq -\Tr[\rho\ln\rho]$ is the von Neumann
entropy. We use natural logarithms throughout, so entropies and
communication rates are measured in nats; the figures are reported
in bits. For continuous ensembles, the sums above are replaced by
integrals.

Maximising the Holevo information over all ensembles
whose average input obeys the energy constraint defines the
energy-constrained Holevo information of the channel:
\bb
\chi(\Phi,E) \coloneqq \sup_{(p_i,\rho_i)_i:\ \Tr[\bar\rho H_m] \leq E}\chi\big(\Phi;(p_i,\rho_i)_i\big) \, .
\label{channel_holevo}
\ee
Here and below, the optimisation includes continuous ensembles with finite mean energy. The corresponding \emph{classical capacity}, measured per use of the entire channel $\Phi$, is
\bb
C(\Phi,E) = \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\chi\big(\Phi^{\otimes n},nE\big) \, .
\label{capacity_regularisation}
\ee
This is the capacity for vanishing average decoding error~\cite{HolevoBook,HolevoShirokov2006}.

After removing an irrelevant output displacement, a single-mode Gaussian channel acts on first and second moments of an arbitrary input state as $d\mapsto Xd$ and $V\mapsto XVX^{\mathsf{T}} + Y$, where the real $2\times2$ matrices $X,Y$ satisfy $Y \geq 0$ and $\det Y \geq (1 - \det X)^2$~\cite{HolevoBook}.  We call a Gaussian unitary without displacements a \emph{symplectic unitary}; its action on quadratures is $U^\dag R U = SR$ for a real symplectic matrix $S$. If $\tau \coloneqq \det X \neq 0$ and $Y>0$, an input phase rotation and an output symplectic unitary bring the channel to the form specified by
\bb
X = \sqrt{k}\,\diag(1,\varsigma),\qquad Y = y\,\diag(\xi^{-1},\xi) \, ,
\label{canonical}
\ee
where $k \coloneqq |\tau|$, $\varsigma \coloneqq \sgn\tau$, $0<\xi \leq 1$, and $y>0$ satisfies $y \geq |1 - \tau|$. We refer to~\eqref{canonical} as the canonical form~\cite{Schafer2013}; the reduction leading to it is given in Appendix~\ref{canonical_app}. Because the required input rotation preserves photon number and the output unitary preserves the Holevo information, this reduction does not affect the communication problem under consideration. The form~\eqref{canonical} includes attenuators ($0<\tau<1$), amplifiers ($\tau>1$), phase conjugators ($\tau<0$), and additive-noise channels ($\tau=1$) with positive definite noise, while the phase-insensitive subclass is recovered for $\xi=1$.

In what follows, we shall work under the nondegeneracy assumptions $\det X\neq0$ and $Y>0$. The remaining cases are recovered by continuity in Appendix~\ref{continuity_app}, thus ensuring that our final capacity result holds for all single-mode bosonic Gaussian channels.


\subsection{Gaussian signal ensembles}

A Gaussian state $\rho$ is uniquely determined by its first moments and covariance matrix \cite{AdessoRagyLee2014,BUCCO}. For one mode its von Neumann entropy depends only on the symplectic eigenvalue $\mu = \sqrt{\det V} \geq 1$ and is given by \cite{HolevoWerner2001}
\bb
S(\rho)=G(\mu) \coloneqq \frac{\mu + 1}{2}\ln\frac{\mu + 1}{2} - \frac{\mu - 1}{2}\ln\frac{\mu - 1}{2} \, .
\label{gaussian_entropy}
\ee
In particular, $G(\mu)$ is the entropy of the thermal state with covariance matrix $V = \mu\id$, whose mean photon number is $(\mu - 1)/2$.

A Gaussian signal ensemble consists of a fixed Gaussian input state, also known as the seed, together with a Gaussian distribution of displacements applied to it. The displacement distribution may be supported on a single quadrature; for example, displacement only in $p$ leaves the $x$ displacement equal to zero for every signal. We denote by $\chiG(\Phi,E)$ the supremum in~\eqref{channel_holevo} restricted to such ensembles, and call it the \emph{energy-constrained Gaussian Holevo information}. Throughout, $\chiG(\Phi,E)$ refers to this single-use optimisation.

Fix a Gaussian channel in the canonical form~\eqref{canonical}. To calculate $\chiG(\Phi,N)$, we first fix the average input state $\bar\rho$ to be the centred Gaussian state with covariance matrix $\diag(z,w)$, where $z,w>0$ and $zw \geq 1$, and optimise over Gaussian pure-state ensembles whose average is exactly $\bar\rho$. Only after this optimisation do we vary $z,w$ under the energy constraint. The fact that we can take $\bar\rho$ to have diagonal covariance matrix is proved in~\cite[Sec.~III, after Eq.~(14)]{Schafer2016}. For the fixed average state, the first term of the Holevo information is
\bb
h(z,w) \coloneqq G\!\left(\sqrt{(kz + y/\xi)(kw + y\xi)}\right) .
\label{h}
\ee

For a fixed average state $\bar\rho$, maximising the Holevo information therefore amounts to minimising the average output entropy of the individual signal states. It suffices to use a pure Gaussian seed with Gaussian-distributed random displacements~\cite[Supplemental Material, Eqs.~(55)--(62)]{Schafer2013}.

A pure squeezed vacuum $\gamma_u$ has covariance matrix $V_u = \diag(u,1/u)$ and output entropy $h(u,1/u)$. To obtain the prescribed $\bar\rho$ from displaced copies of $\gamma_u$, the centred Gaussian distribution of displacements must have covariance matrix $[\diag(z,w) - V_u]/2$. This is a valid covariance matrix precisely when $1/w \leq u \leq z$. Since displacements leave output entropy unchanged, optimising the pure-state ensemble with this average input amounts to choosing the squeezed vacuum with the least output entropy in that interval:
\bb
\varepsilon(z,w)&\coloneqq \min_{1/w \leq u \leq z}h(u,1/u) = h(\hat u,1/\hat u),\\
\hat u &\coloneqq \min\!\left\{z,\max\!\left\{1/w,1/\xi\right\}\right\} \, .
\label{eps}
\ee
Here $\hat u$ is the point in $[1/w,z]$ closest to $1/\xi$: indeed, $u\mapsto h(u,1/u)$ decreases up to $1/\xi$ and increases thereafter. Without the restriction on the average state, $\gamma_{1/\xi}$ attains the minimum output entropy $S_{\min} = G(k + y)$~\cite{GHG2015,Schafer2013}. The function $\varepsilon$ is nonincreasing in either variable, because increasing $z$ or $w$ enlarges the interval over which the minimum in~\eqref{eps} is taken.

For this fixed $\bar\rho$, the optimal Gaussian ensemble has Holevo information $h(z,w) - \varepsilon(z,w)$. Optimising over the covariance matrix of $\bar\rho$ gives the standard single-use Gaussian optimisation~\cite[Eq.~(11)]{Schafer2013}, written here in terms of the two diagonal moments:
\bb
\chiG(\Phi,N) = \max_{\substack{z,w>0,\ zw \geq 1\\ z + w = 4N + 2}}\big\{h(z,w) - \varepsilon(z,w)\big\} \, .
\label{gaussian_cap}
\ee

There is a smallest input energy for which the ensemble can use the minimum-output-entropy state $\gamma_{1/\xi}$ and, through random displacements, make the average output covariance matrix proportional to the identity. According to~\cite[Corollary~2, Eq.~(12)]{Schafer2013} and~\cite[Eq.~(28)]{Schafer2016}, its value is
\bb
N_{\rm th} \coloneqq \frac12\left[\frac1\xi - 1 + \frac{y(\xi^{-1} - \xi)}{2k}\right] \, .
\label{threshold}
\ee
For input energies $N \geq N_{\rm th}$, the same ensemble attains both the largest entropy of the average output allowed by the energy budget and the smallest entropy of every individual output. Consequently, in this regime
\bb
\chiG(\Phi,N) = G\!\left(k(2N + 1) + \frac{y(\xi + \xi^{-1})}{2}\right) - S_{\min} \, .
\label{above_threshold}
\ee
When the input energy satisfies $0<N<N_{\rm th}$, with $N_{\rm th}$ defined in~\eqref{threshold}, it is not possible to use an input ensemble with seed $\gamma_{1/\xi}$ and a thermal average output state. The known optimum instead has $z = u_*<1/\xi$ and uses $\gamma_{u_*}$ displaced only in $p$~\cite[Sec.~III.B, Eqs.~(31), (43)--(46)]{Schafer2016}\cite[Eq.~(27)]{Karpov2013}. At $N = 0$ the only input is vacuum and the Holevo information vanishes. Appendix~\ref{gaussian_app} recalls this optimisation.

The distinction between a single-use optimisation and a regularised capacity matters here. The Gaussian capacity defined in~\cite[Eq.~(10)]{Schafer2013} is regularised, and~\cite[Corollary~2, Eq.~(12)]{Schafer2013} proves Gaussian additivity for input energies $N \geq N_{\rm th}$, with $N_{\rm th}$ defined in~\eqref{threshold}. It does not establish additivity when $0<N<N_{\rm th}$. By contrast,~\cite[Sec.~III, Eq.~(10)]{Schafer2016} calculates a single-use Gaussian optimisation. Our capacity result in Sec.~\ref{capacity_sec} removes this energy restriction and identifies the single-use Gaussian value with the unrestricted classical capacity at every input energy.

\section{A sharp output-entropy bound}\label{entropy_sec}

The Holevo information in~\eqref{ensemble_holevo} is the difference of two entropy terms. The entropy of the average output can be bounded from above by subadditivity and by replacing each marginal with the Gaussian state having the same moments; the main task is therefore to lower-bound the entropy of the individual outputs. We prove a bound that depends only on the input quadrature second moments and is tight for squeezed vacua. Keeping both moments is essential when the input energy is below the threshold in~\eqref{threshold}, because the optimal Gaussian seed then differs from the unconstrained minimum-output-entropy input.

\begin{theorem}[(Output entropy from second moments)]\label{entropy_thm}
Let $\Phi_1,\dots,\Phi_m$ be single-mode Gaussian channels in the canonical form~\eqref{canonical}. For every state $\rho$ of $m$ modes with finite mean energy, possibly entangled and non-Gaussian,
\bb
S\bigl((\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m)(\rho)\bigr) \geq \sum_{j = 1}^m\varepsilon_j\bigl(2\langle x_j^2\rangle_\rho,2\langle p_j^2\rangle_\rho\bigr) \, .
\label{entropy}
\ee
Here $\varepsilon_j$ is the function $\varepsilon$ defined in~\eqref{eps}, evaluated with the parameters $k_j,y_j,\xi_j$ of the $j^{\,\text{th}}$ channel.
Equality holds for every product of squeezed vacua $\gamma_{u_j}$. Furthermore, for all $z_j,w_j>0$ such that $z_jw_j \geq 1$, the bound is tight under the corresponding upper bounds on the second moments:
\bb
\sum_{j = 1}^m\varepsilon_j(z_j,w_j) = \min\Big\{ &S\bigl((\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m)(\rho)\bigr):\\
&2\langle x_j^2\rangle_\rho \leq z_j,\ 2\langle p_j^2\rangle_\rho \leq w_j\ \,\forall\, j\Big\}\, .
\label{entropy_tightness}
\ee
The minimum can be attained with all the moment constraints saturated.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $z_j = 2\Tr[\rho x_j^2]$ and $w_j = 2\Tr[\rho p_j^2]$. We first obtain lower bounds that are affine in these moments. The two auxiliary ingredients used in the proof, Proposition~\ref{epr_prop} and Lemma~\ref{witness_lemma}, are established in Appendix~\ref{entropy_app}. For each channel, choose a squeezed vacuum $\gamma_{u_j}$, with $u_j\neq1/\xi_j$, independently of the actual input $\rho$. Lemma~\ref{witness_lemma} constructs a dilation by a symplectic unitary on the input mode $A_j$ and two auxiliary modes $B_j,E_j$, initially in vacuum. After a local squeezing of the receiver $A_j$, the resulting unitary $\widetilde V_{j,u_j}$ sends $\gamma_{u_j}\otimes\ketbra{0}_{B_jE_j}$ to a two-mode squeezed vacuum on $A_jB_j$ and vacuum on $E_j$. Apply the product of these unitaries to $\rho_A$ and the auxiliary vacuum, then trace out $E = E_1\ldots E_m$:
\bb
\Omega_{AB} \coloneqq \Tr_E\Bigg[&\left(\bigotimes\nolimits_j\widetilde V_{j,u_j}\right)\big(\rho_A\otimes\ketbra{0}_{BE}\big)\left(\bigotimes\nolimits_j\widetilde V_{j,u_j}\right)^\dag\Bigg] \, .
\label{entropy_dilation}
\ee
Here $A = A_1\ldots A_m$, $B = B_1\ldots B_m$, and $|0\rangle_{BE}$ is the joint vacuum of the $2m$ auxiliary modes. The state $\Omega_{AB}$ has finite energy, but in general will be mixed and potentially non-Gaussian.
Its marginal on $A$ is unitarily equivalent to $(\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m)(\rho)$, so it has the same entropy.

We apply the mixed-state form of the many-pair entropy inequality, Proposition~\ref{epr_prop}, to $\Omega_{AB}$. Lemma~\ref{witness_lemma} chooses the coefficients of each quadratic observable so that its expectation depends on only one input quadrature moment. Substituting its identity~\eqref{pullback} into~\eqref{epr}, for $u_j<1/\xi_j$ on every mode, gives
\bb
&S\big((\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m)(\rho)\big)\\
&\quad\geq\sum_{j = 1}^m\left[h_j(u_j,1/u_j) + \left.\frac{d}{dv}h_j(v,1/v)\right|_{v = u_j}(z_j - u_j)\right] \, .
\label{main_tangent}
\ee
Here $h_j$ is the function $h$ defined in~\eqref{h}, evaluated with the parameters $k_j,y_j,\xi_j$ of the $j^{\,\text{th}}$ channel.
Each summand is the tangent to the squeezed-vacuum output entropy as a function of its position second moment. On any mode we may instead choose $u_j>1/\xi_j$, obtaining the tangent to $w\mapsto h_j(1/w,w)$ at $w = 1/u_j$, evaluated at $w_j$. The many-pair inequality is essential here: separate bounds on the marginal output entropies would not give this lower bound on their joint entropy.

We can now choose $u_j$ so that the affine corrections vanish. If $z_j<1/\xi_j$, take $u_j = z_j$; if $w_j<\xi_j$, take $u_j = 1/w_j$. These cases are mutually exclusive because $z_jw_j \geq 1$, and the resulting summand is exactly $\varepsilon_j(z_j,w_j)$. Otherwise both $z_j\geq1/\xi_j$ and $w_j\geq\xi_j$, so take $u_j\to1/\xi_j$. The seed entropy tends to $S_{\min,j}$ and its derivative tends to zero, giving the same conclusion. This derivative limit also holds when $k_j + y_j = 1$: the output symplectic eigenvalue minus one vanishes quadratically in $u_j - 1/\xi_j$, which compensates for the logarithmic divergence of the entropy derivative. Taking these scalar limits mode by mode proves~\eqref{entropy}.

For a product of squeezed vacua the interval in~\eqref{eps} collapses to a single point on each mode, so equality holds in~\eqref{entropy}. To prove~\eqref{entropy_tightness}, every feasible state satisfies
\bb
S\big((\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m)(\rho)\big) &\geq \sum_j\varepsilon_j\big(2\langle x_j^2\rangle_\rho,2\langle p_j^2\rangle_\rho\big)\\
&\geq \sum_j\varepsilon_j(z_j,w_j),
\ee
where the second inequality uses that each $\varepsilon_j$ is nonincreasing in both arguments. Let $\hat u_j$ be the minimiser in~\eqref{eps} for the $j^{\,\text{th}}$ channel. The product $\bigotimes_j\gamma_{\hat u_j}$ attains this value and obeys the constraints. Moreover, displacing its $j^{\,\text{th}}$ mode to have first moments $\sqrt{(z_j - \hat u_j)/2}$ and $\sqrt{(w_j - 1/\hat u_j)/2}$ makes both constraints equalities, while leaving every output entropy unchanged.
\end{proof}

The same affine bounds also establish the joint convexity of $\varepsilon$, which will be used in the capacity argument. For a single channel they give
\bb
\!\!\!\!\varepsilon(z,w) = \max\Bigg\{&S_{\min},\\
&\sup_{0<u<1/\xi}\left[h(u,1/u) + \frac{d}{du}h(u,1/u)(z - u)\right],\\
&\sup_{0<v<\xi}\left[h(1/v,v) + \frac{d}{dv}h(1/v,v)(w - v)\right]\Bigg\} \, .
\label{eps_affine}
\ee
Indeed, for any feasible $q\in[1/w,z]$, the tangent bounds evaluated on $\gamma_q$, together with their nonpositive slopes and the inequalities $q\leq z$ and $1/q\leq w$, show that every affine function on the right-hand side is no larger than $h(q,1/q)$. Minimising over such $q$ therefore bounds the right-hand side by $\varepsilon(z,w)$. Equality is obtained by choosing $u=z$ when $z<1/\xi$, or $v=w$ when $w<\xi$; when both $z\geq1/\xi$ and $w\geq\xi$, the constant branch $S_{\min}$ is attained. Hence $\varepsilon$ is a supremum of affine functions and is jointly convex.


\section{Capacity and energy allocation}\label{capacity_sec}

We now use the output-entropy bound of Theorem~\ref{entropy_thm} to control the Holevo information of an arbitrary ensemble. The theorem provides a lower bound on the average entropy of the individual outputs, so it remains to bound from above the entropy of the average output. By subadditivity, this entropy is no larger than the sum of the marginal output entropies, and each marginal entropy can only increase when the state is replaced by the Gaussian state with the same moments. The determinant of the resulting covariance matrix is at most the product of its two diagonal second moments, yielding
\bb
S\big((\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m)(\bar\rho)\big)
\leq \sum_{j=1}^m h_j(z_j,w_j).
\ee
Combining the two bounds gives the following result.

\begin{theorem}[(Moment-resolved Gaussian optimality)]\label{holevo_thm}
Let $\Phi_1,\dots,\Phi_m$ be single-mode Gaussian channels in the canonical form~\eqref{canonical}. For every ensemble $(p_i,\rho_i)_i$ of states of $m$ modes with finite mean energy, possibly entangled and non-Gaussian, whose average has second moments $z_j = 2\langle x_j^2\rangle$ and $w_j = 2\langle p_j^2\rangle$,
\begin{equation}
\chi\big(\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m;(p_i,\rho_i)_i\big) \leq \sum_{j = 1}^m\big[h_j(z_j,w_j) - \varepsilon_j(z_j,w_j)\big].
\label{holevo}
\end{equation}
Here $h_j$ and $\varepsilon_j$ are the functions defined in~\eqref{h} and~\eqref{eps}, respectively, evaluated with the parameters $k_j,y_j,\xi_j$ of $\Phi_j$. For all admissible $(z_j,w_j)$, equality is attained by a product of Gaussian ensembles with these second moments.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{entropy_thm} to every signal state in the ensemble. Since $\varepsilon_j$ is convex and second moments are affine in the state, Jensen's inequality bounds the average signal output entropy from below by $\sum_j\varepsilon_j(z_j,w_j)$. Subtracting this from the upper bound on the average output entropy proves~\eqref{holevo}. The product of the Gaussian displacement ensembles constructed after~\eqref{eps} attains equality, because its average covariance matrix is diagonal and every displaced signal has output entropy $\sum_j\varepsilon_j(z_j,w_j)$.
\end{proof}

Since $h_j$ is jointly concave and $\varepsilon_j$ is convex, the difference
$h_j(z,w)-\varepsilon_j(z,w)$ is jointly concave. Theorem~\ref{holevo_thm}
therefore reduces the ensemble optimisation to a concave optimisation over
the admissible diagonal second moments, with every feasible point attained
by a product Gaussian ensemble.
For an average input state with total mean photon number at most $E$, the second moments satisfy
$\sum_{j=1}^m (z_j+w_j)\leq 4E+2m$.
Using the reduction to canonical form, which preserves the input photon number, together with the continuity argument for the remaining degenerate channels given in Appendix~\ref{continuity_app}, we obtain the following.

\begin{corollary}[(Capacities and water-filling)]\label{capacity_cor}
For arbitrary single-mode Gaussian channels $\Phi_1,\dots,\Phi_m$,
\bb
\chi(\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m,E)
=
\max_{\substack{N_j\geq0\\ \sum_jN_j\leq E}}
\sum_{j=1}^m \chiG(\Phi_j,N_j).
\label{waterfilling}
\ee
Here $\chiG(\Phi,N)$ is obtained by restricting the optimisation in
Eq.~\eqref{channel_holevo} to Gaussian signal ensembles. For each $\Phi$,
the function $N\mapsto\chiG(\Phi,N)$ is concave. In particular:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item for every single-mode Gaussian channel $\Phi$,
\[
\chi(\Phi^{\otimes n},nN)=n\,\chiG(\Phi,N),
\]
and hence
\[
C(\Phi,N)=\chiG(\Phi,N)
\]
at every input energy;

\item for any two finite products $\Phi$ and $\Psi$ of single-mode Gaussian channels,
\[
\chi(\Phi\otimes\Psi,E)
=
\max_{\substack{E_1,E_2\geq0\\E_1+E_2\leq E}}
\big[\chi(\Phi,E_1)+\chi(\Psi,E_2)\big];
\]

\item the classical capacity of
$\Phi_1\otimes\cdots\otimes\Phi_m$ at total energy $E$ per use is given by
the water-filling formula~\eqref{waterfilling}.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

Statement (i) completes the single-use result of Ji~\cite{Ji2026} and, together with Refs.~\cite{GGCH2014,GHG2015,Schafer2013}, determines the classical capacity of all single-mode Gaussian channels. When the input energy is below $N_{\rm th}$ in~\eqref{threshold}, the capacity is the Gaussian value determined by the transcendental optimisation of Refs.~\cite{Schafer2016,Karpov2013}, which is therefore not merely the best Gaussian rate. Figure~\ref{capacity_fig}(a) shows this for an attenuator with squeezed thermal noise. In the low-energy regime, the exact capacity lies well below the simpler bound obtained by maximising the entropy of the average output and subtracting the minimum output entropy; that bound becomes tight only for $N\geq N_{\rm th}$.

Statement (ii) is an energy-constrained strong additivity result within the class of single-mode Gaussian channels~\cite{MSW2004,Shor2004}: correlated encodings across different channel blocks provide no advantage, and the optimal Holevo information is obtained by dividing the total energy between the blocks and optimising them separately.

Statement (iii) turns this into an explicit energy-allocation rule for a collection of different Gaussian channels. Since each $\chiG(\Phi_j,N_j)$ is concave in $N_j$, the optimal allocation has the usual water-filling structure: among the channels that receive nonzero energy, the marginal gains $\partial_{N_j}\chiG(\Phi_j,N_j)$ are equalised. In the present quantum setting, however, the structure of the optimal signal within each channel also depends on the allocated energy~\cite{Schafer2011}. A channel may be left unused, operated below its threshold $N_{{\rm th},j}$ with a squeezed seed displaced in only one quadrature, or operated above threshold with displacements in both quadratures. Different carrier frequencies or mode costs are included by assigning positive costs $c_j$ and replacing the constraint by $\sum_j c_jN_j\leq E$, in which case the active modes equalise $\partial_{N_j}\chiG(\Phi_j,N_j)/c_j$.
