\section{Communication with correlated noise}\label{memory_sec}

Correlated noise is a natural model for bosonic channels with memory~\cite{KretschmannWerner2005,Caruso2014}. In the quadrature ordering $(x_1,\ldots,x_n,p_1,\ldots,p_n)$, consider a Gaussian channel with transfer matrix
$X=\sqrt{k}(\id_n\oplus\varsigma\id_n)$ and noise covariance matrix
$Y=Y_x\oplus Y_p$, where $\varsigma=\pm1$. The matrices $Y_x$ and $Y_p$
describe correlations across channel uses, with no position--momentum cross
block. This class includes lossy channels with correlated environments
($\varsigma=1$, $k<1$)~\cite{Pilyavets2008,LPM2009,PLM2012}, additive
correlated noise ($k=1$)~\cite{Schafer2009,Schafer2011}, and the corresponding
amplifying and conjugating channels.

When $Y_x$ and $Y_p$ commute, the correlations can be diagonalised by the same passive transformation in both quadratures. The channel then decomposes into independent normal modes, so the capacity result of the previous section applies directly.

\begin{corollary}[(Memory channels)]\label{memory_cor}
If $Y_xY_p=Y_pY_x$, the $n$-use channel is equivalent, through a passive transformation that preserves the total photon number, to a product of single-mode channels with noise $\diag(y_{x,l},y_{p,l})$, where $y_{x,l}$ and $y_{p,l}$ are the eigenvalues of $Y_x$ and $Y_p$ on a common eigenvector. The capacity of this block, when independently repeated with energy $E$ per block, is given by Eq.~\eqref{waterfilling} over these normal modes.
\end{corollary}

Indeed, if $O$ is an orthogonal matrix diagonalising both $Y_x$ and $Y_p$, the transformation $x\mapsto Ox$, $p\mapsto Op$ is a passive linear-optical network acting identically on the input and output quadratures, and it commutes with the transfer matrix.

As a concrete example, consider the lossy memory channel of Lupo, Pilyavets and Mancini~\cite{LPM2009}, for which
$Y_x=(1-\eta)(2T+1)e^{s\Delta}$ and
$Y_p=(1-\eta)(2T+1)e^{-s\Delta}$, where $\Delta$ is the adjacency matrix of a chain. The normal modes are attenuators with squeezed thermal environments of covariance matrix
$(2T+1)\diag(e^{2s\cos\theta_l},e^{-2s\cos\theta_l})$, with
$\theta_l=\pi l/(n+1)$. Previous work obtained upper and lower bounds for this model, which coincide only at zero temperature and in part of the parameter space~\cite{LPM2009}. Corollary~\ref{memory_cor} instead determines the Holevo information of every finite block exactly. In the limit $n\to\infty$, the capacity per mode converges to
\bb
C_{\rm mem}(N)
\coloneqq
\sup_{\substack{N_\theta\geq0\\
\pi^{-1}\int_0^\pi N_\theta\,d\theta\leq N}}
\frac1\pi\int_0^\pi
\chiG(\Phi_\theta,N_\theta)\,d\theta .
\label{memoryintegral}
\ee
This limiting capacity is shown in Fig.~\ref{capacity_fig}(b); for the parameters considered there, correlated noise permits a larger rate than independent noise with the same bulk single-use marginals. Appendix~\ref{memory_app} establishes that the limiting rate is operationally achievable along the growing sequence of blocks.

The same reduction applies more broadly whenever the position and momentum noise matrices commute. This includes the circulant Gauss--Markov model of Ref.~\cite{Schafer2009} and, more generally, stationary models of this form: their known finite-block Gaussian Holevo optima~\cite{Schafer2009,Schafer2011} are therefore also unrestricted optima. For a growing sequence of correlated blocks, however, identifying the finite-block optimum is not by itself sufficient to establish an operational asymptotic capacity; one must also control the limiting mode distribution and the associated coding and energy constraints. These additional conditions are treated in Appendix~\ref{memory_app}.

\section{Communication above capacity: decoding error and strong converse}\label{strong_converse_sec}

Having determined the capacity, we next examine the average outputs of optimal and near-optimal signal ensembles and the rates achievable when a nonzero decoding error is allowed. The decoding error is the receiver's probability of misidentifying the transmitted message, and we retain the same mean-input-energy constraint as in the vanishing-error problem.

\subsection{Optimal and near-optimal average outputs}

Quantum relative entropy provides a useful description of optimal signal ensembles~\cite{SchumacherWestmoreland2001}. In our setting, the capacity-achieving Gaussian average output supplies a reference state against which the average output of any ensemble can be compared. Let $H_n=\sum_{j=1}^n a_j^\dag a_j$ be the total photon-number operator, and let $D(\rho\|\sigma)\coloneqq\Tr[\rho(\ln\rho-\ln\sigma)]$ denote the quantum relative entropy.

\begin{proposition}[(Tight Gaussian reference)]\label{radius_prop}
Fix a single-mode Gaussian channel in the canonical form~\eqref{canonical} and $N>0$. Let $\sigma_*$ be the centred Gaussian average output of an optimal ensemble at energy $N$, and let $\lambda = \partial_N C(\Phi,N)$. Then, for every $n$-mode input $\rho$ with finite energy,
\bb
D\Bigl(\Phi^{\otimes n}(\rho)\Big\|\sigma_*^{\otimes n}\Bigr) \leq  nC(\Phi,N) + \lambda\big(\Tr[\rho H_n] - nN\big).
\label{radius}
\ee
In particular, the supremum of the left-hand side over $\Tr[\rho H_n] \leq  nN$ equals $nC(\Phi,N)$.
\end{proposition}

The proof in Appendix~\ref{radius_app} combines the entropy bound of Theorem~\ref{entropy_thm} with the logarithm of $\sigma_*$ to bound the relative entropy in terms of the input second moments. At the capacity-achieving point, the optimality condition makes the coefficients of the two quadrature moments coincide, so their contribution depends only on the total input photon number.

Under the assumptions of Proposition~\ref{radius_prop}, consider any ensemble $(p_i,\rho_i)_i$ with average $\bar\rho=\sum_i p_i\rho_i$ satisfying $\Tr[\bar\rho H_n]\leq nN$. Write $\bar\sigma_n=\Phi^{\otimes n}(\bar\rho)$ and $\chi_n=\chi\big(\Phi^{\otimes n};(p_i,\rho_i)_i\big)$. Averaging Eq.~\eqref{radius} and separating off the Holevo information gives
\bb
D\big(\bar\sigma_n\big\|\sigma_*^{\otimes n}\big)
\leq nC(\Phi,N)-\chi_n \eqqcolon \Delta_{\chi,n}.
\label{average_output_deficit}
\ee
The short derivation, which also applies to continuous ensembles, is given in Appendix~\ref{radius_app}. An ensemble attaining $\chi_n=nC(\Phi,N)$ must therefore have $\bar\sigma_n=\sigma_*^{\otimes n}$: the optimal average output is unique, although its ensemble decomposition need not be.

More generally, Pinsker's inequality in our natural-logarithm convention gives $\frac12\|\bar\sigma_n-\sigma_*^{\otimes n}\|_1\leq\sqrt{\Delta_{\chi,n}/2}$, so a small total Holevo deficit implies closeness of the full average output in trace distance. A vanishing per-use deficit, $\Delta_{\chi,n}/n\to0$, instead guarantees vanishing relative entropy per use, but need not imply global trace-distance convergence. The one-mode average-output marginals nevertheless converge to $\sigma_*$ on average over the channel uses; Appendix~\ref{radius_app} gives the quantitative bound.

\subsection{Exact nonzero-error capacity}

We now turn to the reliability of communication under the mean-energy constraint. Consider an $n$-use code with $M_n$ equiprobable messages, average decoding error $\epsilon_n$, success probability $p_{\rm succ}^{(n)}=1-\epsilon_n$, and rate $R_n=n^{-1}\ln M_n$; we suppress the superscript on $p_{\rm succ}$ when the block length is fixed. The input energy averaged over the messages is required to be at most $nN$. For $0\leq\epsilon<1$, let $C_\epsilon(\Phi,N)$ be the supremum of rates $R$ for which a sequence of such codes satisfies $\liminf_{n\to\infty}R_n\geq R$ and $\limsup_{n\to\infty}\epsilon_n\leq\epsilon$.

\begin{theorem}[(Exact nonzero-error classical capacity)]\label{epsilonexact_cor}
Under the mean-input-energy constraint, with both energy and decoding error averaged over equiprobable messages, every single-mode Gaussian channel $\Phi$ satisfies
\bb
C_\epsilon(\Phi,N) = C\!\left(\Phi,\frac{N}{1 - \epsilon}\right),
\qquad N\geq0,\quad 0\leq\epsilon<1.
\label{exactepsilon}
\ee
\end{theorem}

Allowing an average decoding error $\epsilon$ therefore changes the energy argument of the vanishing-error capacity from $N$ to $N/(1-\epsilon)$; the resulting rate is generally different from $C(\Phi,N)/(1-\epsilon)$. This determines the entire asymptotic rate--error trade-off under the original mean-energy constraint, including the regime in which the input energy is below $N_{\rm th}$ in~\eqref{threshold}, as shown in Fig.~\ref{converse_fig}(a).

The achievable direction follows by diluting a reliable higher-energy code with the vacuum, as in Eq.~\eqref{dilution}, while the converse proves that arbitrary quantum encodings cannot improve this trade-off. The proof in Appendix~\ref{error_app} combines that construction with the photon-number-cutoff converse of Proposition~\ref{strong_sc_thm} below and a projection estimate, Proposition~\ref{transfer_prop}. The estimate transfers the cutoff converse to mean-energy-constrained codes while retaining coherences between the low- and high-photon-number sectors.

\subsection{A complementary converse under a photon-number cutoff}

The same dilution construction explains why a mean-energy constraint does not, in general, admit a strong converse. Whenever $C(\Phi,N)<R<C(\Phi,N')$ for some $N'>N>0$, mixing each codeword of a reliable code at energy $N'$ with vacuum, with weight $N/N'$ on the original codeword, gives a code at mean energy $N$ whose success probability stays at least $N/N'$ asymptotically. Thus success need not tend to zero above $C(\Phi,N)$. This obstruction occurs even with a separate mean-energy bound for each codeword, as shown for the pure-loss channel~\cite{WildeWinter2014}.

A strict photon-number cutoff excludes those higher-energy components by requiring every codeword to be supported on the span of $n$-mode Fock states with total photon number at most $\lfloor nE\rfloor$. Under this stronger constraint the vanishing-error capacity is unchanged, but the success probability decays exponentially at every rate above it.

\begin{proposition}[(Exponential strong converse under a photon-number cutoff)]\label{strong_sc_thm}
For every single-mode Gaussian channel $\Phi$, every $E>0$, and every $R>C(\Phi,E)$, there exist $a>0$ and $n_0$ such that every $M_n$-message code with $\ln M_n \geq nR$, whose codewords are all supported on the span of $n$-mode Fock states with total photon number at most $\lfloor nE\rfloor$, satisfies
\bb
p_{\rm succ}^{(n)}<e^{-an},\qquad n \geq  n_0.
\label{strong_sc}
\ee
The encodings may be non-Gaussian and entangled across all channel uses, and the decoder may be collective. The vanishing-error capacity under this cutoff equals $C(\Phi,E)$. At $E = 0$, all inputs are vacuum and $p_{\rm succ}^{(n)} = 1/M_n$.
\end{proposition}

Proposition~\ref{strong_sc_thm} extends the known photon-number-constrained converses for phase-insensitive channels~\cite{Bardhan2015} to the exact capacity of every single-mode Gaussian channel at every input energy. Its energy-filter proof uses the capacity bound of Corollary~\ref{capacity_cor} and is given in Appendix~\ref{energyfilter_app}; cutoff achievability is established in Appendix~\ref{cutoffach_app}.

The energy-filter proof also yields a computable exponent $\mathcal{E}_{\rm filt}(R,E)>0$ for every $R>C(\Phi,E)$ under the photon-number cutoff, including when $E<N_{\rm th}$ in~\eqref{threshold}; an explicit expression is given in Appendix~\ref{filterquant_app}. It applies to arbitrary codewords satisfying the cutoff, although its constants are conservative. A separate photon-number-tail argument gives the simpler exponent $\mathcal{E}_{\rm ph}$ derived in Appendix~\ref{photon_sc_app}, which is positive whenever $R>U(\Phi,E)$, where
\bb
N_{\rm out}(E)&=kE+\frac{y(\xi+\xi^{-1})+2k-2}{4},\\
U(\Phi,E)&=G(2N_{\rm out}(E)+1)-S_{\min}.
\label{scthreshold}
\ee

For a Gaussian channel in the canonical form~\eqref{canonical}, $U(\Phi,E)=C(\Phi,E)$ whenever $E\geq N_{\rm th}$, with $N_{\rm th}$ given by~\eqref{threshold}. Thus, above threshold, the simpler photon-tail bound is already positive at every rate strictly above capacity under the cutoff. When $E<N_{\rm th}$, one can instead have $U(\Phi,E)>C(\Phi,E)$, so this simpler bound covers only part of the above-capacity region, whereas $\mathcal{E}_{\rm filt}$ remains positive throughout. Figure~\ref{converse_fig}(b) illustrates the photon-tail exponent. For comparison, a separate R\'enyi calculation gives an explicit exponent for the optimal Gaussian signal family when the displacement energy of each codeword is constrained (Appendix~\ref{gauss_sc_app}); these Gaussian codewords need not have strict photon-number support. None of these exponent bounds is claimed to be optimal.

For the lossy memory channel of Fig.~\ref{capacity_fig}(b), the exact mean-energy rate--error relation extends to the growing sequence of correlated blocks:
\bb
C_{{\rm mem},\epsilon}(N)
=
C_{\rm mem}\!\left(\frac{N}{1-\epsilon}\right),
\qquad N\geq0,\quad 0\leq\epsilon<1.
\label{memoryepsilon}
\ee
Here $C_{{\rm mem},\epsilon}(N)$ denotes the capacity along this sequence under the mean-energy constraint and an average decoding error at most $\epsilon$. Under a photon-number cutoff, the decoding success probability instead decays exponentially at every rate above $C_{\rm mem}(E)$. The normal-mode dilations obey bounds that are uniform at fixed memory strength and noise, allowing the energy-filter argument to pass to the limit (Appendix~\ref{memory_sc_app}). More generally, the same method gives a photon-number-cutoff converse and the corresponding mean-energy rate--error relation for any fixed finite-mode Gaussian channel in terms of its regularised Holevo capacity, even when that capacity has no known explicit formula (Appendix~\ref{filtermultimode_app}).

