\section{Discussion and outlook}\label{discussion_sec}
The result identifies the optimal transmitter for single-mode Gaussian links throughout the energy range where squeezing and random displacements compete for the available energy. It also establishes the same conclusion jointly for different links sharing a power budget. For the correlated-noise channels considered here, independent squeezed signals are optimal in the normal-mode basis; transforming back can produce entanglement between physical channel uses. Thus the absence of an advantage from entangled inputs for independent links is consistent with the usefulness of collective encoding in a channel with memory.

For channels in the canonical form at positive energy, the optimal average output is also unique, and the total Holevo deficit bounds the relative entropy of any near-optimal average output from this reference. This constrains the ensemble average without fixing its decomposition into individual signals.

