\begin{abstract}
We obtain an explicit separation of the coupled gravitational and electromagnetic radiative equations on nonextremal Kerr--Newman--(anti-)de Sitter black holes with nonzero charge and rotation. The construction uses a matrix with constant determinant, chosen independently of the frequency and azimuthal number, and treats the positive- and negative-spin sectors within the same formulation. We establish that this transformation is smooth and invertible throughout the open AdS exterior and between the event and cosmological horizons in dS. The resulting radial and angular systems are valid at arbitrary complex frequencies. We also express regularity of the radiative Newman--Penrose variables at the rotation axes and future horizons as conditions on the separated solutions. In the asymptotically flat limit, the transformation is related to Hintz's matrix integrating factor by the allowed frame freedom. These results provide a basis for studying quasinormal modes and black-hole stability in Kerr--Newman--(anti-)de Sitter spacetimes, as well as exploring holographic applications to strongly coupled systems.
\end{abstract}


\maketitle
\section{Introduction} 

Linear perturbations of black holes encode rich information about their dynamical properties, such as linear stability and quasinormal-mode (QNM) spectra~{\cite{Berti:2009kk}. Characterized by complex frequencies, QNMs describe the characteristic oscillations and decay of perturbations~\cite{Vishveshwara:1970zz}, providing valuable insights from both theoretical and observational perspectives. In particular, they govern the ringdown phase of gravitational waves following black hole mergers~\cite{LIGOScientific:2016aoc,LIGOScientific:2016lio}, while perturbation analyses can also reveal dynamical instabilities, including those associated with superradiance under suitable conditions~\cite{Brito:2015oca}. For black holes in anti-de Sitter (AdS) spacetime, linear perturbations acquire an additional significance through the AdS/CFT correspondence~\cite{Maldacena:1997re,AmmonErdmenger2015}. An AdS black hole is holographically dual to a thermal state of a conformal field theory~\cite{Witten:1998zw}, and its perturbations characterize the response of the dual strongly coupled system to small disturbances. In this framework, black hole QNMs correspond to poles of retarded correlation functions in the dual field theory~\cite{Son:2002sd,Kovtun:2005ev}. Their complex frequencies encode the response of perturbations, with the imaginary parts determining characteristic relaxation timescales as the system approaches thermal equilibrium~\cite{Horowitz:1999jd,Berti:2009kk,Cardoso2014}.


The study of linear perturbations of stationary, rotating black holes has long been a central topic in black hole physics. A major breakthrough was achieved by Teukolsky, who exploited the Petrov type-D structure of the Kerr spacetime to derive decoupled master equations for gravitational and electromagnetic perturbations. These equations admit separation into radial and angular ordinary differential equations, providing a powerful framework for investigating black hole dynamics, QNM spectra, and stability~\cite{Teukolsky1972,Teukolsky1973}. For Kerr, this separated structure also underlies the classical proof of mode stability~\cite{Whiting:1988vc}. However, despite sharing the same Petrov type-D classification, the Kerr--Newman (KN) black hole presents a considerably more challenging problem. The presence of a nontrivial background Maxwell field introduces coupling between gravitational and electromagnetic perturbations, preventing a straightforward extension of the Teukolsky separation procedure. Consequently, despite extensive analytical and numerical investigations, an exact separation of the coupled gravitoelectromagnetic radiative equations had remained elusive for decades~\cite{Chitre:1976bb,Chandrasekhar1983,KalninsWilliams1998,Dudley:1977zz,Berti:2005eb,Pani:2013wsa,Dias:2015wqa,Giorgi2022,DiasGodazgarSantos2022}. Recently, Hintz made a significant advance by introducing a matrix integrating factor that achieves exact separation of the coupled radiative equations for asymptotically flat KN black holes~\cite{Hintz2026}. Subsequent computations of QNM frequencies based on these separated equations showed excellent agreement with independent numerical results, providing strong support for the construction~\cite{Destounis:2026ljd}. The separated system has since been used to obtain charge-regular radiative amplitudes and tidal Love numbers~\cite{Motohashi:2026wac,Chakraborty:2026kyf}. These developments open a new avenue for studying perturbations of charged rotating black holes. In particular, a nonzero cosmological constant modifies both the perturbation equations and the spacetime boundary structure, making the extension to asymptotically AdS and dS backgrounds a nontrivial problem~\cite{DiasSantos2013,Hintz2018dS}. Motivated by these advances, we investigate whether Hintz's separation approach can be generalized to KN black holes with a cosmological constant.


Here we construct an explicit matrix transformation that separates the coupled gravitoelectromagnetic radiative equations on nonextremal Kerr--Newman--(anti-)de Sitter (KN--(A)dS) black holes with nonzero rotation and charge. Independent of the frequency and azimuthal number, the matrix applies to both positive- and negative-spin sectors, yielding radial and angular systems valid for arbitrary complex frequencies. We establish its smooth invertibility outside the event horizon in AdS and between the event and cosmological horizons in dS. We also derive regularity conditions for the radiative Newman--Penrose (NP) variables at the rotation axes and future horizons, and relate our transformation to Hintz's matrix integrating factor in the asymptotically flat limit.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:setup} introduces the background and perturbation equations, while Sec.~\ref{sec:separation} presents the exact separation. Section~\ref{sec:ds} extends the construction to dS, and Sec.~\ref{sec:points} examines axis and horizon regularity. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} concludes with a discussion of the results and open questions. The appendices provide supporting derivations, regularity proofs, and the asymptotically flat comparison.
\section{Background and perturbation equations} \label{sec:setup}
We consider the Kerr–Newman–AdS black hole in geometric units and with signature $(+---)$. The line element and gauge potential are given by
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} 
\dd s^2= &\frac{\Delta}{\Sigma} \left(\dd t-\frac{a\sin^2\theta}{\Xi}\dd\phi\right)^2 -\frac{\Sigma}{\Delta}\dd r^2 -\frac{\Sigma}{T^2}\dd\theta^2 \\
&-\frac{T^2\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma} \left[a\,\dd t-\frac{r^2+a^2}{\Xi}\dd\phi\right]^2 \,,\\
\mathcal A= &-\frac{Qr}{\Sigma} \left(\dd t-\frac{a\sin^2\theta}{\Xi}\dd\phi\right) \,,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\Delta&=(r^2+a^2)\left(1+\frac{r^2}{L^2}\right)-2Mr+Q^2 \,,\\
\Sigma&=r^2+a^2\cos^2\theta \,,\quad \Xi=1-\frac{a^2}{L^2} \,,\\
T&=\sqrt{1-\frac{a^2}{L^2}\cos^2\theta} \,.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Here $L$ is the AdS radius, with $\Lambda=-3/L^2$, and $M$, $a$ and $Q$ are the mass, rotation and charge parameters. The limit $L\to\infty$ gives the asymptotically flat Kerr--Newman solution~\cite{Newman:1965my}. We take $M>0$, $Q\ne0$ and $0<|a|<L$, and consider a nonextremal black hole with outer horizon $r=r_+>0$. We work outside the horizon, where $r>r_+$ and $\Delta>0$. The de Sitter case is discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:ds}.


We write $f=r+\ii a\cos\theta$ and $g=\bar f$, so that $fg=\Sigma$. In coordinates $(t,r,\theta,\phi)$, the generalized Kinnersley tetrad~\cite{Kraniotis2019} has components
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} \label{eq:tetrad}
\ell^\mu&=\frac1\Delta \left(r^2+a^2, \Delta, 0, a\Xi\right)  \,,\\
n^\mu&=\frac1{2\Sigma} \left(r^2+a^2, -\Delta, 0, a\Xi\right) \,,\\
m^\mu&=\frac1{\sqrt2f} \left( \ii \frac{ a\sin\theta}{T}, 0, T, \ii \frac{\Xi}{T\sin\theta}\right) \,.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The null tetrad is normalized by $\ell^\mu n_\mu=1$ and $m^\mu\bar m_\mu=-1$. We fix the NP conventions~\cite{Newman:1961qr} by writing $e_1=\ell^\mu\partial_\mu$, $e_2=n^\mu\partial_\mu$, $e_3=m^\mu\partial_\mu$, and $e_4=\bar m^\mu\partial_\mu$. The components of the NP spin connection are defined by $\gamma^{\mathrm{NP}}_{ijk}=g_{\mu\nu} e_j^\nu e_k^\lambda (\nabla_\lambda e_i^\mu)$, with $\gamma^{\mathrm{NP}}_{ijk}=-\gamma^{\mathrm{NP}}_{jik}$ by metric compatibility. In particular, $\kappa_{\mathrm{NP}}=-\gamma^{\mathrm{NP}}_{311}$ and $\sigma_{\mathrm{NP}}=-\gamma^{\mathrm{NP}}_{313}$.


We use the curvature convention $[\nabla_\mu, \nabla_\nu]v^\alpha = R^\alpha{}_{\beta\mu\nu} v^\beta$. The Weyl scalars are defined by $\Psi_0=-C_{1313}, \Psi_1=-C_{1213}, \Psi_2=-C_{1342}, \Psi_3=-C_{1242}, \Psi_4=-C_{2424}$. For the electromagnetic field $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu\mathcal A_\nu-\partial_\nu\mathcal A_\mu$, the Maxwell scalars are $ \Phi_0=F_{13}, \Phi_1=\tfrac12(F_{12}+F_{43}), \Phi_2=F_{42}$. Here $C_{ijkl}$ and $F_{ij}$ denote the components of the Weyl tensor and electromagnetic field in the null frame.


For the KN--AdS background and the null tetrad~\eqref{eq:tetrad}, the associated nonvanishing NP spin coefficients are
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned} 
 \rho_{\mathrm{NP}}&=-\frac{1}{\bar f},
 &\mu_{\mathrm{NP}}&=-\frac{\Delta}{2f\bar f^{\,2}},\\
 \pi_{\mathrm{NP}}&=\frac{\mathrm{i}aT\sin\theta}
                         {\sqrt{2}\bar f^{\,2}},
 &\tau_{\mathrm{NP}}&=-\frac{\mathrm{i}aT\sin\theta}
                          {\sqrt{2}\Sigma},\\  
 \beta_{\mathrm{NP}}&=\frac{T\cot\theta+ \partial_\theta T}{2\sqrt{2}f},
 &\alpha_{\mathrm{NP}}&=\pi_{\mathrm{NP}} -\overline{\beta_{\mathrm{NP}}},\\
 \gamma_{\mathrm{NP}}&=\mu_{\mathrm{NP}}+\frac{\partial_r \Delta}{4\Sigma}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
The remaining coefficients satisfy $\kappa_{\mathrm{NP}}=\sigma_{\mathrm{NP}} =\lambda_{\mathrm{NP}}=\nu_{\mathrm{NP}}=\varepsilon_{\mathrm{NP}}=0$. The background Weyl and Maxwell scalars are $\Psi_2=\frac{Q^2-Mf}{f\bar f^{\,3}}, \Phi_1=\frac{Q}{2\bar f^{\,2}}$,
with $\Psi_0=\Psi_1=\Psi_3=\Psi_4=0$ and $\Phi_0=\Phi_2=0$.


We now consider linear gravitational and electromagnetic perturbations of this background, denoting first-order perturbations by a superscript $(1)$. Since the background Maxwell scalar $\Phi_1$ is nonzero for $Q\ne0$, we use the infinitesimal null-rotation freedom of the tetrad to impose the phantom gauge, $\Phi_0^{(1)}=\Phi_2^{(1)}=0$.


In the NP formalism, the radiative perturbations are described by two sets of coupled first-order equations, one involving fields of positive spin weights $+1,+2$ and the other involving fields of negative spin weights $-1,-2$~\cite{Chandrasekhar1983}. Under $m^\mu\mapsto e^{\ii\chi}m^\mu$ and $\bar m^\mu\mapsto e^{-\ii\chi}\bar m^\mu$, with $\ell^\mu,n^\mu$ fixed and $\chi$ real, a component of weight $w$ acquires the phase $e^{\ii w\chi}$. In particular, the perturbed Weyl scalars $\Psi_0^{(1)},\Psi_1^{(1)}$ have spin weights $+2,+1$, while $\Psi_4^{(1)},\Psi_3^{(1)}$ have spin weights $-2,-1$. We will use a single invertible matrix $X$ to separate the positive- and negative-spin systems. Following~\cite{Hintz2026}, the positive-spin variables are
\begin{equation} 
\psi_2 =\Psi_0^{(1)} \,, \enspace \psi_1 =\sqrt2g\Psi_1^{(1)} \,, \enspace
k =\frac{\kappa_1}{\sqrt2g^2} \,, \enspace s=\frac{f\sigma_1}{g^2} \,.
\end{equation}
Here $\kappa_1=\kappa_{\mathrm{NP}}^{(1)}$ and $\sigma_1=\sigma_{\mathrm{NP}}^{(1)}$ denote the linearized NP spin coefficients. For the negative-spin sector, we define
\begin{equation}
\psi_{-2}=g^4\Psi_4^{(1)} \,, \psi_{-1}=\frac{g^3}{\sqrt2}\Psi_3^{(1)} \,, k_-=\frac{fg}{\sqrt2}\nu_1 \,, s_-=\frac g2\lambda_1 \,,
\label{eq:negative-variables}
\end{equation}
where $\nu_1=\nu_{\mathrm{NP}}^{(1)}$ and $\lambda_1=\lambda_{\mathrm{NP}}^{(1)}$. The two quadruples $(\psi_2,\psi_1,k,s)$ and $(\psi_{-2},\psi_{-1},k_-,s_-)$ have spin weights $(2,1,1,2)$ and $(-2,-1,-1,-2)$, respectively.


Stationarity and axisymmetry allow us to consider modes proportional to $e^{-\ii\omega t+\ii m\phi}$, with $\omega\in\mathbb C$ and $m\in\mathbb Z$. For such a mode, we define the radial and angular operators by
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} 
\mathcal D_j&=\partial_r+\frac{\ii K}{\Delta} +j\frac{\partial_r \Delta}{\Delta} \,,\\
\mathcal L_j&=T\partial_\theta+\mathcal U +j(T\cot\theta+ \partial_\theta T) \,,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $K=a\Xi m-(r^2+a^2)\omega$ and $\mathcal U=(\Xi m\csc\theta-a\omega\sin\theta)/T$. The daggered operators $\mathcal D_j^\dagger$ and $\mathcal L_j^\dagger$ are obtained from $\mathcal D_j$ and $\mathcal L_j$ by replacing $(\omega,m)$ with $(-\omega,-m)$, without complex conjugation.


Following the phantom-gauge treatment of asymptotically flat KN perturbations in~\cite{Chandrasekhar1983}, we carry out the corresponding reduction on the KN--AdS background. Combining the linearized Bianchi and Ricci identities with the source-free Maxwell equations, and using the variables and differential operators defined above, we obtain the following coupled first-order system for the positive-spin sector:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} \label{eq:np4}
\left(\mathcal L_2-\frac{3F_0}{g}\right)\psi_2
-\left(\mathcal D_0+\frac{3}{g}\right)\psi_1 &=-2kF_+   \,,\\
\Delta\left(\mathcal D_2^\dagger-\frac{3}{g}\right)\psi_2
+\left(\mathcal L_{-1}^\dagger+\frac{3F_0}{g}\right)\psi_1 &=2sF_-  \,,\\
\left(\mathcal D_0+\frac{3}{g}\right)s
-\left(\mathcal L_{-1}^\dagger+\frac{3F_0}{g}\right)k &=\frac{f\psi_2}{g^2} \,,\\
\Delta\left(\mathcal D_2^\dagger-\frac{3}{g}\right)k
+\left(\mathcal L_2-\frac{3F_0}{g}\right)s &=\frac{2f\psi_1}{g^2} \,,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $F_0=\ii aT\sin\theta$ and $F_\pm=3(M-Q^2/f)\pm Q^2g/f^2$. In the limit $L\to\infty$, this system reduces to the first-order system for asymptotically flat KN perturbations~\cite{Chandrasekhar1983,Hintz2026}.

The negative-spin equations can be related to the positive-spin system~\eqref{eq:np4} by defining the rescaled fields $\widehat\psi_2 =\Delta^{-2}\psi_{-2}, \widehat\psi_1=-\Delta^{-1}\psi_{-1}, \widehat k=-\Delta^{-2}k_-, \widehat s=\Delta^{-1}s_-$. These fields satisfy~\eqref{eq:np4} with $(\omega,m)$ replaced by $(-\omega,-m)$. We exploit this relation to treat the two sectors simultaneously below.
\section{Exact separation}\label{sec:separation}
Following~\cite{Hintz2026}, we label the positive- and negative-spin sectors by $\varsigma=+1$ and $-1$, respectively, and define
\begin{subequations} \label{eq:chiralvars}
\begin{equation}  
 y_\pm^{(+)}=\Delta^{5/4}\sqrt{T\sin\theta}
 \begin{pmatrix}
  \psi_2\pm\ii\Delta^{-1/2}\psi_1\\
  -2Q(k\pm\ii\Delta^{-1/2}s)
 \end{pmatrix} \,,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}  
 y_\pm^{(-)}=\Delta^{-3/4}\sqrt{T\sin\theta}
 \begin{pmatrix}
  \psi_{-2}\mp\ii\sqrt\Delta\,\psi_{-1}\\
  2Q(k_-\mp\ii\sqrt\Delta\,s_-)
 \end{pmatrix} \,.
\end{equation}
\end{subequations}
The superscript labels the spin sector, while the subscript $\pm$ labels the two chiral combinations within each sector. In terms of these variables, the four coupled equations in each spin sector take the compact form
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} \label{eq:chiral}
(\Dop-A)y_+^{(\varsigma)}   &=(c^{(\varsigma)}I-B^{(\varsigma)})y_-^{(\varsigma)}  \,,\\
(\bar\Dop+A)y_-^{(\varsigma)}   &=(c^{(\varsigma)}I+B^{(\varsigma)})y_+^{(\varsigma)} \,,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\Dop=\sqrt\Delta\,\partial_r-\ii T\partial_\theta$, $\bar\Dop=\sqrt\Delta\,\partial_r+\ii T\partial_\theta$, and $I$ is the $2\times2$ identity matrix. The coefficients are
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} 
 A&=\begin{pmatrix}
       0&-\dfrac{\ii Qg}{f^2}\\
       \dfrac{\ii Qf}{g^2}&0
     \end{pmatrix} \,,\\
 B^{(\varsigma)}&=\begin{pmatrix}
       \ii b^{(\varsigma)}&\dfrac{3\ii(Mf-Q^2)}{Qf}\\
       \dfrac{3\ii Qf}{g^2}&-\ii b^{(\varsigma)}
     \end{pmatrix} \,,\\
b^{(\varsigma)}&=-\varsigma\mathcal U -\frac32(T\cot\theta+\partial_\theta T)+\frac{3F_0}{g} \,,\\
c^{(\varsigma)}&=\frac{\ii\varsigma K}{\sqrt\Delta} +\frac{3\sqrt\Delta}{g}-\frac{3\partial_r\Delta}{4\sqrt\Delta} \,.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Only the terms proportional to $\omega,m$ depend on $\varsigma$. The matrix $A$ is the same in both sectors and is Hermitian, with $\tr A=\tr B^{(\varsigma)}=0$. The cosmological constant enters through the background functions $\Delta,T,\Xi$.

We apply the same field transformation in both sectors,
\begin{equation}  \label{eq:change}
z_+^{(\varsigma)}=(\det X) X^{-1}y_+^{(\varsigma)} \,,\qquad z_-^{(\varsigma)}=X^\dagger y_-^{(\varsigma)} \,.
\end{equation}
Here $X(r,\theta)$ is invertible and independent of $\omega,m,\varsigma$, and $X^\dagger$ denotes its Hermitian conjugate. In the transformed $z_+^{(\varsigma)}$ equation, the matrix multiplying $z_-^{(\varsigma)}$ is
\begin{equation}  \label{eq:transformedcoupling}
C^{(\varsigma)} =(\det X) X^{-1}(c^{(\varsigma)}I-B^{(\varsigma)})(X^\dagger)^{-1} \,.
\end{equation}
We choose $X$ so that this matrix separates into
\begin{equation} \label{eq:separatedC}
C^{(\varsigma)}= R_0^{(\varsigma)}(r)I +\ii R_1^{(\varsigma)}(r)\sigma_2 +\ii\Theta_1^{(\varsigma)}(\theta)\sigma_1 +\ii\Theta_0^{(\varsigma)}(\theta)\sigma_3 \,,
\end{equation}
where $\sigma_j$ are the Pauli matrices.


The algebraic separation condition does not fix the transformation matrix uniquely. The separated form~\eqref{eq:separatedC} is preserved in both sectors by
\begin{equation}  \label{eq:freedom}
 X\longmapsto XE,\qquad
 E=s_r(r)s_\theta(\theta)e^{u(r)\sigma_2}e^{\ii v(\theta)\sigma_2},
\end{equation}
where $s_r,s_\theta$ are positive smooth functions and $u,v$ are real smooth functions, all independent of $\omega,m,\varsigma$. The matrix $C^{(\varsigma)}$ transforms as $C^{(\varsigma)}\longmapsto (\det E)E^{-1}C^{(\varsigma)}(E^\dagger)^{-1}$. The radial exponential mixes the coefficients of $I$ and $\sigma_2$, while the angular exponential rotates those of $\sigma_1$ and $\sigma_3$. Below we use this freedom to make $\det X$ constant.
\subsection{An explicit choice of X} 
\begin{widetext}
We now present an explicit choice of $X$ and the four coefficients of $C^{(\varsigma)}$, with the construction given in Appendix~\ref{app:algebraic-checks}. Define the positive constants
\begin{equation} \label{eq:alg-parameters}
\kappa=\sqrt{\left[M^2-Q^2\left(1+\frac{a^2}{L^2}\right)\right]^2 -\frac{4a^2Q^4}{L^2}} \,, \quad \eta=\frac{2Q^2} {M^2-Q^2(1+a^2/L^2)+\kappa} \,.
\end{equation}
Using the scalar freedom in~\eqref{eq:freedom}, we choose the normalized representative
\begin{equation}  \label{eq:alg-frame}
X=\frac1{\kappa Q\sqrt{A_rD_\theta}}
 \begin{pmatrix}
  \dfrac{\ii MaA_r\cos\theta-\nu D_\theta}{f}
   &\dfrac{\ii Q(aTA_r\sin\theta-D_\theta\sqrt{\Delta})}{f}\\[6pt]
  -\dfrac{\ii Q(aTA_r\sin\theta+D_\theta\sqrt{\Delta})}{g}
   &\dfrac{\ii MaA_r\cos\theta+\nu D_\theta}{g}
 \end{pmatrix}  \, .
\end{equation}
Here $A_r=r^2-\eta a^2$, $D_\theta=a^2(\cos^2\theta+\eta)$, and $\nu=Mr-Q^2$. The scalar factor $(A_rD_\theta)^{-1/2}$ leaves $C^{(\varsigma)}$ unchanged and gives the constant determinant $\det X=-1/(\kappa Q^2)$. For the rotating, charged, nonextremal KN--AdS background considered here, $X$ and the field transformation~\eqref{eq:change} are smooth and invertible throughout $r>r_+$, $0<\theta<\pi$; see Appendix~\ref{app:invertibility}. Appendix~\ref{app:flat-equivalence} relates $X$ to Hintz's matrix integrating factor~\cite{Hintz2026} in the limit $L\to\infty$. For this choice, $C^{(\varsigma)}$ takes the form~\eqref{eq:separatedC}.
\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:alg-separated-coefficients}
Explicitly, the radial coefficients are
\begin{align}  
 R_0^{(\varsigma)}=& \frac{3\partial_r\Delta}{4\sqrt\Delta} -\frac{3r\sqrt\Delta}{A_r} -\frac{\ii\varsigma K}{\sqrt\Delta} +\frac{2\ii\varsigma aQ^2\sqrt\Delta}{\kappa A_r} [a(1+\eta)\omega-\Xi m] \,,\\
 \ii R_1^{(\varsigma)}=& -\frac{3[M(r^2+\eta a^2)-2Q^2r]}{2QA_r} +\frac{2\ii\varsigma aQ\nu}{\kappa A_r}   [a(1+\eta)\omega-\Xi m] \,,
\end{align}
and the angular coefficients are
\begin{align}  
 \Theta_1^{(\varsigma)}=& \frac{3Ma^2(\cos^2\theta-\eta)}{2QD_\theta}
 +\frac{2\varsigma a^2MQ\cos\theta}{\kappa D_\theta} [a(1+\eta)\omega-\Xi m] \,,\\
 \Theta_0^{(\varsigma)}= & -\frac32(T\cot\theta+\partial_\theta T) -\frac{3a^2T\sin\theta\cos\theta}{D_\theta} -\varsigma\mathcal U -\frac{2\varsigma a^2Q^2T\sin\theta}{\kappa D_\theta} [a(1+\eta)\omega-\Xi m] \,.
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Here $K$ and $\mathcal U$ retain their definitions in Sec.~\ref{sec:setup}.
\end{widetext}
\subsection{Transformed perturbation equations}

Applying~\eqref{eq:change} with the normalized matrix~\eqref{eq:alg-frame} to~\eqref{eq:chiral} gives
\begin{equation}
\begin{split} \label{eq:alg-chiral}
[\Dop+(h_r+h_\theta)\sigma_2]z_+^{(\varsigma)} &=C^{(\varsigma)}z_-^{(\varsigma)} \,,\\
[\bar\Dop-(h_r+h_\theta)\sigma_2]z_-^{(\varsigma)} &=\adj C^{(\varsigma)}z_+^{(\varsigma)} \,,
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation} 
h_r=\frac{M\eta a^2-Q^2r}{QA_r},\qquad
h_\theta=\frac{M\eta a^2}{QD_\theta} \,.
\end{equation}
They are real and the same in both spin sectors, with $h_r$ depending only on $r$ and $h_\theta$ only on $\theta$. The normalization of $X$ removes the scalar derivative term. The derivation is given in Appendix~\ref{app:algebraic-checks}.
\subsection{Radial and angular equations}

To separate the fields in~\eqref{eq:alg-chiral}, write $\chi^{(\varsigma)}(\theta)=(\chi_1^{(\varsigma)}(\theta), \chi_2^{(\varsigma)}(\theta))^{\mathsf T}$ and introduce the ansatz
\begin{equation}  \label{eq:ansatz}
z_\pm^{(\varsigma)} =\tfrac12\big[u_1^{(\varsigma)}(r)I \pm\ii u_2^{(\varsigma)}(r)\sigma_2\big]\chi^{(\varsigma)}(\theta) \,.
\end{equation}
Substituting~\eqref{eq:ansatz} into~\eqref{eq:alg-chiral}, we add and subtract the two equations and separate the radial and angular dependence with a separation constant $\lambda_\varsigma$. Writing $\mathbf u^{(\varsigma)}= (u_1^{(\varsigma)}, u_2^{(\varsigma)}) ^{\mathsf T}$, the radial equations take the form
\begin{equation} \label{eq:radial}
\sqrt\Delta\,\partial_r\mathbf u^{(\varsigma)} =\big[ R_0^{(\varsigma)}\sigma_3 +R_1^{(\varsigma)}\sigma_1  +(h_r+\lambda_\varsigma)\sigma_2\big] \mathbf u^{(\varsigma)} \,.
\end{equation}
The corresponding angular equations are
\begin{equation}\label{eq:angular}
T\partial_\theta\chi^{(\varsigma)} =\big[ -\Theta_1^{(\varsigma)}\sigma_1 -\Theta_0^{(\varsigma)}\sigma_3  +\ii(\lambda_\varsigma-h_\theta)\sigma_2\big] \chi^{(\varsigma)} \,.
\end{equation}
In separating the equations, we used $\sigma_2^2=I$ and $\sigma_2\sigma_j=-\sigma_j\sigma_2$ for $j=1,3$.

The separated equations hold for complex $\omega$ in the region $r>r_+$. For either spin sector, combining radial and angular solutions with the same $\lambda_\varsigma$ in~\eqref{eq:ansatz} determines $z_\pm^{(\varsigma)}$. Inverting~\eqref{eq:change} and~\eqref{eq:chiralvars} then gives a solution of the original perturbation equations:~\eqref{eq:np4} for $\varsigma=+1$ and~\eqref{eq:negative-np4} for $\varsigma=-1$.
\section{Extension to de Sitter}\label{sec:ds}
The construction extends to KN--dS by replacing $L^{-2}$ with $-L^{-2}$ in the KN--AdS background functions,
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\Delta &= (r^2+a^2)\left(1-\frac{r^2}{L^2}\right)-2Mr+Q^2 \,,\\
T^2 &= 1+\frac{a^2}{L^2}\cos^2\theta \,, \qquad \Xi = 1+\frac{a^2}{L^2} \,.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The same replacement must be made in the parameters $\kappa$ and $\eta$. Here $L>0$ is the de Sitter radius, with $\Lambda=3/L^2$. We assume $M>0$, $aQ\neq0$, and consecutive positive simple event and cosmological horizons $0<r_b<r_c$, with $\Delta>0$ between them.

With these parameters, the algebraic identities underlying the separation remain valid. Consequently, the same normalized matrix $X$ yields the radial and angular equations for both spin sectors at arbitrary complex frequencies. As shown in Appendix~\ref{app:invertibility}, $X$ is smooth and invertible throughout the region between the event and cosmological horizons.
\section{Behavior at horizons and rotation axes}\label{sec:points}
Here, we examine the behavior of the separated solutions near the rotation axes and horizons for both spin sectors $\varsigma=\pm1$. We translate regularity of the radiative NP fields there into boundary conditions on the radial and angular functions.
\subsection{Rotation axes}
Near the north and south axes, we set $t=\theta$ and $t=\pi-\theta$, respectively, and define
\begin{equation}  
 \alpha_N=\varsigma m+\frac32 \,,\qquad
 \alpha_S=\frac32-\varsigma m \,.
\end{equation}
Using $T(0)=T(\pi)=\sqrt{\Xi}$, the angular equation~\eqref{eq:angular} takes the asymptotic form
\begin{equation}  
 \frac{\dd\chi^{(\varsigma)}}{\dd t}
 =\left[\frac{\alpha}{t}\sigma_3+O(1)\right]
 \chi^{(\varsigma)},
\end{equation}
where $\alpha=\alpha_N$ or $\alpha_S$. The indicial exponents are $\pm\alpha$. The branch with exponent $-|\alpha|$ gives singular radiative NP variables and is excluded; the regular branch takes the form
\begin{equation}
 \chi^{(\varsigma)}=t^{|\alpha|}
 \begin{cases}
 \begin{pmatrix}
  1 & 0\\
  0 & t
 \end{pmatrix}
 \mathbf{c}(t^2) \,, & \alpha>0 \,,\\[6pt]
 \begin{pmatrix}
  t & 0\\
  0 & 1
 \end{pmatrix}
 \mathbf{c}(t^2) \,, & \alpha<0 \,,
 \end{cases} \label{eq:physical-axis-domain}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{c}=(c_1,c_2)^{\mathsf T}$, with $c_1,c_2$ smooth near $t=0$. As shown in Appendix~\ref{app:physical-boundaries}, this branch corresponds to smooth radiative NP variables.
\subsection{Simple horizons}
At a simple horizon $r=r_h$, define $b_h^{(\varsigma)}=3/4-\ii\varsigma K(r_h)/\Delta'(r_h)$. Expanding the radial system near $r=r_h$, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\frac{d\mathbf u^{(\varsigma)}}{dr} =\left[\frac{b_h^{(\varsigma)}\sigma_3}{r-r_h} +O(|r-r_h|^{-1/2}) \right]\mathbf u^{(\varsigma)} \,.
\end{equation}
To specify the horizon conditions, define
\begin{equation}
H(r)=\exp\left(\ii\int^r\frac{K(s)}{\Delta(s)}\,ds\right) \,.
\end{equation}
The radial functions satisfy
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned} \label{eq:physical-horizon-data}
u_1^{(\varsigma)} &\in H^{\zeta_h} \Delta^{1/4-\zeta_h\varsigma/2}C^\infty \,,\\
u_2^{(\varsigma)} &\in H^{\zeta_h} \Delta^{1/4-\zeta_h\varsigma}C^\infty \,,
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\zeta_h=+1$ for the ingoing condition at the future event horizon and $\zeta_h=-1$ for the outgoing condition at the future cosmological horizon. Here $C^\infty$ denotes functions that extend smoothly to $r=r_h$. The derivation is given in Appendix~\ref{app:physical-boundaries}.
\section{Discussion and outlook}\label{sec:discussion}
We have separated the gravitoelectromagnetic perturbations in the positive-spin ($\varsigma=+1$) and negative-spin ($\varsigma=-1$) sectors on nonextremal KN--(A)dS backgrounds with nonzero charge and rotation. The normalized matrix~\eqref{eq:alg-frame}, independent of $\omega$ and $m$, converts the supplied chiral equations into the radial and angular systems~\eqref{eq:radial} and~\eqref{eq:angular}, written uniformly for $\varsigma=\pm1$. The freedom~\eqref{eq:freedom} is used to set $\det X=-1/(\kappa Q^2)$ and remove the scalar derivative terms from both systems. Smooth invertibility holds outside the event horizon in AdS and between the event and cosmological horizons in dS.


We have also translated smoothness of the radiative NP variables at the rotation axes and future horizons into boundary conditions for the separated solutions. At the axes, these conditions select the regular branch of the angular system.
In the asymptotically flat limit, our matrix $X$ is related to Hintz's integrating factor by the frame freedom~\eqref{eq:freedom}. These results provide a basis for further studies of KN--(A)dS perturbations, particularly the computation of quasinormal-mode spectra and the investigation of black hole stability. For KN--AdS, the separated system provides a starting point for computing holographic QNMs at finite charge and rotation and for extracting the late-time relaxation timescales that govern thermalization in the dual field theory~\cite{Horowitz:1999jd,Son:2002sd,Kovtun:2005ev,Cardoso2014}.


Several questions remain for future work. One is the reconstruction of metric and Maxwell perturbations from the separated radiative variables. Existing methods developed for Kerr~\cite{Merlin2016,BerensGravelyLupsasca2024,HollandsToomani2026} provide useful guidance, but their extension to KN–(A)dS must accommodate gravitational–electromagnetic coupling and variations of the black hole parameters. Another is the angular spectral problem at complex frequencies, where generalized eigenfunctions may be required~\cite{FinsterSmoller2016}. A further challenge is to impose gravitational and electromagnetic boundary conditions at the AdS boundary that are compatible with the field equations~\cite{IshibashiWald2004,Warnick2015,DiasSantos2013}. Addressing these questions would facilitate systematic studies of quasinormal modes and superradiant instabilities in KN--AdS, as well as the radiative spectrum of KN--dS black holes.


\textit{Note added.} While preparing this manuscript, we became aware of related work by Yoshida~\cite{Yoshida:2026yly} on the separation of gravitoelectromagnetic perturbations of KN--dS black holes, which overlaps with part of our results.


