\begin{abstract}
We investigate wave packets generated by a local pulse electric field in a Tomonaga-Luttinger liquid (TLL) with a U(1) charge $q$. Bosonization shows that a local pulse field creates two counterpropagating packets with opposite accumulated charges $\pm qK$ and velocity $v$, where $K$ and $v$ are the TLL parameter and velocity. The packet motion produces a linearly growing charge polarization $P(t)=2 qDt$ in the conducting TLL at non-equilibrium, allowing the Drude weight $D=vK$ to be extracted from real-time local dynamics. We test this local-packet description by time-dependent variational principle simulations of the spin-1/2 XXZ chain. The simulations visualize the density, current, energy, and entanglement profiles in the space-time plane and confirm the predicted packet charge and propagation velocity. We also show that excess energy and entanglement remaining near the pulse region are suppressed in the weak-pulse limit. Finally, the Drude weight obtained from the packet-induced polarization agrees with the exact Bethe-ansatz result, demonstrating local-packet spectroscopy of intrinsic transport in a TLL.
\end{abstract}


\maketitle
\section{Introduction}

The propagation of locally generated charge excitations provides an intuitive real-space picture of transport. This viewpoint is particularly natural in mesoscopic and quantum Hall edge systems, where voltage pulses and quantum point contacts are used to create, partition, and manipulate propagating charge packets~\cite{Levitov1996,KKL2006,Dubois2013,Glattli2017,Feve2007,Kamata2014,Bocquillon2014}. 
In such settings, transport observables are often interpreted in terms of the accumulated effects of individual wave packets. The properties of the packets are usually inferred from repeated pulse measurements of currents, noise, correlations, or interference, rather than by directly following the real-space profile of a single packet.


In contrast, in strongly correlated systems on one-dimensional lattices including quantum magnets, one can ask a more direct question on their many-body properties: what kind of real-space charge or spin packet is generated by a local perturbation, and how do its density, current, energy, and entanglement profiles evolve in time?
In such systems, dynamics is more commonly characterized by correlation functions and dynamical structure factors, as in the standard Tomonaga-Luttinger
liquid (TLL)~\cite{Giamarchi2003}. Transport has also been extensively studied through conductivities and Drude weights~\cite{ShastrySutherland1990, Zotos1999, RMP2021}, including the conductance problem of TLL wires connected to reservoirs~\cite{SafiSchulz1995,Kawabata1996}. 
More recently, hydrodynamic and generalized-hydrodynamic descriptions have provided another powerful framework for one-dimensional transport~\cite{Doyon2020}.
Although these approaches have been highly successful, they do not by themselves specify what kind of real-space packet is created by a local operation. A complementary line of work has therefore studied real-space dynamics after local quantum quenches.
For example, local spin or energy perturbations in XXZ chains have been used to study ballistic and diffusive spreading of spin and energy densities~\cite{Langer2009, Langer2011}, with relevance to thermal transport in one-dimensional quantum magnets~\cite{Hess2007, Sologubenko2007}. 
Related local-quench protocols have been connected to transport coefficients,
including Drude weights, through time-dependent Einstein relations~\cite{Karrasch2014,Karrasch2017},
while wave-packet protocols have been used to analyze nonlinear charge
fractionalization in critical chains~\cite{Ramos2024}.
Wave-packet injection in interacting fermion chains has likewise provided a direct visualization of spin-charge separation and nonlinear fractionalization effects~\cite{Al-Hassanieh2013}. 
These works show that local nonequilibrium dynamics is a useful probe of one-dimensional transport.



The present work addresses a different, more specific question. 
We ask what kind of real-space charge or spin packet is generated by a local U(1) twist, or equivalently by a spatially localized electric-field impulse, and how the resulting packet motion is related to the intrinsic transport coefficient of a TLL. 
Because the perturbation is generated by the conserved density, it is distinct from a generic local density, temperature, or single-particle quench. 
Bosonization~\cite{Giamarchi2003} shows that the accumulated charge of each packet is fixed by the TLL parameter $K$, while the U(1) charge polarization generated by the ballistic separation of the two packets grows with a slope determined by the Drude weight.
We test this local-packet picture using time-dependent variational principle (TDVP)~\cite{Haegeman2011, Haegeman2016} simulations of the spin-\(1/2\) XXZ chain, visualizing the density, current, energy, and entanglement profiles and comparing the Drude weight extracted from the packet-induced polarization with the exact Bethe-ansatz result.


The difference between spatially uniform currents and local wave-packet dynamics is not merely technical but also conceptual. 
Standard transport coefficients, such as the optical conductivity and the Drude weight, are formulated in terms of current correlations or the response to a spatially uniform vector potential within a fixed total U(1) charge sector. 
By contrast, a local electric-field pulse preserves the total charge but creates spatially separated positive and negative U(1) charge accumulations. 
It therefore provides a real-space probe of local charge transport, rather than only a spatially uniform current response. 
The present study shows that these two viewpoints are quantitatively tied together in a gapless TLL: the same stiffness that governs the uniform-current response also controls the dipole motion of locally generated charge packets~\cite{TadaOshikawa2026}. 
This provides a local-packet spectroscopy of the intrinsic Drude weight.

