\section{Bosonization description}


We consider a one-dimensional system with a conserved U(1) charge such as the spin-1/2 XXZ chain and interacting fermions under the local pulse electric field or equivalently a U(1) twist.
We provide a bosonization description at the TLL fixed point, where the low-energy Hamiltonian is given by  
\begin{align}
H_{\rm TLL} = \frac{v}{2\pi}\int dx
\left[
K(\partial_x \theta)^2 + \frac{1}{K}(\partial_x \phi)^2
\right].
\end{align}
The parameter $v$ represents the excitation velocity and $K$ is the TLL parameter~\cite{Giamarchi2003}.
The fields $(\phi,\theta)$ are the canonical conjugate pair satisfying $[\phi(x),\partial_x\theta(x')/\pi]=i\delta(x-x')$.


It is useful to distinguish the present protocol from the voltage-pulse protocols commonly discussed in mesoscopic systems~\cite{Levitov1996,KKL2006,Dubois2013,Glattli2017,Feve2007,Kamata2014,Bocquillon2014}. In electron quantum optics and quantum Hall edge setups, a time-dependent voltage applied at a contact or a gate generates a propagating packet whose spatial shape is determined by the temporal profile of the pulse through the propagation of the edge mode. The pulse is therefore local in space but extended in time, and it can be viewed as injecting a controlled packet from an external reservoir or contact.
In the present work, we instead apply a spatially extended but temporally impulsive field, or equivalently a local U(1) twist, directly to the bulk of an isolated lattice TLL (Fig.~\ref{fig:schematic}(a) and (b)). The profile of the generated packet is controlled by the spatial shape of the twist, and the total U(1) charge is conserved. The real-space twist approach has been widely used for studying low-energy spectrum of strongly correlated lattice systems~\cite{LSM1961,AffleckLieb1986,YOA1997,Koma2000,Tasaki2022inbook}, and this method can be used not only for static properties but also for dynamical ones~\cite{TadaOshikawa2026}. In a nonchiral TLL, the local pulse creates a pair of counterpropagating packets with opposite accumulated charges. Thus, our protocol is complementary to the usual temporal-pulse setup: it provides a bulk, real-space probe of local charge transport in a strongly correlated lattice system.
This protocol is also different from generic local quenches of density,
magnetization, or energy profiles~\cite{Langer2009, Langer2011,Karrasch2014,Karrasch2017}.  
Since the perturbation is a gauge twist
generated by the conserved U(1) density, the resulting low-energy packet is
constrained by the TLL parameters, as shown below.
\begin{figure}[t]
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig/fig_schematic.pdf}
\caption{Schematic of the local pulse electric field protocol. (a) Spatial profile of the electric field $\mathcal{E} (x,t)$ at $t=0$ and $t \ne 0$, corresponding to the vector potential quench. (b) Illustration of the electric field $\mathcal{E}(x,t)$, applied to the interval $I$ with width $\ell$ in the one-dimensional chain.}
\label{fig:schematic}
\end{figure}

We assume that the low-energy carriers have charge $q$ which is directly coupled to an applied electric field ${\mathcal E}$. 
The charge and current densities are defined as
\begin{align}
\rho(x) = \rho_0 -\frac{q}{\pi}\partial_x \phi(x), \qquad
j_x(x) = \frac{q v K}{\pi}\partial_x \theta(x),
\end{align}
where $\rho_0$ is the average charge density.
In the presence of a vector potential $A$, one has to make the substitution $\partial_x\theta/\pi \to (\partial_x\theta-qA) /\pi$.
This can be implemented by a U(1) twist
\begin{align}
U[\vartheta] = \exp\!\left(i\int dx\, \vartheta(x)n(x)\right)
\label{eq:UTLL}
\end{align}
with $\partial_x\vartheta=qA$ and $n(x) = \rho/q $ is the density-operator, by which the $\theta$-field is shifted as $U\theta(x)U^{\dagger}=\theta(x)-\vartheta(x)$.
The function $\vartheta(x)$ is chosen so that it varies over a region $I$ of width $\ell$ and satisfies $\int_I dx \partial_x \vartheta(x)=2\pi.$
Typically, we consider a simple twist 
\begin{align}
\vartheta(x) = \frac{2\pi }{\ell}(x-x_0)1_{I}(x),
\label{eq:theta}
\end{align}
where $1_I(x)$ is an indicator function, $1_I(x\in I)=1, 1_I(x\notin I)=0$, for the region $I=[x_0,x_0+\ell]$.
The twist function Eq.~\eqref{eq:theta} is often used in the context of the Lieb-Schultz-Mattis theorem and it can generate a low-energy excitation~\cite{LSM1961,AffleckLieb1986,YOA1997,Koma2000,Tasaki2022inbook}.
Note that the present twist operator Eq.~\eqref{eq:UTLL} is spatially local and therefore can generate localized wave packets, which is distinct from the global twist over the whole space.

Now we consider a quench of the vector potential~\cite{Nakagawa2016}, $A(x,t)=A(x)$ for $t\leq 0$ and $A(x,t)=0$ for $t>0$,
for which the electric field is $\mathcal{E}(x,t)=-\partial A(x,t)/\partial t=A_0(x)\delta(t)$ with an amplitude $A_0 (x)=(2\pi/\ell) 1_{I}(x)$ (Fig. ~\ref{fig:schematic}(a) and (b)).
Correspondingly, the current density operator contains $\vartheta(x)$ for $t \leq 0$, while it does not for $t>0$.
Just before the quench, the system is in the ground state and the current density is zero,
\begin{align}
\ev{j_x(x,t=0^-)}
=\frac{q v K}{\pi}\ev{\partial_x(\theta - \vartheta)}=0.
\end{align}
Since the quantum state remains unchanged immediately after the instantaneous quench, we have
\begin{align}
\ev{j_x(x,t=0^+)}
=\frac{q v K}{\pi}\ev{\partial_x\theta}=\frac{q v K}{\pi} \partial_x\vartheta.
\end{align}
In the TLL with right- and left-moving components, dynamics of the $\theta$-field is described by the chiral fields $\phi_R(x-vt)=K\theta(x,t)-\phi(x,t)$ and $\phi_L(x+vt)=K\theta(x,t)+\phi(x,t)$.
Correspondingly, the current density is decomposed into two components as $K\partial_x\theta(x,t)/\pi=\hat{\rho}_R(x-vt)-\hat{\rho}_L(x+vt)$,
where $\hat{\rho}_{R,L}=\pm \partial_x \phi_{R,L} /2\pi$.
Therefore, the current density at time $t>0$ will be given by
\begin{align}
\ev{j_x(x,t)}
=\frac{q v K}{2\pi} \left(\partial_x\vartheta(x-vt) + \partial_x\vartheta(x+vt)\right).
\end{align}
This expression should be valid for the low-energy sector which is described as a TLL, although there may be high energy components beyond the TLL description especially when the amplitude $2\pi/\ell $ is not small.
Indeed, it agrees well with the TDVP results, as discussed in the next section.

Similarly, the expectation value of the excess charge density $\delta \rho=q(\hat{\rho}_R+\hat{\rho}_L)$ is given by
\begin{align}
\ev{\delta \rho(x,t)}
=\frac{q K}{2\pi} \left(\partial_x\vartheta(x-vt) - \partial_x\vartheta(x+vt)\right).
\end{align}
For the electric field profile $\mathcal{E}(x)\sim (2\pi/\ell) 1_I(x)$, this means that the charge packet has a width $W\sim \ell$ and a height $h\sim qK/\ell$.
Correspondingly, the charges of the right- and left-moving packets for time $t \gtrsim \ell /v$ are
\begin{align}
\begin{aligned}
Q_{\rm right} &= \int_{x>x_0+\ell} dx\, \ev{\delta\rho(x,t)} = +qK, \\
Q_{\rm left}  &= \int_{x<x_0} dx\, \ev{\delta\rho(x,t)} = -qK.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:Q=qk}
\end{align}
The packet charge is controlled by the TLL stiffness through $K$. The packet charge $Q$ is small for a ``hard" system with $K<1$ (repulsive interaction case), while it is large for a ``soft" system with $K>1$ (attractive interaction case).
This is intuitively reasonable and a large charge accumulation will be created for a system with a superconducting (or ferromagnetic) quasi-long-range order.

As a result of the dipole-like charge accumulation $\pm qK$, we have charge polarization 
\begin{align}
P(t)&=\int dx\, x\ev{\delta\rho(x,t)}=vtQ_{\rm right} + (-vt)Q_{\rm left}.
\end{align}
Interestingly, the charge polarization can be rewritten as 
\begin{align}
P(t)=2q{D}t,
\label{eq:Pt_TLL}
\end{align}
where ${D}=vK$ is the Drude weight. 
A similar expression can also be obtained within the standard linear response theory for general lattice models~\cite{TadaOshikawa2026}. Note that, although the present bosonization description is fully consistent with the lattice model result at low-energy, non-Drude terms arising from finite frequency contributions of the conductivity are contained in the latter.
Interestingly, the charge polarization $P(t)$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:Pt_TLL} is well defined for a conducting system out of equilibrium which is clearly distinguished from the equilibrium charge polarization in an insulator~\cite{KSV1993,VKS1993,Resta2007}.
We stress that the two quantities $P$ and ${D}$ correspond to distinct physical phenomena, since
the charge polarization characterizes a spatially asymmetric charge density profile while the Drude weight is associated with the spatially uniform current.
Conceptually,
the positive and negative charge accumulations in the former are closely related to the energy gap for creating charged excitations.
On the other hand, the charge density is uniform in the latter and thus it is directly related to the energy gap for generating neutral excitations. Thus, the simple relation $P(t)=2q{D}t$ connects the two distinct properties of the system through real-time dynamics due to the local pulse electric field. Since both of the charge-neutral excitations and the charge-carrying excitations are described by a single boson U(1) field in the bosonization theory, the derivation of $P(t)=2q{D}t$ is straightforward as demonstrated above.
