\section{TDVP calculation}
In this section, 
guided by the bosonization results, we test the following three predictions in the lattice model: (i) the local twist generates two counterpropagating packets, (ii) the accumulated charge is given by \(Q=\pm qK\), and (iii) the packet-induced polarization grows as \(P(t)=2qDt\).
We discuss the $S=1/2$ XXZ spin chain defined on the lattice $\{0,1,\cdots,L-1\}$ under the open boundary condition as a prototypical example of TLLs.
The Hamiltonian is
\begin{align}
H_{\rm XXZ} = J \sum_j \left(
S_j^x S_{j+1}^x + S_j^y S_{j+1}^y
+ \Delta S_j^z S_{j+1}^z
\right),
\label{eq:XXZ}
\end{align}
where the system is gapless for $-1 < \Delta \le 1$.
In this regime, the low-energy physics is described by a TLL with
the exact parameters~\cite{Giamarchi2003,ShastrySutherland1990,Zotos1999}
\begin{align}
v(\Delta) &= \frac{J \pi \sin\gamma}{2\gamma}, \\
K(\Delta) &= \frac{\pi}{2(\pi - \gamma)},
\end{align}
where $\Delta = \cos \gamma$ $(0 \le \gamma < \pi)$.
Since the elementary excitation carries U(1) charge $q=1$, the general results reduce to
\begin{align}
\label{eq:Q}
Q_{\rm right} = +\frac{\pi}{2(\pi - \gamma)}, \qquad
Q_{\rm left}  = -\frac{\pi}{2(\pi - \gamma)}.
\end{align}
As already mentioned, the charge accumulation is large when the system has a ferromagnetic (or superconducting) quasi-long-range order, although the propagating velocity becomes slow.
Note that the XXZ model can be mapped to interacting hard-core bosons and the spin accumulation in the former corresponds to the particle number accumulation in the latter.
\begin{figure*}[t]
\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{fig/Q_W_h.pdf}
\caption{
(a) Packet charge $Q_{\rm right}$ (red circles) and $Q_{\rm left}$ (blue squares) as a function of the Luttinger parameter $K$, evaluated from the relaxed states. The dotted lines are linear fits, $Q_{\rm right} = 1.00K$ and $Q_{\rm left} = -1.02K$. (b) Packet width $W$ and (c) packet height $h$ of the right (red circles) and left (blue squares) packets. $W$ is the full width at half maximum of the $\ev{S^z_j}$ after Gaussian smoothing ($\sigma = 2$ sites), and $h$ is the average of the unsmoothed $\ev{S^z_j}$ within this width. Both are averaged over $25 \le t \le 30$. The dashed lines in (c) are linear fits. The gray dotted lines show $W = \ell$ in (b) and $h = \pm K/\ell$ in (c). The twist width is $\ell = 18$ and the system size is $L = 100$.}
\label{fig:Q}
\end{figure*}

The real-time dynamics of the wave packets can be simulated by the TDVP~\cite{Haegeman2011, Haegeman2016}.
We use the open boundary condition for better numerical accuracy and consider a time-scale for which the wave packets do not reach the open ends. We use bond dimensions in the range \(\chi=400\)--\(800\) for which the results are well converged.
In the numerical calculation, we first obtain the ground state $\ket{\psi_0}$ of the Hamiltonian $H_{\rm XXZ}$ by using density-matrix renormalization group (DMRG)~\cite{White1992, DMRG2005} and construct the boosted state $\ket{U}=U\ket{\psi_0}$ by applying the local twist operator 
\begin{align}
U=\exp\left(i\sum_{j\in I}\vartheta_jS^z_j \right),
\label{eq:UXXZ}
\end{align}
where $\vartheta_j=(2\pi/\ell)(j-j_0)1_I(j)$ with $j_0=(L-\ell)/2-1$~ \cite{Yokoyama2026}.
One can show that the excess energy generated by the twist measured from the ground state energy $E^{(0)}$ is $\Delta E=\ev{U|H_{\text{XXZ}}|U}-E^{(0)}=O(1/\ell)$ and it is vanishingly small for large $\ell$.
Besides, the two states $\ket{\psi_0}$ and $\ket{U}$ are orthogonal for large $\ell$ under the periodic boundary condition, and therefore $\ket{U}$ is a low-energy excited state in the gapless phase, which is known as the Lieb-Schultz-Mattis theorem~\cite{LSM1961,AffleckLieb1986,YOA1997,Koma2000,Tasaki2022inbook}.
In the present study, the interval $I=\{j_0,j_0+1,\cdots,j_0+\ell-1\}$ is chosen as the middle region of the system.
Then, we calculate the time evolution of the state $U\ket{\psi_0}$ with the Hamiltonian $H_{\rm XXZ}$ and the physical quantities are evaluated with the time-evolved state 
\begin{align}
\ket{U(t)}=e^{-iH_{\rm XXZ}t}U\ket{\psi_0}.
\label{eq:Ut}
\end{align}
This protocol exactly corresponds to an application of the local pulse electric field $\mathcal{E}_j=(2\pi/\ell) 1_I(j)\delta(t)$ as discussed in the previous section, since the state $U\ket{\psi_0}$ is the ground state of the twisted Hamiltonian $UH_{\rm XXZ}U^{\dagger}$ and the time-evolution is described by the Hamiltonian $H_{\rm XXZ}$ without the twist (vector potential)~\cite{TadaOshikawa2026,Nakagawa2016}.


In Fig.~\ref{fig:J04_xt}, we show a typical example of the calculation results of the spin density $\ev{S_j^z}$, spin current density $\ev{J_j}=iJ/2\ev{S^+_jS^-_{j+1}-S^-_jS^+_{j+1}}$, excess energy density $\Delta E_j=J\ev{S_j^x S_{j+1}^x + S_j^y S_{j+1}^y+ \Delta S_j^z S_{j+1}^z}-E_j^{(0)}$, and excess bipartition entanglement entropy $\Delta S_j=-{\rm tr}_{A_j}\rho_{A_j}\log \rho_{A_j}-S_j^{(0)}$ with $A_j=\{0,1,\cdots,j\}$.
$E_j^{(0)}$ and $S_j^{(0)}$ are the energy density and the entanglement entropy in the ground state, respectively.
There are two wave packets created by the local pulse electric field. The positive spin accumulation propagates to the right and the negative one to the left, which gives a non-zero net current over the entire system.
These qualitative behaviors are common for all the interactions $\Delta$ in the TLL regime, although there are quantitative differences.
Note that the propagation is restricted within the Lieb-Robinson light cone and there is no propagation outside of the light cone~\cite{LR1972,BHV2006,NS2010}.

We also note that the wave packets show no appreciable decay on the simulated time scale in the weak-pulse regime.
This is because the twist Eq.~\eqref{eq:UXXZ} generates low-energy excitations for large $\ell$. 
This behavior is in sharp contrast to wave packets with substantial high-energy components, which can broaden or decay under lattice nonlinearities~\cite{RMP2021,Ramos2024,Al-Hassanieh2013}.

To test the bosonization prediction for the packet charge, we evaluate
$Q_{\rm right}$ and $Q_{\rm left}$ from the density profile once the packets
have relaxed into a steadily propagating form. As shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:Q}(a), $Q\propto K$ over the whole range studied, with a slope
close to unity ($q=1$), in agreement with
$Q_{\rm right}=-Q_{\rm left}=qK$ [Eq.~\eqref{eq:Q=qk}]. The packet width and
height are $W\simeq\ell$ and $h\simeq K/\ell$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:Q}(b),(c)],
consistent with $\abs{Q} \simeq Wh\simeq K$. Together with the ballistic propagation
in Fig.~\ref{fig:J04_xt}, these results show that the local pulse creates a
controlled low-energy TLL packet rather than a generic density disturbance,
unlike local quenches of density, magnetization, or energy, where
transport is diagnosed from the spreading of the profile
\cite{Langer2009, Langer2011, Karrasch2014, Karrasch2017}.
It would be interesting to extend this diagnostic to exotic TLLs, such as
spin-nematic TLLs~\cite{Hikihara2008} with $q=2$ bound-magnon excitations,
or chiral TLLs at fractional quantum Hall edges~\cite{Wen1990,
GuerreroSuarez2025}, where the packets should carry fractional anyon charge.
