Compared with the spin density and current density, there remain some energy density and entanglement entropy in the middle region even in the long time limit.
However, this is due to the finiteness of the twist width $\ell$ or equivalently because the applied electric field $\mathcal{E}\propto 1/\ell$ is not small enough.
To discuss the effects of finite $\ell$, we calculate the average excess energy $\overline{\Delta E}_{\ell}=(1/\ell)\sum_{j\in I}\Delta E_j$ in the twisted region $I$ of width $\ell$. We also calculate the average excess entanglement entropy $\overline{\Delta S}_{\ell}=(1/\ell)\sum_{j \in I}\Delta S_j$, obtained by averaging the excess entanglement entropy $\Delta S_{j}$ of the subsystem $A_j = \{0,\cdots, j | j \in I \}$.
A non-zero $\mathcal{E}$ may give a local energy proportional to $\mathcal{E}^2\sim 1/\ell^2$ and indeed,
as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:J04_l} (a) and (b), the excess energy behaves as $\overline{\Delta E}_{\ell}\sim 1/\ell^2 \to 0$ for $\ell \to \infty$.
Similarly, the excess entanglement entropy behaves as $\overline{\Delta S_{\ell}}\sim a\log\ell/\ell^2+b/\ell^2\to0$ for $\ell\to\infty$, where $a,b$ are non-universal constants.
Since each wave packet is contained in the region $A_j$ ($j \in I$) or its complement, the vanishing excess entanglement entropy means that the two wave packets are not entangled in the large $\ell$ limit.
The residual excess entanglement entropy ($\overline{\Delta S}_{{\ell}}$) between the wave packets for finite $\ell$ may be regarded as a signature of deviation from the TLL, since no entanglement will be generated between the left- and right-moving components within a pure TLL. Indeed, the exactly vanishing entanglement in a pure TLL can easily be understood from the twist operator in the Heisenberg representation,
\begin{align}
U_{\text{H}}(t) 
&= e^{iH_{\rm TLL}t}Ue^{-iH_{\rm TLL}t} \nonumber\\
&= e^{i\int dx\, \vartheta(x)\hat{\rho}_L(x+vt)} e^{i\int dx\, \vartheta(x)\hat{\rho}_R(x-vt)}.
\end{align}
Therefore, the finite excess entanglement observed in the lattice calculation should originate from components of the boosted state outside the ideal TLL fixed-point description.
When $\ket{U(t)}$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:Ut}) is written as a superposition of a TLL state $\ket{\psi_{\rm TLL}(t)}$ and a non-TLL state $\ket{\psi_{\rm nonTLL}(t)}$,
\begin{align}
\ket{U(t)}=a\ket{\psi_{\rm TLL}(t)} + b\ket{\psi_{\rm nonTLL}(t)},
\end{align}
the coefficient $b$ is expected to be $b\sim \mathcal{E}\sim 1/\ell$ in the first order perturbation.
Then, it is natural to consider that the change of a Schmidt value $\lambda$ is governed by the weight of the non-TLL state, $\Delta (\lambda_{n})^2 \sim |b|^2\sim 1/\ell^2$.
For Schmidt values that remain finite in the weak-pulse limit, the entropy correction is analytic and scales as $1/\ell^2$. The logarithmic factor originates from the singular behavior of $-\lambda ^2 \log\lambda^2$ near $\lambda=0$. Since a gapless TLL has a dense low-lying entanglement spectrum, finite-pulse corrections can populate Schmidt sectors with $\lambda^2 \lesssim 1/\ell^2$, producing a contribution of order $\log\ell/\ell^2$.

The entanglement dynamics found here is distinct from the entanglement generation in standard global or local quenches~\cite{CalabreseCardy2005,CalabreseCardy2007,EislerPeschel2007,Lauchli2008,Gruber2021}. In those problems, entanglement growth is commonly interpreted as the emission and propagation of entangled quasiparticles. By contrast, our local pulse is a local U(1) twist generated by the conserved density. Within the ideal TLL theory, its time evolution factorizes into independent left- and right-moving unitary operators, so that the separated packets do not generate additional entanglement across the center bipartition. The finite excess entanglement observed in the TDVP data is therefore a finite-$\ell$ correction, or equivalently a signature of deviations from the ideal TLL fixed point, rather than an intrinsic entanglement production mechanism of the TLL packet itself.

