\section{$J_1$-$J_2$ model}
In this section, to verify that the local-packet picture is not specific to integrable models, we repeat the same protocol for the non-integrable  spin-$1/2$ $J_1$-$J_2$ XXZ chain, in which the next-nearest-neighbor interaction
\begin{equation}
H^{J_2}_{\text{XXZ}} = J_2 \sum_j \left( S_j^x S_{j+2}^x + S_j^y S_{j+2}^y
+ \Delta S_j^z S_{j+2}^z \right)
\end{equation}
is added to the nearest-neighbor Hamiltonian $H_{\rm XXZ}$ [Eq.~\eqref{eq:XXZ}], with the same anisotropy $\Delta$. 
We prepare the ground state of $H_{\rm XXZ} + H^{J_2}_{\text{XXZ}}$, apply the same local $U(1)$ twist Eq.~\eqref{eq:UXXZ} with twist width $\ell$, and evolve the boosted state $\ket{U(t)}$ with TDVP.
\begin{figure}[t]
\includegraphics[width=1.0\columnwidth]{fig/J2_01_P.pdf}
\caption{Polarization $P(t)$ as a function of time $t$ for
the anisotropy $\Delta = 0.4$, the next-nearest-neighbor interaction $J_2=0.1J$, the system size $L=200$, and twist width $\ell = 18$.}
\label{fig:J2_01_P}
\end{figure}

Figure ~\ref{fig:J2_01_J04_xt} shows the resulting space-time plots of the spin-density $\langle S_j^z\rangle$, current density$\langle J_j\rangle$, excess energy density $\Delta E_j$, and excess entanglement entropy $\Delta S_j$ to be compared directly with Fig.~\ref{fig:J04_xt} for the pure integrable XXZ chain ($J_2=0$). 
The local twist creates two ballistically propagating wave packets. We also find that the residual excess energy $\overline{\Delta E}_{\ell}$ and excess entanglement entropy $\overline{\Delta S}_{\ell}$ in the twisted region decay with increasing $\ell$ in the same way as in the integrable XXZ chain (Fig.~\ref{fig:J2_01_l}(a) and (b)). These results suggest that the bosonization argument holds not only for integrable systems but also for non-integrable ones. The generation of ballistic wave packets and their transport by a local pulse field are generic properties of Tomonaga-Luttinger liquids, not specific to an integrable system.


We also compute the polarization $P(t)$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:Pt_TLL}) for the $J_1$-$J_2$ chain and confirm that it grows linearly in time, $P(t) \propto t$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:J2_01_P}). 
Because the $J_1$-$J_2$ XXZ chain is not integrable, we do not have an independent exact value of $D$ for comparison.  
Nevertheless, we can still evaluate the Drude weight from the linear growth of the polarization, which is identified with the
Drude weight entering the uniform current response according to the TLL argument in the main text.

