\section{Scientific context and open questions}
Water megamaser emission is a powerful probe of warm, dense gas in the central parsec of active galactic nuclei (AGN, see \cite{Lo2005} for a review). Individual clouds of Earth-sized maser spots can be resolved in position and velocity with Very Long Baseline Interferometry (VLBI) in Keplerian rotation about supermassive black holes (disk masers), in jets and outflows (jet masers), or, occasionally, both. Disk masers have been used to make precise and accurate measurements of {\it geometric} distances, supermassive black hole (SMBH) masses, and accretion disk geometries in galaxies within $\sim$150 Mpc. Such measurements have made significant contributions to cosmology, galaxy evolution, and AGN physics. While most studies so far have been carried out at 22\,GHz, sub-mm water megamaser studies, coupled with a next-generation sub-mm/mm interferometer (hereafter, ALMA2040), would complement, if not rival, the scientific constraints from current and future cm-wave observatories.

\subsection{Geometric distances and $\mathbf{H_0}$}

{The Hubble Constant, $H_0$, the rate of the Universe's current expansion, was first estimated nearly a century ago by Edwin Hubble, and its value has been refined and hotly debated ever since. Currently, the measurements from observations of the early Universe, the cosmic microwave background (CMB), and baryon acoustic oscillations (BAO) with big bang nucleosynthesis (BBN), are in sharp tension with those from the late Universe, differing by $5\sigma$ to $7\sigma$ (\citealt{CAI2026} and references therein). These discrepancies between early and late times may reflect new fundamental physics. Many solutions have been proposed (\citealt{DiValentino:2021}, for review). However, whether the observed discrepancies truly reflect new physics or, rather, measurement errors in one or more of the methods requires further theoretical and observational work. %Future surveys will improve statistical precision, but this alone cannot settle this discrepancy, since both the early- and late-time measurements are already limited by systematics and model assumptions rather than by statistics. What is needed instead are independent measurements with different systematics. Water megamasers provide exactly this: they yield purely geometric distances to galaxies, independent of the distance ladder, stellar physics, empirical calibrations, and independent of any cosmological model \citep{Reid:2013, Pesce:2020}.

Most late-Universe measurements rely on ``standard candles'', such as Cepheids and Type~Ia supernovae (e.g., \citealt{Freedman2001, Riess2022, Casertano2026}). Rubin/LSST will enable constraints on $H_0$ to $<1$\% precision from standard candles \citep{Jones:2019}. However, standard candles rely on assumptions and may be vulnerable to unknown systematics. Masers, instead, can be used to measure geometric distances by tracking the motion of gas in Keplerian rotation around a black hole (BH; \citealt{Pesce_2020a}), directly in the Hubble flow. Currently, the value based on six megamaser galaxies of $H_0 = 73.9 \pm 3.0$ km\,s$^{-1}$ Mpc$^{-1}$ \citep{Pesce2020} agrees with other late-Universe measurements, but its uncertainty is still too large to exclude the early-Universe value. Since the systematic errors of $H_0$ from each maser system are uncorrelated, the precision on $H_0$ based on $N$ systems decreases as $\sqrt(N)$. The typical error for a single galaxy is $\sim$5-10\%, implying a sample of $\sim$25-100 for a 1\% $H_0$ measurement. Improvements will come not only from additional systems, but also from better precision in the disk modeling of currently known systems. If the distance error can be reduced to $\sim$3\%, only $\sim$10 systems would be needed to reach a precision of 1\% on $H_0$.

\subsection{Maser masses: BH-galaxy co-evolution and intermediate mass black holes (IMBHs)}

Two fundamental constituents in galaxy evolution are i) the correlation between SMBH masses and host galaxies at low masses and ii) the abundance of intermediate-mass black holes (IMBHs) in the Universe. Currently, better understanding of both of these fields is stymied by the lack of precise and accurate mass measurements in low-mass galaxies. 

It is well established that all massive galaxies, with masses comparable to or larger than the Milky Way, host SMBHs at their center and that the SMBH masses scale with the properties of their host galaxies such as stellar velocity dispersion ($\sigma$), bulge mass ($M_{Bulge}$) and stellar mass ($M_*$) (e.g., \citealt{Kormendy2013}). The AGN fraction in massive galaxies is also well studied and can reach tens of percent, depending on the selection criteria (e.g., \citealt{Kauffmann2003, Hickox2018ARA&A}. However, in low-mass galaxies, the situation is less clear. 

Theoretical models make different predictions for the BH occupation fraction at low masses, ranging from 50\% to 100\% (e.g. \cite{Gallo2023}). Very few nuclear BHs in low-mass galaxies are found to be accreting (e.g. \cite{Bellovary2019}), and predictions of BH-galaxy scaling relations at the low-mass end depend on the models of BH seed formation and growth in the early Universe (e.g. \cite{Volonteri2010, Volonteri2021}). 

Although intermediate mass black holes (IMBHs; $M_{BH} \sim 10^2$--$10^5\,M_\odot$) which bridge the mass gap between stellar-mass BHs ($M_{BH} \sim 10$--$100\,M_\odot$) and SMBHs ($M_{BH} \gtrsim 10^5\,M_\odot$), are predicted to exist to seed the growth of SMBHs \citep{Volonteri2021} their abundance is highly debated and, again, depends on BH seed formation and growth (e.g., \citealt{Mezcua2017, Volonteri2021}). If they exist, some should be at the centers of low-mass galaxies. BHs in low-mass galaxies are generally smaller, which leads to smaller spheres of influence and lower Eddington luminosity, so they are harder to detect both dynamically and through signatures of continuous accretion. There are only a handful of BHs in low-mass galaxies with dynamical mass measurements, and most other methods, such as single-epoch optical spectra, have errors of at least a factor of a few \citep{Greene2020}. 
Disk megamasers provide the most precise measurements of IMBH masses, but only one low-mass AGN, IC~750, is known to host one. Its mass, $1.4 \times 10^5$ M$_\odot$, falls at the edge of the IMBH range and lies two orders of magnitude below the $M_{BH}$--$\sigma$ relation and one to two orders of magnitude below the $M_{BH}$--$M_{\rm bulge}$ and $M_{BH}$--$M_\star$ relations \citep{Zaw2020}. Such systems are difficult to detect and resolve: maser detection rates fall with galaxy mass \citep{Rosenthal2020}, and smaller BHs will have more compact maser disks. Major gains in sensitivity  and resolution would be needed to detect additional systems in order to understand whether this object is an outlier or signals a change to our picture of BH formation and growth.

\subsection{Accretion disk geometry and gas dynamics}
\label{maser_disk}

Water megamasers also provide a unique means of probing the physical conditions, dynamics, and structure of molecular gas in AGN accretion disks. Those scales are prohibitive to access at high resolution, especially for thermal molecular gas emission, which is typically observed with ALMA.  Megamasers, in contrast, display exceptionally high brightness temperatures, allowing observation at milliarcsecond (mas) resolution and thus sub-pc scales. Recent high-sensitivity VLBI observations of NGC~1068 at 22\,GHz, for example, have detected three times more masers compared to original VLBI imaging \citep{Greenhill1996}, demonstrating the impact of improved sensitivity in maser studies. The masers revealed a complex distribution, tracing a nearly edge-on disk with Keplerian velocity around a ($\sim1.7\times10^7\,M_\odot$) BH \citep{Gallimore2023}. These observations additionally revealed for the first time the existence of so-called `spokes', i.e. narrow, nearly parallel maser filaments, suggestive of an ordered magnetic field threading the accretion disk. Even more direct evidence for the role of magnetic fields was obtained by the first direct detection of linearly polarized water masers \citep{Gallimore2024}. Precise \(0.3\,\mathrm{mas}\) astrometry on the continuum was key to registering the exact location of the SMBH with its mid-infrared counterpart (3–13 $\mu$m; \citealt{Violeta2022}), and thus the dust distribution seen by MATISSE and GRAVITY \citep{Violeta2022, Gravity2020}. Finally, recently emerging evidence shows that masers may be lifted from the disk mid-plane, in a way that is reminiscent of magnetically supported "prominences" in the Sun. These findings clearly highlight the need for improved line sensitivity and for more lines of sight through the disk, requiring additional sub-mm lines. 

