\section{Sub-mm/mm maser emission}
\label{sec:submm} 

Until very recently, extragalactic megamaser science was dominated by the 22\,GHz ($6_{16}-5_{23}$) transition of water. This was historically due to the availability of cm-wavelength receivers for VLBI, combined with the difficulty of observing at sub-mm wavelengths, where atmospheric water vapour requires an exceptionally dry, high-altitude site. However, it has long been known that the water molecule has a plethora of additional maser transitions, which remain largely unexplored in extragalactic sources. These span both low-energy states (e.g., the 183\,GHz line at $E_u \sim 200$\,K) and relatively high-energy states (e.g., the 321\,GHz line at $E_u \sim 1860$\,K).  Additional transitions of note include those with rest frequencies 325, 437, 439, 471 and 658\,GHz, which have been observed to be strong Galactic masers \citep{Gray2016}. 

Access to high frequencies is critical for the 658\,GHz transition, which arises from the first excited bending-mode state ($v_2=1$) of water, $\sim$2300--2400\,K. Maser models predict strong inversion of this line in hot, dense gas, with kinetic temperatures $\sim$1000--1500\,K and $n({\rm H_2O})\sim10^5$--$10^6$\,cm$^{-3}$ \citep{Gray2016}. The 658\,GHz line is expected to trace the hottest and potentially innermost molecular gas able to survive close to the BH, at twice the resolution of the 321\,GHz line ($\sim$2\,mas for 50\,km baselines). At the other extreme, lower-excitation transitions, such as the 183\,GHz line, trace cooler material at larger radii and heights than the 22\,GHz masers, as already demonstrated in Circinus (Sect.~\ref{maser_disk}).  Broad frequency coverage, extending to Band~1, is additionally essential for high-redshift science (Sect.~\ref{sec:highz}). Together with predicted masers e.g., at 899 and 922\,GHz, these lines span the full ALMA frequency range.

Some of the high-frequency water megamasers have been detected towards multiple extragalactic targets, e.g., those at 183, 321 and 380\,GHz \citep[e.g.,][]{Pesce2016, Pesce2023, Hagiwara2013, Hagiwara2021, Yang2023}.  ALMA has made first studies of sub-mm water maser lines possible, but so far only in a handful of nearby galaxies.  A 10-fold increase in line sensitivity will be required to make the science systematic. 

\subsection{From individual maser spots to complete accretion disks}
\label{maser_disk}

High-frequency masers are crucial probes of accretion disks, not only because of the potential for higher angular resolution compared to 22\,GHz, but also because different transitions require different temperature and density conditions \citep{Gray2016}, which will arise from different regions of the circumnuclear disk. 

Recent observations of the Circinus galaxy have enabled, for the first time, to resolve the sub-mm maser disk (see Figure~\ref{fig:1}C from Venselaar et al. in prep.). Furthermore, multiple sub-mm line detections (183, 321 and 325\,GHz; Fig.~\ref{fig:1}A,B), could all be traced to the circumnuclear disk. The resolved 183\,GHz emission has a larger vertical extent and occurs at larger radii than the 22\,GHz masers, implying cooler, less dense material; 321\,GHz, on the other hand, traces hotter, more compact regions, potentially mapping the innermost molecular disk and thus marking the transition between the parsec-scales and the accretion flow feeding the BH. 

The idea of ``super masers'' is also an interesting one \citep{Gray2016} as it rests on models of water maser ``backbone'' lines (22, 183, 380\,GHz). They predict that, depending on saturation, the 380\,GHz line could outshine the 22\,GHz line (an earlier similar prediction comes from \citealt{Neufeld1991}). There is work ongoing to find new super masers with ALMA, but a major limiting factor is line sensitivity. Propagating the level of detail achieved for nearby galaxies to a larger sample of AGN will require enabling sub-mm maser searches at comparable sensitivity achieved in the cm-regime. 

\subsection{Maser polarization}
\label{sec:polarization}

The magnetic field properties near the accretion disk and outflows provide insights into jet launching and BH feeding, and these can be traced by masers. Because water is a non-paramagnetic molecule, circular (Zeeman) polarization of maser emission is expected to be weak ($<1\%$), but linear polarization $>1\%$ can arise from anisotropic pumping and/or saturation and directly traces the plane-of-sky field orientation. Modeling of the maser emission \citep{Lankhaar2019} predicts that sub-mm maser lines become strongly polarized once they saturate, with both the 321\,GHz and 183\,GHz maser transitions becoming significantly polarized ($\gtrsim 3\%$) once saturated, with the 183\,GHz line reaching even higher fractional polarization (up to $\sim$14\%). Moreover, the sub-mm masers largely avoid the Faraday depolarization that has hampered polarization work at 22\,GHz, making them a cleaner probe of the magnetic field close to the BH \citep{Surcis2020}. Additionally, recent polarization modeling predicts strong anisotropic pumping for the 183\,GHz transition capable of generating non-Zeeman circular polarization \citep{Lankhaar:2024}.

Maser polarization, however, is also intrinsically sensitivity limited. Measurements of extragalactic maser emission are effectively restricted to the brightest components ($\gtrsim150$\,mJy), yielding only a handful of polarization vectors (Figure~\ref{fig:1}A,B). Recovering the coherent magnetic-field geometry across the disk, i.e., the magnetic-field tomography of these sub-parsec environments, will test whether the field follows the rotating disk, traces vertical or radial structures, or changes at the disk–wind interface.


\subsection{Towards high-z maser emission} \label{sec:highz}

The same backbone transitions providing motivation for sub-mm searches offer a route to extending the megamaser technique to cosmological distances. The 380\,GHz line can be shifted into current ALMA Bands 1-3-6-7, which will allow VLBI observations to resolve high-z maser disks at $\sim 20–40\,\upmu$as. These cosmological masers can provide an independent geometric, distance-ladder-independent anchor, as well as accurate BH masses deep into the early Universe. The 22\,GHz line alone is not enough; being intrinsically faint, it is easily swamped by the stronger radio continuum, as was the case for the first (and to date only) megamaser detection in the lensed quasar MG~0414+0534 at $z=2.6$ \citep{Impellizzeri2008,Castangia2011}. 

The 380\,GHz line has the potential to outshine the 22\,GHz line \citep{Maloney2002,Gray2016}, and to better stand out against the continuum. However, this line emission from nearby galaxies cannot be observed from the ground due to atmospheric opacity; it can only be observed when redshifted to wavelengths with high atmospheric transmission. Evidence is slowly catching up with the theory: a recent systematic ALMA survey of 12 lensed HyLIRGs at $z=1.8$–3.5 has detected the line in 9 of the 12 targets (83\%), with narrow spectral components pointing to a genuine maser contribution (Kuo et al. in preparation).

Current high-z 380\,GHz programs are nevertheless sensitivity-limited: reaching the $\approx$0.4\,mJy rms needed for a $5\sigma$ gigamaser detection already costs tens of hours per target, restricting us to the most extreme, most massive Compton-thick quasars. An order-of-magnitude sensitivity gain would open up the broader high-z AGN population to statistical studies.

\section{ALMA2040: Technical requirements}

\subsection{Requirements for key science}

A major increase in both line sensitivity and angular resolution relative to current ALMA is essential to make resolved measurements of maser disk kinematics on sub-pc scales, BH masses, and ultimately geometric distances. These requirements are coupled: the astrometric precision of an unresolved maser scales approximately as $\sigma_{\rm pos}\propto {\rm FWHM_{beam}}/{\rm SNR}$, so a 10$\times$ gain in sensitivity combined with a 3$\times$ finer beam at fixed flux density improves the positional precision by a factor of $\sim$30. For example, at 321\,GHz a maser currently detected at ${\rm SNR}=10$ in a $\sim$15\,mas beam has $\sigma_{\rm pos}\approx0.9$\,mas, whereas the same maser with ALMA2040 (${\rm SNR}=100$, $\sim$5\,mas beam) achieves $\sigma_{\rm pos}\approx0.03$\,mas ($30\,\upmu$as).

ALMA2040 will be able to reach these goals at larger distances. ALMA2040 alone would be able to resolve and detect a maser disk to $\sim$40\,Mpc ($1$\,mas is $\simeq0.1$\,pc at 20\,Mpc), with VLBI baselines extending to even larger distances, thanks to improved resolution. This is particularly important for intermediate-mass BHs, for which the sphere of influence and Keplerian maser disk scale with BH mass ($r\propto M_{\rm BH}/v^2$). So for example, the $\sim$0.2\,pc ($\sim$3\,mas) disk of IC\,750 is smaller than the $\sim$4--6\,mas ALMA2040 beam at 321\,GHz but can be mapped by centroiding velocity-resolved components at ${\rm SNR}\gtrsim10$ ($\sigma_{\rm pos}\sim0.3$\,mas).

For ALMA2040, the sensitivity gain is required not only to detect additional maser galaxies but also to recover the faint maser complexes necessary to constrain disk geometry and dynamics (Section~\ref{maser_disk}). Maintaining a high spectral resolution is also critical because maser lines are typically extremely narrow. Spectral resolutions of at least $\sim$0.1--0.5\,km\,s$^{-1}$ are required together with broad instantaneous bandwidth to encompass the systemic and high-velocity maser complexes.

Full-Stokes capability with excellent polarization purity is required to take advantage of the few-percent linear polarization expected from sub-mm water masers. Detecting 3\% polarization at $3\upsigma$ requires ${\rm SNR}\gtrsim100$ in Stokes~I, so the same $\sim10\times$ improvement in line sensitivity will enable polarization measurements of sources down to $\sim$15\,mJy (compared with $\sim$150\,mJy today), opening magnetic-field tomography across the disk. Instrumental polarization purity must be well below the expected signal ($\lesssim$0.3\%). 

Ultra-wide frequency coverage is a key requirement. The water molecule hosts a rich ladder of potentially masing transitions spanning different excitation temperatures and conditions, providing complementary probes of the temperature, density, and geometries. At $z\sim0$, the 96, 183, 321/325/354, 437--471, and 621/658\,GHz lines fall in ALMA Bands~3, 5, 7, 8, and~9, and the 899/922\,GHz lines in Band~10. Redshift moves these lines to lower bands; the 380\,GHz line is accessible in Band~7 for $z\gtrsim0.02$ and reaches Band~3 at $z\sim2.3$--3.5, and 183\,GHz reaches Band~1 at $z\gtrsim2.7$ (Sect.~\ref{sec:highz}). ALMA2040 should therefore retain access to Bands~1--10, with Bands~5--9 of highest priority for nearby disks (see Section~\ref{maser_disk}).

Integration of ALMA2040 into global VLBI arrays is an essential requirement. The much smaller beam provided by VLBI is needed to resolve maser disks on sub-parsec scales beyond the local Universe, and thus be able to estimate distances. ALMA2040 must improve on the current ALMA capabilities to operate as a phased array. The inclusion of a small number of larger-diameter antennas within ALMA2040 could be considered a powerful route to increasing instantaneous phasing efficiency. High-frequency VLBI requires deriving phase solutions within the short coherence time ($\leq$10\,s in Band~7, shorter than the $\approx$18\,s phasing loop on current ALMA; \citealt{Crew2023}). Larger collecting elements provide higher instantaneous sensitivity and enable phasing on fainter targets, which is particularly important for the frequency phase transfer (FPT) experiments, where lower-frequency continuum emission (e.g., at $\sim$86\,GHz) is tracked simultaneously with high-frequency emission, extending the coherence time. In the simulations presented below (See Fig.\ref{fig:2}, FPT from compact continuum emission at $\sim$86\,GHz extends the coherence time and allows disks to be resolved at distances where conventional high-frequency VLBI fails (e.g., at 160\,Mpc). ALMA2040 should, therefore, provide simultaneous ultra-wide-bandwidth observing.

\subsection{Simulated data}
\label{simulations}
