To simulate these maser systems, we generate ground-truth megamaser disk configurations using a standard warped-disk model \citep[e.g.,][]{Herrnstein_1999,Humphreys_2013,Pesce_2020a}, drawing individual redshifted, blueshifted, and systemic maser spot populations from a power-law flux distribution and placing them at radii separations of ${\approx}0.2$\,pc around a $10^7$\,M$_{\odot}$ BH. Each spot is assigned a position and line-of-sight velocity determined by its location inside the disk and the disk's orientation on the sky, for which we adopted an inclination angle of $85^{\circ}$ and a position angle of $90^{\circ}$, with a modest warp in both dimensions. We set the overall luminosity such that the peak flux density in the spectrum is ${\approx}0.2$\,Jy at a distance of 100\,Mpc, similar to the 22\,GHz maser system in NGC~5765b \citep{Gao_2016}.

We conducted synthetic observations of the 321\,GHz maser line for both ALMA2040-only and ALMA2040+EHT VLBI configurations using the {\sc ngehtsim} package \citep{Pesce_2024}. The 321\,GHz transition is particularly favorable: while the 183 and 325\,GHz lines could reach higher flux densities, observations at these frequencies are more strongly affected by atmospheric opacity,  while the 183\,GHz line is not easily accessible to a large fraction of existing and planned EHT stations. We assume a current ALMA sensitivity (i.e., $1\sigma$ RMS noise level) in a one-hour integration of 4~mJy\,beam$^{-1}$ per 0.5\,km\,s$^{-1}$ channel at 321\,GHz, and we scale the noise level with source redshift according to the relative telescope atmospheric opacity. We assume that ALMA2040 has $10\times$ the sensitivity and $3\times$ the angular resolution of current ALMA. For each spectral channel, we define the intrinsic on-sky position as the flux-weighted centroid of all contributing maser spots, to which we then add noise perturbations with $\sigma_{\text{pos}} = 0.6 \left( \frac{\text{FWHM}_{\text{beam}}}{\text{SNR}} \right)$.

ALMA2040 simulations show a significant improvement over ALMA-WSU in both the detection and spatial mapping of megamaser systems. The combination of increased sensitivity and angular resolution enables recovery of the disk spatial and kinematic features, enabling detailed disk modeling and opening the possibility of deriving BH masses and geometric distances directly from sub-mm masers. In this regime, ALMA2040 moves the field from detecting individual sub-mm maser lines to using them as probes of the accretion disk dynamics and physical conditions. For an intrinsically luminous system like NGC~5765b, the simulations show that ALMA2040 could detect the 321\,GHz transition out to $z\approx0.3$, where the line is redshifted into ALMA Band~6. Fig.~\ref{fig:2} shows a simulated disk at 40\,Mpc. However, spatially resolved information is substantially more demanding, and ALMA-only mapping becomes more challenging beyond $z\approx0.05$.

Synthetic ALMA2040+EHT observations show that, for a Circinus-like 321\,GHz maser, detections on the longest baselines are restricted to the nearby Universe, even with ALMA2040, favorable observing conditions (PWV $\ll$1\,mm), and an expanded future EHT array \citep{Doeleman2023, Namene2024IAUGA}. A source beyond $\approx15$\,Mpc may not be recovered on long baselines. This limitation is primarily driven by baseline sensitivity and atmospheric coherence, but may be alleviated only by improved phased-array sensitivity and FPT \citep{Issaoun2025}, i.e. using simultaneous lower-frequency observations. Figure~\ref{fig:2} (bottom right) shows the remarkable ability of ALMA2040 to recover the VLBI disk out to 160\,Mpc, enabled by the improved phase coherence through FPT. The effectiveness of this approach will depend on the multi-frequency capabilities of ALMA2040 and the wider VLBI array.



In an optimistic scenario, combining an intrinsically luminous disk maser similar to NGC~5765b with FPT from bright compact continuum emission at $\approx86$\,GHz, the simulations extend the 321\,GHz VLBI detection horizon up to $\sim300$\,Mpc ($z\sim0.08-0.09$). This gain requires compact 86\,GHz continuum emission at the level of approximately 10--100\,mJy.  It is to be noted that, because of the very small sub-mm VLBI beam, even relatively low-SNR detections of compact maser spots can yield well-constrained positions, allowing informative disk mapping close to the detection limit.

\section{Synergies}

\subsection{LSST/Rubin Observatory}

The Vera C. Rubin Observatory recently began its 10-year Legacy Survey of Space and Time (LSST), which will probe the dynamic Universe by continuously monitoring 18000 deg$^2$ of sky \citep{Ivezic:2019}. LSST will enable constraints on $H_0$ to high precision from standard candles by increasing the number of detected Type 1a supernovae by a factor of 100 out to $z\approx1$ \citep{Jones:2019}, from geometric time delays from hundreds of strong lenses, potentially out to $z\approx5$ \citep{Liao:2019}, and from strongly lensed Type 1a supernovae \citep{Birrer:2022}, among others. These methods require assumptions, both empirical relations and model assumptions (e.g. \citealt{Raney:2020}). Geometric distance measurements from maser discs from ALMA2040 would complement these studies by enabling calibration of standard candles and strong lenses to correct systematic errors. 

In addition, the Rubin Observatory and other current and future multi-wavelength facilities, e.g. JWST, Lynx, LISA, AXIS, will be sensitive to IMBH candidates (e.g. \citealt{Saini:2022}). ALMA2040 observations could provide accurate masses for newly discovered candidates as well as discover additional ones.

\subsection{SKA, ngVLA, and ngEHT}

The forthcoming Square Kilometre Array (SKA) and proposed next-generation Very Large Array (ngVLA) will both provide unparalleled resolution and sensitivity at lower wavelengths \citep{Bonaldi:2026,Selina:2018}. They will be able to push forward studies of 22\,GHz megamasers in the local Universe (ngVLA; \citealt{Braatz:2019}) and redshifted megamasers (SKA; \citealt{Rygl:2026}), complementing ALMA2040 studies of high-frequency water masers. In addition, a planned next-generation EHT (ngEHT; \citealt{Doeleman:2023}) will introduce additional dishes and simultaneous multi-band observing \citep{Johnson:2023} that will pave the way for ALMA2040-VLBI.

\section{Summary}




Sub-mm/mm masers complement the established 22\,GHz technique by probing different excitation conditions and disk regions, constraining the physical conditions of the accreting gas. The reduced free-free absorption in the sub-mm/mm can reveal the far side of the disk, and the order-of-magnitude smaller mm-VLBI beam sharply improves localization of individual maser spots. We propose that ALMA2040 high-resolution, high-sensitivity imaging of nearby disks would yield the most precise BH masses and independent geometric distances, while increased sensitivity would detect sources out to high redshifts. ALMA2040-VLBI of the most local systems would probe fundamentally new gas regions and finer scales than possible at 22\,GHz, resolving compact disks around low-mass BHs. Mapping sub-mm masers across the full disk, including the far side, would constrain properties such as warps, connecting the sub-pc disk to the larger-scale accretion flow and inward to the BH. Water megamaser observations with ALMA2040 will reveal the magnetic structure of AGN accretion disks, discover IMBHs and constrain theories of BH-galaxy coevolution, and provide a precise and accurate measurement of the Hubble constant.

