\begin{abstract}
    The recent realization of bulk superconductivity in the quintuple-layer nickelate La$_6$Ni$_5$O$_{12}$ opens new opportunities to probe the pairing symmetry of the reduced Ruddlesden--Popper (RP) nickelate family. Using an effective five-layer Hubbard model and variational Monte Carlo calculations, we find that a spin-singlet $d_{x^2-y^2}$-wave state is energetically favored, while extended-$s$-wave pairing is strongly suppressed. Based on the optimized superconducting state, we further calculate several experimentally accessible observables. The tunneling density of states exhibits a characteristic V-shaped low-energy spectrum, the superfluid density shows a linear low-temperature depletion, the Knight shift decreases below \(T_c\), and the spin-lattice relaxation rate follows $1/T_1T\propto T^2$, together providing consistent signatures of a clean nodal superconductor. Extending the same framework to related reduced RP nickelates, we find a robust $d$-wave tendency in infinite-layer LaNiO$_{2}$, while trilayer La$_4$Ni$_3$O$_{8}$ at its nominal filling lies close to the boundary between $d$-wave superconductivity and a non-superconducting state. These results establish bulk La$_6$Ni$_5$O$_{12}$ as a promising platform for determining the pairing symmetry of nickelate superconductors and suggest a common $d_{x^2-y^2}$-wave pairing tendency across the reduced RP nickelate family.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

Superconductivity in reduced Ruddlesden--Popper (RP) nickelates has opened a new route to unconventional pairing beyond the cuprates~\cite{Li2019,Li2020,Zeng2020,Pan2022,Pan2026,Chow2025,2026Lib,NomuraReview2022,WangReview2024,2025Chene,Hepting_review,Parzyck2025,Sahib2025,Dong2025,Si2020Hydrogen,Hepting2020}. A central question is the symmetry of the superconducting order parameter~\cite{NomuraReview2022,WangReview2024,2025Chene}. Theoretical studies of infinite-layer \(R_{1-x}A_x\mathrm{NiO}_2\) (112) have predominantly favored spin-singlet \(d_{x^2-y^2}\)-wave pairing~\cite{Anisimov1999,LeePickett2004,Jiang2019,Nomura2019,Hu2019,Ryee2020,Jiang2020Spin,Zhang2020S,Gu2020,Werner2020,Lechermann2020,Botana2020,Wu2020, Sakakibara2020, Kitatani2020, Wang2020, Adhikary2020, Kreisel2022, Chen2025}, and recent experiments have provided evidence for a nodal superconducting gap~\cite{Cheng2024, 2025Hwangi,2023Ariando,Ranna2025}. Yet a definitive characterization of the pairing state remains difficult because superconductivity in these systems has so far been realized primarily in epitaxial thin films~\cite{Li2019,Li2020,Zeng2020,Pan2022}, strongly restricting conventional bulk probes such as nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR), penetration depth, and thermodynamic measurements~\cite{NomuraReview2022,WangReview2024,2025Chene}.

The recent realization of bulk superconductivity in the quintuple-layer nickelate \(R_6\mathrm{Ni}_5\mathrm{O}_{12}\) (6512) provides a new opportunity to overcome this limitation~\cite{2026Chen}. Finite-layer nickelates provide a natural extension of the infinite-layer compounds. As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:model}, the nominal Ni electron count in the \(R_{n+1}\mathrm{Ni}_n\mathrm{O}_{2n+2}\) series is \(d^{9-1/n}\), connecting the \(d^9\) infinite-layer limit to the formal \(d^8\) endpoint at \(n=1\), represented by \(\mathrm{La}_2\mathrm{NiO}_4\)~\cite{Pan2022,Worm2022,LaBollita2022}. Relative to the \(d^9\) configuration, this corresponds to a nominal hole doping of \(x=1/n\), while undoped 112 compounds have a nominal hole doping $x=0$, with doped holes introduced by self-doping from rare earth $5d$ orbitals~\cite{Jiang2019,Nomura2019} and external doping from rare earth substitutions. As the number of layers decreases, the nominal hole doping increases, reaching \(x=0.2\) in 6512~\cite{Pan2022,Pan2026} and \(x=1/3\) in trilayer \(R_4\mathrm{Ni}_3\mathrm{O}_8\) (438). Recent reports of ambient-pressure superconducting signatures in substituted 438 single crystals~\cite{Ahn2026} further motivate a comparison across the reduced RP nickelate family.

The nominal filling of 6512 is particularly appealing because it lies close to the superconducting doping regime of infinite-layer nickelates~\cite{Li2020,Zeng2020,Pan2022,Pan2026}. Several electronic-structure calculations support a low-energy description of quintuple-layer nickelates dominated by the Ni \(d_{x^2-y^2}\) orbital and find close similarities to hole-doped infinite-layer nickelates at comparable nominal filling~\cite{Worm2022,LaBollita2022,Botana2022,Hausoel2025}. An alternative multiorbital picture involving substantial Ni \(d_{z^2}\) flat-band contributions near the Fermi level has also been proposed~\cite{Lechermann2022}. Bulk 6512 therefore provides an experimentally accessible setting in which the pairing physics of reduced RP nickelates can be examined using complementary bulk-sensitive probes.

Previous theoretical studies have described finite-layer nickelates using effective single-band Hubbard models and obtained \(d\)-wave superconductivity mediated by antiferromagnetic spin fluctuations~\cite{Worm2022,Hausoel2025}. Complementary calculations based on a five-layer $t-J$ model have also found dominant $d_{x^2-y^2}$-wave pairing and determined layer-resolved superconducting gaps within a large-$N$ saddle-point treatment~\cite{Pan2022}. These results motivate an examination of pairing in the five-layer Hubbard model, together with predictions for bulk-sensitive experimental probes. 

Here, we address these questions using variational Monte Carlo (VMC) calculations for a realistic five-layer model of 6512. We find that a spin-singlet \(d_{x^2-y^2}\)-wave state is energetically favored and examine its layer-resolved pairing amplitudes. Based on this correlated superconducting state, we further calculate the quasiparticle density of states, superfluid density, Knight shift, and nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate $1/T_1T$, providing direct predictions for bulk experiments. We further compare 6512 with trilayer 438 and infinite-layer 112, examining how pairing tendencies differ across nickelate compounds with different layer numbers.
Our results identify bulk 6512 as a particularly valuable platform for probing the pairing symmetry of nickelate superconductors and for connecting superconductivity across the broader reduced RP nickelate family.


