\section{DFT Computational Method}\label{appendix:dft}
Our first-principles calculations were performed within the framework of density functional theory (DFT), as implemented in the Vienna \textit{ab initio} Simulation Package (VASP) \cite{Kresse1996,Kresse1996PRB}. The projector augmented-wave (PAW) method was employed to describe the electron-ion interactions \cite{Blochl1994, Kresse1999}. The exchange--correlation energy was treated using the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) in the Perdew--Burke--Ernzerhof (PBE) parametrization \cite{PBE1996}. The plane-wave kinetic-energy cutoff was set to 500~eV, and the Brillouin zone was sampled using a 7 $\times$ 7 $\times$ 7 $k$-point mesh. The convergence criterion for the electronic self-consistent calculations was set to $10^{-8}$~eV.


\section{Variational wave function and optimization}
\label{appendix:vmc}

The mean-field ansatz $|\Phi_{\rm mf}\rangle$ entering Eq.~\eqref{eq:vmc_ansatz} is the ground state of the free-fermion Hamiltonian
\begin{align}
    H_{\rm mf} =& \sum_{\bk,\sigma}\sum_{\ell=1}^{5}\left[\varepsilon^0_{\ell}(\bk)-\widetilde{\mu}_{\ell}\right]c_{\bk\ell\sigma}^\dagger c_{\bk\ell\sigma}\notag\\
    &+\sum_{\bk,\sigma}\sum_{\ell=1}^{4}\widetilde{\chi}_{\ell,\ell+1}(\bk)\left(c_{\bk\ell\sigma}^\dagger c_{\bk,\ell+1,\sigma}+\text{h.c.}\right)\notag\\
    &+\sum_{\bk}\sum_{\ell=1}^{5}\left[\widetilde{\Delta}_{\ell}(\bk)c_{\bk\ell\uparrow}^\dagger c_{-\bk\ell\downarrow}^\dagger+\text{h.c.}\right].
    \label{eq:Hmf}
\end{align}
Here $\varepsilon^0_\ell(\bk)=\sum_{m=1}^3t_{m\ell}\gamma_{m\bk}$ denotes the physical intralayer dispersion. The auxiliary layer-dependent chemical potentials $\widetilde{\mu}_\ell$ are optimized independently for the three layer types. The auxiliary interlayer matrix elements retain the physical momentum dependence,
\begin{equation}
    \widetilde{\chi}_{\ell,\ell+1}(\bk)=-2\widetilde{\chi}_{\ell,\ell+1}(\cos k_x-\cos k_y)^2.
\end{equation}
Mirror symmetry imposes $\widetilde{\chi}_{12}=\widetilde{\chi}_{45}=\widetilde{\chi}_{oi}$ and $\widetilde{\chi}_{23}=\widetilde{\chi}_{34}=\widetilde{\chi}_{ic}$, together with identical intralayer variational parameters on layers $1,5$ and on layers $2,4$.

For the two paired ansatzes, the nearest-neighbor spin-singlet pairing fields are
\begin{align}
    \widetilde{\Delta}^{d}_\ell(\bk)&=2\eta^{d}_\ell(\cos k_x-\cos k_y),\\
    \widetilde{\Delta}^{s}_\ell(\bk)&=2\eta^{s}_\ell(\cos k_x+\cos k_y).
\end{align}
All variational parameters are real, and no explicit interlayer pairing field is included. The normal reference is obtained by setting the nearest-neighbor pairing amplitudes to zero. The Gutzwiller projector multiplies each configuration amplitude by $\exp[-\sum_\ell g_\ell D_\ell]$, where $D_\ell=\sum_i n_{i\ell\uparrow}n_{i\ell\downarrow}$ counts the doubly occupied sites on layer $\ell$.

Thus, each paired ansatz has eleven variational parameters: three auxiliary chemical potentials, three pairing amplitudes, three Gutzwiller parameters, and two interlayer hopping amplitudes. The non-superconducting reference state has eight variational parameters, with pairing amplitudes excluded.


\section{Calculation of the superfluid density}
\label{appendix:sd}

We calculate the in-plane superfluid density from the static transverse electromagnetic response of the renormalized effective mean field Hamiltonian. In the superconducting state, the effective Hamiltonian takes the BdG form. In the five-layer Nambu basis $\Psi_{\bk}=(c_{\bk 1\uparrow},\ldots,c_{\bk 5\uparrow},c^\dagger_{-\bk 1\downarrow},\ldots,c^\dagger_{-\bk 5\downarrow})^T$, the Hamiltonian matrix is
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{H}_{\rm eff}(\bk)=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        h(\bk) & \Delta(\bk)\\
        \Delta^\dagger(\bk) & -h^T(-\bk)
    \end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $h(\bk)$ contains the KR-renormalized intralayer and interlayer hoppings, onsite energies, and density-matched layer-dependent chemical potentials. The pairing matrix $\Delta(\bk)$ contains the optimized $d$-wave pairing amplitudes. All parameters are held fixed as temperature varies. We denote the ten eigenvalues and normalized eigenvectors by $E_{n\bk}$ and $|n,\bk\rangle$, respectively, and write $|n,\bk\rangle=(u_{n\bk},v_{n\bk})^T$, where $u_{n\bk}$ and $v_{n\bk}$ are five-component electron and hole vectors.

The current response to an external vector potential is written as
\begin{equation}
    j_\mu(\bq,\omega)=-\sum_\nu K_{\mu\nu}(\bq,\omega)A_\nu(\bq,\omega),
\end{equation}
where $\mu,\nu=x,y$ denote the in-plane directions and $K_{\mu\nu}$ is the electromagnetic response kernel. The superfluid stiffness is obtained from its static transverse limit,
\begin{equation}
    \rho_{s,\mu}(T)=\lim_{\substack{\bq\rightarrow0\\ \bq\perp\hat{\boldsymbol e}_\mu}}\operatorname{Re}K_{\mu\mu}(\bq,\omega=0;T).
    \label{eq:sd_transverse}
\end{equation}
We drop the common electromagnetic and geometrical factors, which cancel in the normalized stiffness reported in the main text. Momentum sums below are normalized per five-layer in-plane unit cell.

The response kernel consists of diamagnetic and paramagnetic contributions,
\begin{equation}
    K_{\mu\nu}(\bq,\omega)=D_{\mu\nu}+\Pi_{\mu\nu}(\bq,\omega).
\end{equation}
Introducing the vector potential through Peierls phases in the hopping terms gives the kinetic velocity and curvature matrices
\begin{equation}
    v_\mu(\bk)=\frac{\partial h(\bk)}{\partial k_\mu},
    \qquad
    w_{\mu\nu}(\bk)=\frac{\partial^2 h(\bk)}{\partial k_\mu\partial k_\nu}.
    \label{eq:sd_vertices}
\end{equation}
These derivatives include the momentum dependence of both intralayer and interlayer hoppings. The pairing matrix is held fixed in constructing the electromagnetic vertices. For the real, inversion-symmetric normal-state matrix used here, $h(\bk)=h^T(\bk)=h(-\bk)$, the current vertex in Nambu space is
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{J}_\mu(\bk)=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        v_\mu(\bk) & 0\\
        0 & v_\mu(\bk)
    \end{pmatrix}.
    \label{eq:sd_current_vertex}
\end{equation}

The diamagnetic contribution is determined by the superconducting-state electronic density matrix,
\begin{equation}
    D_{\mu\nu}(T)=\frac{1}{N_{\bk}}\sum_{\bk,\sigma}\sum_{\ell,\ell^\prime=1}^{5}[w_{\mu\nu}(\bk)]_{\ell\ell^\prime}\langle c^\dagger_{\bk\ell\sigma}c_{\bk\ell^\prime\sigma}\rangle.
\end{equation}
Using the BdG eigenvectors and spin symmetry, this becomes
\begin{equation}
    D_{\mu\nu}(T)=\frac{2}{N_{\bk}}\sum_{\bk}\sum_{n=1}^{10}f(E_{n\bk})\,u^\dagger_{n\bk}w_{\mu\nu}(\bk)u_{n\bk},
    \label{eq:sd_diamagnetic}
\end{equation}
where $f(E)=[\exp(\beta E)+1]^{-1}$ and $\beta=(k_{\rm B}T)^{-1}$. The factor of two accounts for spin degeneracy.

The paramagnetic contribution is evaluated from the current-current correlation function. The Nambu Green's function has the spectral representation
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{G}(\bk,i\omega_m)=\left[i\omega_m I_{10}-\mathcal{H}_{\rm eff}(\bk)\right]^{-1}=\sum_{n=1}^{10}\frac{|n,\bk\rangle\langle n,\bk|}{i\omega_m-E_{n\bk}},
\end{equation}
where $\omega_m=(2m+1)\pi/\beta$ is a fermionic Matsubara frequency. In the long-wavelength limit, the current-current response is
\begin{equation}
    \Pi_{\mu\nu}(\bq,i\Omega_\ell)=\frac{1}{\beta N_{\bk}}\sum_{\bk,m}\operatorname{Tr}\left[\mathcal{G}(\bk+\bq,i\omega_m+i\Omega_\ell)\mathcal{J}_\mu(\bk+\bq/2)\mathcal{G}(\bk,i\omega_m)\mathcal{J}_\nu(\bk+\bq/2)\right],
    \label{eq:sd_current_bubble}
\end{equation}
with $\Omega_\ell=2\ell\pi/\beta$ a bosonic Matsubara frequency and the trace taken over the ten Nambu components. We set the external frequency to zero before taking the transverse long-wavelength limit.

Substituting the spectral representation into Eq.~\eqref{eq:sd_current_bubble}, the Matsubara sum is evaluated using
\begin{equation}
    \frac{1}{\beta}\sum_m\frac{1}{(i\omega_m-E_a)(i\omega_m-E_b)}
    =\mathcal{F}_T(E_a,E_b),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{F}_T(E_a,E_b)=
    \begin{cases}
        \displaystyle\frac{f(E_a)-f(E_b)}{E_a-E_b}, & E_a\neq E_b,\\[6pt]
        f'(E_a), & E_a=E_b.
    \end{cases}
    \label{eq:sd_divided_difference}
\end{equation}
The equal-energy limit is retained for both diagonal terms and degenerate states. The resulting static transverse paramagnetic contribution is
\begin{equation}
    \Pi_{\mu\mu}^{\rm tr}(T)=\frac{1}{N_{\bk}}\sum_{\bk}\sum_{n,m=1}^{10}\mathcal{F}_T(E_{n\bk},E_{m\bk})\left|\langle n,\bk|\mathcal{J}_\mu(\bk)|m,\bk\rangle\right|^2.
    \label{eq:sd_paramagnetic}
\end{equation}
Both positive- and negative-energy BdG states are included, and all intraband and interband current matrix elements are retained.

Combining Eqs.~\eqref{eq:sd_diamagnetic} and \eqref{eq:sd_paramagnetic}, the superfluid stiffness is
\begin{equation}
    \rho_{s,\mu}(T)=D_{\mu\mu}(T)+\Pi_{\mu\mu}^{\rm tr}(T).
\end{equation}
We report the in-plane average $\rho_s(T)=[\rho_{s,x}(T)+\rho_{s,y}(T)]/2$ and its normalized depletion $[\rho_s(0)-\rho_s(T)]/\rho_s(0)$.

The Brillouin-zone integrals are evaluated numerically, with locally refined momentum meshes around all superconducting nodes. For calculations down to $0.5$ K, we use a $2000\times2000$ background grid, a local sampling density equivalent to $24001\times24001$ within radius $0.08$ of the nodes, and a further refinement equivalent to $96001\times96001$ within radius $0.015$.


\section{Tight-binding model and parameters for 438}
\label{appendix:trilayer}

We describe the Ni $d_{x^2-y^2}$ sector of the trilayer compound La$_4$Ni$_3$O$_8$ by a two-dimensional three-band TB model fitted to the DFT band dispersions, preserving the ordering of the layer occupations obtained from DFT. Following the notation used for 6512, the layers are ordered as outer--inner--outer, with $\ell=1,2,3$, respectively, and the two outer layers share the same parameters. In the basis $C_{\bk\sigma}=(c_{\bk1\sigma},c_{\bk2\sigma},c_{\bk3\sigma})^T$, the kinetic Hamiltonian is
\begin{equation}
    \cH_t^{(438)}=\sum_{\bk,\sigma}C_{\bk\sigma}^\dagger h_{438}(\bk)C_{\bk\sigma},
    \qquad
    h_{438}(\bk)=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \varepsilon_{o,\bk} & \tau_{oi}(\bk) & \tau_{oo}(\bk)\\
        \tau_{oi}(\bk) & \varepsilon_{i,\bk} & \tau_{oi}(\bk)\\
        \tau_{oo}(\bk) & \tau_{oi}(\bk) & \varepsilon_{o,\bk}
    \end{pmatrix}.
    \label{eq:438_hopping_matrix}
\end{equation}
Here $o$ and $i$ denote the outer and inner layer types. Notably, we retain both outer--inner hopping and hopping between the two outer layers within a trilayer block.

The intralayer dispersion includes hopping up to the third nearest neighbor,
\begin{equation}
    \varepsilon_{\ell,\bk}=\epsilon_\ell+\sum_{m=1}^{3}t_{m\ell}\gamma_{m\bk},
    \qquad \ell=o,i.
    \label{eq:438_intralayer}
\end{equation}
$\epsilon_\ell$ denotes the layer-dependent onsite energy shift. The three corresponding harmonics are defined as in the main text.

Both interlayer matrix elements have the same form as in the main text,
\begin{equation}
    \tau_{\ell\ell^\prime}(\bk)=-2t_\perp^{\ell\ell^\prime}(\cos k_x-\cos k_y)^2,
    \qquad \ell\ell^\prime=oi,oo.
    \label{eq:438_interlayer}
\end{equation} 
The $oo$ term couples layers 1 and 3 within the same block. The obtained dispersion and corresponding Fermi surface are depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig:438_normal}.

