\section{\label{sec1}Introduction}
Quantum simulation~\cite{feynman1982,lloyd1996universal} is widely regarded as one of the most promising applications of quantum information processors, as it offers the possibility of efficiently reproducing the dynamics of many-body quantum systems that are intractable on classical computers~\cite{RevModPhys.86.153,PhysRevLett.98.070201}. Finding the time evolution of a general unitary operator is an important task in quantum simulation which is used to implement the quantum circuit of a physical Hamiltonian. There are different ways to study this aspect, such as employed in imaginary time evolution~\cite{PRXQuantum.2.010342}, Suzuki–Trotterization~\cite{trotter1959product,PhysRevX.11.011020} and variational method~\cite{mcardle2019variational,cirstoiu2020variational,kalev2021quantum}. These methods are approximate in a usual sense as these involve either truncating the series expansion of the unitary operator~\cite{PhysRevLett.114.090502,ostmeyer2023optimised} or approximating the exponential term by suitable measurement operators~\cite{PhysRevLett.131.110602}. In most digital quantum simulation approaches, the target Hamiltonian is decomposed into a sum of noncommuting terms~\cite{suzuki1985decomposition}, and the corresponding time-evolution operator is approximated using Suzuki–Trotter product formulas~\cite{PhysRevX.11.011020,dhand2014stability}. While Trotterization provides a systematic route towards controllable approximations, its accuracy depends on the number of Trotter steps~\cite{ostmeyer2023optimised}, causing circuit depth and gate count to increase with simulation time and desired precision~\cite{lane2025ordering}. Consequently, Trotter errors accumulate during long-time evolution~\cite{chen2026trotter}, and the large number of gates required often exceeds the coherence times of current noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) devices~\cite{Preskill2018quantumcomputingin}.

Recent efforts have therefore focused on developing alternative Hamiltonian simulation strategies capable of reducing the circuit depth while preserving accuracy~\cite{PhysRevLett.129.070501,PhysRevA.105.032420}.
For instance, one can use the certain symmetries of the qubit Hamitonian~\cite{d2006decompositions,daugli2008general} and simplify the unitary evolution based on this symmetry Lie group~\cite{carter1995lectures}.
The method is based on Lie-algebraic methods based on Cartan decomposition~\cite{khaneja2001cartan,earp2005constructive} that separates the dynamical Lie algebra generated by a Hamiltonian into a compact subalgebra and its complementary subspace, enabling the construction of exact or near-exact factorizations of the corresponding unitary evolution~\cite{khaneja2001cartan,d2006decompositions}. In quantum computing, Cartan decompositions have played a central role in quantum circuit synthesis~\cite{PhysRevA.105.032420}, quantum compilation~\cite{alsheikh2025redcard}, and optimal gate construction~\cite{PhysRevLett.129.070501} by expressing a unitary transformation as a sequence of local operations surrounding a reduced set of nonlocal generators. %Such decompositions exploit the underlying algebraic structure of the Hamiltonian rather than relying on small-time approximations, thereby providing a fundamentally different route to quantum simulation.

The advantage of using Cartan decomposition based Hamiltonian simulation  is accounted for by the fact that it generates a fixed-depth quantum circuit that is independent of the simulation time~\cite{PhysRevLett.129.070501} in contrast to Suzuki–Trotter approach, where the circuit depth scales with the evolution time~\cite{chen2026trotter}. Moreover, such algebraic Cartan constructions generate exact simulation circuits with substantially fewer gates~\cite{PhysRevLett.129.070501,PhysRevA.105.032420} and significantly improved accuracy compared with product formula methods for broad classes of spin and fermionic models~\cite{l88j-p4f3}. %the number of required gates to implement optimized unitary evolution is minimal as compared to other approximation order . Kökcü *et al.* demonstrated that ,while simultaneously providing deeper insight into the geometric structure of the underlying dynamics. 
These developments suggest that Cartan-based approaches constitute a promising route toward resource-efficient quantum simulation on near-term and fault-tolerant quantum architectures~\cite{PhysRevA.108.052607}.

In this work, we generalize the Cartan decomposition method to the system of collective spin ensembles, where the dynamics is naturally restricted to a permutation symmetric subspace of identical atoms~\cite{PhysRevA.78.052101}. Ensemble-based implementations offer an attractive platform for quantum information processing because collective spin degrees of freedom enable an exponential reduction of the Hilbert-space dimension~\cite{moroder2012permutationally} while preserving many-body correlations and entanglement properties~\cite{PhysRevA.88.012305}. Various research works have been performed on the implementation of unitary circuits with the qubit spin Hamiltonian~\cite{PhysRevLett.129.070501,PhysRevA.105.032420,PhysRevA.108.052607}, but none of the work has been performed on permutation symmetric manifolds. By constructing the dynamical Lie algebra from collective spin operators and identifying the corresponding Cartan subalgebra, we derive compact circuit representations of the target dynamics in terms of experimentally accessible collective spin operations with an equivalent qutid picture. We further compare the resulting Cartan circuits with conventional Trotterized evolutions to estimate the reduced circuit complexity. Our results establish a systematic framework for Lie-algebraic circuit synthesis in collective quantum systems and highlight the advantages of ensemble-based quantum simulation for scalable quantum technologies.



The paper is structured as follows.  In Sec. \ref{sec2} we review the 
Cartan decomposition method of a Lie algebra and introduce the physical system to study the time evolution of the collective spin Hamiltonian. In Sec. \ref{sec3} we develop the formalism to perform the Cartan decomposition of the Lie algebra based on collective spin operators in permutationally invariant space and introduce ensemble based gates and optimize circuit construction. In Sec. \ref{sec4} we check the numerical performance of the method by calculating spin expectation values, error with the evolution time as a function of different system sizes, and compare it with the standard Suzuki Trotter method for the spin squeezing Hamiltonian dynamics. In Sec. \ref{sec5}, we provide a possible experimental implementation to build circuit elements based on atomic ensembles. Finally in Sec. \ref{sec6}, we summarize the main conclusion of our work.  
























\section{Theoretical Elements} 
\label{sec2}

In this section, we first review the theory that is required to construct the Cartan decomposition method for the permutation symmetric systems. For this, we introduce the spin system and its structure that obeys a symmetry under the exchange of qubits. Then we discuss the principle behind Cartan decomposition method~\cite{d2006decompositions,drury2008constructive} that is used to split a spin Hamiltonian Lie algebra into two orthogonal subspaces. This provides an efficient way of building the time evolution of a unitary corresponding to the spin Hamiltonian.



\subsection{Spin Hamiltonian and permutation symmetry}
The physical system we shall consider consists of an assembly of identical spin-$1/2$ qubits such that the system is symmetric under the exchange of the particles (Fig.~\ref{fig0}) and the individual control of the particles is not necessary to consider. This is easily realized for neutral atomic ensembles, or BECs, where each atom has two populated internal states~\cite{timquantumoptics2020}. If $g_l,e_l$ represents the annihilation operators for the ground state and the excited states in the $l^{\text{th}}$ ensemble respectively, then the collective spin can be defined using the Schwinger boson operators as
\begin{align}
   S^x_l & = e^\dagger_l g_l + g^\dagger_l e_l \nonumber \\
    S^y_l & = -ie^\dagger_l g_l + i g^\dagger_l e_l \nonumber \\
     S^z_l & = e^\dagger_l e_l - g^\dagger_l g_l 
\end{align}

Since the system is symmetric under particle exchange, one defines a symmetric operator 
\begin{align}
    P & = P^{(1,2\dots n)}_{1} \otimes P^{(1,2\dots n)}_{2} \otimes \dots \nonumber \\  
    & = \prod_l P^{(1,2\dots n)}_{l}
    \label{eq:perm_symm}
\end{align}
where $P_l$ is the permutation group consisting of the set of operators that exchange the particle label in the $l^{\text{th}}$ ensemble. 

With this, the collective spin operators can also be described in terms of the sum of the individual Pauli operators for the atoms as
\begin{align}
    S^\alpha_l & =  \sum_{n=1}^N \sigma^\alpha_{l,n} 
     \label{eq:spinopsensemble}
\end{align}
where $ \alpha \in \{ x,y,z \} $ and $ \sigma^\alpha_{l,n} $ is a Pauli operator along the $ \alpha $ direction for the $n^{\text{th}}$ atom in the $l^{\text{th}}$ ensemble. %We note that $(S^\alpha_l)^2 \neq I $ in contrast to the Pauli operators $(\sigma^\alpha_{l,n})^2 = I$.
We consider the number of atoms $N$ in each ensemble to be equal for simplicity. 
The commutation relation for the spin operators follows
\begin{align}
[S^\alpha_l,S^\beta_m]=2i\epsilon_{\alpha\beta\gamma} S^\gamma \delta_{lm},   \label{commutator}
\end{align}
where $\epsilon_{\alpha\beta\gamma}$ is the Levi-Civita symbol.

Spin operators~\eqref{eq:spinopsensemble} remain invariant under the action of the symmetric operator~\eqref{eq:perm_symm}, i.e.
\begin{align}
     P S^\alpha_l P^\dagger = S^\alpha_l
\end{align}
The spin coherent states for $N$ uncorrelated atoms in an ensemble is defined as
\begin{align}
    |\theta, \phi\rangle\rangle_l & =  \frac{1}{\sqrt{N!}} \left(\cos \frac{\theta}{2} e^{-i\phi/2 }e^\dagger_l  + \sin \frac{\theta}{2} e^{i\phi/2 }g^\dagger_l \right)^N |\text{vac}\rangle \nonumber \\
    & = \sum_k c_k |k_l\rangle
    \label{spincoherentstate}
\end{align}
where $\theta, \phi$ are the angles on the Bloch sphere; $|\text{vac}\rangle$ is the vacuum state that contains no atoms and $c_k$ is the amplitude coefficient of the state when it is written in terms of the Dicke state $|k_l\rangle$ defined as,
\begin{align}
   |k_l\rangle = \frac{(e^\dagger_l)^k(g^\dagger_l)^{N-k}}{\sqrt{k!(N-k)!}} |\text{vac}\rangle
   \label{basisstate}
\end{align}
such that the Dicke states are eigenstates of the $S^z$ spin operator
\begin{align}
    S^z_l |k_l\rangle = (2k-N) |k_l\rangle.
\end{align}
It then follows that if the initial state is symmetric under particle interchange and only symmetric operators are used to manipulate the state, the state remains in the symmetric subspace \cite{timquantumoptics2020}. 

\begin{figure}[t]%
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1.pdf}
\caption{Permutation symmetric system of $l$ interacting atomic ensembles: Each particle in an ensemble interacts with other particles in an identical way denoted by dashed lines. Neighboring ensembles are coupled through identical interactions~\eqref{ham_ensemble} denoted by arrows.}
\label{fig0}
\end{figure}


We can construct the physical spin Hamiltonian with the possible combination of the spin operators $S^\alpha_l$ that follows the permutation symmetry~\eqref{eq:perm_symm}, the most common choice is 
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{H} = \sum_j \xi_j \mathcal{S}_j
    \label{genHam}
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{S}_j$ is the string of the collective spin operators, i.e. elements of the $l$-ensemble point group $\mathcal O_l = \{I,S^x,S^y,S^z\}^{\otimes l}$  and $\xi_j$ is the interaction coefficient. 
For instance, the spin squeezing interaction between two ensembles~\cite{kitzinger2020} is the product of two spin operators, i.e. it involves quadratic terms $\mathcal{S}_2 = S^\alpha_l S^\alpha_{l+1}$. Fig.~\ref{fig0} represents the schematic diagram of the interacting ensemble systems.

Finding the unitary $e^{-i \mathcal{H}t}$ for a particular spin Hamiltonian and its realization towards an optimized quantum circuit is an interesting problem. The usual Suzuki trotterization method can be used that involves approximating the series expansion, however,  it is less efficient in the sense that it generates the circuit depth that depends on the simulation time. We will be using the Cartan decomposition method to construct this unitary.  













\subsection{Cartan decomposition of a Lie algebra}
Here we review the formalism for decomposing the Hamiltonian algebra into two orthogonal components that was proposed in detail in Refs. \cite{carter1995lectures,d2006decompositions,drury2008constructive}. 

In order to implement optimized circuit of a particular Hamiltonian time evolution, we use its underlying Lie algebraic structure.
Let $\mathfrak{g}$ be a real semi simple real algebra associated with $\mathcal{H}$. The Cartan decomposition expresses $\mathfrak{g}$ as
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{l} \oplus \mathfrak{m}
\label{cartan decomposition}
\end{equation}
where $\mathfrak{l}$ is the maximal compact subalgebra, i.e., a subspace that closes under commutation operation and it forms a Lie subalgebra and $\mathfrak{m}$ is its complementary subspace, which is not a Lie algebra and  does not close under commutation. This partition is performed by the involution function $\theta$ defined as,
\begin{equation}
    \theta (g) = -g^{T}
    \label{eq:involution}
\end{equation}
for any $g \in \mathfrak{g} $. However this choice is not unique and alternative involutions can be constructed by selecting an appropriate unitary transformation~\cite{drury2008constructive}. \newline
The action of the involution~\eqref{eq:involution} partitions the Lie algebra into two complementary subspaces, corresponding to the eigenspaces associated with the positive and negative eigenvalues, respectively with the following criterion,
\begin{align}
    \theta(\mathfrak{l}) & = \mathfrak{l} \nonumber \\
    \theta(\mathfrak{m}) & = -\mathfrak{m}
    \label{eq:orthogonalsplit}
\end{align}

Moreover, elements of this decomposition satisfy the following Lie bracket relations,
\begin{align}
[\mathfrak{l},\mathfrak{l}] & \subseteq \mathfrak{l} \nonumber \\
[\mathfrak{l},\mathfrak{m}] & \subseteq \mathfrak{m} \nonumber \\
[\mathfrak{m},\mathfrak{m}] & \subseteq \mathfrak{l} 
\label{commutationrelations}
\end{align}
A set of mutually commuting elements can be selected from $\mathfrak{m}$ that forms a maximal abelian subalgebra such that $\mathfrak{h} \subseteq \mathfrak{m}$. The resulting Cartan decomposition of the Lie algbera $\mathfrak{g}$ and the new subspaces is shown schematically in Fig. \ref{fig2}.

With this construction, the Cartan decomposition theorem~\cite{carter1995lectures,d2006decompositions} confirms that there exists an element
\(K\in \mathfrak{l}\) and a Cartan element
\(h\in\mathfrak{h}\) such that an element $m \in \mathfrak{m}$ can be expressed as, 
\begin{equation}
    m = K h K^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
In practice, the unitary transformation \(K\) is constructed from
the generators \(k_j\in\mathfrak{l}\),
\begin{align}
    K(\boldsymbol{\eta}) = \prod_j e^{i\eta_j k_j},
\end{align}
where the coefficients \(\boldsymbol{\eta}=\{\eta_j\}\) are determined variationally by maximizing the Hilbert--Schmidt overlap between the rotated Hamiltonian and the chosen Cartan subalgebra, i.e.,
\begin{align}
    \boldsymbol{\eta}_\text{opt} =
   \max_{\boldsymbol{\eta}} \mathrm{Tr} \left[h K^\dagger(\boldsymbol{\eta})
    \mathcal{H} K(\boldsymbol{\eta})
    \right].
    \label{eq:trace_optimization}
\end{align}
The resulting optimal transformation identifies the Cartan frame in which the Hamiltonian is maximally aligned with the given Hamiltonian.

Choosing the physical Hamiltonian \(\mathcal{H}\in\mathfrak{m}\),
one obtains
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{H}^c = K h K^\dagger ,
    \label{cartandecom_ham}
\end{align}
which yields the Cartan time-evolution operator for the Hamiltonian as 
\begin{align}
    U(t) = e^{-i\mathcal{H}^ct} = K e^{-iht} K^\dagger .
    \label{unitary}
\end{align}
where $t$ is the dimensionless time.
Since all generators belonging to the Cartan subalgebra $\mathfrak{h}$ commute mutually, the implementation of $e^{-iht}$ reduces to a set of commuting rotations that can be
efficiently realized as a shallow quantum circuit. It is observed that the complexity of simulating the original interacting Hamiltonian is transferred to the determination of the optimal Cartan transformation $K$, while the dynamical evolution within the Cartan sector remains analytically tractable and experimentally efficient.









