\section{Cartan Decomposition in the Permutationally invariant subspace}
\label{sec3}
In this section, we generalize the idea of Cartan decomposition method to the set of the collective spin operators and use it to simulate the Hamiltonian expressed in (\ref{genHam}) for atomic ensembles obeying permutation symmetry. 

To begin with, we construct the Hamiltonian Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}_\text{ens}$ associated with the ensemble Hamiltonian~\eqref{genHam} by recursively evaluating all possible commutators among the Hamiltonian terms and the operators subsequently generated from them. The procedure is continued until \textit{algebraic closure} is reached, resulting in the full Hamiltonian Lie algebra as
\begin{align}
    \mathfrak{g}_\text{ens} = \text{span}\{\mathcal{S}_j,[\mathcal{S}_j,\mathcal{S}_{j'}],[\mathcal{S}_j,[\mathcal{S}_{j'},\mathcal{S}_{j''}]],\dots\}
    \label{eq:Ham algebra}
\end{align}
The resulting Lie algebra~\eqref{eq:Ham algebra} encapsulates the symmetry structure and controllable dynamics of the system and serves as the starting point for the Cartan decomposition of the associated unitary evolution.

Permutation symmetry plays a crucial role in reducing the size of the dynamical Lie algebra~\cite{PhysRevA.78.052101}, thereby simplifying the resulting circuit construction based on collective spin degrees of freedom. The key simplification arises from the fact that symmetry decomposes the full Hilbert space into irreducible collective-spin sectors~\cite{thompson2008angular}. Specifically, under the joint action of $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ and the permutation group~\eqref{eq:perm_symm}, one obtains
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{H} = \underset{s}{\oplus} (\mathcal{H}_s \otimes \mathcal{M}_s)
\end{align}
where the total spin $s$ assumes values $0,\frac{1}{2}, 1, \dots, \frac{N}{2}$ arising from the allowed addition rules for the spin angular momentum. The part $\mathcal{H}_s$ carries an irreducible spin-$s$ representation with dimension $2s+1$, while the part $\mathcal{M}_s$ is a multiplicity space carrying permutation symmetry~\cite{moroder2012permutationally}. 

The symmetric part of the Hamiltonian corresponds to the space with Dicke basis in the maximal spin length $s=\frac{N}{2}$ with unit multiplicity such that 
\begin{align}
  \mathcal{H} =  P \mathcal{H} P^\dagger
  \label{Ham_invariance}
\end{align}
satisfying 
\begin{align}
    [\mathcal{H} , P] = 0
\end{align}
With this, the full symmetric operator algebra~\eqref{eq:Ham algebra} consists of an irreducible tensor operator of rank $q$ under global $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ rotation as,
\begin{align}
   \mathfrak{g}_\text{ens}= \overset{N}{\underset{q=0}{\oplus}} \mathcal{T}^{(q)}
   \label{permutation_liealgebra}
\end{align}
each sector with dimension $\text{dim}(\mathcal{T}^{(q)})=2q+1$, where $\mathcal T^{(0)}$ corresponds to the scalar sector comprising rotation invariant spin terms of the form $\vec S_l.\vec S_m$, $\mathcal T^{(1)}$ to the collective-spin generators~\eqref{eq:spinopsensemble}, and $\mathcal T^{(2)}$ to bilinear product of the spin operators with terms $S^\alpha_l S^\beta_m$, and similarly the higher-rank sectors describe increasingly nonlinear collective correlations.

Symmetry ensures that any commutator algebra of this tensor structure is always closed and lies in the symmetric space, i.e.,
\begin{align}
    [\mathcal{T}^{(q_1)}, \mathcal{T}^{(q_2)}] \subseteq \overset{q_1+q_2}{\underset{q'=|q_1-q_2|} {\oplus}} \mathcal{T}^{(q')}
    \label{eq:alg_closure}
\end{align}
that is identical to the familiar $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ algebraic structure~\eqref{commutator}.


This is in contrast to calculating the required spin Hamiltonian algebra~\eqref{eq:Ham algebra} for a system of distinct spins ($\propto  \sum_{n=1}^N \sigma^\alpha_{l,n} $) with increased algebraic complexity, which, in general, increases rapidly with $N$ as shown in the Appendix~\ref{appendix_1}. The lack of permutation symmetry leads to a much larger operator space and a more involved Cartan decomposition procedure. For instance, for a generic local controls and interactions the associated dynamical Lie algebra can grow to $\mathfrak{su}(2^N)$ with dimension $4^N-1$. In contrast, for permutation-symmetric collective-spin systems, the dynamics can be restricted to the symmetric subspace, whose Hilbert-space dimension scales only as O$(N+1)$, providing a substantially more compact representation of the system dynamics (Appendix~\ref{appendix_1}).

\subsection{Example: Two ensemble based interaction} 
We consider the two-axis spin-squeezing interaction between two atomic ensembles, which has previously been investigated to generate spin-squeezed entangled states~\cite{kitzinger2020}. Here, we employ the Cartan decomposition to find exact Lie-algebraic structure of its dynamics, time evolution operator and propose a fully optimized quantum circuit based on the ensemble gates. 
The squeezing Hamiltonian is defined as,
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{H}_{\text{ens}} & =  S^x_1 S^x_2 +  S^y_1 S^y_2  \nonumber \\
    & = \frac{1}{2} (S^+_1 S^-_2 + S^-_1 S^+_2) \nonumber \\
    & \in \{ \mathcal{T}^{(0)}, \mathcal{T}^{(2)} \}
    \label{ham_ensemble}
\end{align}
where $S^{\pm}_l = S^x_l \pm i S^y_l$ are the raising and lowering spin operators for $l^{\text{th}}$ ensemble.

The Hamiltonian Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g}_{\text{ens}}$ is obtained by computing the commutator of the various spin terms~\eqref{eq:Ham algebra} for the various spin sectors satisfying~\eqref{eq:alg_closure}.
At the first level, the exact expression for the commutator between $C_1$ and $C_2$ is calculated  as
\begin{align}
    [C_1,C_2] & =  \frac{i}{2} \Bigg[ M\left( J_2(J_2+1)-J_1(J_1+1) \right )+ \nonumber \\ 
   & \left(J_1(J_1+1) + J_2(J_2+1) + \frac{M^2}{2}\right)D - \frac{D^3}{2}\Bigg]  
   \label{eq:commutation_C1C2}
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
    C_1 & = \frac{1}{2} (S^+_1 S^-_2 + S^-_1 S^+_2) \in  \mathcal{T}^{(0)} \oplus \mathcal{T}^{(2)} \nonumber \\
    C_2 & = \frac{1}{2i} (S^+_1 S^-_2 - S^-_1 S^+_2) \in \mathcal{T}^{(1)} \nonumber \\
    D & = S^z_1 - S^z_2 \in \mathcal{T}^{(1)}
\end{align}
and $M=S^z_1+S^z_2$ is the total spin angular momentum of two ensembles. $J_1$ and $J_2$ are the spin angular momentum numbers for the first and second ensembles respectively.

It is easy to show that,
\begin{align}
    [M,C_1]= [M,C_2] = 0
\end{align}
Hence, apart from constant contributions and mutually commuting terms that do not generate new elements under commutation, the nontrivial generators of the dynamical Lie algebra ($\mathfrak{g}_{\rm ens}$) arise from the operator structures appearing in Eq.~\eqref{eq:commutation_C1C2}.
\begin{align}
   [C_1,C_2] & = a_1 D + a_2D^3 \equiv f_o(D) \nonumber \\
   & \in \mathcal{T}^{(1)} \oplus \mathcal{T}^{(3)} 
\end{align}
where $a_1,a_2$ are the constant values for a given $N$ and the operator function $f_o(D)$ represents the \textit{odd} polynomial powers in $D$.

At next level, the contribution of the first and third order term in $D$ evaluates to
\begin{align}
[C_1,D] & =  -2iC_2 \nonumber \\
    [C_2,D] & =  2iC_1  \nonumber \\
[C_1,D^3] & =  -2i(C_2 D^2 + DC_2D + D^2 C_2 ) \nonumber \\
    [C_2,D^3] & = 2i(C_1 D^2 + DC_1D + D^2 C_1 )
\end{align}
introducing new terms of the product of the polynomial powers $f(D)$ with $C_1$ and $C_2$.

Hence, the full commutation algebra $\mathfrak{g}_{\text{ens}}$ is calculated as
\begin{align}
  \{ C_1, C_2, f_{e,o}(D),  F_{e,o;1,2}(D)\}
\label{Ham_algbera_ensemble}  
\end{align}
where $f_{e,o}(D)$ are the functions of even or odd powers of $D$ respectively
and the symmetrized product between $f_{e,o}(D)$ with $C_1$ and $C_2$ is defined by
\begin{align}
   F_{e,1}(D) & =  \frac{f_{e}(D) C_{1} + C_{1} f_{e}(D)}{2} \nonumber \\
   F_{o,1}(D) & =  \frac{f_{o}(D) C_{1} + C_{1} f_{o}(D)}{2}  \nonumber\\
   F_{e,2}(D) & =  \frac{f_{e}(D) C_{2} + C_{2} f_{e}(D)}{2} \nonumber \\
   F_{o,2}(D) & =  \frac{f_{o}(D) C_{2} + C_{2} f_{o}(D)}{2} 
\end{align}




\subsection{Optimized unitary}
To construct an efficient Cartan decomposition of the ensemble Hamiltonian, we introduce the involution function as
\begin{align}
    \theta_{\text{ens}}(g) = -AgA^\dagger
    \label{inv_ensemble}
\end{align}
where $A$ denotes the swap operator between two systems $1 \leftrightarrow 2$ and $g \in \mathfrak{g}_{\text{ens}}$. The action of~\eqref{inv_ensemble} partitions the dynamical Lie algebra~\eqref{Ham_algbera_ensemble} into the Cartan pair
\begin{align}
    \mathfrak{g}_{\text{ens}} = \mathfrak{l}_{\text{ens}} \oplus \mathfrak{m}_{\text{ens}}
\end{align}
The corresponding subspaces are spanned by
\begin{align}
    \mathfrak{l}_{\text{ens}} & = \{C_2, f_o(D),  F_{e,2}(D), F_{o,1}(D)   \} \nonumber \\
    \mathfrak{m}_{\text{ens}} & = \{C_1,f_e(D), F_{e,1}(D), F_{o,2}(D)   \} 
    \label{cartan_gen_ensemble}
\end{align}
satisfying the standard symmetric-space relations~\eqref{commutationrelations}
The maximal abelian group $\mathfrak{h}_{\text{ens}}\in\mathfrak{m}_{\text{ens}}$  is chosen as
\begin{align}
   \mathfrak{h}_{\text{ens}} = \{C_1 \} 
   \label{hensemble}
\end{align}
Consequently, the ensemble Hamiltonian based on the Cartan construction can be expressed as~\eqref{cartandecom_ham}
\begin{align}
\mathcal{H}^c_{\mathrm{ens}} =
K_{\mathrm{ens}}
 h 
K_{\mathrm{ens}}^\dagger ,
\qquad
h\in\mathfrak{h}_{\mathrm{ens}}, 
\label{cartanhamiltonian}
\end{align}
where
\begin{align}
K_{\mathrm{ens}} =
\prod_j e^{i\eta_j k_j},
\qquad
\forall k_j\in\mathfrak{l}_{\mathrm{ens}}.
\label{kensemble}
\end{align}
The coefficients $\{\eta_j\}$ are determined variationally by maximizing the overlap between the rotated Hamiltonian and the Cartan subalgebra~\eqref{eq:trace_optimization}. For the present ensemble model with $N=3$, the optimization yields
\begin{align}
    (\eta_1, \eta_2,\eta_3, \eta_4) = \left(0, 0, -\frac{\pi}{11}, \frac{\pi}{7}  \right)
\end{align}

