\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig4.pdf}
\caption{Ensemble based optimized circuit implementation of the interaction Hamiltonian~\eqref{ham_ensemble}: (a) Ensemble as a symmetric Qutid: An ensemble of $N$ identical spins restricted to the fully symmetric Dicke manifold is represented as an effective $(N+1)$-dimensional collective qudit register. The Dicke basis states encode the collective excitation sectors of the ensemble; (b) Various ensemble gates in the collective-qudit encoding: the Dicke-state mapping~\eqref{Dicke basis} enables a circuit realization for various collective gates. We consider collective Phase gate, $\Phi_D(\theta)$, Entangling gate $\Phi_{E,\tilde{E}}(t)$ and controlled Entangling gate $\Phi_{F_{e,o;j}}(\theta)$. Action of these gates on Dicke basis is defined in Eqs.~\eqref{phasegate_ensemble},~\eqref{entanglinggate_ensemble},~\eqref{controlled_entanglinggate_ensemble} respectively; (c) Implementation of the unitary circuit~\eqref{unitary_cartan} for the collective interaction Hamiltonian~\eqref{ham_ensemble} using Cartan decomposition method where the circuit is decomposed into layers $K_\text{ens},h$ and $K^\dagger_\text{ens}$ using ensemble based gates.}
\label{fig4}
\end{figure*}

The resulting Cartan circuit realizes the time-evolution operator of the ensemble Hamiltonian in the form
\begin{align}
 U_{\text{Cartan}}(t) =  \left(\prod_j e^{i \eta_j k_j}\right) e^{-iht} \left(\prod_j e^{i \eta_j k_j}\right)^\dagger 
   \label{unitary_cartan}
\end{align}
thereby reducing the simulation of the full interacting Hamiltonian to evolution within the Abelian sector $\mathfrak{h}_{\text{ens}}$.
To check the accuracy of this construction, we compare it with the exact evolution operator defined as 
\begin{align}
    U_{\mathrm{Exact}}(t) = e^{-i\mathcal{H}_\text{ens}t}
    \label{unitary_exact}
\end{align}
as well as with Suzuki Trotterized approximation 
\begin{align}
    U_{\text{Trotter}}(t) = (e^{-iS^x_1S^x_2 \delta t}e^{-iS^y_1S^y_2 \delta t})^{n_{t}}
    \label{unitary_suzukitrotter}
\end{align}
where $\delta t = t/n_t$ and $n_t$ denotes the number of Trotter steps.
This comparison verifies the accuracy and efficiency of the Cartan-based implementation compared to conventional digital simulation protocols.



\subsection{Ensemble based circuit implementation}
Implementing unitary~\eqref{unitary_cartan} to an equivalent quantum circuit requires ensemble based gates. In this section, we provide a proposal for implementing basic circuit elements employing permutation symmetric systems.

An effective way to build this is to use symmetry arguments, as each ensemble of $N$ spin-$1/2$ particles is restricted to the fully symmetric Dicke manifold of dimension $N+1$, thus defining an effective collective qudit as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig4}(a). 
Then, collective-spin operators $S^\alpha_l$ act as logical generators on this encoded qudit space.

For instance, the collective angular momentum eigenstate $|J,M_J\rangle$ constitutes an effective basis for a logical qutid such that
\begin{align}
    S^z |J=N/2,M_J\rangle = M_J |J=N/2,M_J\rangle
\end{align}
where $M_J$ takes values between $[-J,J]$ in integral steps and represents the orientation of the total spin quantum number $J$ along the $z$ direction.

The mapping to the symmetric qudit is evident as it defines a basis
\begin{align}
    |k\rangle_L = \left|J=\frac{N}{2},M_J=k-\frac{N}{2}\right\rangle
    \label{Dicke basis}
\end{align}
with dimension $N+1$.
For example, with $N=2$, the symmetric Dicke basis 
\begin{align}
    |0\rangle_L & \equiv | J=1, M_J=-1\rangle \nonumber \\
    |1\rangle_L & \equiv | J=1, M_J=0\rangle \nonumber \\
    |2\rangle_L & \equiv | J=1, M_J=1\rangle 
\end{align}
behaves exactly like a logical qutrit. In terms of circuit terminology, an ensemble can be viewed as a \textit{physical unit} representing a logical $(N+1)$-level register (qutid).

With this notation, we can easily understand the action of the important gates as
\begin{align}
    D |k_1,k_2\rangle_L &= 2(k_1-k_2) |k_1,k_2\rangle_L \nonumber \\
   e^{-i D \theta} |k_1,k_2\rangle_L & = e^{-2i (k_1-k_2) \theta} |k_1,k_2\rangle_L
    \label{phasegate_ensemble}
\end{align}
$e^{-i D \theta}$ is a collective \textit{phase gate} that gives information about the relative phases between collective qutids. In the ensemble circuit representation, we denote this gate by $\Phi_D(\theta)$ as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig4}(b).

Similarly, the action of the term $S^+_1S^-_2$ is understood as transferring the population among different Dicke levels,  
\begin{align}
    S^+_1S^-_2 |k_1,k_2\rangle_L & =  |k_1+1,k_2-1\rangle_L 
\end{align}
Hence, the operator $C_{1}$ acts as,
\begin{align}
    C_{1} |k_1,k_2\rangle_L & \propto   |k_1+1,k_2-1\rangle_L +  |k_1-1,k_2+1\rangle_L 
     \label{entanglinggate_ensemble}
\end{align}
that creates a superposition of different excitation partitions generating entanglement. Therefore, time evolution $e^{-iC_1t}$ is an \textit{entangling gate} that acts on the Dicke-register space by coherently redistributing collective excitations between ensembles. This gate is denoted by $\Phi_E(t)$ as shown in the Fig.~\ref{fig4}(b).

Using the Holstein Primakoff approximation, the collective spin operators map in terms of two bosonic modes $a,b$ as
$S^+_{1} (S^-_{1})\to a^\dagger(a)$ and $S^+_{2} (S^-_{2})\to b^\dagger(b)$.
Hence, it follows that 
\begin{align}
    C_1^{\text{HP}}  & \sim   (a^\dagger b + b^\dagger a)
\end{align}
is effectively similar to a bosonic beam splitter transformation~\cite{PhysRevResearch.4.023054}.
The same interpretation follows for the action of the operator $C_2$ and we represent it schematically as $\Phi_{\tilde{E}}(t)$ in Fig.~\ref{fig4}(b). 

The action of the product operator $e^{-iF_{e,o;j}}$ represents the combined action of the collective phase gate and the entangling gate accordingly. For instance 
\begin{align}
    D C_1 |k_1,k_2\rangle_L  \propto & (k_1-k_2+2)  |k_1+1,k_2-1\rangle_L  \nonumber \\ & + (k_1-k_2-2)   |k_1-1,k_2+1\rangle_L 
    \label{controlled_entanglinggate_ensemble}
\end{align}
In a circuit picture, $e^{-i D C_1 t}$ can be viewed as a \textit{controlled entangling gate}, where the exchange strength is conditioned on the relative phase of the qutids set by the action of the operator $D$. The similar interpretation follows for the higher power products $f_{\text{e,o}}(D)$ with $C_1(C_2)$ respectively. We choose it to represent schematically as $\Phi_{F_{e,o;j}}(\theta)$ while using in the circuit in Fig.~\ref{fig4}(b). The experimental realization of these gates has been discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec5}.

With these definitions, we construct the optimized quantum circuit to realize the unitary evolution of the permutation symmetric spin Hamiltonian~\eqref{ham_ensemble} in Fig.~\ref{fig4}(c).




\section{Numerical Performance}
\label{sec4}
In this section, we evaluate the numerical performance of the optimized unitary~\eqref{unitary_cartan} constructed using the Cartan decomposition technique and calculate the time evolution of physical quantities such as the spin expectation values and variances of the conjugate spin components. We compare the time evolution of the system against the exact unitary~\eqref{unitary_exact} and the approximate unitary using Suzuki Trotter~\eqref{unitary_suzukitrotter} method. 






\subsection{Average spin expectation values and variances}
The average spin expectation values are expressed as,
\begin{align}
    \langle S^\alpha_l(t)\rangle = \frac{\langle \psi(t)|S^\alpha_l|\psi(t)\rangle}{\langle \psi(t)|\psi(t)\rangle}
    \label{spin_expect}
\end{align}
where the time evolved wavefunction of the system through unitary $U(t)$ is 
\begin{align}
    |\psi(t)\rangle = U(t) |\psi(0)\rangle
\end{align}
and $|\psi(0)\rangle$ is the initial wavefunction of the ensemble~\eqref{spincoherentstate} and is written in terms of the Dicke basis states~\eqref{Dicke basis}
\begin{align}
    |\psi(0)\rangle = \sum_{k_1,k_2} c_{k_1,k_2} |k_1,k_2\rangle_L
\end{align}
where $c_{k_1,k_2}$ are the amplitude coefficients of the state such that
\begin{align}
    \sum_{k_1,k_2} |c_{k_1,k_2}|^2 =1
\end{align}
Fig.~\ref{fig5}(a) calculates the time evolution of the collective spin expectation values~\eqref{spin_expect} obtained from the exact dynamics~\eqref{unitary_exact} generated by the Hamiltonian and compares with the Cartan decomposition evolution~\eqref{unitary_cartan} and Suzuki Trotter evolution~\eqref{unitary_suzukitrotter}. It is observed that the exact dynamics (solid lines) exhibit the coherent transfer of spin polarization between the two ensembles. For a given spin polarized initial state~\eqref{spincoherentstate}, $S^x_l$ spin expectation value undergoes large-amplitude oscillations, while $S^z_l$ develops a substantial negative value and $S^y_l$ remains comparatively small throughout the evolution, indicating that the dynamics occur primarily within the $x$-$z$ plane of the collective Bloch sphere. The Cartan evolution (points) matches the qualitative structure of the exact time evolution, implying that the chosen Cartan generators~\eqref{cartan_gen_ensemble} give rise to exact dynamics~\eqref{unitary}. On the other hand, the approximate Suzuki unitary~\eqref{unitary_suzukitrotter} shows large deviations in the time evolved spin expectation values, which implies that it does not resemble with the exact system dynamics. For different initial spin polarized state (Fig.~\ref{fig5}(c)), spin dynamics from exact and Cartan unitary are in agreement with each other while Suzuki Trotter shows deviation.

\begin{figure}[t]%
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig5.pdf}
\caption{Time evolution of system dynamics using exact and Cartan unitary: (a),(c) the collective spin expectation values~\eqref{spin_expect};  (b),(d) the variances~\eqref{spin_variances} in the combination of the spin components as a function of the interaction time $t$ for different initial states~\eqref{spincoherentstate} for $N=3$ per ensemble. Solid curves denote the exact quantum evolution, while the dashed curves correspond to the Suzuki Trotter based dynamics. The initial spin polarized state is chosen as $|\frac{\pi}{2},0 \rangle \rangle_1 \otimes |\frac{\pi}{2},0 \rangle \rangle_2$ for (a),(b), while the initial spin polarized state is chosen as $|\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{\pi}{8} \rangle \rangle_1 \otimes |\frac{\pi}{4},\frac{\pi}{8} \rangle \rangle_2$ }
\label{fig5}
\end{figure}


We also plot the variances of a certain combination of spin components as the dynamics generate spin squeezed states as studied in~\cite{kitzinger2020}.
We define the variance in the uncertainty for a spin observable $S^\alpha_l$ as
\begin{align}
    \text{Var}(S^\alpha_l) = \langle (S^{\alpha}_{l})^2\rangle - \langle S^\alpha_l\rangle^2
    \label{spin_variances}
\end{align}
In Fig.~\ref{fig5}(b) it is clearly seen that the variance Var($S^y_1+S^y_2$) is reduced below its initial level and the variance in the conjugate direction is increased Var($S^x_1-S^x_2$) signaling the emergence of spin squeezing as the exact  time evolution redistributes the quantum noise between conjugate collective modes. The Cartan based evolution captures a similar trend in squeezing dynamics in agreement with the exact time evolution. In particular, it matches the timing of the squeezing minima and the corresponding anti-squeezing maxima. However, the Suzuki Trotterization fails to capture these features.
This shows the advantage of Cartan-decomposition-based simulation as it can compress the dynamics into a shallow circuit while still preserving the principal many-body structure of the exact evolution which is true for any initial spin state (Fig.~\ref{fig5}(d)).



\subsection{Error}
We estimate the closeness of the unitary matrix of the Cartan or Suzuki trotter method with the exact unitary evolution matrix as the norm measure
\begin{align}
    \varepsilon_{\text{Cartan,Trotter}}(t) = \frac{|| U_{\text{Exact}}(t)- U_{\text{Cartan,Trotter}}(t)||}{N}
    \label{error}
\end{align}
where $||A||=\sqrt{Tr(A^\dagger A)}$ is the norm value for a matrix $A$. This can also be interpreted as the error measure, as it measures the distance between the approximate evolution operator and the exact unitary evolution generated by the exchange Hamiltonian.

Fig.~\ref{fig6}(a) shows the error value for the Cartan-decomposed evolution and the Suzuki–Trotter approximation. It can be seen that the Cartan error remains zero as it matches the exact time unitary operation and does not involve any approximation.
\begin{align}
    \varepsilon_{\text{Cartan}} = 0
\end{align}
Also, the Cartan error is less than the error in Trotter method throughout the evolution. 
\begin{align}
    \varepsilon_{\text{Cartan}} < \varepsilon_{\text{Trotter}}
\end{align}
The Trotter error grows rapidly  and remains substantially larger, which is accounted for by the fact that it approximates the evolution by repeated products of noncommuting pieces, causing local truncation errors to accumulate during propagation~\cite{chen2026trotter}. We derive the error bound for the Suzuki–Trotter decomposition in Appendix~\ref{appendix_2}. On the other hand,  Cartan based circuit construction preserves the underlying Lie algebraic structure of the Hamiltonian dynamics more faithfully than the product-formula decomposition. This offers a significantly more accurate representation of the system dynamics than the corresponding Trotterized evolution for the same computational complexity. We calculate Cartan error bound arising from the parameter imperfections in Appendix~\ref{appendix_2}.

