\subsection{Scaling with Ensemble size}
We study the dependence of the error averaged over the simulation time as a function of the ensemble size.
The averaged error is defined as,
\begin{align}
    \bar{\varepsilon} =\frac{1}{T}\sum_{j} \varepsilon(t_j)
    \label{eq:timeaverged error}
\end{align}
where $T$ is the total simulation time and the averaging is performed over the sampled evolution times.

Fig.~\ref{fig6}(b) compares the mean error of the Cartan method with that of Suzuki Trotter method as the ensemble size increases. As we have seen the Cartan method generates exact time dynamics for the system, hence it's mean value is also zero and is independent of the ensemble size. 
However, mean error for the Suzuki trotter method has a non zero value and is larger than that of Cartan method. One can see that for small system size, it is relatively low error, however, as the system size increases, the mean error of the Trotter approximation grows rapidly. We study the dependence of the errors on the system size in details in Appendix~\ref{appendix_2}.

This demonstrates that the Cartan generators capture the dominant dynamical directions of the collective-spin manifold more efficiently than a fixed-order product-formula expansion.
The better scaling of the Cartan approach originates from its Lie-algebraic construction. Rather than approximating the dynamics through repeated short-time decompositions, as in Suzuki–Trotter methods, the Cartan circuit incorporates collective generators that directly encode the symmetry-adapted structure of the permutation-invariant spin ensemble. 
Consequently, the required circuit depth remains comparatively compact, while  the essential many-body correlations responsible for collective dynamics and spin squeezing are preserved. 






\section{Experimental Implementation}
\label{sec5}
In this section, we discuss the experimental viability of the physical system and ensemble gate operations in the optimized circuit construction described previously.  A key advantage of the present approach is that it does not require microscopic control or individual addressing of the constituent qubits in an ensemble. Consequently, the protocol is naturally suited to physical platforms composed of permutation-symmetric spin ensembles.
Such system involves the use of hyperfine states of $^{87}$Rb atoms, specifically $F=1,M_F=-1$ and $F=2,M_F=1$, which forms an effective two-level atom system and has been widely used for quantum information processing and quantum metrology~\cite{pezze2018quantum}. Such a system of collection of indistinguishable atoms addressing permutation symmetry can be implemented using either hot or cold atomic ensembles or BECs~\cite{timquantumoptics2020}.  

The circuit construction to implement unitary terms requires only a small set of experimentally accessible operations, namely collective spin rotations, ensemble phase gates~\eqref{phasegate_ensemble}, entangling gates between two ensembles~\eqref{entanglinggate_ensemble}, and controlled entangling operations~\eqref{controlled_entanglinggate_ensemble}. 
Quantum non demolition (QND) measurements is an effective method to determine the relative phases of ensembles without introducing backaction effects~\cite{PhysRevLett.112.233602,kuzmich1998atomic} that shows the feasibility of realizing the ensemble based phase gates~\eqref{phasegate_ensemble}.
Furthermore, QND interactions have been widely employed as an effective method for generating spin squeezing and inducing entanglement generation in atomic ensembles~\cite{julsgaard2001experimental,aristizabal2021quantum,PhysRevA.105.022443,PhysRevA.108.032420}, providing a direct experimental route for realizing the entangling gates required in the optimized Cartan circuit.
In addition, controlled entangling operations~\eqref{controlled_entanglinggate_ensemble} can be employed by combining collective phase shifts with these entangling interactions, allowing the complete gate set required in the circuit construction.

Ideally, the Cartan decomposition exactly reproduces the target unitary evolution within the chosen Lie algebra yielding zero error, however imperfections arising from ensemble gates implementation such as finite measurement efficiency, atom loss, photon loss and spontaneous emission may introduce deviations from the ideal dynamics.
Nevertheless, extensive theoretical and experimental studies have demonstrated that QND based collective operations remain remarkably robust against these dominant decoherence mechanisms, as noted in Refs.~\cite{PhysRevLett.122.163601,gao2023optical,PhysRevA.97.063613,PhysRevLett.111.253001,gao2025photon}.  Overall, we estimate that the proposed framework provides a realistic route towards implementing optimized quantum circuits for collective spin Hamiltonians on existing neutral-atom and atomic-ensemble architectures. 










\section{Summary and Conclusions}
\label{sec6}
In this work, we introduce a Cartan decomposition framework to implement the effective time unitary of the physical Hamiltonian  based on permutation-symmetric spin systems and collective atomic ensembles. By exploiting the reduced-dimensional symmetric subspace, we construct a hierarchy of collective generators that enables the decomposition of ensemble Hamiltonian into experimentally accessible unitary blocks. Unlike conventional Suzuki Trotter method that factorizes the product terms, the present approach takes advantage of the collective spin symmetries to optimize the circuit representations directly in the Dicke manifold. This translates the many-body collective dynamics into efficient quantum circuits while preserving the underlying permutation symmetry associated with the physical system.

