\section{Introduction}

The paradigm of a simple unstable theory is the theory $T_{\mathrm{ind}}$ of the random graph, the motivating example of simple theories \cite{Shelah:1980b,Grossberg-Iovino-Lessmann:2002}.
The random graph has two modes of presentation:
it is the Rado graph, the Fraïssé limit of the finite graphs, and it is the almost sure isomorphism type of a random network of couplings on countably many sites \cite{Rado:1964,Erdos-Renyi:1963}.
Quantum error correction offers a structure of the same kind.
Its errors and checks are Pauli strings, tensor products of Pauli matrices on the individual qubits, and a Pauli string on $n$ qubits is determined, up to a phase, by its label, the vector $(x,z)\in\FF_2^{2n}$ that records which qubits carry a factor $X$ and which carry a factor $Z$.
Two Pauli strings either commute or anticommute, and their labels decide which:
 they anticommute exactly when $\omega(v,w)=1$, where $v$ and $w$ are their labels and $\omega$ is the nondegenerate alternating form on $\FF_2^{2n}$ given by $\omega((x,z),(x',z'))=x\cdot z'+z\cdot x'$.
 We identify each Pauli string with its label;
 the syndrome bit of an error $e$ with respect to a check $g$ is then $\omega(g,e)$.

In \cref{sec:pauli} we show that, with this form, the Pauli group on countably many qubits is the Fraïssé limit of the finite alternating spaces over the two-element field $\FF_2$, and that its theory, like that of the random graph, is simple and unstable.
For both paradigms the proof verifies the axioms of Kim and Pillay for an explicit independence relation.
The Pauli group is thus the counterpart of the random graph in quantum error correction, and in this paper we study the two paradigms side by side.
In \cref{sec:dictionary} we give the dictionary between simplicity theory and quantum error correction:
Clifford encoding circuits implement the homogeneity of the limit,
the logical errors of a code are the undetectable errors whose type over the stabilizer group and the logical operators forks over the stabilizer group,
and the independence theorem is the rule by which the syndromes of independent checks are prescribed separately.
The two paradigms look alike as first-order structures;
they differ, as we prove, once preparations are mixed.

The paper is the third of a series on bipartite observables.
A bipartite observable compares a preparation of Alice with a probe of Bob and returns a number.
In the first paper \cite{Duenez-Iovino:2026a} we showed that the observable is stable, in the sense of Shelah, exactly when its idealized responses are continuous;
in the second \cite{Duenez-Iovino:2026b}, that it is NIP exactly when they are of the first Baire class, and the classification of Rosenthal compacta of Todorčević therefore applies.
The next dividing line of Shelah is simplicity.
Stability and NIP are properties of the topological closure of a family of responses.
Let us notice that simplicity is a property of a different kind:
it governs the joint satisfiability of conditions on an unknown probe, and it fails when one can build a tree of conditions whose branches are consistent while the siblings exclude one another.
In error correction, the unknown probe is the error, and the conditions are statements about its syndrome.

Observables enter in \cref{sec:observables}.
In the Rado observable, Alice and Bob each hold a site of the network, and the observable reports whether the two sites are coupled;
in the Pauli observable, Alice holds a check and Bob an error, and the observable reports whether the two anticommute.
Alice’s mixed preparations are probability measures on her labels;
a single mixed preparation can therefore carry unboundedly many labels, and her response is affine in the mixture.
Bob’s probes are not the same in the two paradigms, as we establish in \cref{lem:rado-probes,prop:channels}:
for the Rado observable they are arbitrary probability measures on sets of neighbors,
and for the Pauli observable they are the Pauli channels, whose idealized pure elements are the Pauli strings of infinite support that carry the noise of a memory on infinitely many qubits.

In \cref{sec:collapse} we prove that, once preparations are mixed, the real-valued theory of every diagonal observable with the independence property, and in particular of both paradigms, has the tree property of the second kind:
a uniform mixture over a finite set of labels, compared with a uniform mixture over half of it, pins a probe down to that half, and two different halves exclude each other.
The tree has witnesses of two kinds:
in \cref{sec:pauli-tp2,sec:free} we find that for the Pauli observable actual preparations suffice, while for the Rado observable only idealized ones do.

\begin{keytheorem}[Main results]\label{thm:main}
  \leavevmode
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\Alph*)},leftmargin=*]
  \item
    The Rado graph and the countable symplectic space over $\FF_2$, which is the Pauli group on countably many qubits modulo phases, are simple and unstable (\cref{thm:rado-simple,thm:symp-simple}).
  \item
    Let a stabilizer code be given, with stabilizer labels $S$ and logical operators $L$.
    An undetectable error is a logical error exactly when its type over $S\cup L$ forks over $S$, and all logical errors have the same type over $S$, which does not fork over the empty set.
     Clifford encoding circuits implement automorphisms of the Fraïssé limit, and the independence theorem is the gluing of syndromes (\cref{thm:witt-clifford,thm:forking-pauli}, and \cref{prop:logical,prop:glue}).
    The probes of the Pauli observable are the Pauli channels, and the noise of a memory on countably many qubits is carried by idealized errors (\cref{prop:channels}).
  \item
    For every diagonal observable whose pure responses have the independence property,
    and in particular for the Rado observable and the Pauli observable, the real-valued theory of its mixed preparations, probes and response has the tree property of the second kind, witnessed by the difference of two responses.
    Hence a simple diagonal observable is NIP (\cref{thm:collapse}).
  \item
    For the Pauli observable, the indicator of every syndrome sector is the difference of the responses of two uniform mixtures, and the tree property of the second kind is witnessed by actual preparations:
    the position of the first check to fire in a block, for instance the first domain wall of a bit flip in a repetition code, is a dividing condition (\cref{prop:sector}, \cref{thm:pauli-tp2} and \cref{exa:repetition}).
  \item
    For the Rado observable, and more generally for every observable whose pure responses are freely independent,
    such as the random bipartite graph, the conditions on a probe given by instances of a quantifier-free formula with actual mixtures as parameters are compatible along every sequence:
    no such sequence is uniformly $k$-inconsistent, for any $k$.
    Hence the tree property of the second kind in~(C) is witnessed only by idealized preparations (\cref{thm:free,thm:free-qf}, and \cref{cor:rado}).
  \end{enumerate}
\end{keytheorem}

The difference lies in the probes.
For the Pauli observable we show in \cref{prop:channels} that the pure probes are the Pauli strings of arbitrary support, which form a compact group, and that the pure responses of Alice are its characters, up to an affine change.
Characters multiply, and averages of responses over cosets therefore recover the indicators of syndrome sectors (see \cref{prop:sector});
this is the familiar formula for the projector onto a syndrome sector as an average over the stabilizer group \cite{Gottesman:1997}, and with it a single mixture can say “the checks before the $j$-th are silent and the $j$-th fires”.
For the Rado observable, by contrast, \cref{lem:rado-probes} identifies the pure probes with all sets of neighbors, which form the whole Cantor cube, and the pure responses with its coordinates.
Averages of coordinates see only the marginals of a probe, and a gliding hump argument in the proof of \cref{thm:free} shows that conditions on marginals given by actual mixtures are compatible on disjoint blocks.
In the paradigm, the Rado graph, the tree of mixtures therefore needs idealization;
in its quantum counterpart, the Pauli group, the linear relations among the labels, which are the algebra of syndromes, put the tree within reach of actual preparations.
\section{Simple theories and the theorem of Kim and Pillay}\label{sec:simple}

We begin by fixing the vocabulary.
We work in a monster model $\MM$ of a complete first-order theory $T$;
sets of parameters are small.

\begin{definition}\label{def:dividing}
  Let $\varphi(x,y)$ be a formula and let an integer $k\ge2$ be given.
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
  \item
    A sequence $(b_j)_{j\in\NN}$ is \emph{$k$-inconsistent for $\varphi$} if for all $j_1<\dots<j_k$ there is no $x$ with $\varphi(x,b_{j_1})\wedge\dots \wedge\varphi(x,b_{j_k})$.
  \item
    The formula $\varphi$ has the \emph{tree property} if, for some
    $k\ge2$, there is a tree $(b_s)_{s\in\NN^{<\NN}}$ such that for every $\eta\in\NN^\NN$ the set $\{\varphi(x,b_{\eta\restriction n}):n\in\NN\}$ is consistent and for every $s$ the sequence $(b_{s^\frown j})_{j\in\NN}$ is $k$-inconsistent for $\varphi$.
  \item
    The formula $\varphi$ has the \emph{tree property of the first kind} if there is a tree $(b_s)_{s\in\NN^{<\NN}}$ such that for every $\eta\in\NN^\NN$ the set $\{\varphi(x,b_{\eta\restriction n}):n\in\NN\}$ is consistent and for all incomparable $s,t\in\NN^{<\NN}$ the set $\{\varphi(x,b_s),\varphi(x,b_t)\}$ is inconsistent.
  \item
    The formula $\varphi$ has the \emph{tree property of the second kind}
    (TP$_2$) if, for some $k\ge2$, there is an array $(b_{ij})_{i,j\in\NN}$ such that every row $(b_{ij})_{j\in\NN}$ is $k$-inconsistent for $\varphi$ and for every $f\colon\NN\to\NN$ the set $\{\varphi(x,b_{if(i)}):i\in\NN\}$ is consistent.
  \item
    The theory $T$ is \emph{simple} if no formula has the tree property.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Every stable theory is simple, and the tree property of the second kind implies the tree property;
by a theorem of Shelah, a theory is simple exactly when it has neither TP$_2$ nor the tree property of the first kind (see \cite[Theorem~III.7.11]{Shelah:1990a}).
A simple theory is stable if and only if it is NIP (see \cite{Shelah:1980b,Grossberg-Iovino-Lessmann:2002}), so a simple unstable theory has the independence property.
To prove simplicity we do not build trees;
instead, we exhibit an independence relation directly.

\begin{fact}[Kim and Pillay]\label{fact:kp}
  Let $\ind$ be a ternary relation on small subsets of $\MM$, written $A\ind_C B$, and assume that it satisfies the following:
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\roman*)},leftmargin=*]
  \item
    \emph{invariance} under automorphisms of $\MM$;
  \item
    \emph{symmetry}:
    the relation $A\ind_C B$ holds if and only if $B\ind_C A$ holds;
  \item
    \emph{monotonicity and transitivity}:
    the relation $A\ind_C BD$ holds if and only if both $A\ind_C B$ and $A\ind_{CB}D$ hold;
  \item
    \emph{finite character}:
    the relation $A\ind_C B$ holds if and only if $a\ind_C b$ holds for all finite tuples $a$ from $A$ and $b$ from $B$;
  \item
    \emph{local character}:
    for every finite tuple $a$ and every set $B$ there is $B_0\subseteq B$ with $|B_0|\le|T|$ and $a\ind_{B_0}B$;
  \item
    \emph{extension}:
    for every tuple $a$ and all sets $C\subseteq B$ there is $a'$ with $\tp(a'/C)=\tp(a/C)$ and $a'\ind_C B$;
  \item
    \emph{independence theorem over models}:
    if $M$ is a model, $B\ind_M D$, $a_1\ind_M B$, $a_2\ind_M D$ and $\tp(a_1/M)=\tp(a_2/M)$, then there is $a$ with $\tp(a/MB)=\tp(a_1/MB)$, $\tp(a/MD)=\tp(a_2/MD)$ and $a\ind_M BD$.
  \end{enumerate}
  Then the theory $T$ is simple and the relation $\ind$ coincides with nonforking independence (see \cite[Theorem~4.2]{Kim-Pillay:1997}).
\end{fact}
\section{The two paradigms}\label{sec:pauli}

Here we prove \cref{thm:main}(A):
the Rado graph and the Pauli group on countably many qubits, taken modulo phases and with commutation as its relation, are simple unstable structures.
In the subsections below we show that both are Fraïssé limits with quantifier elimination, and that in both the relation of Kim and Pillay is read off from the closure operator:
equality for the graph, linear span for the Pauli group.
\subsection{The Rado graph}
The class of finite graphs has the amalgamation property, since two graphs that share an induced subgraph can be glued along it without new edges.
Its Fraïssé limit is the Rado graph $(\Gamma,E)$, and a countable graph is this limit if and only if it has the \emph{extension property}:
for all finite disjoint $\Gamma_1,\Gamma_0\subseteq\Gamma$ there are infinitely many $v\in\Gamma\setminus(\Gamma_1\cup\Gamma_0)$ adjacent to every vertex of $\Gamma_1$ and to no vertex of $\Gamma_0$ \cite{Fraisse:1954a,Rado:1964}.
The theory of $(\Gamma,E)$ is $\aleph_0$-categorical and eliminates quantifiers, so the type of a tuple over a set $D$ is determined by its equalities with elements of $D$ and its edges to $D$ and within itself.
By saturation, the monster model has the extension property for small disjoint sets $\Gamma_1,\Gamma_0$.

The relation $E(x,y)$ has the independence property:
for distinct $a_1,\dots,a_n$ and every set of indices among $1,\dots,n$, the extension property gives a vertex adjacent to $a_i$ exactly when $i$ is in the set.
Hence the theory of the Rado graph is unstable.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:rado-simple}
  The theory of the Rado graph is simple, and the relation $A\ind_C B$ holds if and only if $A\cap B\subseteq C$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  We verify the hypotheses of \cref{fact:kp}.
  The first five are set algebra, and extension and the independence theorem follow from the extension property of $\MM$.
  Invariance, symmetry and finite character are immediate.
  Monotonicity and transitivity hold together, since the conditions $A\cap B\subseteq C$ and $A\cap D\subseteq C\cup B$ say jointly that every element of $A\cap(B\cup D)$ lies in $C$.
  For local character, given a finite tuple $a$ and a set $B$, the finite set $B_0=B\cap a$ satisfies $a\cap B\subseteq B_0$, which is the relation $a\ind_{B_0}B$.

  For extension, let $a$ and $C\subseteq B$ be given, and write $a=(c,a_0)$, where $c$ lists the entries of $a$ in $C$.
  The type of $a_0$ over $C$ prescribes only the equalities and edges of $a_0$ within itself and to $C$.
  Hence the extension property of $\MM$, applied one entry at a time with the excluded set enlarged by $B\setminus C$, gives a tuple $a_0'$ disjoint from $B$ with the same type over $C$, and the tuple $a'=(c,a_0')$ satisfies $a'\cap B\subseteq C$.

  For the independence theorem, let $M$, $B$, $D$, $a_1$ and $a_2$ be as in \cref{fact:kp}(vii).
  Since $\tp(a_1/M)=\tp(a_2/M)$, the two tuples have the same entries in $M$ and the same remaining equalities;
  write $a_\ell=(m,a_\ell')$ with $a_\ell'$ disjoint from $M$.
  The hypotheses $a_1\ind_MB$ and $a_2\ind_MD$ say that $a_1'$ is disjoint from $B$ and $a_2'$ from $D$.
  We look for a tuple $x$, disjoint from $M\cup B\cup D$, whose edges within itself and to $M$ are those of $a_1'$ (equivalently, of $a_2'$), whose edges to $B\setminus M$ are those of $a_1'$, and whose edges to $D\setminus M$ are those of $a_2'$.
  These prescriptions concern disjoint sets of vertices, because $B\cap D\subseteq M$;
  the extension property of $\MM$ therefore provides such an $x$, and by quantifier elimination the tuple $a=(m,x)$ is as required.
\end{proof}
\subsection{Pauli strings}
A qubit is the Hilbert space $\CC^2$, and its Pauli matrices are
\[
  X=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
  Z=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad XZ=-ZX.
\]
A Pauli string on $n$ qubits is, up to a phase, a tensor product $X^{x_1}Z^{z_1}\otimes\dots\otimes X^{x_n}Z^{z_n}$ with $x_k,z_k\in\FF_2$, and it is labeled by the vector $(x,z)\in\FF_2^{2n}$;
products of strings correspond to sums of labels.
For a label $v=(x,z)$ we write $P_v$ for the Hermitian string $\mathrm{i}^{x\cdot z}X^{x_1}Z^{z_1}\otimes\dots\otimes X^{x_n}Z^{z_n}$, where $x\cdot z$ is the number of indices $k$ with $x_k=z_k=1$;
it satisfies $P_v^2=I$, and the label $(1,1)$ of one qubit gives $P_{(1,1)}=\mathrm{i}XZ=Y$.
Two strings either commute or anticommute, and
\[
  P_{(x,z)}P_{(x',z')}=(-1)^{\omega((x,z),(x',z'))}P_{(x',z')}P_{(x,z)},
  \qquad \omega((x,z),(x',z'))=x\cdot z'+z\cdot x'.
\]
The form $\omega$ is bilinear, alternating and nondegenerate on $\FF_2^{2n}$.
The \emph{syndrome} of an error $e$ with respect to checks $g_1,\dots,g_m$ is the vector $(\omega(g_1,e),\dots,\omega(g_m,e))$:
measuring the checks reveals which of them anticommute with the error.
On countably many qubits we let $V=\bigoplus_{k\in\NN}\FF_2^2$, with the same form;
its elements are the Pauli strings that act nontrivially on finitely many qubits.
\subsection{The Pauli group as a Fraïssé limit}
In the language with $+$, $0$ and a binary relation for $\omega(x,y)=1$, substructures are $\FF_2$-subspaces with the restricted form.
Finite alternating spaces amalgamate:
if $A\subseteq B$ and $A\subseteq C$, write $B=A\oplus B'$ and $C=A\oplus C'$, and declare $\omega(B',C')=0$ on $A\oplus B'\oplus C'$.
Every finite alternating space embeds in a nondegenerate one, and by the theorem of Witt \cite{Artin:1957} every isometry between subspaces of a finite nondegenerate alternating space extends to the whole space.
A back-and-forth argument along symplectic bases therefore shows that $(V,\omega)$ is the Fraïssé limit of the finite alternating spaces over $\FF_2$.
Its theory is $\aleph_0$-categorical and eliminates quantifiers, so the type of a tuple $a$ over a set $D$ is determined by the linear relations of $a$ over $\spn(D)$ and the values of $\omega$ on $\spn(aD)$.
The algebraic closure of a set is its span.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:generic}
  If $a_1,\dots,a_n\in V$ are linearly independent and $\varepsilon_1,\dots,\varepsilon_n\in\FF_2$, then there is $b\in V$ with $\omega(a_i,b)=\varepsilon_i$ for $i=1,\dots,n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
  The vectors $a_i$ lie in a finite-dimensional subspace $W$ spanned by part of the symplectic basis, and the form is nondegenerate on $W$.
  Hence the functionals $\omega(a_i,\cdot)$ restricted to $W$ are linearly independent, and the linear map $b\mapsto(\omega(a_i,b))_{i\le n}$ from $W$ to $\FF_2^n$ is onto.
\end{proof}

By \cref{lem:generic}, the formula $\omega(x,y)=1$ has the independence property:
any set of syndromes is realized by some error.
Hence the theory of $(V,\omega)$ is unstable.
By saturation, the monster model $\MM$ realizes, over each small subspace $S$, every finite-dimensional alternating space $S\oplus\spn(x_1,\dots,x_n)$;
in particular, for every linear functional $f$ on $S$ there is $b$ with $\omega(\cdot,b)=f$ on $S$.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:symp-simple}
  The theory of $(V,\omega)$ is simple, and the relation $A\ind_C B$ holds if and only if $\spn(AC)\cap\spn(BC)=\spn(C)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  The proof follows that of \cref{thm:rado-simple}, with linear span in place of equality:
  the first five axioms are linear algebra, and extension and the independence theorem come from the freedom to prescribe the form on a complement.
  We verify the hypotheses of \cref{fact:kp}.
  Invariance and symmetry are immediate, and finite character holds because a vector of $\spn(AC)\cap\spn(BC)$ outside $\spn(C)$ is a combination of finitely many elements of $A$, $B$ and $C$.

  For monotonicity and transitivity, put $Q_1=\spn(AC)$, $Q_2=\spn(BC)$, $Q_3=\spn(BCD)$ and $Q_0=\spn(C)$;
  these subspaces satisfy $Q_2\subseteq Q_3$ and $\spn(ABC)=Q_1+Q_2$.
  It remains to be shown that $Q_1\cap Q_3=Q_0$ if and only if $Q_1\cap Q_2=Q_0$ and $(Q_1+Q_2)\cap Q_3=Q_2$.
  Suppose that $Q_1\cap Q_3=Q_0$.
  Since $Q_2\subseteq Q_3$, the intersection $Q_1\cap Q_2$ is contained in $Q_0$.
  If $u_1+u_2\in Q_3$ with $u_1\in Q_1$ and $u_2\in Q_2$, then the vector $u_1$ also lies in $Q_3$, hence in $Q_1\cap Q_3=Q_0\subseteq Q_2$;
  thus the sum lies in $Q_2$.
  Conversely, every $u\in Q_1\cap Q_3$ lies in $(Q_1+Q_2)\cap Q_3=Q_2$, and therefore in $Q_1\cap Q_2=Q_0$.

  Local character follows from finite dimension.
  Given a finite tuple $a$ and a set $B$, the space $\spn(a)\cap\spn(B)$ is finite, so it is contained in $\spn(B_0)$ for some finite $B_0\subseteq B$.
  If $u+w\in\spn(B)$ with $u\in\spn(a)$ and $w\in\spn(B_0)$, then the vector $u$ lies in $\spn(a)\cap\spn(B)\subseteq\spn(B_0)$.
  It follows that $\spn(aB_0)\cap\spn(B)=\spn(B_0)$, which is the relation $a\ind_{B_0}B$.

  For extension, let $a$ and $C\subseteq B$ be given.
  Replacing $a$ by a maximal subtuple linearly independent over $\spn(C)$, we may assume that $a$ is linearly independent over $\spn(C)$, since the remaining entries are then determined.
  Choose a complement $B'$ of $\spn(C)$ in $\spn(BC)$.
  By saturation, the alternating space $\spn(BC)\oplus\spn(x)$ in which $x$ has the values of $\omega$ prescribed by $\tp(a/C)$ on $\spn(xC)$ and is orthogonal to $B'$ is realized over $\spn(BC)$ by a tuple $a'$;
  this tuple has the type of $a$ over $C$ and is linearly independent over $\spn(BC)$.

  The independence theorem is a gluing of functionals.
  Let $M$, $B$, $D$, $a_1$ and $a_2$ be as in \cref{fact:kp}(vii).
  As before, we may assume that $a_1$ is linearly independent over $M$;
  then so is $a_2$, and the hypotheses $a_1\ind_MB$ and $a_2\ind_MD$ make $a_1$ linearly independent over $\spn(MB)$ and $a_2$ over $\spn(MD)$.
  For each $k$, the functionals $\omega(a_{1,k},\cdot)$ on $\spn(MB)$ and $\omega(a_{2,k},\cdot)$ on $\spn(MD)$ agree on $M$, because $\tp(a_1/M)=\tp(a_2/M)$.
  Since $B\ind_MD$, their domains meet in $\spn(MB)\cap\spn(MD)=M$, and the two functionals glue to a linear functional on $\spn(MBD)$.
  Realize over $\spn(MBD)$ a tuple $a$, linearly independent over $\spn(MBD)$, with these functionals and with the values of $\omega$ on $a$ given by $\tp(a_1/M)$.
  By quantifier elimination, the type of $a$ over $MB$ is that of $a_1$, and its type over $MD$ is that of $a_2$;
  the relation $a\ind_MBD$ holds by construction.
\end{proof}

In both structures, then, nonforking independence is the independence of the closure operator,
equality for the graph and linear span for the Pauli group, and the independence theorem is the freedom to prescribe the remaining relations, edges or values of the form, on each side separately.

\begin{remark}\label{rem:fields}
  The proof of \cref{thm:symp-simple} applies over any finite field $\FF_q$, in the language with a unary function for multiplication by each scalar and a binary relation for each value of the form.
  For symmetric or arbitrary bilinear forms, the identification of the limit through symplectic bases and the theorem of Witt is no longer available in characteristic two,
  but the generic limit exists by free amalgamation, and the verification of \cref{fact:kp} goes through with the values $\omega(x,x)$ prescribed by the type.
\end{remark}
\section{Stabilizer codes as a structure: a dictionary}\label{sec:dictionary}

Three notions of model theory, namely Fraïssé limits, forking and the independence theorem, are already at work in quantum error correction under other names, and in this section we identify them.
After a brief account of stabilizer codes, in each subsection we state a theorem, prove it, and interpret it twice:
once for the physicist, who knows stabilizer codes but not model theory, and once for the logician, who knows model theory but not stabilizer codes.
A single example runs through the section:
the five-qubit code of Laflamme, Miquel, Paz and Zurek \cite{Laflamme-Miquel-Paz-Zurek:1996} and of Bennett, DiVincenzo, Smolin and Wootters \cite{Bennett-DiVincenzo-Smolin-Wootters:1996}, the smallest code that corrects an arbitrary error on one qubit.
