\subsection{Stabilizer codes in brief}
We recall what the logician needs (see \cite{Gottesman:1997}).
A \emph{stabilizer group} on $n$ qubits is a group $\mathcal{G}$ of pairwise commuting Pauli strings that does not contain $-I$.
Its common eigenspace for the eigenvalue $1$ is the \emph{code space};
if $\mathcal{G}$ has $n-k$ independent generators, the code space has dimension $2^k$ and encodes $k$ qubits.
In the labels of \cref{sec:pauli}, the group $\mathcal{G}$ corresponds, up to signs, to an isotropic subspace $S\subseteq\FF_2^{2n}$, that is, a subspace on which $\omega$ vanishes.
An error is a Pauli string $e$;
measuring the generators $g_1,\dots,g_{n-k}$ of $\mathcal{G}$ on the corrupted state returns the \emph{syndrome} $(\omega(g_1,e),\dots,\omega(g_{n-k},e))$, and the decoder guesses the error from the syndrome.
An error with zero syndrome is undetectable.
It is harmless if it lies in $\mathcal{G}$, since it then acts trivially on the code space, and it is a \emph{logical error} otherwise.
On labels, the errors with zero syndrome form the \emph{centralizer} $S^\perp=\{e\in\FF_2^{2n}:\omega(s,e)=0\text{ for all }s\in S\}$, which contains $S$ and has dimension $2n-(n-k)=n+k$;
a set $L$ of \emph{logical operators} is a set of $2k$ labels with $S^\perp=S\oplus\spn(L)$, and the values $\omega(\ell,e)$ for $\ell\in L$ record which logical operator an error of $S^\perp$ applies to the encoded qubits.
The \emph{distance} of the code is the least number of qubits on which a logical error acts.
A \emph{Clifford unitary} is a unitary operator $U$ that maps Pauli strings to Pauli strings under conjugation, up to a phase;
on labels, conjugation by $U$ is a linear map of $\FF_2^{2n}$ that preserves $\omega$.

\begin{example}\label{exa:five-qubit}
  The five-qubit code has the stabilizer generators
  \[
    g_1=XZZXI,\quad g_2=IXZZX,\quad g_3=XIXZZ,\quad g_4=ZXIXZ,
  \]
  where $XZZXI$ stands for $X\otimes Z\otimes Z\otimes X\otimes I$, and it encodes one qubit, with logical operators $\bar{X}=XXXXX$ and $\bar{Z}=ZZZZZ$.
  Each of the fifteen errors that act as $X$, $Y$ or $Z$ on a single qubit has its own nonzero syndrome, and there are exactly fifteen nonzero syndromes of four bits;
  for instance, the error $XIIII$ has syndrome $(0,0,0,1)$ and the error $IIZII$ has syndrome $(0,0,1,0)$.
  The code therefore corrects every error on one qubit, and it has distance $3$.
\end{example}
\subsection{Fraïssé limits and Clifford circuits}
In \cref{prop:age,thm:witt-clifford} below, we show that every finite piece of commutation structure occurs among Pauli strings, and that every symmetry between finite pieces is implemented by a Clifford circuit.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:age}
  If $\Theta$ is a symmetric $d\times d$ matrix over $\FF_2$ with zero diagonal, then there are Pauli strings $P_1,\dots,P_d$ on at most $d$ qubits such that $P_i$ and $P_j$ anticommute exactly when $\Theta_{ij}=1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
  The matrix $\Theta$ defines an alternating form on $\FF_2^d$.
  Let $W_0$ be its radical and choose a complement $W$ of $W_0$;
  the form is nondegenerate on $W$, which therefore has a symplectic basis of $r$ pairs, where $2r=\dim W$.
  Send the $r$ pairs to the labels of $X$ and $Z$ on the first $r$ qubits, and a basis of $W_0$ to the labels of $X$ on $\dim W_0$ further qubits.
  This linear map preserves the form, and the images of the standard basis vectors of $\FF_2^d$ are the required strings, on $r+\dim W_0\le d$ qubits.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:witt-clifford}
  Let $W$ and $W'$ be subspaces of $\FF_2^{2n}$ and let $f\colon W\to W'$ be a linear bijection with $\omega(f(v),f(w))=\omega(v,w)$ for all $v,w\in W$.
  Then there is a Clifford unitary $U$ on $n$ qubits with $UP_vU^*=\pm P_{f(v)}$ for all $v\in W$.
  If, moreover, the subspace $W$ is isotropic and the vectors $v_1,\dots,v_m$ form a basis of $W$, then the unitary $U$ can be chosen with $UP_{v_i}U^*=P_{f(v_i)}$ for all $i\le m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  By the theorem of Witt, the map $f$ extends to a linear bijection $\tau$ of $\FF_2^{2n}$ that preserves $\omega$.
  Every such $\tau$ is induced by conjugation by a Clifford unitary \cite{Calderbank-Rains-Shor-Sloane:1998,Dehaene-deMoor:2003};
  let $U$ be one.
  Since the operator $UP_vU^*$ is a Pauli string up to a phase, Hermitian and with square $I$, the phase is a sign.
  For the second statement, let $\varepsilon_i\in\{\pm1\}$ be the sign with $UP_{v_i}U^*=\varepsilon_iP_{f(v_i)}$.
  The vectors $f(v_i)$ are linearly independent, so by nondegeneracy there is $b\in\FF_2^{2n}$ with $(-1)^{\omega(f(v_i),b)}=\varepsilon_i$ for all $i$.
  Conjugation by $P_b$ multiplies $P_{f(v_i)}$ by $(-1)^{\omega(f(v_i),b)}$, so the Clifford unitary $P_bU$ has the required property.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:encoding}
  Any two stabilizer groups on $n$ qubits with the same number of independent generators are conjugate by a Clifford unitary.
  In particular, every code that encodes $k$ qubits into $n$ has an encoding circuit made of Clifford gates, which maps the trivial code with generators $Z_1,\dots,Z_{n-k}$ onto it.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
  Let $g_1,\dots,g_m$ and $g'_1,\dots,g'_m$ be independent generators, with labels $v_i$ and $v'_i$.
  Both families span isotropic subspaces, so the linear map $v_i\mapsto v'_i$ preserves $\omega$, and \cref{thm:witt-clifford} gives a Clifford unitary $U$ with $Ug_iU^*=g'_i$ for all $i$.
  The signs of the generators are absorbed as in the proof of \cref{thm:witt-clifford}.
\end{proof}

On countably many qubits, every finite-dimensional subspace of $V$ lies in the labels of the first $n$ qubits for some $n$.
Applying \cref{thm:witt-clifford} there, and extending by the identity on the remaining qubits, shows that every isometry between finite-dimensional subspaces of
$(V,\omega)$ extends to an automorphism of $(V,\omega)$ implemented by a Clifford circuit on finitely many qubits.

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{For the physicist.}
A Fraïssé limit is the unique countable
structure that contains every finite configuration of a given kind and in which every symmetry between two finite configurations extends to a symmetry of the whole structure.
By \cref{prop:age}, every finite pattern of commutation relations occurs among Pauli strings, and by \cref{thm:witt-clifford}, every symmetry between finite pieces extends to a global one, implemented by a Clifford circuit.
The Pauli group on countably many qubits is therefore the Fraïssé limit of finite commutation patterns, and Clifford circuits on finitely many qubits implement the symmetries that witness its homogeneity.
The existence of encoding circuits for stabilizer codes, usually proved by an explicit construction, is the shadow of this homogeneity:
by \cref{cor:encoding}, the five-qubit code is the image of the trivial code with generators $Z_1,Z_2,Z_3,Z_4$ under a Clifford circuit, because the two generating sets have the same commutation pattern, namely none at all.

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{For the logician.}
The structure $(V,\omega)$ of
\cref{sec:pauli} is the Pauli group modulo phases, and the Clifford group acts on it through the symplectic group, which is the automorphism group of each finite piece $(\FF_2^{2n},\omega)$.
Ultrahomogeneity, which in \cref{sec:pauli} came from the theorem of Witt and a back-and-forth argument, is implemented by unitary operators on a Hilbert space, the gates of a quantum computer.
Stabilizer codes are finite isotropic subspaces, and \cref{cor:encoding} is ultrahomogeneity for isotropic subspaces, with the signs of the generators as additional data handled by \cref{lem:generic}.
\subsection{Forking and logical errors}
In a simple theory, forking is the notion of an extension of a type that adds information.
The next theorem computes it for the Pauli group, using the description of nonforking independence in \cref{thm:symp-simple}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:forking-pauli}
  Let $e$ be an element of a monster model $\MM$ of the theory of $(V,\omega)$, and let $D\subseteq B$ be small subsets of $\MM$.
  Then the type $\tp(e/B)$ forks over $D$ if and only if $e\in\spn(B)\setminus\spn(D)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  By \cref{thm:symp-simple}, the type $\tp(e/B)$ does not fork over $D$ exactly when $\spn(eD)\cap\spn(B)=\spn(D)$.
  If $e\in\spn(D)$, the spans $\spn(eD)$ and $\spn(D)$ coincide, and the equality holds.
  Otherwise the span of $eD$ is $\spn(D)\oplus\{0,e\}$, and the equality fails if and only if some vector $e+d$ with $d\in\spn(D)$ lies in $\spn(B)$, that is, if and only if $e\in\spn(B)$.
\end{proof}

By \cref{thm:forking-pauli}, commutation data never fork:
the type of $e$ over $B$ records the values $\omega(b,e)$ for $b\in B$, and these values can be prescribed freely without forking.
Forking happens only when $e$ becomes a product of the operators in $B$.
In a stabilizer code this criterion separates the two kinds of undetectable error.
The \emph{weight} of an error is the number of qubits on which it acts.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:distance}
  Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a stabilizer group with set of labels $S$, and let $e$ be a nonzero label with zero syndrome.
  Then the type $\tp(e/S)$ forks over $\emptyset$ if and only if $e$ is harmless, that is, $e\in S$.
  Consequently, the distance of the code is the least weight of a nonzero error with zero syndrome whose type over $S$ does not fork over $\emptyset$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
  Apply \cref{thm:forking-pauli} with $D=\emptyset$ and $B=S$;
  the span of $S$ is $S$ itself, and $\spn(\emptyset)=\{0\}$.
  The second statement is the definition of distance, since a logical error is a nonzero label with zero syndrome outside $S$.
\end{proof}

In \cref{prop:logical} below, we show that the stabilizer group alone does not tell logical errors apart, because they all have the same type over
$S$, and that the logical operators, once known, characterize them by forking.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:logical}
  Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a stabilizer group with set of labels $S$, let $L$ be a set of logical operators, and let $e$ be a label with zero syndrome.
  Then the following hold:
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\alph*)}]
  \item
    the label $e$ is a logical error if and only if $\tp(e/S\cup L)$ forks over $S$;
  \item
    all logical errors have the same type over $S$, and this type does not fork over $\emptyset$.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
  (a) Since the syndrome of $e$ is zero, the label $e$ lies in $S^\perp=\spn(S\cup L)$.
  By \cref{thm:forking-pauli} with $D=S$ and $B=S\cup L$, the type $\tp(e/S\cup L)$ forks over $S$ if and only if $e\in\spn(S\cup L)\setminus S=S^\perp\setminus S$, which is the set of logical errors.

  (b) By quantifier elimination, the type over $S$ of a label
  $e\notin S$ is determined by its linear relations over $S$, of which there are none, and by the values of $\omega$ on $\spn(eS)$, which are $\omega(s,e)=0$ and $\omega(e,e)=0$ together with the values on $S$.
  Hence all labels of $S^\perp\setminus S$ have the same type over $S$, and it does not fork over $\emptyset$ by \cref{cor:distance}.
\end{proof}

In the five-qubit code, the errors $g_1g_2=XYIYX$ (up to a phase) and $\bar{X}=XXXXX$ both have zero syndrome.
The first lies in the stabilizer group, so its type over $S$ forks over $\emptyset$;
it is harmless.
The second does not, so its type does not fork over $\emptyset$;
it flips the encoded qubit, and it acts on five qubits.
The least weight of an error of the second kind is $3$, attained for instance by $\bar{X} g_1=IYYIX$ up to a phase.
With $L=\{\bar{X},\bar{Z}\}$, the logical errors $\bar{X}$, $\bar{Z}$ and $\bar{X}g_1$ have the same type over $S$;
over $S\cup L$ each of them lies in $\spn(S\cup L)$, whence its type becomes algebraic and forks over $S$, as \cref{prop:logical}(a) predicts.

\par\medskip\noindent\textbf{For the physicist.}
Forking is the model-theoretic notion of an
observation that pins something down, as opposed to one that merely narrows it.
In the Pauli group, \cref{thm:forking-pauli} shows that syndromes only narrow:
on infinitely many qubits, no finite set of checks determines an error, however many bits it reveals.
An error forks exactly when it becomes a product of known operators.
In a code, by \cref{cor:distance}, this is the difference between a harmless undetectable error, which is a product of stabilizers and therefore forks, and a logical error, which does not fork.
The distance of the code, its single most important parameter, is the least weight of a nonforking undetectable error.
\cref{prop:logical} completes the picture:
the stabilizer group alone sees all logical errors alike, and each logical error forks as soon as the logical operators join the known operators.

