\begin{abstract}
Mutual coupling between elements of low-frequency arrays is a leading systematic for 21~cm cosmology, leaking bright foregrounds into otherwise clean regions of the power spectrum. Many mitigation models describe coupling as a linear combination of uncoupled signals re-radiated from neighboring feeds, but data suggest additional mechanisms. Inspired by antenna scattering theory, we decompose coupling into an \emph{antenna mode}, constructed from translated isolated responses and expected to be removable by linear combination models, and a \emph{structural mode}, the remainder. Full-wave simulations of HERA-like feeds and dishes in a 37-element hexagonal array show that the antenna mode accounts for only about $1\%$ of the beam perturbation power, suggesting limited scope for mitigation with linear models. Two effects nevertheless lead to substantially better suppression. First, correlating beams and weighting them by the sky changes the modes' relative importance: correcting the antenna mode can remove more than half of the coupling power in simulated visibilities. Second, relaxing the coefficient relationships imposed by re-radiation allows linear corrections to remove structural-mode coupling too. Beam-derived corrections can reduce coupling power by over an order of magnitude in our noiseless simulations of perfectly redundant arrays. Fitting directly to known isolated visibilities reduces residuals further on the fitted field, but this target is generally unavailable in observations. Practical correction therefore requires additional constraints to separate the unknown sky from coupling while preserving the cosmological signal. We introduce an extension of holographic back-projection to coupled beams, providing an intuitive picture of these perturbations in the aperture plane.
\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large radio interferometric arrays are targeting the cosmological 21~cm signal across a wide range of redshifts, both to probe the Cosmic Dawn and Epoch of Reionization (EoR), and to map large-scale structure in the post-reionization Universe through to the present day. These experiments must contend with instrumental systematics whose control requirements are extraordinarily stringent: foregrounds exceed the signal by many orders of magnitude, and although they are intrinsically spectrally smooth, they acquire chromaticity through uncalibrated instrumental response, leaking power into the modes used to measure the cosmological signal. The field and its challenges are reviewed in \citet{furlanetto_et_al_2006, mesinger_2016, liu_shaw_2020}. While significant progress has been made---with increasingly sensitive upper limits at high redshift \citep{eastwood_et_al_2019, hera_collaboration_et_al_2023, mertens_et_al_2025, ceccotti_et_al_2025, nunhokee_et_al_2025, munshi_et_al_2025, hera_collaboration_et_al_2026}, and post-reionization detections in cross-correlation with galaxy surveys \citep{chime_collaboration_et_al_2023, cunnington_et_al_2023, chime_collaboration_et_al_2024} and in autocorrelation \citep{paul_et_al_2026, chime_collaboration_et_al_2025}---these instruments are often systematics- rather than noise-limited, especially on the largest scales.

Among the most challenging systematics is mutual coupling: electromagnetic interactions between antennas that modify their effective radiation patterns, a problem extensively studied in the antenna engineering literature \citep[reviewed by][]{craeye_gonzalez_ovejero_2011}. In a densely packed array, the resulting direction- and frequency-dependent beam perturbations introduce spectral structure into the measured visibilities that can overwhelm the cosmological signal with foregrounds over a broad range of spatial scales. Full-wave simulations of the HERA Phase~I receiver system likewise show that mutual coupling extends the instrumental delay response, with its extent depending on array size and antenna position \citep{fagnoni_et_al_2021b}. PAPER \citep{parsons_et_al_2010} and LOFAR \citep{van_haarlem_et_al_2013} already mention it as a possible limiting systematic, and subsequent 21~cm power-spectrum analyses have repeatedly named it as a leading candidate or principal source of excess power \citep{ali_et_al_2015, beardsley_et_al_2016, ewall_wice_et_al_2016, mertens_et_al_2020, hera_collaboration_et_al_2023, hera_collaboration_et_al_2026}.

Several mitigation strategies have been explored. A natural approach is to measure or simulate the coupled per-element responses and adopt them, in place of the idealized isolated response, in calibration and analysis. For the Murchison Widefield Array (MWA), embedded-element beam models account for coupling among the 16 dipoles within each tile \citep{sutinjo_et_al_2015,sokolowski_et_al_2017,nunhokee_et_al_2025}, and \citet{chokshi_et_al_2024} showed in simulations that foreground leakage from tile-to-tile beam variations can be mitigated if a precise model of each beam's perturbation is known and used in calibration. CHIME incorporates empirical beam models in its analysis, inferred from observations of catalogued radio sources and validated against independent holographic and solar measurements \citep{chime_collaboration_et_al_2023,chime_collaboration_et_al_2025}. LOFAR-EoR analyses fit direction-dependent station gains from the observations, accounting for beam and propagation effects when predicting and subtracting foreground emission \citep{gan_et_al_2023,mertens_et_al_2025}. Beam mapping and full-wave simulations remain demanding, especially at the precision required for cosmological sensitivity \citep{ohara_et_al_2025}. Recent accelerated solvers have nevertheless reduced the computational cost for large irregular arrays \citep{gueuning_et_al_2025}, enabling calculations of the embedded-element patterns of the 320-element HERA core \citep{gueuning_et_al_2026}.

A more direct strategy is to exclude heavily contaminated baselines from the analysis altogether. In MWA Phase~II, entire baseline classes were discarded because of a persistent common-mode systematic, for which mutual coupling remains a possible, albeit unconfirmed explanation \citep{kolopanis_et_al_2023,elder_et_al_2025}. CHIME's cosmological analyses omit intra-cylinder baselines partly because of enhanced cross-talk---a related form of coupling in which receiver noise, rather than sky signals, is radiated between feeds \citep{chime_collaboration_et_al_2025}. Such cuts trade sensitivity and baseline coverage for reduced contamination.

Another method leverages the fact that coupling signatures occupy characteristic regions of fringe-rate and delay space. \citet{kern_et_al_2019} developed and validated in simulations a subtraction method that models coupling systematics using a singular-value decomposition in time-delay space, restricting the model to low fringe rates to limit cosmological signal loss; \citet{kern_et_al_2020} then demonstrated it on HERA Phase~I data, for slowly varying systematics whose physical origin remained uncertain. \citet{rath_et_al_2025} found that HERA's fiducial main-lobe fringe-rate filter reduces coupling power in simulations by roughly a factor of~100 overall---though suppression is less effective on baselines with short east--west projections, because the desired sky signal varies slowly and overlaps the near-zero-fringe-rate component of the coupling; preserving that signal therefore retains more contamination. Even with this reduction, they find that the residual coupling contamination remains well above the simulated 21~cm signal across much of the EoR window.

A complementary framework---which is the focus of this work---models coupling as a linear transformation of uncoupled signals. Concretely, antenna $i$'s coupled voltage $v_i^c$ is written as a linear combination of uncoupled voltages $v_j^{(0)}$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:coupled_voltage_linear_model}
  v_i^{\rm c} = v_i^{(0)} + \sum_j \epsilon_{ij}v_j^{(0)},
\end{equation}
where the $\epsilon_{ij}$ factors are \emph{coupling coefficients}. The corresponding visibility model is
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf V_{\rm cpl}
    = \mathbf M\,\mathbf V_{\rm iso}\,\mathbf M^{\dagger},
    \label{eq:visibility_mixing_matrix}
\end{equation}
where $N_{\rm ant}$ is the number of antennas and all matrices involved have size $N_{\rm ant}\times N_{\rm ant}$; the coupling matrix is $\mathbf M=\mathbf I+\boldsymbol\epsilon$, with $\mathbf I$ the identity and $\boldsymbol\epsilon$ a matrix populated with the coupling coefficients. The correction can thus be obtained either by determining $\mathbf M$ and inverting it, provided it is invertible, or by fitting entries of $\mathbf{M}^{-1}$. A more general form, which we identify with a horizontal bar over the symbols, consists in defining a much larger $N_{\rm bl}\times N_{\rm bl}$ matrix $\mathbf{\bar{M}}$ acting as
\begin{equation}
    {\mathbf{\bar{V}}}_{\rm cpl} = \mathbf{\bar{M}}\,\mathbf{\bar{V}}_{\rm iso},
    \label{eq:baseline_visibility_mixing_matrix}
\end{equation}
where here, $\bar{\mathbf V}$ collects the visibilities into an $N_{\rm bl}$-long vector. While it is always possible to write an $\mathbf{M}$ coupling matrix from \cref{eq:visibility_mixing_matrix} in \cref{eq:baseline_visibility_mixing_matrix}'s $\mathbf{\bar{M}}$-form via $\bar{M}_{(ij),(kl)} = M_{ik}M_{jl}^*$, the converse is not necessarily true; $\mathbf{\bar{M}}$ may not admit an antenna-level representation of the form in \cref{eq:visibility_mixing_matrix}. \Cref{eq:baseline_visibility_mixing_matrix} thus has more freedom to describe coupling, but is less constrained. Here $N_{\rm bl}$ counts the baseline entries used, including both orientations and any retained autocorrelations; nominally redundant baselines can be combined to reduce the matrix size, although this discards information about any differences in their responses that may be unaccounted for. The correction can be obtained by fitting an operator that acts as $\mathbf{\bar{M}}^{-1}$, without first determining $\mathbf{\bar{M}}$; if the model is imperfect or $\mathbf{\bar{M}}$ is singular, this fitted operator need not be an exact inverse.

\citet{kern_et_al_2019} developed the first semi-analytic, first-order model for re-radiative mutual coupling based on a 2-element model for HERA Phase I (equivalent to isolating elements from the $\mathbf{M}$ matrix to form $2\times2$ pairwise coupling matrices), showing good phenomenological agreement with the Phase I data. \citet{josaitis_et_al_2022} derived a semi-analytic first-order model for populating a full, dense $\mathbf{M}$-like matrix based on impedance mismatch and re-radiation, and \citet{rath_et_al_2025} found that it reproduces characteristic features of HERA data qualitatively, but the data's perturbations remain significantly brighter, suggesting, among other possible causes, additional coupling mechanisms not captured by re-radiation alone. \citet{chime_collaboration_et_al_2022} also applies a re-radiation model and highlights its limitations. For SKA-Low, \citet{ohara_et_al_2026} fit an antenna-level correction matrix, analogous to $\mathbf{M}^{-1}$, by approximating isolated responses with linear combinations of coupled beams over a chosen field of view. They propagate this beam-derived correction to visibilities through a transformation of the form in \cref{eq:visibility_mixing_matrix}. Their simulations show accurate reconstruction within the fitted region, while residual sidelobe contamination limits recovery of the EoR window for a full sky.

The model of \cref{eq:coupled_voltage_linear_model} implicitly describes \emph{antenna-mode} scattering---re-radiation originating at the neighboring feeds' ports, which produces translated copies of the isolated response. In reality, a significant fraction of the coupling may be \emph{structural-mode} scattering, arising from electromagnetic scattering off the physical structure of the neighboring elements, whose pattern need not entirely lie in the span of translated isolated responses, and is thus unreachable by the model.

In this paper, we introduce a framework for analyzing and decomposing mutual coupling that tests how much coupling linear corrections can remove in simulations based on full-wave isolated and coupled beam models. We first present an aperture-plane approach that localizes each neighbor's coupling contribution to compact features on the aperture plane, making the distinction between the antenna and structural modes visually explicit. We then show how much of the perturbation is attributable to either mode in a HERA-like array element's beam, and evaluate coupling removal through both coupling-matrix inversion and freely weighted linear visibility corrections. The latter need not result from self-consistent products of antenna-level correction coefficients; we examine the additional coupling removal this freedom permits and its dependence on the sky. \Cref{sec:coupling_in_aperture_plane} introduces the aperture-plane representation. \Cref{sec:antenna_structural_decomposition} defines the two coupling modes and presents the fraction of either mode in our simulated beams. \Cref{sec:coupling_in_kernel_space} asks how much coupling-matrix inversion and free-weight fits can remove in an idealized scenario where the coupled and uncoupled beams are known perfectly, and explores how baseline coverage may improve on those results. \Cref{sec:coupling_in_simulated_visibilities} evaluates these corrections in simulated visibilities, and \cref{sec:discussion} discusses the requirements for applying them to real data. \Cref{sec:conclusion} is a conclusion. Throughout the paper, we use a simplified HERA-like antenna model described in \Cref{app:cst_setup}.
\section{Mutual coupling in the aperture plane}
\label{sec:coupling_in_aperture_plane}

Fourier back-projection of complex beam maps is established in radio holography, where aperture-plane images reveal reflector illumination, blockage, and surface errors \citep{bennett_et_al_1976, scott_ryle_1977}. We extend its use to mutual coupling by back-projecting the difference between coupled and isolated beams. This gives a spatial view of perturbations with an intuitive physical interpretation, complementary to existing analysis approaches that look at their effects on S-parameters, angular-space beams, visibilities, and power spectra (e.g., \citealt{rath_et_al_2025,ohara_et_al_2026}).

The far-field radiation pattern of an antenna and its aperture-plane field distribution are related by a two-dimensional Fourier transform. Let $\vec E(\theta, \phi)$ be the complex far-field voltage pattern---where $\theta$ and $\phi$ are the zenith and azimuthal angles, respectively. We perform an orthographic projection of the beam's front hemisphere using direction-cosine coordinates $(\ell, m) = (\sin\theta\cos\phi, \sin\theta\sin\phi)$, and write the aperture-plane field as
\begin{equation}
  \vec A(x, y) = \iint_{\rm visible}\! \vec E(\ell, m) e^{-2\pi i (\ell x + m y)/\lambda} d\ell dm, 
  \label{eq:beam_to_aperture_transform}
\end{equation}
where $(x,y)$ are physical coordinates in the aperture plane and $\lambda$ is the free-space observing wavelength. With the normalization of \cref{eq:beam_to_aperture_transform}, the inverse relation is $\vec E(\ell,m)=\lambda^{-2}\iint\vec A(x,y) e^{2\pi i(\ell x+my)/\lambda}\,dx\,dy$. We refer to $\vec A$ as the \emph{reconstructed aperture-plane field}: it is the pattern that, when Fourier-transformed, reproduces the measured or simulated front hemisphere's far-field beam, and not a direct measurement of the physical fields on any real surface. The field $\vec A = (A_\theta, A_\phi)$ carries the two complex transverse components inherited from $\vec E$; wherever magnitudes appear we write $|\vec{A}|^2 \equiv |\vec{A}_\theta|^2 + |\vec{A}_\phi|^2$ for the pointwise power and $\Vert \vec{A}\Vert^2 \equiv \iint |\vec{A}|^2\, dx\, dy$ for its integral over the aperture plane. Inner products and least-squares fits likewise sum over the two components. For a single dish, $|\vec{A}|^2$ is mostly contained within a region of diameter $D$ (the physical dish diameter). Coupling to a neighbor at baseline vector $\vec{b}$ produces a perturbation to the aperture-plane field near $\vec{b}$ with spatial extent comparable to $D$.

To illustrate this approach, we first show two simulations using a simplified HERA element model \citep{fagnoni_et_al_2021}, which consists of a Vivaldi-style feed floating above a 14~m dish---see \cref{app:cst_setup} for simplification details and model images. In all simulations, we only excite the feed plane aligned with the north--south axis. One simulation contains a single isolated element (labeled ``iso''), while the second includes two (\texttt{lin2}), separated by 14.6~m, based on HERA's shortest east--west baseline. \Cref{fig:beam_and_aperture_coupling_comparison} illustrates the beam-to-aperture relationship for the two simulations, along with their complex difference, at 150~MHz. The orthographic view (top row) shows the broad fringe pattern induced by the coupled neighbor across the sky; the aperture view (bottom row) localizes the same perturbation to a compact, dish-sized feature near the neighbor's position at $(14.6, 0)$~m, mostly visible in the difference plot (third column). In all aperture plots, concentric Gibbs ringing appears as the result of Fourier transforming a function with finite support; apodizing the projection would attenuate that feature, at the expense of attenuating horizon sensitivity and biasing quantitative results based on those aperture planes (see, e.g., \cref{sec:coupling_in_kernel_space}); plus, it would smear out fine features in the aperture plane. We therefore use unwindowed transforms throughout.

