The feature in the bottom right of \cref{fig:beam_and_aperture_coupling_comparison} is our first diagnostic of the coupling's nature. If each neighbor simply re-radiated a copy of the signal it received, as assumed in \cref{eq:coupled_voltage_linear_model}, the aperture-plane difference would just be a faint, faithful copy of the isolated pattern (bottom left), shifted onto the neighbor's position---with possibly an even fainter copy at the origin, for higher-order reflections. Here, the perturbation carries an extra, asymmetric feature on the far rim of the neighbor's dish. The same representation scales from a pair to a full array. \Cref{fig:hex37_layout_and_coupled_aperture} shows our reference array---37 elements in a close-packed layout at 14.6~m shortest spacing, henceforth \texttt{hex37}---together with the aperture power of its coupled central element and the difference against the isolated element: one compact, dish-sized feature appears at each neighbor, each carrying that neighbor's contribution to the coupling.

