\end{figure*}

We note that multiple reflections can introduce long propagation delays without placing coupling features at correspondingly greater distances from the origin in the aperture plane. Indeed, their locations reflect where the incident sky radiation first interacts with the array, whether at a feed or elsewhere on its structure; subsequent reflections change each contribution’s complex amplitude and frequency dependence without moving that point of entry. The coupling features therefore remain associated with the array’s physical structures (except for the ringing described above) rather than the accumulated propagation distance.
\section[Decomposition into antenna and structural modes]{Decomposition into antenna and structural modes}
\label{sec:antenna_structural_decomposition}

\subsection{Definitions}
\label{subsec:scattering_mode_definitions}

Mutual coupling can be modeled as an additive field superposed on the antenna's primary far-field beam. We describe this field as the result of scattering, and borrow from the antenna scattering literature \citep{green_1963, hansen_1989, collin_2003, knott_et_al_2004, best_kaanta_2009}, which separates scattered fields into two modes---antenna and structural. We reuse those labels to define two corresponding coupling modes. \emph{Antenna-mode} coupling occurs when a signal received at a neighboring feed reaches its terminals, and some fraction, set by the load impedance, is re-radiated into free space, ending at the dish under test via any path (\Cref{subfig:antenna_mode_scattering_paths}). This last qualifier is important: in this paper, we purposefully do not keep track of the number or type of reflections as a signal travels from one feed terminal to another, but instead group all possible paths together; this framework is detailed in \Cref{app:all_paths_coupling_coefficients}. We represent the antenna-mode scattered field as a load-dependent copy of the neighbor's isolated beam, so that the resulting coupling feature can be written as a linear combination of the neighboring elements' beams; \cref{eq:coupled_voltage_linear_model} assumes coupling of this mode. \emph{Structural-mode} coupling, by contrast, begins with scattering off the neighbor's physical structure, an interaction that does not require excitation of its feed port and would persist even if the structure were not functioning as an antenna at all (\Cref{subfig:structural_mode_scattering_paths}). It is defined as whatever remains of the total scattered field once the antenna-mode contribution is removed. 
