In the single-neighbor case, one writes the scattered field as
\begin{equation}
  \begin{aligned}
    \vec E_{\rm scattered}(Z_L) &= \vec E_{\rm antenna}(Z_L) + \vec E_{\rm structural} \\
    &= \epsilon(Z_L)\,e^{2\pi i(b_x\ell + b_y m)/\lambda}\,\vec E_{\rm iso} + \vec E_{\rm structural},
  \end{aligned}
  \label{eq:load_dependent_scattered_field_decomposition}
\end{equation}
where $\vec b=(b_x,b_y)$ is the baseline vector from the element under consideration to its neighbor. The phase factor accounts for that displacement, so the antenna-mode term corresponds to a translated isolated response in the aperture plane, with the load dependence described by a complex scalar $\epsilon(Z_L)$. This scalar includes the phase accumulated along the coupling paths, but not the direction-dependent interferometric phase written explicitly here. We use $\epsilon$ to denote this scalar deliberately, as it is precisely the coupling coefficient that appeared in \cref{eq:coupled_voltage_linear_model}. In scattering literature, a reference load $Z_L'$ is chosen and the structural field is defined as $\vec{E}_{\rm structural}\equiv\vec{E}_{\rm scattered}(Z_L')$; the antenna-mode coefficient therefore satisfies $\epsilon(Z_L')=0$ by construction. Unless stated otherwise, all passive antenna ports in our simulations are terminated at a reference impedance $Z_0=100~\Omega$; the ``total'' (including both modes) coupled beam is the response with all neighbors at $Z_0$, so that $\epsilon(Z_0)$ is the antenna-mode coefficient at this operating load.

\subsection{A new definition of the antenna-mode coefficients}
\label{subsec:projected_antenna_mode_definition}

The scattering literature offers at least four conventions for the special load $Z_L'$, and hence for the antenna-mode coefficient and the decomposition itself; we review them and compare their results in \cref{app:antenna_mode_load_conventions}. However, rather than choosing among them, we use this ambiguity as license to define the decomposition by what we need it to do: make explicit how much of the coupling perturbation can be represented by a linear combination of isolated responses. This fifth convention, which we call the \emph{projected} definition, has an intuitive form in the aperture plane, where the antenna mode is obtained by projecting the total perturbation onto the isolated pattern translated to the neighbor's position. We first demonstrate the convention in the simple \texttt{lin2} system; in \cref{subsec:array_aperture_mode_decomposition} we generalize to larger arrays. We define 
\begin{equation}
  \epsilon_{\rm proj}(\nu) = \frac{\langle \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x - \vec b),\,\vec A_{\rm tot}(\vec x)-\vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x)\rangle}{\|\vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x)\|^2},
  \label{eq:single_neighbor_projection_coefficient}
\end{equation}
where $\vec b$ is the $14.6$~m east--west baseline vector for the \texttt{lin2} system. The inner product is defined over the aperture plane as $\langle \vec F,\vec G\rangle \equiv \sum_\beta\iint F_\beta^*(x,y)\,G_\beta(x,y)\,dx\,dy$, where $\beta\in\{\theta,\phi\}$ labels the E-field components. The structural mode is the residual orthogonal to this translated isolated-response template,
\begin{equation}
  \Delta \vec A_{\rm sm}(\vec x) \equiv \left(\vec A_{\rm tot}(\vec x) - \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x)\right) - \epsilon_{\rm proj}\, \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x - \vec b).
  \label{eq:projected_structural_aperture_residual}
\end{equation}
The decomposition satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \Delta \vec A = \Delta \vec A_{\rm am} + \Delta \vec A_{\rm sm}, \qquad \langle \Delta \vec A_{\rm am}, \Delta \vec A_{\rm sm} \rangle = 0.
  \label{eq:orthogonal_aperture_mode_decomposition}
\end{equation}
With this projected definition, the structural mode is precisely the perturbation left after removing everything that is shaped like a translated copy of the isolated response. We thus associate the structural mode with an aperture-space residual, the fraction of the original perturbation power that remains when the antenna mode is removed,
\begin{equation}
  r_\epsilon(\nu) \equiv
  \frac{\|\Delta\vec A-\Delta\vec A_{\rm am}\|^2}{\|\Delta\vec A\|^2}
  =\frac{\|\Delta\vec A_{\rm sm}\|^2}{\|\Delta\vec A\|^2},
  \label{eq:aperture_projection_residual_fraction}
\end{equation}
using the same aperture-plane norm as in the projection.

Like the definitions from the literature, the projected definition makes the antenna-mode field proportional to the translated isolated response, but additionally makes the structural residual orthogonal to that template by construction. The same fit could be performed in the far field, using the phase-shifted isolated response in \cref{eq:load_dependent_scattered_field_decomposition} and integrating over $d\ell\,dm$: by Parseval's theorem, the aperture-plane inner product and squared norm both acquire the same factor $\lambda^2$, which cancels in the fitted coefficient. We use the aperture-plane form because it makes the translated templates, and hence the content represented by the model, easier to visualize.

This fifth, ``projected'' definition is the most useful one for our purposes for two reasons. It defines a unique field for the specified inner product using only the isolated and multi-element simulations---without requiring a separate simulation with load $Z_L'$---and it absorbs into the antenna mode exactly the portion of the perturbation that is shaped like a translated isolated response, no more and no less. Meanwhile, its physical interpretation comes with two caveats: the projected antenna-mode field can pick up scattering that is structural and load independent in nature, but that happens to overlap with the span of the translated isolated pattern; and unlike the four load conventions of \Cref{tab:antenna_mode_reference_loads} (\cref{app:antenna_mode_load_conventions}), the projected coefficient has no closed-form expression in terms of the antenna and reference impedances---it is defined operationally, from the full-wave simulations. Neither caveat affects the mitigation strategies outlined in this paper.

\subsection{Decomposition in the aperture plane for arrays}
\label{subsec:array_aperture_mode_decomposition}

The single-neighbor projection of \cref{eq:single_neighbor_projection_coefficient} extends directly to a full array. For a central element $i$ in an array of $N$ antennas at positions $\{\vec p_j\}_{j=1}^{N}$ (taken relative to the central element, so that one of them, $\vec p_i = 0$, is the central element itself, included to capture self-coupling), we define the antenna-mode part of the perturbation as a complex-weighted sum of isolated patterns shifted to each antenna's position,
\begin{equation}
  \Delta \vec A_{\rm am}(\vec x) = \sum_{j=1}^{N} \epsilon_{j}\, \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x - \vec p_j),
  \label{eq:array_antenna_mode_template_expansion}
\end{equation}
with the coefficients $\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_{\rm proj} = (\epsilon_1,\dots,\epsilon_{N})^{\top} \in \mathbb{C}^{N}$ obtained at each frequency by jointly minimizing $r_\epsilon(\nu)$ in \cref{eq:aperture_projection_residual_fraction} over all $N$ complex coefficients. Since the denominator is independent of the coefficients, this is the least-squares fit of the total perturbation $\Delta \vec A = \vec A_{\rm tot} - \vec A_{\rm iso}$ onto the shifted-isolated basis. Explicitly, the coefficients solve the normal equations
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{k=1}^{N} G_{jk}\, \epsilon_k = c_j,
  \qquad
  G_{jk} = \bigl\langle \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x - \vec p_j),\, \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x - \vec p_k) \bigr\rangle,
  \qquad
  c_j = \bigl\langle \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x - \vec p_j),\, \Delta \vec A \bigr\rangle,
  \label{eq:array_projection_normal_equations}
\end{equation}
i.e.\ the projection of \cref{eq:single_neighbor_projection_coefficient} generalized to the (nearly diagonal) Gram matrix of the shifted templates. This multi-neighbor fit preserves the orthogonality between the structural mode and the span of the translated templates.

\Cref{fig:dish_aperture_mode_decomposition} shows the resulting decomposition at 150~MHz, for \texttt{lin2} (top row) and \texttt{hex37} (bottom row): the total perturbation $|\Delta \vec A|^2$, its antenna-mode part $|\Delta \vec A_{\rm am}|^2$, and the structural-mode residual $|\Delta \vec A_{\rm sm}|^2$. In both systems the antenna-mode field is a superposition of translated isolated patterns, yet the individual copies appear slightly distorted in the \texttt{hex37} power map (lower center) because their Gibbs ringing patterns overlap and interfere. The structural mode carries almost all of the perturbation power and is concentrated toward the neighbors' rims, as well as near the dish centers, suggesting a contribution from HERA's large Vivaldi feeds. %
