\section[Coupling correction in uv space]{Coupling correction in $uv$ space}
\label{sec:coupling_in_kernel_space}

The previous sections examine coupling as a perturbation to each element's beam, but interferometric visibilities involve beam \emph{products}, so the aperture-plane budget does not by itself tell us how much contamination a linear model can remove. We now propagate the perturbations into those products and their Fourier transforms, the \emph{beam kernels}, to test the model before introducing sky convolution and baseline sampling in \cref{sec:coupling_in_simulated_visibilities}.

\subsection{Beam kernels}
\label{subsec:beam_kernel_definition}

The visibility measured by two elements with far-field voltage patterns $\vec E_i(\hat s)$ and $\vec E_j(\hat s)$ depends on their separation in wavelengths. We assume a coplanar array and choose the $z$ axis normal to its plane, so that $b_z=0$. We write $\vec u=(u,v)=(b_x,b_y)/\lambda$, where $\lambda$ is the observing wavelength; frequency dependence is omitted thereafter for simplicity. Restricting the sky integral to the front hemisphere, as in our beam reconstruction, gives
\begin{equation}
  V_{ij}(\vec u)= \int_{\rm front} T(\hat s)\,\vec E_i(\hat s)\cdot\vec E_j^*(\hat s)\,e^{-2\pi i(u\ell+vm)}\,d^2\hat s,
  \label{eq:visibility_sky_integral}
\end{equation}
where $\hat s=(\ell,m,n)$ is a unit direction vector, $n=\sqrt{1-\ell^2-m^2}$, and $T$ is the sky brightness. The solid-angle measure is $d^2\hat s=d\ell\,dm/n$.
Using the planar Fourier convention $\mathcal F[f](\vec u)\equiv\iint f(\ell,m)e^{-2\pi i(u\ell+vm)}\,d\ell\,dm$, with functions extended by zero outside the visible disk, the integral factorizes as
\begin{equation}
  V_{ij}(\vec u) = \bigl(\tilde T*K_{ij}\bigr)(\vec u),
  \label{eq:visibility_sky_kernel_convolution}
\end{equation}
where $\tilde T=\mathcal F[T/n]$ and $K_{ij}=\mathcal F[\vec E_i\cdot\vec E_j^*]$. This two-dimensional factorization, with a beam kernel independent of $w=b_z/\lambda$, is exact for the coplanar geometry assumed here. For nonzero $w$, it requires either retaining the additional direction-dependent phase or restricting the field sufficiently that this phase can be neglected. The detailed derivation is presented in \cref{app:beam_kernel_derivation}.

The beam kernel describes how the beam product convolves the sky's Fourier modes. At a given frequency, it is a sky-independent complex pattern on the $uv$ plane. For identical isolated dishes, it peaks at $\vec u=0$ and falls off over a scale set by $D/\lambda$, where $D$ is the dish diameter. The beam kernel has been defined previously, for instance, as the \emph{beam power pattern in $uv$ space} in \citet{morales_matejek_2009}, and in fast holographic deconvolution literature as the $uv$-beam \citep{sullivan_et_al_2012,barry_et_al_2019}. This factorization places the sky in $\tilde T$ and the antenna responses, including coupling, in $K_{ij}$. In aperture-plane terms, the kernel is proportional to the cross-correlation of the two elements' aperture fields, summed over the two E-field components. Writing each field as the isolated response plus a perturbation gives
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  K_{ij}(\vec u)&= \frac{1}{\lambda^2}\int \vec A_i(\vec x)\cdot\vec A_j^*(\vec x-\lambda\vec u)\,d^2x\\
  &= K_{\rm iso}(\vec u)+ \frac{1}{\lambda^2}\int\Big[\vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x)\cdot\Delta\vec A_j^*(\vec x-\lambda\vec u)\\
  &\qquad+\Delta\vec A_i(\vec x)\cdot\vec A_{\rm iso}^*(\vec x-\lambda\vec u)+\Delta\vec A_i(\vec x)\cdot\Delta\vec A_j^*(\vec x-\lambda\vec u)\Big]\,d^2x.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:aperture_cross_correlation_kernel}
\end{equation}
where we omit the mode decomposition for simplicity (restored in \cref{subsec:kernel_mode_decomposition}). \Cref{eq:aperture_cross_correlation_kernel} reduces to the autocorrelation of $\vec{A}_{\rm iso}$ for the simple case of two identical, uncoupled antennas. Mode subscripts on kernels correspond to those on aperture fields throughout: $K_{\rm iso}$ for the isolated autocorrelation, $K_{\rm tot}$ for a fully coupled pair, $K_{\rm am}$ and $K_{\rm sm}$ for synthetic beams carrying the corresponding coupling mode; an unsubscripted $K_{ij}$ refers to generic elements $i$ and $j$. \Cref{fig:isolated_and_coupled_beam_kernels} shows kernels for \texttt{lin2}, in both the dish and feed-only models: the isolated kernel $K_{\rm iso}$, the coupled pair's $K_{\texttt{lin2}}$, and their difference (mode decomposition omitted). The feature changes caused by coupling alter which Fourier modes of the sky each baseline is sensitive to compared to the isolated case. We avoid proposing an intuition for the predicted effect of those features on visibilities, as the affected modes depend on each feature's position relative to the sampled baseline, and their contribution to the visibility depends on the observed sky. We do note that coupling tends to spread the beam kernel's power over a wider region of $uv$ space, which can introduce visibility power at fringe rates not expected from the isolated response and produce image artifacts on finer angular scales.

