\subsection[Mode decomposition and correction in uv space]{Mode decomposition and correction in $uv$ space}
\label{subsec:kernel_mode_decomposition}

To carry the decomposition of \cref{sec:antenna_structural_decomposition} into kernel space, we write
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
  \vec A_{i}(\vec x) &= \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x) \;+\; \Delta\vec A_{{\rm am},i}(\vec x) \;+\; \Delta\vec A_{{\rm sm},i}(\vec x)\\
  &=\sum_{k=1}^{N} M_{ik}\, \vec A_{\rm iso}(\vec x - \vec b_{ik}) \;+\; \Delta\vec A_{{\rm sm},i}(\vec x)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:array_aperture_mixing_and_structural_residual}
\end{equation}
where, in the second line, we have used the fact that the antenna mode consists of shifted copies of the isolated pattern to group them into a matrix-generated contribution: $M_{ik} = \delta_{ik} + \epsilon_{ik}$, $\vec b_{ik} = \vec x_k - \vec x_i$, the $\epsilon_{ik}$ are the projected coefficients of \cref{eq:array_antenna_mode_template_expansion}, and $\delta_{ik}$ is the Kronecker delta. Substituting \cref{eq:array_aperture_mixing_and_structural_residual} into \cref{eq:aperture_cross_correlation_kernel} produces
\begin{equation}
