\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}
Quantum entanglement is one of the most remarkable phenomena predicted by quantum physics: measurements on spatially separated particles can exhibit correlations that cannot be explained by local realistic theories~\cite{PhysRev.47.777}. Greenberger, Horne and Zeilinger asked what happens when more than two particles are entangled~\cite{Greenberger1989}. The resulting Greenberger--Horne--Zeilinger (GHZ) states, such as
\[
    |GHZ_{3,2}\rangle=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left(|000\rangle+|111\rangle\right),
\]
were experimentally observed and used to rule out local-realistic descriptions~\cite{PhysRevLett.82.1345,Pan2000}. Today, GHZ states are central objects in quantum information theory, with applications in quantum secret sharing~\cite{PhysRevA.59.1829}.

{Multipartite entanglement is closely connected to the experimental work for which Anton Zeilinger received the 2022 Nobel Prize in Physics~\cite{nobel}. Increasing both the number of particles and the local dimension of GHZ states is important for foundational tests of quantum mechanics as well as for practical quantum-information tasks. Photonic technology is one of the main experimental platforms for this goal~\cite{Gu2020}. Motivated by this, Krenn, Gu, Zeilinger and co-authors developed graph-based descriptions and automated design methods for quantum-optical experiments~\cite{Quantum_graphs,Quantum_graphs_2,Quantum_graphs_3,krenn2019questions}.}

{The key insight is that a quantum-optical experiment with probabilistic photon sources and linear optics can be encoded by an edge-coloured, edge-weighted graph. Edges represent photon-pair sources, edge colours represent photonic modes, and edge weights represent complex amplitudes. A successful detection event in which every detector receives exactly one photon corresponds to a perfect matching. Thus the quantum question
\[
    \text{``Can one create a high-dimensional GHZ state by such an experiment?''}
\]
becomes a graph-theoretic question about cancellations among weighted perfect matchings.}

{Despite substantial experimental and computational effort, no construction is known that produces a GHZ state of dimension $d>2$ with more than $n=4$ photons, perfect quality, finite count rates, and no additional resources~\cite{krenn2019questions}. This led Krenn and Gu to conjecture that such a construction is impossible. In graph-theoretic terms, their conjecture says that every GHZ graph on more than four vertices has dimension at most $2$; see \Cref{krenn_conjecture}. The conjecture has been widely publicised among graph theorists and quantum-information researchers~\cite{mixon_website,krenn_website}. A counterexample would yield a new high-dimensional multipartite GHZ experiment, while a proof would reveal structural limitations of this photonic model.}

We now state the graph-theoretic formulation. For a high-level explanation of the physical encoding, we refer the reader to Section~2.2 of~\cite{CG1}; for the full quantum-optical model, see~\cite{Quantum_graphs,Quantum_graphs_2,Quantum_graphs_3,krenn2019questions}.

\subsection{Problem definition}
We consider finite loopless undirected multigraphs; parallel edges are allowed, but loops are not.
For a graph $G$, let $V(G)$ and $E(G)$ denote its vertex and edge sets.
For a connected graph $G$, the \emph{edge connectivity} $\lambda(G)$ is the minimum number
of edges whose deletion disconnects $G$, where parallel edges are counted with multiplicity.
We set $\lambda(K_1)=0$.
If $G$ is connected and not complete, the \emph{vertex connectivity} $\kappa(G)$ is the
minimum size of a vertex set $T\subseteq V(G)$ such that $G-T$ is disconnected; for complete
graphs we use the standard convention $\kappa(K_n)=n-1$.
For disconnected graphs, we set $\lambda(G)=\kappa(G)=0$.
We write $\Delta_v(G)$ and $\delta_v(G)$ for the maximum and minimum number of neighbours
of a vertex, and $\Delta_e(G)$ and $\delta_e(G)$ for the corresponding maximum and minimum
numbers of incident edges. For simple graphs, these coincide with the usual maximum and
minimum degrees, denoted $\Delta(G)$ and $\delta(G)$. An edge is \emph{redundant} if it
belongs to no perfect matching. Removing all redundant edges from $G$ gives its
\emph{matching-covered graph}, denoted $mcg(G)$.

An edge colouring $c:E(G)\to\mathbb{N}$ assigns a single colour to each edge; 
physically, the colour represents a photonic mode number. All definitions in this 
paper use this ordinary edge-colouring model. In particular, an edge has one colour, 
not one colour at each endpoint. The more general bichromatic, or half-edge, model 
assigns colours separately to the two ends of an edge and is discussed only when 
comparing with prior work. An edge weight function 
$w:E(G)\to\mathbb{C}\setminus\{0\}$ assigns a non-zero complex amplitude to each 
edge. A graph $G$ together with $c$ and $w$ is denoted by $G_c^w$ and is called an 
edge-coloured, edge-weighted graph. For $e\in E(G)$, $c(e)$ and $w(e)$ denote the 
colour and the weight of $e$, respectively.

