{Every perfect matching $M$ of $G_c^w$ induces a vertex colouring $vc_M:V(G)\to\mathbb{N}$ as follows: each vertex receives the colour of the unique edge of $M$ incident with it. In the quantum-optical interpretation, this records the mode detected at each photon detector. We call $vc_M$ an \emph{inherited vertex colouring}. It is \emph{monochromatic} if all vertices receive the same colour. For a vertex colouring $vc$, let $\mathcal{M}_{vc}$ be the set of perfect matchings inducing $vc$. If $\mathcal{M}_{vc}\neq\emptyset$, then $vc$ is \emph{feasible}; otherwise it is \emph{infeasible}.}

\begin{definition}
{The weight of a perfect matching $M$ is
\[
    w(M)=\prod_{e\in M}w(e).
\]}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
{The weight of an inherited vertex colouring $vc$ is
\[
    w(vc)=\sum_{M\in\mathcal{M}_{vc}}w(M).
\]
If $\mathcal{M}_{vc}=\emptyset$, we set $w(vc)=0$.}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
{An edge-coloured, edge-weighted graph $G_c^w$ is a \emph{GHZ graph} if:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item every feasible monochromatic inherited vertex colouring has weight $1$; and
    \item every non-monochromatic inherited vertex colouring has weight $0$.
\end{enumerate}}
\end{definition}

{Thus, in a GHZ graph, all unwanted non-monochromatic detection patterns cancel by destructive interference, while the desired monochromatic patterns remain with weight $1$. Figure~\ref{fig:perfect_matchings} explains this definition for the graph in Figure~\ref{fig:main_example}. The perfect matchings in Figures~\ref{fig:pm1} and~\ref{fig:pm2} induce the same non-monochromatic inherited vertex colouring $|011000\rangle$, and their weights cancel. The perfect matchings in Figures~\ref{fig:pm3} and~\ref{fig:pm4} induce the monochromatic inherited vertex colourings $|111111\rangle$ and $|000000\rangle$, each with weight $1$. Hence Figure~\ref{fig:main_example} is a GHZ graph of dimension $2$.}

\begin{definition}
The dimension of a GHZ graph $G^w_c$, denoted $\mu(G,c,w)$, is the number of
feasible monochromatic inherited vertex colourings.
\end{definition}

If $G^w_c$ is not a GHZ graph, then $\mu(G,c,w)$ is not defined.  For a
loopless multigraph $G$, let
\[
WC(G)=\{(w,c):G^w_c \text{ is a GHZ graph}\}.
\]
The matching index of $G$ is
\[
\mu(G)=\max_{(w,c)\in WC(G)} \mu(G,c,w),
\]
where the maximum over the empty set is defined to be $0$.

This maximum is taken over the fixed graph $G$ itself: no parallel copies of
edges are added unless they are already present in $G$.  It is easy to see that
\[
\mu(G)=\mu(\operatorname{mcg}(G)).
\]

For a simple graph $H$, if one instead maximizes over all loopless multigraphs
whose skeleton is $H$, we denote the resulting quantity by
$\mu_{\mathrm{skel}}(H)$.  Thus Figure~\ref{fig:k2} shows that
$\mu_{\mathrm{skel}}(K_2)=\infty$.  In the rest of the paper, and in particular
in Theorems~\ref{thm:edge_connectivity}, \ref{thm:vertex_connectivity}, and
\ref{thm:six_eight_vertices}, $\mu(G)$ always denotes the fixed-graph quantity above.

