A graph with a perfect matching has an even number of vertices. Most of our bounds concern graphs with an even number of vertices and at least four vertices; the edge-connectivity bound in \Cref{thm:edge_connectivity} also applies to graphs with two vertices. From \cref{fig:3dimk4}, we know that $\mu(K_4)\geq 3$ and, despite the use of huge computational resources \cite{AI1,AI2,neugebauer}, this is the only known simple graph on at least four vertices, up to isomorphism, with matching index at least $3$. Any graph with a matching index of at least $3$ and $n>4$ vertices would lead to a new GHZ state of dimension at least $3$ with $n>4$ entangled particles.
In the ordinary edge-colouring setting considered here, the Krenn--Gu conjecture asserts that 

\begin{conjecture}[{Krenn--Gu Conjecture}]
\label{krenn_conjecture}
For every loopless multigraph $G$ with $|V(G)|>4$, we have $\mu(G)\le 2$.
\end{conjecture}
{This conjecture has been extensively publicized, including through several cash rewards~\cite{mixon_website,krenn_website}. Its resolution would have direct implications for quantum optics. A counterexample would reveal a new destructive-interference mechanism in multi-photon systems and would immediately give a new construction of high-dimensional multipartite GHZ states. A proof, on the other hand, would identify a fundamental limitation of this optical model and would clarify which additional resources are necessary for producing larger high-dimensional GHZ states. Proving the conjecture for broad graph classes is also valuable, since it narrows the search space for computational tools such as PyTheus~\cite{AI2}.}

\subsection{Related work on the conjecture}
Some special cases of \cref{krenn_conjecture} have been studied by prior work. We discuss those in this subsection. 

\textbf{No destructive interference.} For the case of all edge weights being positive real numbers, it is easy to see that there cannot be any non-monochromatic perfect matching. Therefore, all perfect matchings are monochromatic. Bogdanov \cite{bogdanov,Quantum_graphs} observed that for this case, the matching index of $K_4$ is $3$, and for simple graphs non-isomorphic to $K_4$, the matching index is at most $2$. Due to this result, when there is no destructive interference, every simple matching-covered graph with a perfect matching, non-isomorphic to $K_4$, can achieve a maximum dimension of $1$ or $2$ and thus can be classified into Type $1$ and Type $2$ graphs (See \cite{CG1} for detailed discussion). Chandran and Gajjala~\cite{CG1} gave a structural classification for Type $2$ graphs. They further proved that for any half-edge colouring and edge weight assignment on a simple Type $2$ unweighted uncoloured graph, a dimension of $3$ or more cannot be achieved. The computational aspects of the vertex colourings arising from these experiments were studied by Vardi and Zhang \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2209-13063,VardiZ23}\rev{.}


\textbf{Absence of bichromatic edges or multi-edges.} 
In the full photonic model, the two photons produced by one source may have different 
mode numbers. Graph-theoretically, this is represented by giving the two ends of an 
edge different colours; such an edge is called \emph{bichromatic}, and the resulting 
colouring is sometimes called a \emph{half-edge colouring}. This half-edge model is 
more general than the ordinary edge-colouring model used in Definitions~1--4, where 
each edge has a single colour. The problem becomes easier when bichromatic edges or 
multi-edges are forbidden, and this restriction has led to several lines of work. 
Cervera-Lierta et al.~\cite{CerveraLierta2022designofquantum} used SAT solvers to 
prove that if the number of vertices is $6$ or $8$, the maximum dimension achievable 
is $2$ or, at most, $3$, respectively. They further conjectured that the maximum 
dimension achievable for an $n>4$ vertex graph is less than $\dfrac{n}{2}$ when 
bichromatic edges are absent. Chandran and Gajjala~\cite{CG2} proved that the 
maximum dimension achievable for an $n>4$ vertex simple GHZ graph, even when 
bichromatic edges are allowed, is less than $\dfrac{n}{\sqrt{2}}$.

\textbf{Unrestricted results.} {For the unrestricted setting, where both bichromatic edges and multi-edges are allowed, Chandran, Gajjala and Illickan~\cite{CG3} introduced a graph-reduction framework for the Krenn--Gu conjecture. They used this framework to prove the conjecture for graphs of vertex connectivity at most $2$ and for cubic graphs. Their argument relies on a computer-assisted result of Mantey~\cite{Kevin}, which shows that every graph on four vertices has matching index at most $3$. It remains open to give a short purely combinatorial proof of this four-vertex result.}

\subsection{Our Results}
{Our results give new structural evidence for the Krenn--Gu conjecture by showing that standard graph-connectivity parameters impose strong restrictions on the dimension of GHZ graphs. This is useful from both sides of the graph--physics bridge: it gives new graph-theoretic upper bounds on the matching index, and it rules out broad families of photonic experimental designs as candidates for high-dimensional GHZ-state generation. Such bounds are particularly relevant for computational searches using automated quantum-experiment design tools such as PyTheus~\cite{AI2}.}

Let $\Delta_{v}(G)$ denote the maximum number of neighbours of a vertex over all vertices, and let $\Delta_{e}(G)$ denote the maximum number of edges incident on a vertex over all vertices. Recall that, for multigraphs, $\Delta_{e}(G)\geq \Delta_{v}(G)$.
\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:max_degree}
For a multigraph $G$ with $|V(G)|>4$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $\Delta_{v}(G) \leq 3$, then $\mu(G) \leq 2 \leq |V(G)|-3$.
    \item If $\Delta_{v}(G) \geq 4$, then $\mu(G)\leq \Delta_{v}(G)-2 \leq |V(G)|-3$.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}
{In physical terms, Theorem~\ref{thm:max_degree} rules out the creation, within this graph model, of GHZ states of dimension at least $n-2$ using $n>4$ parties. A weaker bound, $\mu(G)<|V(G)|-1$, also follows from a result of~\cite{Kostochka2012LargeRM}. We prove Theorem~\ref{thm:max_degree} in \Cref{sec:max_degree}.} We also prove bounds in terms of edge connectivity.

{We next restrict attention to simple graphs, in which at most one edge joins any pair of vertices. For these graphs, we prove upper bounds in terms of vertex connectivity, and we obtain new combinatorial proofs for the smallest non-trivial cases.}

