Towards a contradiction, let $\mu(G) \geq 4$. Therefore, there exists a  colouring $c$ and weight function $w$, such that $\mu(G,c,w)\geq 4$. Let four of its non-redundant colours be $1$ (red), $2$ (green), $3$ (blue) and $4$ (brown). Consider the union of a perfect matching of two different colours. Their union is a disjoint union of even cycles with alternating colours. Since $G$ is simple and the matchings are edge-disjoint, every such cycle has length at least $4$. With $8$ vertices, their union is therefore either an $8$-cycle or two $4$-cycles.

\textbf{Case 1.} We first consider the case where the union of any two
monochromatic perfect matchings of different colours is always two disjoint
$4$-cycles.  Consider a red and a green perfect matching.  Their union forms
two disjoint cycles $1234$ and $5678$, where
\[
(1,2),(3,4),(5,6),(7,8)
\]
are red and
\[
(1,4),(2,3),(5,8),(6,7)
\]
are green.

Consider the non-monochromatic vertex colouring in which $1234$ gets colour
red and $5678$ gets colour green.  By \Cref{colour_split_lemma}, this colouring has weight
\[
w(1_{\{1,2,3,4\}})\,w(2_{\{5,6,7,8\}}),
\]
and this weight must be zero.  Hence one of the two factors is zero.  Without
loss of generality, assume
\[
w(1_{\{1,2,3,4\}})=0.
\]
Since the red edges $(1,2)$ and $(3,4)$ are present, this forces the red edges
$(1,3)$ and $(2,4)$ to be present and
\[
w((1,2))w((3,4))+w((1,3))w((2,4))=0.
\]

Now consider the vertex colouring in which $1234$ gets colour green and
$5678$ gets colour red.  Again by \Cref{colour_split_lemma}, one of
$w(2_{\{1,2,3,4\}})$ and $w(1_{\{5,6,7,8\}})$ must be zero.  But
\[
w(2_{\{1,2,3,4\}})=w((1,4))w((2,3))\ne 0.
\]
Therefore
\[
w(1_{\{5,6,7,8\}})=0.
