\section{Introduction}
Superconducting circuits are a leading platform for quantum information processing and simulation \cite{krantz_quantum_2019,Arute2019QuantumSU,kjaergaard_superconducting_2020,devoret_fqi}, necessitating systematic Hamiltonian formalisms describing the dynamics of lumped-element circuits. Historically, circuit theory has guided the discovery of the transmon, fluxonium, GKP, and $0-\pi$ qubit architecture \cite{koch2007,kitaev_encoding,manucharyan_fluxonium_2009,0pi}. Moreover, there are proposals for protected qubits (such as the dualmon \cite{le_doubly_2019}) that require increasingly exotic circuit elements which cannot be readily engineered in near-term devices, such as quantum phase slip junctions. As circuit QED (cQED) architectures are fundamentally designed as lumped-element circuits, a Hamiltonian description of their dynamics directly informs the engineering of qubits and other quantum information processing systems. 

Non-reciprocity, defined as the lack of transmission symmetry when the source and detector are swapped, is fundamental to several superconducting-circuit architectures. The most immediate is qubit readout: the circulators and isolators that protect a qubit from amplifier back-action are intrinsically non-reciprocal devices. However their massive ferrite components occupy substantial real-estate at the mixing-chamber stage of a dilution refrigerator. Using on-chip superconducting non-reciprocal elements, instead, leaves more space for additional qubits or control hardware, but requires a quantization framework for non-reciprocal circuits. Recent work uses Floquet engineering of non-reciprocal couplings to realize high-fidelity parametric gates between detuned qubits \cite{unigate,pargate}, and non-reciprocal elements appear as essential components in proposals for noise-protected qubits \cite{gyratorqubit,grimm_stabilization_2020,stable}. Non-reciprocity also provides a means to break time-reversal symmetry in simulated lattice Hamiltonians, making them a platform for studying chiral topological Bose-Hubbard physics \cite{martinez,roushan_chiral_2017}.

 The gyrator is a linear, non-reciprocal, two-port element that couples the voltage across one port to the current through the other. Widely-used non-reciprocal circuit elements, such as the circulator or isolator, can be synthesized from gyrators and reciprocal elements \cite{carlin}. Moreover, gyrators can model the dynamics of a circuit under Floquet drives (often used to effect synthetic magnetic fields for analog simulation) \cite{martinez,KollarNR,Rosen2024}. Finally, gyrators provide a means to construct circuit elements in otherwise inaccessible parameter regimes. Since its proposal, the gyrator has been known to make circuit elements behave like their electromagnetic dual \cite{tellegen}. Thus, a linear inductor can be realized in a physically small space with a gyrator and a capacitor rather than a relatively massive magnetic coil. The quantum phase slip junction, whose direct fabrication remains a challenge, may instead be realized with a Josephson junction and a gyrator.

The historically dominant circuit quantization schemes, the method of nodes and the method of loops, define the Lagrangian of a circuit in terms of a single coordinate (either node fluxes or loop charges, respectively) and recover the conjugate variable by the Legendre transform \cite{vool_introduction_2017}. However, these methods are plagued by pathological circuit topologies that depend on the coordinate choice and obstruct quantization.  Recent work has extended the Burkard-Koch-DiVincenzo (BKD) framework to non-reciprocal elements \cite{pr2019,rymarz_consistent_2022} and addressed singular circuits through perturbative parasitic capacitances \cite{rymarz_consistent_2022,pr_comment}, but these approaches still demand manual reduction for a broad class of pathological topologies \cite{pr2019} and may implicitly assume extended coordinates (defined on $\mathbb{R}$ rather than $S^1$), an assumption violated by the transmon \cite{pr_comment}. The geometric Faddeev--Jackiw (FJ) formalism solves the general quantization problem for conservative non-reciprocal circuits by constructing Hamiltonians from the kernel of a pre-symplectic two-form, sidestepping parasitic regularization entirely \cite{pr2025}. Meanwhile, the flux-charge symmetric formalism of \cite{fluxcharge} guarantees a Hamiltonian for any well-posed LC circuit by expressing potentials in both flux and charge coordinates, but does not extend to non-reciprocal elements. However, its quantization scheme operates at the Lagrangian level offering physical insights into circuits that are obscured by geometric FJ.

In this paper, we extend the flux-charge symmetric formalism of \cite{fluxcharge} to non-reciprocal circuits. We define a flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian for LCG circuits that admits a symplectic reduction for any nonsingular, well-posed circuit, regardless of non-linearity, together with a taxonomy of constraints that reveals their topological origin before any algebra is performed. Unlike the geometric Faddeev--Jackiw scheme of \cite{pr2025}, which reduces a pre-symplectic form before defining a Lagrangian, our formalism encodes the circuit's structural information in the Lagrangian. Through this, our circuit description enables a series of canonical transformations on their Lagrangian. With these, we extend the notion of circuit duality to non-linear LCG circuits and include a partial-dual point transformation that inserts or removes gyrators from a circuit while preserving the Hamiltonian spectrum. With partial duality, we can define continuous isospectral families of circuits and find an extension of Tellegen's element-mimicking result \cite{tellegen} to non-linear elements such as the Josephson junction and quantum phase slip. With this, circuit dynamics in otherwise inaccessible parameter regimes can be realized. Moreover, we provide a set of gyrator reduction rules congruent with our formalism that act as canonical transformations on the circuit Lagrangian. We use these to provide a graph-theoretic classification of reciprocally-simulable circuits via the non-reciprocal coupling graph (NCG), a derived multigraph whose vertices are the really-connected components (defined in Section \ref{sec:notation}) of the circuit and whose edges record the gyrators connecting them.  We prove that any circuit whose NCG is bipartite admits a unitarily equivalent realization by capacitors, inductors and ideal transformers, and that when the NCG is a forest the realization contains no gyrators at all.

\subsection{Assumptions and circuit descriptors}\label{sec:assumptions}

Three distinct pathologies obstruct circuit quantization: rank-deficient kinetic matrices, dynamically-trivial circuits, and ill-posed constitutive relations. Here, we reconcile the terminology used to describe these problems in the past with our work. However, we must first define the scope of our formalism. If a circuit is comprised of only gyrators, inductors, and capacitors, we say that it is an LCG circuit, our central object of study. For concreteness, we count the coherent quantum phase slip and Josephson junctions as capacitors and inductors respectively. Although we do not explicitly include transformers, they appear in our formalism as composite objects from gyrator cascades (Section \ref{sec:combo}). 

We call a circuit well-posed if its equations of motion (EOM) admit a unique physical solution up to gauge freedom. Thus if a circuit admits no physical solutions or admits multiple distinct solutions it is called ill-posed. Non-linear elements such as a QPS that is not in series with an inductor or a JJ without a parallel capacitance generate constitutive relations of the form $\sin(x) = c x$ for some arbitrary constant $c <1$ for some coordinate $x$ that is not constrained elsewhere. The addition of parasitic elements, series inductance or parallel capacitance, can resolve this in a reciprocal circuit. Meanwhile, two voltage sources with distinct voltages in parallel yield equations that admit no solution. We discuss ill-posedness in the presence of gyrators later, in Section \ref{sec:transformations}. There, we define a transformation on circuit Lagrangians that, as a point transformation, must preserve ill-posedness.

Some well-posed circuits may still lack a Hamiltonian description because they lack dynamics entirely; we call these singular circuits. We discuss singularity in the presence of gyrators and expand on the class of singular circuits in Section \ref{sec:combo}. 

Previously, singularity has been used to refer to the kinetic matrices of a given Lagrangian formalism. In particular, the method of nodes and the method of loops use a capacitance or inductance matrix, respectively, whose inverse defines the Legendre transform \cite{vool_introduction_2017}.  If these matrices are rank-deficient, which are caused by pure-capacitor loops in the method of nodes or pure-inductor cuts in the method of loops, then the Hamiltonian cannot be found without a manual choice of the circuit's canonical coordinates. As we discuss below, our flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian encodes a matrix that is analogous to a kinetic matrix and is, in general, rank-deficient. Nevertheless, our formalism admits a Hamiltonian description  as shown in Section \ref{sec:vars}. 

Finally, we discuss units in our scheme. The flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian developed in the following sections treats node fluxes and loop charges on equal footing, and the duality transformation of Section~\ref{sec:transformations} exchanges them outright. To make this exchange dimensionally consistent we adopt the convention that a reference conductance $G_0$ has been set to unity: with $G_0 = 1$, the ratio of flux units to charge units is dimensionless, so flux and charge become numerically interchangeable. All Lagrangians and Hamiltonians in this paper should be read as scalar-valued functions of dimensionless coordinates in this convention. Physical predictions are recovered by restoring $G_0$ in any expression where dimensions must be carried explicitly; for a circuit containing a single gyrator of ratio $G_*$, the choice $G_0 = G_*$ makes that gyrator's partial dual map (Section \ref{sec:rules}) numerically parameter-preserving; with several gyrators, or when a gyration ratio is varied as in Section \ref{sec:family}, $G_0$ is held fixed and each $G_\fg/G_0$ is a dimensionless coupling.
\subsection{Notation}\label{sec:notation}
We first present a brief overview of our notation essential to the main body of this text. A summary table is provided at the end (Table \ref{tab:notation}) for reference. We relegate the majority of formal definitions and proofs to the appendices. 

A circuit is represented as an oriented planar embedded multigraph $G = (\mathcal V, \mathcal E, \mathcal F)$, with vertices $\mathcal V$, edges $\mathcal E$, and loops $\mathcal F$. We assume that each connected subgraph of a circuit's graph is planar so that loops coincide with faces of its component's embedding and so that, following \cite{fluxcharge}, the duality transformation in Section \ref{sec:transformations} is well-defined. As every loop bounds a unique face of its component's embedding, we do not distinguish between faces and loops and we use $\mathcal F$ for both, a prescription justified in \cite{fluxcharge}. For $v $ in $\mathcal V$, we denote $|v\rangle$ to mean a vector in a $|\mathcal V|$ dimensional real vector space satisfying $\langle u | v\rangle = \delta_{uv}$. We adopt similar meanings for $|e\rangle$ and $|l\rangle$ which span the vector spaces $\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{E}|}$ and $\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{F}|}$, respectively.

The incidence matrix $A$ and orientation matrix $B$ are linear maps on the vector spaces defined above that also satisfy a boundary map condition: 

\begin{subequations}
\label{eqn:boundary_matrices}
\begin{align}
    A_{ev} &=
  \begin{cases}
    +1 & e\text{ directed toward } v \\
    -1 & e\text{ directed away from } v \\
    0  & v \notin e
  \end{cases} \label{eqn:A}\\
     B_{le} &=
  \begin{cases}
    +1 & e, l \text{ oriented alike} \\
    -1 & e, l \text{ oriented unalike} \\
    0  & e \notin l.
  \end{cases}\label{eqn:B}  
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
Equivalently, $A_{ev} = \langle e|A|v\rangle$ and $B_{le} = \langle l|B|e\rangle$. The defining algebraic property of $A$ and $B$ is exactness,
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:exact}
  \ker(B) = \mathrm{im}(A),
\end{equation}
which encodes Kirchhoff's laws as the statement that the composition $BA$ vanishes because the chain complex 
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb R^{|\mathcal V|} \xrightarrow{\,A\,} \mathbb R^{|\mathcal E|} \xrightarrow{\,B\,} \mathbb R^{|\mathcal F|}
\end{equation}
is exact at $\mathbb R^{|\mathcal E|}$ \cite{bamberg_sternberg_v2, branin1966,osborne2023symplectic, fluxcharge}.

We orient circuits so that loops in $\mathcal F$ bound faces uniformly on each component's sphere; see Figure \ref{fig:generic} for an example of the convention. We may further consider a circuit to be constructed from many disjoint connected subgraphs. As will become clear later, our gyrators allow coupling of the dynamics between graph-theoretically disconnected sub-circuits. 
We say that two vertices $u_0,u_1 \in \mathcal{V}$ are \textit{really-connected} if there exists a sequence of vertices $(v_0,v_1,...,v_n)$ such that $v_0 = u_0, \;v_n = u_1$, and for each $1 \leq i \leq n$, the edge $(v_{i-1},v_i) \in \mathcal{E}$ or $(v_i,v_{i-1}) \in \mathcal{E}$. In other words, vertices are really-connected if a path of edges exist between them. This is identical to vertices being "connected" in the graph-theoretic sense; we add this qualifier as gyrators can couple the dynamics between disjoint subgraphs and should not be thought of as "really-connecting" the subgraphs. 

Really-connectedness is an equivalence relation on $\mathcal V$ whose equivalence classes are exactly the $k \geq 1$ disjoint connected components which we will denote with $\mathcal V^{(i)}$ for $i = 1,2,\dots,k$. This partition of the vertices naturally induces a partition of $\mathcal E$: 
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal E^{(i)} = \{e = (u,v) \in \mathcal E \,:\, \{u,v\} \subset \mathcal V^{(i)}\}
\end{equation} 

More generally, $\mathcal X^{(i)}$ with a parenthesized index denotes the restriction of $\mathcal X$ to the $i$-th really-connected component of the graph. Each really-connected component $G^{(i)} = (\mathcal V^{(i)}, \mathcal E^{(i)})$ is regarded as a planar graph embedded on its own sphere $S^2$, and we define $\mathcal F^{(i)}$ to be the face set of that intrinsic embedding, including its own outer face. We take the global loop set to be the disjoint union
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:floopset}
    \mathcal F \;=\; \bigsqcup_{i} \mathcal F^{(i)},
\end{equation}
so that a circuit drawing with $k$ really-connected components carries $k$ outer-face charges rather than one shared outer face. We will maintain our convention of using a superscript to distinguish between different really-connected components. The boundary maps of the connected subgraphs then assemble into the boundary maps of the entire graph,
\begin{subequations}\label{eqn:bmapdecomp}
  \begin{align}
    B &= \bigoplus_{i} B^{(i)} \\
    A &= \bigoplus_i A^{(i)},
  \end{align}
\end{subequations}
with each block exact, $\ker B^{(i)} = \mathrm{im}\, A^{(i)}$, such that $\ker B = \mathrm{im}\, A$. By Euler's formula for a connected planar graph embedded in a sphere, each component satisfies $|\mathcal V^{(i)}| - |\mathcal E^{(i)}| + |\mathcal F^{(i)}| = 2$; each component loop set therefore carries one loop more than its number of independent loops, so that
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:facecount}
    |\mathcal F| = |\mathcal E| - |\mathcal V| + 2k .
\end{equation}
This extra loop per component is what supplies the gauge freedoms counted in Section
\ref{sec:vars} and the duality of Section \ref{sec:transformations}. Explicitly,
\begin{subequations}\label{eqn:gaugekernels}
  \begin{align}
    \ker A^{(i)} &= \mathrm{span}\Big\{\sum_{v \in \mathcal V^{(i)}} \ket v\Big\}\\
    \ker \left(B^{(i)}\right)^\intercal &= \mathrm{span}\Big\{\sum_{l \in \mathcal F^{(i)}} \ket l\Big\},
  \end{align}
\end{subequations}
the first by connectedness of the component and the second because each edge borders two of the component's faces with opposite induced orientations, with no further relation among its loops by (\ref{eqn:facecount}) and rank-nullity. Each kernel is one-dimensional, so the number of null vectors of this sort is precisely twice the number of really-connected components. 

The manifestation of Kirchhoff's rules becomes clearest when we adopt the standard convention that charges and fluxes will be denoted by $q$ and $\phi$ respectively. Particularly, we define these charges and fluxes on topological objects as indicated by its subscript; thus we introduce node fluxes $\phi_v$ and loop charges $q_l$.
We write them as
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
      |\phi\rangle &= \sum_v \phi_v|v\rangle\\
      |q\rangle &= \sum_l q_l|l\rangle
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
Edge variables are derived from boundary maps acting on these variables: the edge flux on edge $e$ is $\Phi_e = \sum_v A_{ev}\phi_v = \langle e|A|\phi\rangle$, and the edge charge is $Q_e = \sum_l q_l B_{le} = \langle q|B|e\rangle$. Together, $|\phi\rangle$ and $|q\rangle$ parameterize a redundant phase space with more coordinates than a complete description of dynamics requires. We describe the reduction of coordinates to a phase space in Section \ref{sec:vars}. 

As this work restricts to LCG circuits, we partition the set of edges in a circuit diagram according to the form of their constitutive relations:
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal E = \mathcal C \sqcup \mathcal I \sqcup \mathcal G,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal C$, $\mathcal I$, and $\mathcal G$ are the sets of capacitive, inductive, and gyrative edges respectively. 
We write
$\mathbb P_{\mathcal C}$, $\mathbb P_{\mathcal I}$, $\mathbb P_{\mathcal G}$ for
the orthogonal projectors onto the corresponding edge-space subspaces, so that
$\mathbb P_{\mathcal X} = \sum_{e \in \mathcal X} |e\rangle\langle e|$ for
$\mathcal X \in \{\mathcal C, \mathcal I, \mathcal G\}$ and importantly:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:projector}
    \mathbb P_{\mathcal C} + \mathbb P_{\mathcal I} + \mathbb P_{\mathcal G}
= \mathbb I,
\end{equation}
the identity matrix. 
We may further partition edges by both their type and by the really-connected component they inhabit:
\begin{subequations}
  \begin{align}
    \mathcal C^{(i)} &= \mathcal C \cap \mathcal E^{(i)} \\ 
    \mathcal I^{(i)} &= \mathcal I \cap \mathcal E^{(i)} \\ 
    \mathcal G^{(i)} &= \mathcal G \cap \mathcal E^{(i)} 
  \end{align}
\end{subequations} 
