Unlike the capacitor and inductor, which are one-port elements, the gyrator is a two-port circuit element coupling the voltage of one port to the current of the other. We therefore regard gyrators graph-theoretically as ordered pairs of edges (Figure \ref{fig:symbol}). The set of gyrator edge pairs of a circuit, which we denote $\fG$, is a collection of ordered edge pairs
\begin{equation}
    \fG \subseteq \mathcal G \times \mathcal G
\end{equation}
that are pairwise disjoint and together cover $\mathcal G = \bigcup_{\mathfrak g \in \fG} \mathfrak g$. 
To each gyrator edge pair $\mathfrak g = (e_1, e_2) \in \fG$ we associate a gyration ratio $G_{\mathfrak g} \in \mathbb R$ with units of conductance; reversing the ordering of an edge pair flips the sign of $G_{\mathfrak g}$, so the edge pairs are taken to be ordered. In conventional notation, the gyrator is additionally labeled by its gyration ratio and an arrow that gives the sign of the constitutive relations. Although we include it in Figure \ref{fig:symbol}, the same information is encoded by the orientation of a gyrator's edges in our notation and thus we exclude it thereafter. Supposing that the gyrator couples the flux and charge through $e_1 = (v_1,v_2)$ to the charge and flux through $e_2 = (v_3,v_4)$, the gyrator has the constitutive relations 
\begin{subequations}\label{eqn:gyrconst}
  \begin{align}
    G_{\mathfrak g}\dot \Phi_{e_1} &=  \dot Q_{e_2} \\
    -G_{\mathfrak g}\dot \Phi_{e_2} &=  \dot Q_{e_1}.
  \end{align}
\end{subequations} 
Only one condition in (\ref{eqn:gyrconst}) is strictly necessary, since the second follows from the first and conservation of energy. Note that the two edges in a gyrator edge pair need not lie in the same really-connected component, thus necessitating the distinction provided by the term.

