\subsection{Brief review of flux-charge symmetric circuit mechanics}\label{sec:review}
Lagrangian systems admit a simple treatment of constraints via Lagrange multipliers, while Hamiltonian descriptions require Dirac brackets to do the same. Lumped-element circuit models generally have many nontrivial constraints arising from either Kirchhoff's laws or constitutive relations manifesting as an excess of coordinate freedom. As such, it is not clear how to produce a Hamiltonian directly from a circuit diagram. Instead we begin with a Lagrangian description and integrate out constraints, incrementally paring down configuration space into a suitable phase space in what amounts to a Hamiltonian principle of least action.

The flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian for reciprocal LC circuit \cite{fluxcharge} is given by 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:fc}
  \mathscr L = \sum_{l,v} q_l M_{lv} \dot\phi_v
  - E
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:connection}
  M = \tfrac{1}{2} B(\mathbb P_{\mathcal C} - \mathbb P_{\mathcal I}) A,
\end{equation}
is the so-called connection matrix. 
The first term of (\ref{eqn:fc}) is called the symplectic term, and $E$ is the sum of energies stored in circuit elements written entirely in terms of $\phi_v$ and $q_l$ variables, which we will call the energetic term. 

The principle of least action gives 
\begin{subequations}\label{eqn:lac}
\begin{align}
    0 &= \sum_v M_{lv} \dot \phi_v  - \frac{\partial E}{\partial q_l}, \\ 
    0 &= -\sum_l   \dot q_l M_{lv} - \frac{\partial E}{\partial \phi_v}.
\end{align}    
\end{subequations}
Supposing for the moment that the connection matrix is invertible, we define 
\begin{equation}
\{f,g\} = \sum_{l,v} (M^{-1})_{vl}\left( \frac{\partial f}{\partial \phi_v}\frac{\partial g}{\partial q_l} - \frac{\partial f}{\partial q_l}\frac{\partial g}{\partial \phi_v}\right),
\end{equation}
for any function $f$ and $g$.
Hence, (\ref{eqn:lac}) can be rewritten succinctly as $\dot f = \{f, E\}$ for any function $f$ of configuration variables. 
The procedure of inverting $M$ in order to define a Poisson bracket is precisely the Legendre transform.
In reality $M$ fails to be invertible, so we must identify vectors $c_v$  such that $\sum_{l,v} M_{lv} c_v = 0$, which give rise to algebraic constraints of the form 
\begin{equation}
    \sum_{v } c_v \frac{\partial E}{\partial \phi_v} = 0.
\end{equation}
Solving such a constraint and rewriting the Lagrangian on-shell reduces the number of coordinates in the symplectic term which must nonetheless remain linear, effectively reducing the size of the connection matrix without reducing its rank. Repeating this procedure for all such null vectors of $M$ yields a symplectic term with a genuinely invertible connection matrix, allowing a straightforward Legendre transform and subsequent canonical quantization.
In the flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian formalism, there is one such algebraic constraint for each independent purely capacitive loop, purely capacitive cut, purely inductive loop, and purely inductive cut, together with the gyrator-mixing constraints of Section \ref{sec:vars}. The assumption of well-posedness is equivalent to the assertion that all such constraints admit unique solutions. 

The energetic term of the Lagrangian can be further decomposed as 
\begin{equation}
    E = \sum_{e \in \mathcal C} E_e^C\left( \langle q | B|e \rangle \right) + \sum_{e \in \mathcal I} E_e^I\left(\langle e|A |\phi\rangle\right)
\end{equation}
where $E_e^C$ ($E_e^I$) is the energy stored in the capacitor (inductor) on edge $e$.
Importantly, the flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian formalism admits a straight-forward notion of circuit duality. 
If $\mathscr L(q_l, \phi_v)$ is the flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian of some circuit, then $\mathscr{L}(\phi_v, q_l)$ is the flux-charge symmetric Lagrangian of its dual. 

\section{Flux--charge symmetric description of gyrators}\label{sec:flux_charge_LCG}

We seek a generic procedure for writing Lagrangians with gyrators that produces the constitutive relations while satisfying Kirchhoff's laws for nonsingular well-posed circuits. 
For every gyrator edge pair $\mathfrak{g} \in \mathfrak{G}$, we introduce the following term to represent its contribution to the Lagrangian:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:gyrterm}
  \mathscr{L}_\mathfrak{g} = \frac{G_\mathfrak{g}}{2}\sum_{u,v}\phi_u A_{e_1u}A_{e_2v}\dot\phi_v - \frac{1}{2G_\mathfrak{g}}\sum_{l,l'} q_l B_{le_1}B_{l'e_2}\dot q_{l'}.
\end{equation}
For a gyrator edge pair $\fg = (e_1, e_2)$ with ratio $G_\fg$, we set $G_{(e_2,e_1)} := -G_{(e_1,e_2)}$. Then $\LL_\fg$ is unchanged under reversal of the pair ordering and reversal of the orientation of either gyrative edge accompanied by $G_\fg \to -G_\fg$. 

We may alternatively write the contribution of $\LL_\fg$ in ket-notation, equal to Equation \ref{eqn:gyrterm} up to a total time derivative, to make the properties of the boundary maps more explicit. For $\mathfrak g = (e_1, e_2) \in \fG$, define
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:gdefn}
  \Gamma_{\mathfrak g} = |e_1\rangle\langle e_2| - |e_2\rangle\langle e_1|,
\end{equation}
which we will refer to as the gyrator's antisymmetric form. We then may write the contribution of a single gyrator to a Lagrangian as:
\begin{equation}
  \mathscr{L}_\mathfrak{g} = \frac{1}{4} \left(G_\mathfrak{g}\langle \phi| A^\intercal \Gamma_\mathfrak{g} A |\dot\phi\rangle - G_\mathfrak{g}^{-1}\langle q| B \Gamma_\mathfrak{g} B^\intercal |\dot q\rangle \right)
\end{equation} 
Direct computation gives $\Gamma_{\mathfrak g}^2 = -(|e_1\rangle\langle e_1| + |e_2\rangle\langle e_2|)$, and since distinct gyrator edge pairs are disjoint, $\Gamma_{\mathfrak g}\Gamma_{\mathfrak g'} = 0$ whenever $\mathfrak g \neq \mathfrak g'$. Summing over $\fG$ yields the identity
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:gammaidents}
  \sum_{\mathfrak g \in \fG} \Gamma_{\mathfrak g}^2 = -\mathbb P_{\mathcal G}
\end{equation}
which we invoke throughout Section \ref{sec:flux_charge_LCG}. 

We will study the circuit Lagrangian 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:no_omega_lagrangian}
  \mathscr L = \langle q | M | \dot \phi\rangle + \sum_{\mathfrak{g} \in \fG} \mathscr{L}_\mathfrak{g} - \sum_{e \in \mathcal C} E_e^C - \sum_{e \in \mathcal I} E_e^I
\end{equation} 
where $E_e^C$ is the energy associated with a capacitor on edge $e$ and must depend only upon $Q_e$, and likewise  $E_e^I $ is the energy stored in an inductor on edge $e$ and may depend only upon $\Phi_e $. 

We have chosen $\mathscr L_{\mathfrak g}$ as notation to emphasize the fact that gyrators do not store energy. Moreover, we remark that the symplectic term together with the contributions to the Lagrangian from gyrators admits an uncomplicated block structure reminiscent of (\ref{eqn:fc}). Namely, 
\begin{equation}
\langle q | M |\dot\phi\rangle + \sum_{\mathfrak g \in \fG} \mathscr L _{\mathfrak g} =
\frac{1}{4}
\begin{pmatrix} \langle \phi | & \langle q | \end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
    \sum_{\mathfrak{g} \in \mathfrak{G}} G_\mathfrak{g}A^\intercal \Gamma_\mathfrak{g}A & -2M^\intercal \\
    2M & -\sum_{\mathfrak{g} \in \mathfrak{G}} G_\mathfrak{g}^{-1}B\Gamma_\mathfrak{g}B^\intercal
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix} |\dot \phi \rangle \\ | \dot q \rangle \end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
which is clearly antisymmetric.

There are a pair of block decompositions that will be valuable to us: 
firstly, 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:omega}
\Omega = 
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \sum_{\mathfrak{g} \in \mathfrak{G}} G_\mathfrak{g}A^\intercal \Gamma_\mathfrak{g}A & -2M^\intercal \\ 2M & -\sum_{\mathfrak{g} \in \mathfrak{G}} G_\mathfrak{g}^{-1}B\Gamma_\mathfrak{g}B^\intercal
    \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
is antisymmetric and immediately calls to mind \cite{faddeevjackiw} and the technology that can be invoked therein. 
Secondly, we may write
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:upsilon}
    \sigma \Omega =    ( B \oplus A^\intercal) \Theta (A \oplus B^\intercal)
\end{equation}
where $\sigma: \mathbb R^{|\mathcal V|}\oplus\mathbb R^{|\mathcal F|} \to \mathbb R^{|\mathcal F|}\oplus\mathbb R^{|\mathcal V|}$, $(x,y)\mapsto(y,x)$, is the block transposition, and with
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Thetablock}
    \Theta = 
    \begin{pmatrix}
         \mathbb{P}_\mathcal{C} - \mathbb{P}_\mathcal{I} & -\sum_{\mathfrak{g}\in \mathfrak{G}} G_\mathfrak{g}^{-1} \Gamma_\mathfrak{g} \\ 
         \sum_{\mathfrak{g}\in \mathfrak{G}} G_\mathfrak{g} \Gamma_\mathfrak{g} & \mathbb{P}_\mathcal{I} - \mathbb{P}_\mathcal{C} \\ 
     \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Of note, $\Theta$ is an involution satisfying $\Theta^2 = 1$ and hence $\sigma \Omega$ is the involution $\Theta$ conjugated by a pair of maps satisfying an exactness condition. As $\sigma$ is an isometry, $\ker\Omega = \ker\sigma\Omega$.

\subsection{Correctness and coordinate choices}\label{sec:coor}


A Lagrangian is ``correct'' if and only if it produces the correct EOM on configuration space. In our case, these EOM are precisely Kirchhoff's laws and the constitutive relations of circuit elements. 
We begin by showing that the circuit constitutive relations follow. 

Take (\ref{eqn:no_omega_lagrangian}), and observe that, for a particular loop $l$, 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:capconst}
    \frac{\delta}{\delta q_l} \int \mathrm d t \mathscr L = \langle l | M | \dot \phi \rangle +  \frac{\delta}{\delta q_l}\int\mathrm dt \sum_{\fg \in \mathfrak{G}}\mathscr L_\fg  - \sum_{e \in \mathcal C} \frac{\partial E_e^{C}}{\partial q_l}
\end{equation}
Rewriting $M = B(\mathbb P_{\mathcal C} + \frac{1}{2}\mathbb P_{\mathcal G} )A$, we find that 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:vecconst}
     \frac{\delta}{\delta q_l} \int \mathrm d t \mathscr L = 
     \sum_{e \in \mathcal C, v} B_{le} \left[ 
       A_{ev} \dot\phi_v - (E_e^C)' 
     \right]
     + 
     \frac{1}{2}\sum_{e \in \mathcal G}B_{le}\left[
        A_{ev}\dot\phi_v - G_{(e,e')}^{-1} \sum_{l'}B_{l'e'}\dot q_{l'}
        \right],
\end{equation}
where $(E_e^C)'$ denotes the derivative of $E_e^C$ with respect to its argument $\sum_l q_l B_{le}$ and $e'$ is the partner in a gyrator edge pair to $e$ in $\mathcal G$. 
We recognize that, since (\ref{eqn:vecconst}) holds on-shell for any $l$, it specifies a right null vector of $B$.  
By the exactness of $B$ and $A$, it follows that each term in the sum above vanishes individually up to the trivial exception that there may exist nodes in a circuit (such as a node connecting two capacitors in series) whose flux does not appear in $\mathscr L$ and may satisfy (\ref{eqn:vecconst}) at any value. See \cite{fluxcharge,osborne2024stochastic} as well as Section \ref{sec:vars} for more discussion. 
The other constitutive relations follow analogously by rearranging $\frac{\delta S}{\delta \phi_v}$ in place of $\frac{\delta S}{\delta q_l}$, demonstrating the validity of (\ref{eqn:gyrterm}) and its agreement with \cite{pr2025}. 

On the other hand, writing $M = -B(\mathbb P_{\mathcal I} + \frac{1}{2} \mathbb P_{\mathcal G})A$ instead yields 
\begin{equation}
    0 =-\sum_{e \in \mathcal I} B_{le} A_{ev}\dot\phi_v -\sum_{e \in \mathcal C} B_{le} (E_e^C)' 
     -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{e \in \mathcal G}B_{le}\left[
        A_{ev}\dot\phi_v + G_{(e,e')}^{-1} \sum_{l'}B_{l'e'}\dot q_{l'}
        \right].
\end{equation}
The first term is transparently the voltage differences across inductors along the loop $l$. The second and third terms may also be interpreted as voltage differences across capacitors and gyrators respectively, now that we have established the imposition of their constitutive relations. Analogously, $\frac{\delta S}{\delta \phi_v}$ imposes conservation of current at a vertex. 

Of course, there are many correct Lagrangians to describe a given electrical circuit. Trivially, the addition of a total time derivative leaves dynamics unchanged. We are also free to introduce and integrate out Lagrange multipliers. For concreteness write $\mathscr L$ as in (\ref{eqn:no_omega_lagrangian}), and consider 
\begin{equation}
    \mathscr L' = \mathscr L + \sum_{e \in \mathcal C \cup \mathcal G} \lambda_e \left(\sum_l q_l B_{l e} - Q_e\right).
\end{equation}
Since $\mathscr L$ has no dependence upon $Q_e$, this change of variables does not affect dynamics and gives rise to only a point transformation. After integrating out $\lambda_e$, we find 
\begin{equation}
    \mathscr L' = \left(\sum_{e \in \mathcal C,v}+
    \frac{1}{2} \sum_{e \in \mathcal G}\right)
    Q_e A_{ev} \dot\phi_v 
    + \sum_{\fg = (e,e')}\Big(\frac{G_{\fg}}{2}\sum_{u,v}  \phi_u A_{eu}A_{e'v}\dot\phi_v  -  \frac{1}{2G_{\fg}}\, Q_e \dot Q_{e'} \Big)
    - \sum_{e \in \mathcal C} E_e^C(Q_e) - \sum_{e \in \mathcal I} E_e^I\left(\sum_v A_{ev}\phi_v\right).
\end{equation}
Since dynamical variables live on edges and vertices in these coordinates, we say that these are edge-vertex coordinates. It is also possible to define face-edge coordinates via the Lagrange multiplier 
\begin{equation}
    \mathscr L' = \mathscr L + \sum_{e \in \mathcal I \cup \mathcal G} \lambda_e \left(\Phi_e- \sum_v A_{ev}\phi_v\right).
\end{equation}

Since the only Lagrangian terms coupling distinct really-connected components are gyrator terms, we are free to choose coordinates on each really-connected component independently. For the remainder of this paper, we will not belabor this Lagrange multiplier manipulation and only remark on it when technically necessary. We emphasize that one may just as easily describe circuit Lagrangians in terms charges on vertices and fluxes on loops which preserve the dynamical description of the circuit despite changing the form of the EOM.

\subsection{Constraints and Quantization}\label{sec:vars}

The problems of constraint resolution and circuit quantization are equivalent provided that the reader is accepting of canonical quantization. 
In the present setting, the question of quantizability has been resolved by \cite{pr2025}, but we nonetheless include some discussion of constraints here. 
In order to manipulate Lagrange's equation into a form compatible with Hamiltonian dynamics, $\Omega$ must be invertible, but it is not. 
Hence, there must arise constraints revealing relationships between overcomplete degrees of freedom, which must in turn be solved in order to reduce the size of $\Omega$ without decreasing its rank. 

To illustrate this point, notice that for any linear combination of functional derivatives 
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\delta}{\delta X} := \sum_v c_v \frac{\delta }{\delta \phi_v} + \sum_l d_l \frac{\delta }{\delta q_l},
\end{equation} 
the principle of least action enforces that, on-shell,
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:plac}
  \frac{\delta }{\delta X} \int \mathrm d t \left(\langle q | M | \dot\phi \rangle + \sum_{\fg \in \fG} \mathscr L_{\fg}\right) = \frac{\delta }{\delta X} \int \mathrm d t \, E = \sum_v c_v \frac{\partial E}{\partial \phi_v} + \sum_l d_l \frac{\partial E}{\partial q_l}.
\end{equation} 
If the left hand side of (\ref{eqn:plac}) vanishes identically, the resulting equation of motion  is called a \emph{null vector constraint} and is purely algebraic in the sense that it does not depend on any time differentiated variable. A central theme of \cite{osborne2023symplectic,fluxcharge} is that there is one such algebraic constraint for every null vector of $\Omega$. 
Effectively, null vectors of $\Omega$ encode Lagrange multipliers inherited from Kirchhoff's laws and constitutive relations alone, which we can use to reduce the total number of variables appearing in $\mathscr L$. 
Although it is always possible to find the null vectors of a matrix numerically, for example, the solubility of the resulting algebraic constraint is not immediate. In particular the assumption of well-posedness is equivalent to the assumption that algebraic constraints admit unique solutions. Solubility aside, null vector constraints always encode some circuit simplification that can be carried out (at least in principle) on the diagram itself and hence we nonetheless discuss the details of what kinds of null vector constraints can arise. 

From the block structure of $\Omega$ in (\ref{eqn:omega}), it is clear that the kernel of $\Omega$ decomposes into two structurally distinct sources. Any $\ket{n} \in \ker(A\oplus B^\intercal )$ satisfies $\Omega\ket{n} = 0$ trivially: these are gauge directions inherited from the boundary maps themselves, namely the right null vectors of $A^{(i)}$ and $(B^{(i)})^\intercal$ for each really-connected component.
These null vectors correspond to the freedom to shift a global flux on each really-connected component's vertex set and a global charge on its loop set. By Equation \ref{eqn:gaugekernels}, each really-connected component contributes one null vector of each kind. The remaining null vectors have genuine dynamical content and are best dissected by examining $\Omega$ in the form of Equation \ref{eqn:upsilon}, where their structure is governed entirely by the central matrix $\Theta$. First we will consider arbitrary partitions of loops and vertices as $L \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ and $U \subseteq \mathcal{V}$ respectively. Additionally, we write:
\begin{subequations}
    \begin{align}
          \ket L &= \sum_{l \in L \subset \mathcal F} \alpha_l B^\intercal \ket{l}\\
          \ket U &= \sum_{v \in U \in V}\alpha_v A\ket v
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
for $\alpha_i \in \mathbb{R}$. Every $\ket x = (\ket\phi, \ket q)^\intercal$ determines a pair of edge vectors $\ket\lambda = (A\ket\phi,\, B^\intercal\ket q)^\intercal = (\ket U, \ket L)^\intercal$, and by (\ref{eqn:gaugekernels}) the map $\ket x \mapsto \ket\lambda$ has a$2k$-dimensional kernel, namely the gauge directions. From (\ref{eqn:upsilon}), $\sigma\Omega\ket x = (B \oplus A^\intercal)\, \Theta\ket\lambda$, so $\ket x$ lies in $\ker\sigma\Omega = \ker\Omega$ precisely when $\Theta \ket\lambda \in \ker(B \oplus A^\intercal)$. By exactness that kernel is $\mathrm{im}(A \oplus B^\intercal)$, so the condition is that $\Theta\ket\lambda$ is again a pair of the same form:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:kerchar}
    \Theta \begin{pmatrix} \ket U \\ \ket L \end{pmatrix}
    = \begin{pmatrix} A\ket{\phi'} \\ B^\intercal \ket{q'} \end{pmatrix}
    \quad\text{for some } \ket{\phi'},\, \ket{q'} .
\end{equation}
The dynamical null vectors are therefore exactly those $\ket\lambda = (\ket U, \ket L)^\intercal$ that $\Theta$ returns to that form, and $\dim\ker\Omega$ exceeds the number of such independent $\ket\lambda$ by the $2k$ gauge directions. Because $\Theta^2 = 1$, $\Theta$ maps this collection of $\ket\lambda$ bijectively onto itself, and an involution on a space splits it into its $+1$ and $-1$ eigenspaces; the collection therefore has a basis of vectors satisfying
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Thetaeigen}
    \Theta\ket\lambda = \pm\ket\lambda .
\end{equation}
We classify the dynamical null vectors through such a basis. Note that a general null vector is a sum of a $+1$ and a $-1$ eigenvector and need not itself be an eigenvector, so (\ref{eqn:Thetaeigen}) characterizes a spanning set rather than every null vector.

Writing $\Theta$ as the block matrix from \eqref{eqn:Thetablock} and expanding \eqref{eqn:Thetaeigen}  gives the pair of conditions
\begin{subequations}\label{eqn:Thetaeigen_expanded}
    \begin{align}
    (\mathbb P_{\mathcal C} - \mathbb P_{\mathcal I})\ket{U} - \sum_{\mathfrak g \in \fG} G_{\mathfrak g}^{-1}\,\Gamma_{\mathfrak g}\,\ket{L} &= \pm  \ket{U}\\
        \sum_{\mathfrak g \in \fG} G_{\mathfrak g}\,\Gamma_{\mathfrak g}\,\ket{U} + (\mathbb P_{\mathcal I} - \mathbb P_{\mathcal C})\ket{L} &= \pm \ket{L}.
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}

Recall that $\ket{\lambda} \in \mathbb{R}^{2|\mathcal{E}|}$ and exist on a space of vectors representing edges (circuit elements). Our first structurally distinct type of $\Theta$ eigenvector then corresponds to cuts or loops composed of only a single element class which we call the \textit{pure null vectors}. The pure null vectors are supported on exactly one of the coordinates block such that they take the form $\ket\lambda = (\ket U,0)^\intercal$ or $\ket\lambda = (0, \ket L)^\intercal$. These pure null vectors correspond, in agreement with the formalism of \cite{fluxcharge}, to cuts and loops composed of only a single element class. They are identifiable directly from the circuit's topology without algebraic computation. We will examine specifically the case for a null vector from a pure-cut as the pure-loop null vectors are easily found by the same argument. Should $\ket{l} =0$ (thus, $\ket{L}= 0$ too), then the conditions become:
\begin{subequations}\label{eqn:pureeig}
    \begin{align}
      (\mathbb P_{\mathcal C} - \mathbb P_{\mathcal I})\ket{U} &= \pm  \ket{U}\\
        \sum_{\mathfrak g \in \fG} G_{\mathfrak g}\,\Gamma_{\mathfrak g}\,\ket{U}  &= 0.
    \end{align}
\end{subequations}
We specify that $\ket{L}= 0$ to emphasize that we are considering a vector that is not in $\ker B^\intercal$, but rather a nul

