\title{Putting a Temperature on Electron Correlation
}

\begin{abstract}
It is common wisdom that a better understanding of how to quantify electron correlation should lead to more efficient treatments of the correlation problem. Here, we not only substantiate this view but turn it into a concrete, systematic framework for ground states. Since prominent correlation measures such as the nonfreeness depend only on the one-particle reduced density matrix, modeling the correlation energy as a function of such a measure naturally places the framework within reduced density matrix functional theory, while keeping the cost at mean-field level. Strikingly, the variable conjugate to the measure emerges as a ``correlation temperature'', thereby connecting correlation measures, functional theory, and effective temperatures. The framework rationalizes methods such as \iDMFT{}, and we illustrate its key concepts analytically for the Hubbard dimer and numerically for small molecular systems. This brings a fundamental question within reach: how much of the correlation problem can be solved at mean-field cost?
\end{abstract}

\maketitle


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Describing electron correlation is one of the central challenges of quantum chemistry, condensed matter physics, and materials science, particularly for strongly correlated systems~\cite{Ganoe-Shee-2024}. Its difficulty stems from the exponential complexity of the many-electron wave function~\cite{Lowdin1958,davidson-book}. In quantum chemistry, the most common starting point for tackling this correlation problem is the Hartree--Fock (HF) method. By restricting the wave function to a single Slater determinant, HF reduces the problem to self-consistent one-particle equations at so-called mean-field cost. Moreover, HF is rarely the end point: perturbative, coupled-cluster, configuration-interaction, and many multireference approaches typically build on the HF reference state and its orbitals. HF thus constitutes the natural first step for most quantum-chemical treatments of electron correlation.


A remarkable insight due to Lieb~\cite{LiebHF,Lieb1977} is that, for positive interactions, the HF method can be recast as the minimization of a functional of the one-particle reduced density matrix (1RDM) over all ensemble $N$-representable 1RDMs. HF thereby becomes the simplest approximation within reduced density matrix functional theory (RDMFT)~\cite{Gilbert75,Valone80,M84,Goedecker1998,PG15}, whose exact functional differs from the HF functional only by the correlation functional $\Fc$. Despite well-developed foundations~\cite{Altunbulak2008,Baldsiefen2015,Schilling18,GR19,liebert23,Fredheim2025}, however, approximations to $\Fc$ are still less mature than in density functional theory (DFT), and their systematic improvement remains a central challenge.

Quantum information theory offers a complementary perspective, starting from a more basic question: what is electron correlation in the first place? Since electrons do not constitute proper subsystems in the operator-algebraic sense, the standard paradigm based on local operations and classical communication does not apply, and no unique measure of electron correlation exists~\cite{AlivertiPiuri2024}. Instead, it can only be quantified ad hoc, for instance by the prominent nonfreeness, the distance of a state from the set of mean-field states~\cite{Gottlieb2005,GottliebMauser2014}. Remarkably, for pure states the nonfreeness is a simple function of the 1RDM. Moreover, these mean-field states are precisely those whose two-particle reduced density matrix underlies the HF functional. Both perspectives thus single out the 1RDM and rest on the same notion of uncorrelated states.

