\end{figure}

This convergence lends weight to the common wisdom that a better understanding of how to quantify electron correlation should lead to more efficient treatments of the correlation problem. Yet it leaves open how a correlation measure could actually be put to work in a computational method. In this perspective, we not only substantiate this view but turn it into a concrete, systematic framework for ground states. The guiding idea is simple: if a correlation measure captures the essential correlation of the $N$-electron wave function, as compressed into its 1RDM, then the correlation energy should be expressible through the measure alone, at least within a specific class of systems, such as a molecule along its dissociation coordinate. Following this idea through naturally places the framework within RDMFT, while keeping the computational cost at the mean-field level. Most importantly, it reveals that three seemingly distinct concepts are intimately connected: correlation measures from quantum information theory, RDMFT, and effective temperatures, whose thermal occupation numbers mimic those of correlated states (Fig.~\ref{fig:concepts}). This connection, a central result of this work, gives such temperatures a clear conceptual origin: unlike the occupation smearing used to improve numerical convergence~\cite{Weinert1992,Rabuck1999}, they follow directly from quantifying electron correlation.

Seen from a different angle, our work addresses a question that, for strongly correlated systems, has received comparatively little attention: rather than gaining accuracy by investing ever more computational resources, how far beyond HF can one go at fixed mean-field cost?
Several methods already explore this territory by introducing fractional occupations through an effective temperature, among them \iDMFT{}~\cite{WangKnowles21-PRA,WangBaerends22-PRL}, thermally-assisted-occupation DFT (TAO-DFT)~\cite{Chai2012,Chai2014,Yeh2022}, and entropy-corrected pair-density functional theory~\cite{Feng2025}. Our framework provides these approaches with a common foundation, which clarifies where they work and how they could be improved systematically.

The perspective is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:corr-measures} reviews correlation measures based on the 1RDM and formulates the conditions they should satisfy. Section~\ref{sec:corr-temp} develops the framework for constructing functional approximations and introduces the correlation temperature. Section~\ref{sec:Hubbard} illustrates the key concepts analytically for the Hubbard dimer, and Section~\ref{sec:num} explores them numerically for H$_4^{2+}$ and H$_2$. Section~\ref{sec:summary} concludes with a summary and outlook.




\section{Correlation measures}
\label{sec:corr-measures}

We denote by $\RDMset$ the set of all 1RDMs that arise from pure or ensemble $N$-particle states, and similarly by $\tRDMset$ the set of all 2RDMs~\cite{coleman2000-RDM-book}. For simplicity, we assume the one-particle Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_1$ to be finite-dimensional, $d=\dim\mathcal{H}_1<\infty$, as is the case for a finite basis set or a discretization scheme.
Every $\g\in\RDMset$ is a Hermitian operator on $\mathcal{H}_1$ and thus has a spectral representation
\begin{equation}\label{1RDM}
\g = \sum_i n_i \ket{\chi_i}\bra{\chi_i}.
\end{equation}
The eigenvalues $\{n_i\}$, always listed in descending order, are the \emph{natural occupation numbers} (NONs) and the eigenvectors $\{\chi_i\}$ the \emph{natural orbitals} (NOs) of the underlying $N$-electron state. For fermions, the Pauli exclusion principle implies $0\leq n_i\leq 1$.

How can the correlation contained in an $N$-electron state be quantified? The most systematic answer to this question comes from quantum information theory, whose central aim is to characterize and quantify correlation and entanglement~\cite{Horodecki2009}. Its paradigm, based on local operations and classical communication (LOCC), rests on the notion of independent subsystems~\cite{Zanardi2001,Zanardi2002,Zanardi2004,Horodecki2009}. For $N$ electrons, this would require splitting the algebra of observables, e.g., as $\mathcal{A}_N\cong\mathcal{A}_1\otimes\mathcal{A}_{N-1}$, separating one electron from the remaining ones, which can be shown to be impossible for indistinguishable particles~\cite{AlivertiPiuri2024}. Electron correlation is thus not correlation between electrons in the strict quantum-information-theoretical sense, and therefore no unambiguous, universally accepted measure of it can be expected. Instead, electron correlation can only be quantified ad hoc, in a way that depends on the perspective or method at hand, be it based on wave functions, 2RDMs, or 1RDMs. All these perspectives share, however, a common notion of which pure states are uncorrelated: single Slater determinants, the eigenstates of non-interacting electron systems. Crucially, this notion can be expressed entirely in terms of the 1RDM. For $N$ particles in a single-determinant pure state, the NONs are
\begin{eqnarray}
    n_i = \left\{ \begin{array}{ll}
         1 & \text{for}\;1\leq i\leq N, \\
         0 & \text{for}\;i>N.
    \end{array}\right.
\end{eqnarray}
In other words, the 1RDM is idempotent, $\g^2=\g$, if and only if the state is pure and has the form of a single determinant (built from the NOs $\chi_i$)~\cite{Coleman1963}. Consequently, any pure state that is not a single determinant, and is thus correlated, has fractional NONs.

For ensemble states, the situation changes: fractional NONs can then also arise from classical mixing, for instance at finite temperature, so that non-idempotency alone no longer signals correlation. This simple observation is the key to Section~\ref{sec:corr-temp}, where correlation is modeled by an auxiliary thermal ensemble. It also suggests how to extend the notion of uncorrelated states: just as Slater determinants are the eigenstates of non-interacting systems, their grand-canonical thermal states are regarded as uncorrelated. These are the \emph{gauge-invariant quasi-free states} of algebraic quantum field theory~\cite[Ex.~5.2.20]{Brattelli-Robinson-book}, which in the literature on correlation measures are simply called \emph{free states}~\cite{Gottlieb2005,Gottlieb2007Properties,Gottlieb2017Nonfreeness,AlivertiPiuri2024}. Free states are grand-canonical states on Fock space for which the expectation value of \emph{every} many-body observable can be calculated exactly from the 1RDM, so that the full density matrix can be reconstructed from $\g$ alone~\cite{Cheong2004}; we denote by $\G_\g^\mathrm{free}$ the free state with 1RDM $\g$. Free states thus contain only one-body information. They include single Slater determinants as zero-temperature limits and are precisely the mean-field states referred to in the Introduction: by Wick's theorem, the 2RDM of $\G_\g^\mathrm{free}$ is exactly the mean-field expression $\Gmf(\g)$ introduced in Eq.~\eqref{eq:mf_2rdm} below~\cite{BachLiebSolovej1994}. Since the set $\RDMset$ of admissible 1RDMs is identical for canonical and grand-canonical ensembles~\cite{Coleman1963}, which would not be the case for $\tRDMset$, working on Fock space poses no restriction at the level of 1RDMs.

The correlation of an arbitrary state $\G$ can then be quantified by its distance from the set of free states, measured by the quantum relative entropy~\cite{Umegaki1962,Vedral2002}, the quantum analogue of the classical Kullback--Leibler divergence~\cite{Kullback-Leibler1951},
\begin{equation}
    D(\G_1\|\G_2) := \trace[\G_1(\log\G_1-\log\G_2)].
\end{equation}
Although the relative entropy is not a proper distance function, as it is neither symmetric nor does it obey the triangle inequality, it serves as a measure of distinguishability, since it is always nonnegative and vanishes if and only if $\G_1=\G_2$. Its minimum over all free states is attained for the free state with the same 1RDM and can be evaluated explicitly~\cite{GottliebMauser2014},
\begin{eqnarray}
    D(\G\|\G_{\g(\G)}^\mathrm{free}) &=& \min\{D(\G\|\G') : \G'\;\text{is free}\} \nonumber\\
    &=& -\sum_i (n_i\log n_i + (1-n_i)\log(1-n_i)) \nonumber \\
    && +\trace[\G\log\G].
\end{eqnarray}
This quantity is the \emph{nonfreeness} of $\G$, introduced by Gottlieb and Mauser~\cite{Gottlieb2005,Gottlieb2007Properties}. Here, the $\{n_i\}$ are the NONs of $\G$; the first term is the von Neumann entropy of the free state $\G_{\g(\G)}^\mathrm{free}$, and the second term is the negative von Neumann entropy of $\G$. This is a crucial observation: apart from the entropy of the state itself, the nonfreeness depends only on the 1RDM. For pure states, $\trace[\G\log\G]=0$, so the nonfreeness is determined by the NONs alone and equals the entropy of the closest free state. We thus arrive at a correlation measure for pure states that is a simple function of the 1RDM, the nonfreeness
\begin{equation}\label{eq:nf}
    \zeta_{\rm nf}(\g) = \Sph(\g) := -\sum_i (n_i\log n_i + (1-n_i)\log(1-n_i)),
\end{equation}
which we denote by $\Sph(\g)$ in later sections, reflecting its particle-hole symmetry. It coincides with the von Neumann entropy of an ideal Fermi gas with discrete modes and is directly related to the Fermi-Dirac distribution, as seen in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ni-dist}. The same function of the NONs has also appeared in other contexts, for instance as a correlation entropy~\cite{GoriGiorgi2002}, as the total orbital correlation minimized over all one-particle bases~\cite{GigenaRossignoli2015,Gigena2020,AlivertiPiuri2024}, and in empirical studies of electron interactions along reaction paths~\cite{FloresGallegos2023}.

The nonfreeness is, however, not the only conceivable correlation measure. To characterize suitable 1RDM-based measures more generally, we model a correlation measure as a function $\zeta:\RDMset\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$. Such a measure is meant to quantify the correlation of \emph{pure} $N$-electron states, such as ground states, whose fractional NONs reflect the electron-electron interaction. For mixed states, a function of the 1RDM alone cannot serve this purpose, since fractional NONs may then also stem from classical mixing; accordingly, the nonfreeness of a mixed state also involves the entropy of the state itself. We nevertheless define $\zeta$ on the convex set $\RDMset$ for technical convenience: unlike the set of pure-state representable 1RDMs~\cite{Altunbulak2008}, it is explicitly known and constitutes the natural domain of RDMFT~\cite{Valone80}. We now collect a set of basic properties that we require from $\zeta$. Some of these properties are necessary for any function $\zeta$ that measures correlation. Others ensure that the resulting method, which incorporates correlation effects through the energy and fractional occupation numbers, retains the computational cost of HF.
As shown in Section~\ref{sec:corr-temp}, this is guaranteed primarily if the measure does not involve the NOs at all. That the measure depends on the NONs alone can also be motivated by requiring invariance under orbital rotations. We formulate a total of six natural conditions.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item\label{item:NON} The correlation measure depends \emph{only} (and explicitly) on the NONs $\{n_i\}$, so that $\zeta(\g)=\zeta(\{n_i\})$.
    \item\label{item:sum} The correlation measure has an additive form in terms of the NONs, without any cross terms, so that $\zeta(\g)=\sum_i f(n_i)$ with $f:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ quantifying the correlation in each orbital.
    \item\label{item:idempotent} A single-determinant state carries no correlation, so $\zeta(\g)=0$ if and only if $\g^2=\g$. In this sense, every correlation measure with this property is also a measure of non-idempotency. Since an idempotent 1RDM has all NONs equal to either 0 or 1, this means $f(0)=f(1)=0$ and $f(n)>0$ for $0<n<1$.
    \item The correlation measure is invariant under the particle-hole transformation $n_i\mapsto 1-n_i$, i.e., $f(n)=f(1-n)$: exchanging the roles of particles and holes does not change the correlation. Importantly, this \emph{particle-hole symmetry} is also an exact condition for correlation functionals in RDMFT~\cite{Yasuda2001}.
    \item The correlation measure is \emph{Schur-concave}, i.e., monotonic with respect to the majorization order of the NONs~\cite{Marshall2011-book}\footnote{For two vectors $\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y}\in\mathbb{R}^d$ with entries sorted in descending order, $x_1^\downarrow\geq\dots\geq x_d^\downarrow$, one says that $\mathbf{x}$ \emph{majorizes} $\mathbf{y}$, written $\mathbf{x}\succ\mathbf{y}$, if $\sum_{i=1}^k x_i^\downarrow\geq\sum_{i=1}^k y_i^\downarrow$ for all $k=1,\dots,d-1$, with equality for $k=d$~\cite{Marshall2011-book,Bhatia1997}. Equivalently, $\mathbf{y}=D\mathbf{x}$ for some doubly stochastic matrix $D$, so that $\mathbf{y}$ is a more disordered, `mixed-up' version of $\mathbf{x}$. Majorization is thus the standard way of comparing the disorder of probability-like vectors and underlies the theory of entanglement transformations~\cite{Nielsen1999}. For Hermitian matrices, majorization is defined through their spectra; for 1RDMs, $\g_1\succ\g_2$ means that the NONs of $\g_1$ majorize those of $\g_2$. A function $\zeta$ is Schur-concave if $\mathbf{x}\succ\mathbf{y}$ implies $\zeta(\mathbf{x})\leq\zeta(\mathbf{y})$. Since all admissible NON vectors satisfy $0\leq n_i\leq 1$ and $\sum_i n_i=N$, the NONs $(1,\dots,1,0,\dots,0)$ of a single Slater determinant majorize all of them, whereas the uniform NONs $n_i=N/d$ are majorized by all of them.}: if the NONs of one state majorize those of another, the former is at most as correlated as the latter. Since the NONs of a single Slater determinant majorize all other admissible NONs, this condition orders states from uncorrelated towards increasingly spread-out occupations. It parallels pure-state entanglement theory, where entanglement monotones are precisely the Schur-concave functions of the Schmidt coefficients~\cite{Nielsen1999,Vidal2000}, and majorization relations of the 1RDM spectrum similarly govern one-body entanglement in fermionic systems~\cite{Gigena2020}. For the additive form of condition~\ref{item:sum}, Schur-concavity is equivalent to \emph{concavity} of $f(n)$~\cite{Marshall2011-book}, which in turn renders $\zeta(\g)$ concave on $\RDMset$.
    \item\label{item:strictly-bijective} In order to uniquely map the orbital energies of a mean-field approximation to NONs (as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:LM-ni} below), $f(n)$ must be \emph{differentiable} on $n\in(0,1)$ with a \emph{bijective} derivative that maps $(0,1)\to\mathbb{R}$. This also renders $\zeta(\{n_i\})$ \emph{strictly} Schur-concave.
\end{enumerate}

Condition~\ref{item:strictly-bijective} also appears in \citet{martinez2023} for a given distribution of NONs. It is straightforward to verify that the nonfreeness $\Sph$ satisfies conditions~\ref{item:NON}--\ref{item:strictly-bijective}. The connection between correlation measures and the correlation functional will be established in Section~\ref{sec:corr-temp}. First, however, we discuss further measures.

A second correlation measure, with a square-root (semicircular) profile, is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zeta-sqrt}
    \zeta_{\rm sqrt}(\g):=\frac 1 2 \sum_i \sqrt{n_i(1-n_i)}.
\end{equation}
In Section~\ref{sec:Hubbard}, this form will be shown to be proportional to the \emph{exact} RDMFT correlation functional of the symmetric Hubbard dimer. It also satisfies all conditions and, in addition, reproduces the known boundary behavior of the exact correlation functional. Since by condition~\ref{item:strictly-bijective} the derivative of $f(n)$ must cover all of $\mathbb{R}$, the derivative $\partial\zeta/\partial n_i$ necessarily diverges at the boundary of the interval $[0,1]$. For the correlation functional of RDMFT, this phenomenon was previously described as the `exchange force'~\cite{schilling2019b}, with the functional varying to lowest order as $\sqrt{n}$ close to the boundary, precisely as in Eq.~\eqref{eq:zeta-sqrt}.
The same phenomenon appears generally in functional theories~\cite{Maciazek2021,Wang2026}, so it could be demanded as a refined version of condition~\ref{item:strictly-bijective}. A consequence thereof will be demonstrated numerically in Section~\ref{sec:combining-measures}.

We also mention the non-idempotency measure
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:zeta_non_idem}
    \zeta_{\rm ni}(\g) := \sum_i n_i(1-n_i)
\end{eqnarray}
with a parabolic profile, which is the standard measure for probing the idempotency $\g=\g^2$ of the 1RDM. The factor $\frac 1 2$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:zeta-sqrt} was chosen such that the maximal values of $\zeta_{\rm sqrt}$ and $\zeta_{\rm ni}$ agree. The non-idempotency measure was suggested as an RDMFT functional to augment DFT methods by energetically favoring non-idempotent 1RDMs~\cite[Eq.~(5)]{Gibney2022}. It was also used as a descriptor of non-dynamic correlation, while $\zeta_{\rm sqrt}(\g)$ represents the full correlation, so that $\zeta_{\rm sqrt}(\g)-\zeta_{\rm ni}(\g)$ roughly quantifies the dynamic part~\cite{RamosCordoba2016,RamosCordoba2017}.
In the asymmetric Hubbard dimer, $\zeta_{\rm ni}$ appears as an estimate for the correlation functional in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:Hubbard-Ecum-estimates} and \eqref{eq:hub_dim_corr_func_approx}.
Note, however, that $\zeta_{\rm ni}$ violates condition~\ref{item:strictly-bijective}, since its derivative remains bounded; as a consequence, the corresponding minimizers can become idempotent, as observed in Section~\ref{sec:Hubbard}. The generalized form (here in its symmetrized variant)
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq:zeta_non_idem_q}
    \zeta_{\rm ni}^q(\g) := \sum_i((n_i- n_i^q) + (1-n_i- (1-n_i)^q))
\end{eqnarray}
of the non-idempotency measure has also been considered for quantifying correlation~\cite{Ziesche1995}. It is related to the entropy $S^q(\g)=\zeta_{\rm ni}^q(\g)/(q-1)$ introduced by \citet{Tsallis1988} for the statistics of nonextensive systems. For $q=2$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:zeta_non_idem} is recovered up to a factor of 2, while the entropy $S^q(\g)$ reduces to $\Sph(\g)$ in the limit $q\to 1$. The R\'enyi entropies form another family with the same limit. A graphical comparison of the correlation measures discussed above is given in Fig.~\ref{fig:corr-measures}.

