\section{Correlation temperature}
\label{sec:corr-temp}

Having established correlation measures based on the 1RDM, we now turn them into a computational method. Following the guiding idea of the Introduction, we model the correlation energy as a function of a correlation measure $\zeta(\g)$. Since the measures of Section~\ref{sec:corr-measures} depend only on the 1RDM, this naturally places the construction within RDMFT~\cite{UnifiedFT2026-preprint}: in a coarse-graining step, the correlation functional, which in general depends on the full 1RDM, is replaced by a function of the single scalar variable $\zeta$. This step goes along with the introduction of a scope, i.e., the set of Hamiltonians that model the quantum-chemical systems of interest. We develop the construction for a general measure satisfying the conditions of Section~\ref{sec:corr-measures} and specialize to the nonfreeness $\Sph(\g)$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:nf} where this leads to particularly transparent results.

RDMFT addresses the ground-state problem for Hamiltonians $\hat H = \hat h + \hat W$ of an $N$-particle system, with $\hat h$ the one-body contribution, typically comprising the kinetic energy and the external potential, and $\hat W$ the two-body interaction. Since the interaction $\hat W$ is fixed, while $\hat h$ can be any Hermitian one-particle operator and couples to the state only through $\trace[\hat h\g]$, the 1RDM $\g$ is singled out as the natural reduced variable replacing the full quantum state~\cite{liebert23,UnifiedFT2026-preprint}.
Within this setting, the universal functional $\F(\g)$ is defined by a constrained search over all $N$-representable 2RDMs $\G^{(2)}\in\tRDMset$ that lead to a given $\g\in\RDMset$ (written as ``$\G^{(2)} \mapsto \g$''),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:F-def}
    \F(\g) := \inf_{\G^{(2)} \mapsto \g} \trace[\hat W \G^{(2)}].
\end{equation}
The ground-state energy then follows from a variation over all admissible 1RDMs instead of the full quantum state,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:E0-variation}
    E_0 = \inf_\g\left\{ \trace[\hat h \g] + \F(\g) \right\}.
\end{equation}
A first, obvious approximation to $\F(\g)$ is obtained from a mean-field approximation to the 2RDM in terms of the 1RDM, where we use the expansion in terms of NOs from Eq.~\eqref{1RDM},
\begin{eqnarray}
  \label{eq:mf_2rdm}\Gmf(\g) &=& \frac{1}{2}(\hat I - \hat X)\,\g \otimes \g, \\
  \label{eq:mfFunc}
  \Fmf(\g) &=& \trace[\hat W \Gmf(\g)] \nonumber \\
           &=& \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j} n_i n_j \bra{\chi_i \chi_j} \hat W \ket{\chi_i \chi_j}.
\end{eqnarray}
Here, the exchange operator is defined as $\hat X(\ket{\chi_i}\otimes\ket{\chi_j})=\ket{\chi_j}\otimes\ket{\chi_i}$, and $\ket{\chi_i \chi_j} = (\ket{\chi_i}\otimes\ket{\chi_j} - \ket{\chi_j}\otimes\ket{\chi_i})/\sqrt{2}$ is a two-orbital Slater determinant.
For a general 1RDM $\g$, the expression $\Gmf(\g)$ is \emph{not} the 2RDM of any $N$-particle state, pure or mixed, and does not contract back to $\g$ in the usual way. Rather, as noted in Section~\ref{sec:corr-measures}, it is the 2RDM of the grand-canonical free state $\G_\g^\mathrm{free}$ on Fock space. Only if $\g$ is idempotent, and thus represents a single-determinant pure state, is $\Gmf(\g)$ precisely the corresponding $N$-particle 2RDM. In this way, the functional $\Fmf(\g)$ is connected to the HF method built on Slater determinants~\cite{LiebHF, Lieb1977}, and it serves as the basis for more refined approximations within RDMFT. To this end, we define the correlation functional as the difference between the exact functional and $\Fmf(\g)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Fc}
    \Fc(\g) := \F(\g) - \Fmf(\g) \leq 0.
\end{equation}
For this inequality to hold for all $\g\in\RDMset$, the interaction $\hat W$ must be positive semidefinite~\cite{LiebHF,Levy87}, as is the case for the most relevant interactions, such as the Coulomb interaction in quantum chemistry, the on-site interaction of the Hubbard model, and the repulsive contact interaction in ultracold atomic gases. We stress that at this level the decomposition $\F(\g)=\Fmf(\g)+\Fc(\g)$ is still \emph{exact}, in the sense that the variation in Eq.~\eqref{eq:E0-variation} yields the exact ground-state energy and 1RDM.

Building on this exact decomposition, the central idea is to coarse-grain the correlation functional: rather than depending on the full 1RDM, it is approximated as a function of a single correlation measure,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:F-tilde}
    \tilde\F(\g)=\Fmf(\g)+\tilde\Fc(\zeta(\g)).
\end{equation}
How should the function $\tilde\Fc$ be chosen? A natural requirement is that it never overestimates the magnitude of the correlation energy, $\tilde\Fc(\zeta(\g))\geq\Fc(\g)$, so that $\tilde\F$ is bounded from below by the exact functional. The sharpest function satisfying this requirement on a given set of 1RDMs is obtained by taking, for each value of $\zeta$, the supremum of $\Fc$ over all 1RDMs sharing this value. Taken over all 1RDMs, however, such a supremum can be very conservative, since a single value of $\zeta$ is shared by 1RDMs of very different systems. Yet in many situations only a limited set of systems needs to be considered, e.g., the dissociation of a certain molecule. We therefore restrict the supremum to the set $\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S})$ of ground-state 1RDMs (including those of ensemble ground states in the case of degeneracies) of a chosen \emph{scope} $\mathcal{S}$ of Hamiltonians of interest, such as various molecular geometries~\cite{UnifiedFT2026-preprint}, and define the coarse-grained correlation functional as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:coarse-func}
    \tilde\Fc(\zeta) = \sup_{\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S})\ni\g\mapsto\zeta}\Fc(\g),
\end{equation}
where the supremum is taken over all $\g\in\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S})$ with $\zeta(\g)=\zeta$, and $\tilde\Fc(\zeta)$ is set to zero if $\zeta$ is not attained in $\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S})$. By construction, $\tilde\Fc(\zeta)\leq 0$ and $\tilde\Fc(\zeta(\g))\geq\Fc(\g)$ for all $\g\in\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S})$, so that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Fc-inequ}
    \F(\g)\leq\tilde\F(\g)\leq\Fmf(\g)\quad\text{for all }\g\in\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S}).
\end{equation}
If the variation is restricted to $\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S})$, the resulting energy can thus never fall below the exact ground-state energy, and the narrower the scope, the sharper the coarse-grained functional, as illustrated for the Hubbard dimer in Section~\ref{sec:Hubbard}. In practice, $\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S})$ is rarely known explicitly and the variation is carried out over the full set $\RDMset$, so that these guarantees no longer hold strictly; approximations such as the linear form discussed below are then obtained by fitting rather than from the supremum.

The approximate variational problem for the ground state then reads
\begin{equation}\label{eq:E0-variation-approx}
\begin{aligned}
    \tilde E_0 &= \inf_\g\left\{ \trace[\hat h \g] + \tilde\F(\g) \right\} \\
    &= \inf_\g\left\{ \trace[\hat h \g] + \Fmf(\g) + \tilde\Fc(\zeta(\g)) \right\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
When varying over $\g\in\RDMset$ in terms of NOs and NONs with the usual Lagrange-multiplier approach, the term $\tilde\Fc(\zeta(\g))$ drops out of the variation with respect to the NOs, as it depends on the NONs only. Because $\Fmf(\g)$ corresponds exactly to the HF functional with fractional occupation numbers, this yields the usual HF equations, with the only difference that the orbital-dependent operators are weighted by the occupation numbers $n_j$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:th-HF}
    \left(\hat h + \sum_j n_j(\hat J_j - \hat K_j)\right) \chi_i = \varepsilon_i \chi_i.
\end{equation}
Here, $\hat J_j$ and $\hat K_j$ are the usual Hartree and exchange operators of HF theory. The variation with respect to the NONs must take $\tilde\Fc(\zeta)$ into account. For a measure of the additive form $\zeta(\g)=\sum_i f(n_i)$, the chain rule yields (assuming differentiability)
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LM-ni}
    \varepsilon_i + \frac{\partial \tilde\Fc}{\partial \zeta}\, f'(n_i) = \mu,
\end{equation}
where the Lagrange multiplier $\mu$, the chemical potential, is fixed by the normalization $\sum_in_i=N$. Defining the variable conjugate to the measure,
\begin{equation}
    \kc(\zeta):=-\frac{\partial \tilde\Fc(\zeta)}{\partial \zeta},
\end{equation}
assuming $\kc>0$, and using that $f'$ is a bijection from $(0,1)$ to $\mathbb{R}$ (condition~\ref{item:strictly-bijective} of Section~\ref{sec:corr-measures}), Eq.~\eqref{eq:LM-ni} is uniquely solved by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ni-general}
    n_i = (f')^{-1}\!\left(\frac{\varepsilon_i - \mu}{\kc(\zeta)}\right).
\end{equation}
The variable $\kc$ thus sets the energy scale on which the occupation numbers are distributed over the orbital energies, much like a temperature. For the nonfreeness, $f(n)=-n\log n-(1-n)\log(1-n)$ and hence $f'(n)=\log(1-n)-\log n$, so that Eq.~\eqref{eq:ni-general} becomes the Fermi-Dirac distribution
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ni-dist}
    n_i = \left(1 + \rme^{(\varepsilon_i - \mu)/\kc(\zeta)}\right)^{-1}.
\end{equation}
Note that $\kc$ still depends on all $\{n_i\}$ through $\zeta$, but Eqs.~\eqref{eq:th-HF} and \eqref{eq:ni-general} must anyway be solved self-consistently. In view of Eq.~\eqref{eq:ni-dist}, we call $\kc$ the \emph{correlation temperature}. It is an auxiliary quantity: an effective temperature that serves as a surrogate for correlation effects. As noted in the Introduction, several methods in the quantum-chemical literature build on a similar idea. Here, however, the fractional occupations follow directly from quantifying electron correlation, which distinguishes them from an effective temperature introduced for purely practical reasons, namely to help with $v$-representability~\cite{Ullrich2002,Kraisler2009,Woods2019}, to guarantee numerical stability~\cite{Weinert1992,Wentzcovitch1992,Marzari1997,Rabuck1999}, or to overcome the so-called ``correlation paradox''~\cite{Ding2020}. A correlation temperature can nevertheless be expected to share these beneficial side effects.

For the nonfreeness and a fixed value of $\kc$, the coupled equations~\eqref{eq:th-HF} and \eqref{eq:ni-dist} correspond exactly to the thermal HF method in the grand-canonical ensemble~\cite{Mermin1963}. Indeed, since the nonfreeness of a pure state equals the entropy of the closest free state, the functional minimized in Eq.~\eqref{eq:E0-variation-approx} with a linear dependence $-\kc\Sph(\g)$ is, up to a constant, precisely the grand-canonical free energy of thermal HF at temperature $\kc$. In particular, the method can be executed at mean-field cost. Only in the grand-canonical ensemble, which allows for particle-number fluctuations around $N$, does a Fermi-Dirac distribution arise from a thermal Gibbs state. This may seem surprising at first, since our description concerns a molecular system with fixed particle number at zero temperature. The open system, however, serves merely as an auxiliary reference system that shares important physical properties, and only through it can the notions of temperature and chemical potential be sensibly introduced~\cite{Baerends2022}. The Fermi-Dirac form of Eq.~\eqref{eq:ni-dist} is thus specific to the nonfreeness; other correlation measures lead to other distributions via Eq.~\eqref{eq:ni-general}.

A practical implementation relies on a reliable approximation to the coarse-grained correlation functional $\tilde\Fc(\zeta)$, or equivalently to the correlation temperature $\kc(\zeta)$, for as broad a range of systems as possible. Since the interaction is fixed in the RDMFT functional $\F(\g)$, the variable parts of the theory are the particle number and the one-particle Hamiltonian $\hat h$, including the external potential. In this respect, the correlation temperature $\kc(\zeta)$ plays a role analogous to the exchange-correlation potential of Kohn-Sham DFT~\cite{KS1965}. The ground state is then approximated by a self-consistent solution of Eq.~\eqref{eq:th-HF}, with the $\{n_i\}$ determined self-consistently from Eq.~\eqref{eq:ni-general}, where $\kc(\zeta)$ in turn depends on the occupation numbers.
Once converged, with $\g_*$ the resulting 1RDM and $\zeta_*=\zeta(\g_*)$, the approximate ground-state energy is evaluated as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
    \tilde E_0 &= \trace[\hat h\g_*] + \Fmf(\g_*) + \tilde\Fc(\zeta_*).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

In the spirit of Jacob's ladder~\cite{Perdew2001}, possible approximations can be organized into rungs. On the zeroth rung, the coarse-grained correlation functional $\tilde\Fc$ is simply a constant that depends only on certain chemical information. This implies a vanishing correlation temperature, $\kc=0$, so that the method reduces to a standard HF calculation followed by a correlation-energy correction. This is essentially the idea of the ``ultrafast correlation energy estimator'', which adds a fixed partial correlation energy for each bond type~\cite{Witkowski2025} and is called `ultrafast' because it can be applied on top of a HF calculation at virtually no additional cost. The same basic idea underlies ``bond additivity corrections''~\cite{Melius2000}.
Any more involved coarse-grained correlation functional also keeps the cost at the mean-field level, requiring only a thermal-HF-like self-consistent calculation with nonzero temperature.
On the first rung of the ladder, the coarse-grained functional genuinely depends on the correlation measure, which thereby enters the self-consistent procedure.
In the simplest case, this is a linear approximation of the form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:tildeFc-linear}
    \tilde\Fc(\zeta) \approx -\kc\zeta - b,
\end{equation}
which corresponds to a constant correlation temperature and can serve as an efficient approximation for $\Fc(\g)$, although it is not guaranteed to respect the bounds of Eq.~\eqref{eq:Fc-inequ} in every setting.
It was reported that, for a scope restricted to diatomic dissociation processes, $\Fc(\g)$ indeed approximately follows this linear behavior for the nonfreeness~\cite{WangKnowles21-PRA}, in line with a ``correlation conjecture''~\cite{Graf2026} that is closely related to the ``Collins conjecture''~\cite{Collins}. Note that this observation alone does not confirm the validity of Eq.~\eqref{eq:tildeFc-linear}, since it only requires $\Fc(\g)\approx -\kc \Sph(\g) - b$ for the ground-state 1RDMs $\g$ along the dissociation curve.
The observed linear relation between $\Sph(\g)$ and $\Fc(\g)$ led to the \iDMFT{} method~\cite{WangBaerends22-PRL}, which employs Eq.~\eqref{eq:tildeFc-linear} with fixed $\kc$ and $b$ and which we consequently place on the first rung of the ladder. Its parameters are usually optimized directly towards accurate energies~\cite{Irimia2023-open-shell,Irimia2023-correlation}.
Functional approximations including terms of second order in $\zeta$ have also been considered and tested numerically~\cite{Hu2024-dispersion}.
Higher rungs could use additional information extracted from the 1RDM. Our derivation suggests that such developments should focus on a more general correlation temperature $\kc(\zeta, [\g])$ that depends on the measure and, potentially, on further information contained in the 1RDM. Self-consistent schemes for determining effective electronic temperatures are known from TAO-DFT~\cite{Lin2017,Chen2022}, and \citet{Gibney2023} derived an estimate for $\kc$ from electron repulsion integrals, coupled to the non-idempotency measure $\zeta_{\rm ni}$.





\section{Illustration with the Hubbard dimer}
\label{sec:Hubbard}

The concepts from the previous section will be demonstrated here using the simple, explicit example of an (asymmetric) Hubbard dimer at half-filling. Interestingly, this setting naturally leads to an occurrence of two of the considered correlation measures, concretely $\zeta_\mathrm{ni}$ and $\zeta_\mathrm{sqrt}$.
We consider the Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hub_ham}
    \begin{split}
        &\left.
        \begin{aligned}
        \hat{H}_\mathrm{HD}=
        &-\sum_{\sigma\in\{\uparrow,\downarrow\}}\Big(t\hspace{2pt}\hat{f}^\dagger_{1\sigma}\hat{f}_{2\sigma}+\mathrm{h.c.}\Big)\\
        &+\frac{V}{2}\sum_{\sigma\in\{\uparrow,\downarrow\}}\Big(\hat{f}^\dagger_{1\sigma}\hat{f}_{1\sigma}-\hat{f}^\dagger_{2\sigma}\hat{f}_{2\sigma}\Big)
        \end{aligned}\quad\right\}=:\hat{h}_{t,V}
        \\
        &\left.\hspace{33.4pt}+\;U\sum_{j\in\{1,2\}}\hat{f}^\dagger_{j\uparrow}\hat{f}_{j\downarrow}^\dagger\hat{f}_{j\downarrow}\hat{f}_{j\uparrow}. \hspace{39pt}\right\}=:\hat{W}
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
The operator $\hat{f}^{(\dagger)}_{i\sigma}$ denotes the canonical fermionic annihilation (creation) operator for a particle at site $i$ with spin polarization $\sigma$. For our example, we consider a real hopping parameter $t\in\mathbb{R}$, a difference in local potentials $V\in\mathbb{R}$, and a positive on-site interaction at strength $U\in\mathbb{R^+}$. The single-particle terms of Eq.~\eqref{eq:hub_ham} are collected in the one-particle Hamiltonian $\hat{h}_{t,V}$, while the pair-interaction is again denoted by $\hat{W}$.
Through its high versatility, combined with its analytical solvability, the Hubbard dimer has assumed a role as a paradigmatic test system for the study of strong correlation and functional approximations~\cite{carrascal2015a}.

For the ground state search of the Hamiltonian in Eq.~\eqref{eq:hub_ham}, we consider states supported on the singlet sector of the two-particle Hilbert space. This subspace is spanned by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hub_dim_singlet_onb}
    \begin{split}
        &\ket{\Phi_1}=\hat{f}^\dagger_{1\uparrow}\hat{f}^\dagger_{1\downarrow}\ket{0},\\
        &\ket{\Phi_2}= \hat{f}^\dagger_{2\uparrow}\hat{f}^\dagger_{2\downarrow}\ket{0},\\
        &\ket{\Phi_3}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Big(\hat{f}^\dagger_{1\uparrow}\hat{f}^\dagger_{2\downarrow}-\hat{f}^\dagger_{1\downarrow}\hat{f}^\dagger_{2\uparrow}\Big)\ket{0},
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\ket{0}$ denotes the vacuum state.

In order to apply the framework from Sec.~\ref{sec:corr-temp}, a sensible choice for a correlation measure (in the sense of Sec.~\ref{sec:corr-measures}) must be identified. For this, we examine properties of the cumulant energy of ground states in our dimer system. Building on the definition of the two-body correlation matrix in Eq.~\eqref{eq:mf_2rdm}, the cumulant energy is defined as~\cite{kutzelnigg2003, Levy87}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:def_e_cum}
    \Ecum(\G)=\trace\left[\hat W\Gamma\right]-\trace\left[\hat{W}\Gmf(\g)\right],
\end{equation}
where $\G$ denotes an $N$-particle state, with $\g$ the corresponding 1RDM. The cumulant energy can be understood as the analogue of the correlation functional at the level of the full state; applying the constrained-search formalism yields the correlation functional, $\Fc(\g)=\inf_{\G\mapsto\g}\Ecum(\G)$.

Besides choosing a sensible correlation measure for the coarse-grained variable, a crucial step in our approximation procedure is specifying a \emph{scope} for our new functional. In prior work by our group, it was established that different scopes for the Hubbard dimer yield distinct sets of $v$-representable 1RDMs~\cite{liebert23}. For illustrating the procedure, the following two scopes are suited particularly well:
\begin{alignat}{3}\label{eq:hub_dim_scopes_1}
        &i)\qquad &\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^\mathbb{R} &:=\left\{\hat h_{t,V}+\hat W: t,V\in\mathbb{R}\right\},\\
        \label{eq:hub_dim_scopes_2}
        &ii) &\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^\mathrm{sym} &:=\left\{\hat h_{t,V}+\hat W: t\in\mathbb{R}, V=0\right\}.
\end{alignat}
The first scope includes the most general form of real-valued symmetric single-particle operators, while the second case corresponds to the setting of the (left-right) symmetric Hubbard dimer with hopping between sites.
Evidently, $\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^\mathrm{sym}\subset\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^{\mathbb{R}}$. 
Crucially, this allows for the derivation of a similar hierarchy for the sets of corresponding $v$-representable 1RDMs: $\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^\mathrm{sym})\subseteq\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^{\mathbb{R}})$.
For illustrative purposes, we restrict ourselves in this section to the sets of \emph{pure-state} $v$-representable 1RDMs, which simplifies the derivation of analytical formulas. From here on, we will denote these sets by $\tilde{\mathcal{G}}(\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^\mathrm{sym})$ and $\tilde{\mathcal{G}}(\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^{\mathbb{R}})$ to emphasize the restriction compared to the general set of ensemble $v$-representable 1RDMs.
The supremum applied during coarse-graining in Eq.~\eqref{eq:coarse-func} translates the relationship between sets into an inequality on the level of the correlation functional:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hub_dim_ineq_scopes_func}
    \tilde{\mathcal{F}}^c_{\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^\mathrm{sym}}(\gamma)
    \leq\tilde{\mathcal{F}}^c_{\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^{\mathbb{R}}}(\gamma),
\end{equation}
where the subscript indicates the scope defining the domain of 1RDMs used for coarse-graining. Inevitably, the dependence on the domain also reflects on the validity range of the above inequalities, such that Eq.~\eqref{eq:hub_dim_ineq_scopes_func} holds for $\g\in\tilde{\mathcal{G}}(\mathcal{S}_\mathrm{HD}^\mathrm{sym})$.

