\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:Intro}

Surface codes provide a route to fault-tolerant quantum computation based on local stabilizer measurements \cite{DennisSurface,TomitaRotatedSurface2014,fowler2012surface}. Together with magic-state preparation \cite{Bravyi12,Chamberland2019Magic1,chamberland2020very}, multi-qubit Pauli measurements support both logical Clifford and non-Clifford operations and hence universal quantum computation \cite{fowler2018low,Litinski19,Chamberland22,Chamberland22b,No_Hooks_Japan}. Lattice surgery realizes these joint logical measurements by temporarily merging surface-code patches and measuring stabilizers in the resulting geometry. It is therefore particularly well suited to implementing logical operations on two-dimensional architectures with local connectivity.

Turning a logical Pauli measurement into a physical implementation requires compatible choices of patch geometry, stabilizer placement, and measurement schedule. These choices must control fault propagation while providing the logical observables and syndrome information needed for decoding. Logical $Y$ measurements introduce additional challenges because twist defects can reduce the effective code distance. Moreover, as more logical qubits participate in a measurement, the merged patch grows, increasing the size of the classical decoding problem. A systematic approach to lattice surgery must therefore connect circuit construction with decoding.

In this work, we introduce \texttt{patchwork}, a lattice-surgery compiler that brings these tasks into a common framework for arbitrary multi-qubit Pauli measurements on a prescribed two-dimensional surface-code layout. We first develop geometric rules that make stabilizer placement systematic. Horizontal and vertical strips constrain stabilizer placement within the routing corridors, while checkpoint lines specify where elongated stabilizers are needed to resolve stabilizer-type and ancilla conflicts. These rules reduce the variability of merged-patch geometries and support automated construction of lattice-surgery operations. We also give algorithms for generating the spacelike and timelike logical observables needed to track logical errors and measurement outcomes throughout a sequence of operations.

We next develop gate-scheduling rules that adapt to the geometry of the merged patch. Cut lines and junction cells specify where stabilizer-measurement schedules must change to control fault propagation relative to the logical operators. For twist-based logical $Y$ measurements, we introduce measurement circuits and schedules together with protruding lobes of the $Y$-extension patch. The lobes occupy available routing corridors and therefore do not require a change to the underlying physical-qubit layout. For the configurations tested at distances $d \leq 11$, exhaustive numerical searches find no logical failures caused by at most $(d-1)/2$ circuit faults, supporting preservation of the full effective code distance in these cases. We also describe alternatives using wider routing corridors or an ancilla-assisted decomposition into $X$ and $Z$ measurements.

A central contribution of this work is the wavefront algorithm for parallel decoding. It partitions merged lattice-surgery patches into spatial commit, buffer, and cleanup regions that are extended into space-time decoding blocks. Commit regions are decoded in parallel using their buffers as context, followed by cleanup decoding of the residual syndrome. We construct the associated local detector error
models and specify how correction effects are combined to update the global syndrome and logical observables. This framework distributes the decoding of a merged patch across multiple classical resources and provides a route toward real-time decoding during logical computation.

We demonstrate the resulting workflow on a sequence of three multi-qubit Pauli measurements involving ten logical qubits, including measurements with logical $Y$ factors. A failure occurs when at least one deterministic logical observable is decoded incorrectly. Across the parameters tested, staged decoding achieves total failure probabilities close to, but slightly higher than, those of monolithic decoding. We also compare the areas of the largest commit and cleanup regions with the full merged-patch area for random Pauli measurements of increasing weight. Together, these studies quantify the size of the local decoding regions and the decoding-accuracy cost of partitioning, connecting automated lattice-surgery construction with parallel error correction.

The paper is organized as follows. \Cref{sec:Surface_code_stabs} reviews the required surface-code stabilizers. \Cref{sec:Layout,sec:MeasureXZ,sec:FaultTolerantY} introduce the layout and geometric rules for multi-qubit Pauli measurements. \Cref{sec:Logic_Auto_Gen,sec:Timelike_log_gen} describe logical-observable generation, and \cref{sec:Gate_Schedule} presents the gate schedules and $Y$-extension refinements. \Cref{sec:spacetime_partition,sec:spacetime_corrections} develop the wavefront construction and detector-error-model partitioning, followed by numerical results in \cref{sec:numerics} and conclusions in \cref{sec:conclusion}. Appendix~\ref{app:graph} gives the mathematical details of the wavefront algorithm, and Appendix~\ref{app:expd-spec} specifies the numerical experiment in \texttt{patchwork}.

