\section{Routing space layout with checkpoint lines}
\label{sec:Layout}

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \subfloat[\label{fig:Layout_ExampleEX}]{\includegraphics[width=.75\textwidth]{images/Layout_Example.pdf}}
    \caption{Layout for multi-qubit Pauli measurements via lattice surgery, illustrated with distance-five logical patches. Yellow vertices denote data qubits, and grey vertices denote ancillas. Dashed grey lines separate unit cells, each containing four logical patches. Green strips mark the allowed stabilizer-placement regions within the routing corridors, with exceptions for patch-boundary connections and $Y$ extensions described in the text. The numbers in yellow indicate the separation between a logical patch and a green strip, measured in rows or columns of data-qubit sites. Dashed red and solid blue lines mark intra-cell and corridor checkpoint lines, respectively, which determine where elongated stabilizers are introduced.}
    \label{fig:Layout_Example}
\end{figure*}

We use a regular arrangement of logical patches and routing corridors to make the construction of lattice-surgery operations systematic. \Cref{fig:Layout_Example} illustrates the layout for $d=5$.

The layout is built from unit cells containing four logical patches, as also considered in Ref.~\cite{Chamberland22b}. Dashed grey lines in \cref{fig:Layout_Example} separate neighboring unit cells. For distance-$d$ patches, vertical routing corridors have width $d+3$, and horizontal routing corridors have height $d+1$. These dimensions accommodate the stabilizer geometries and $Y$-extension constructions developed below. The additional geometric and gate-scheduling requirements for preserving the effective code distance during $Y$ measurements are discussed in \cref{sec:FaultTolerantY,subsec:gate_schedule_y_ext,sec:protruding_lobes}.

Within each routing corridor, the green strips in \cref{fig:Layout_Example} specify where stabilizers may be placed when connecting logical patches. Stabilizers may extend beyond these strips to access patch boundaries or construct $Y$ extensions. To access a vertical boundary for a logical $X$ measurement, stabilizers may extend outside the vertical strip while remaining within the vertical extent of the patch. Similarly, access to a horizontal boundary for a logical $Z$ measurement permits stabilizers to extend outside the horizontal strip while remaining within the horizontal extent of the patch. These boundary-connection rules are developed in \cref{sec:MeasureXZ}; the additional extensions required for logical $Y$ measurements are described in \cref{sec:FaultTolerantY}.

Standard weight-four stabilizers are added along a routing corridor according to the surface-code checkerboard pattern. Continuing this pattern without modification can produce two types of conflict. First, a weight-two boundary stabilizer in the routing region may require an ancilla already used by a weight-two stabilizer of a logical patch. Second, two horizontally or vertically adjacent weight-four stabilizers may have the same Pauli type. Checkpoint lines specify where standard stabilizers must be replaced by elongated stabilizers to avoid these conflicts. We introduce the relevant rules below.

