    \subfloat[\label{fig:VCCL_Example_v2EX}]{\includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{images/VCCL_Example_v2.pdf}}
    \caption{Examples of the standard vertical corridor checkpoint line (VCCL) rule. (a) Left-to-right traversal of a VCCL on the right side of a vertical corridor. (b) Right-to-left traversal of a VCCL on the left side of a vertical corridor. In both cases, the bottom standard stabilizer adjoining the VCCL within the horizontal green strip is $Z$-type, triggering elongation of that stabilizer and the column of stabilizers above it within the strip. Each stabilizer retains its original Pauli type, and the middle inactive sites of the elongated stabilizers lie on the VCCL.}
    \label{fig:Example_VCCL}
\end{figure*}

Intra-cell checkpoint lines (ICLs), shown as dashed red lines in \cref{fig:Layout_Example}, lie between logical patches within a unit cell that are separated by a single row or column of data-qubit sites. We denote vertical and horizontal ICLs by VICLs and HICLs, respectively. The following rule applies when a horizontal routing path reaches a VICL.

\begin{mdframed}[frametitle={VICL Rules}]
\begin{itemize}
    \item Replace each standard weight-four stabilizer encountered at the VICL by a horizontal elongated stabilizer of the same Pauli type. The elongated column must span the full connected transverse extent of the merged patch at the crossing. This requirement is mandatory: subsequent construction steps cannot truncate the column or replace part of it by standard weight-four stabilizers. The data-qubit sites on the VICL become the middle inactive sites of the elongated stabilizer. An example is shown in \cref{fig:Example_VICL}.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

HICLs are relevant only to routing regions near logical $Y$ measurements. Their rules are given in \cref{subsec:standard_corridor_Y}.

\subsection{Vertical corridor checkpoint lines}
\label{subsec:Intercell}

Vertical corridor checkpoint lines (VCCLs) are shown as solid blue vertical lines in \cref{fig:Layout_Example}. A VCCL lies one data-qubit column to the right of the patches on the right side of a unit cell, or one column to the left of the patches on its left side. The corresponding horizontal corridor checkpoint lines (HCCLs) are treated in \cref{subsec:standard_corridor_Y}.

The standard VCCL rules depend on the traversal direction and the Pauli type of the bottom stabilizer adjoining the line. The two cases considered here are as follows.

\begin{mdframed}[frametitle={VCCL Rules}]
For a path through a horizontal routing corridor, identify the standard weight-four stabilizer at the bottom of the horizontal green strip according to the traversal direction:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Left to right.} At a VCCL on the right side of a vertical corridor, select the stabilizer whose top-right and bottom-right data qubits lie on the VCCL (\cref{fig:VCCL_ExampleEX}).
    \item \textbf{Right to left.} At a VCCL on the left side of a vertical corridor, select the stabilizer whose top-left and bottom-left data qubits lie on the VCCL (\cref{fig:VCCL_Example_v2EX}).
\end{itemize}
If the selected stabilizer is $Z$-type, promote it and the column of stabilizers directly above it within the horizontal green strip to horizontal elongated stabilizers. Each promoted stabilizer retains its original Pauli type, and its middle inactive sites lie on the VCCL. After promotion, resume the standard checkerboard construction beyond the elongated stabilizers in the direction of traversal, continuing until the next checkpoint line is reached.
\end{mdframed}



\section{Performing multi-qubit Pauli measurements containing only $X$ and $Z$ operators}
\label{sec:MeasureXZ}

We now combine the layout and checkpoint rules of \cref{sec:Layout} to construct multi-qubit Pauli measurements containing only logical $X_L$ and $Z_L$ factors. The construction proceeds in three stages: select routing trees connecting the participating patches, place stabilizers for the required $X$ and $Z$ factors, and complete the boundaries and junctions of the merged patches.

\subsection{Multi-commodity flow algorithm for connecting logical patches}
\label{subsec:MCFAlgo}

