Consider a set of multi-qubit Pauli measurements (which we refer to as PPMs) $\{P_1,P_2,\ldots\}$ to be performed in parallel. Each measurement requires a connected routing region joining its participating logical patches, while the different measurements compete for the available routing space. We formulate this task as a multi-commodity routing problem \cite{Ahuja1993NetworkFlows}.

The routing network is represented by a graph $G=(V,E)$. A \textit{junction} is a region where horizontal and vertical routing corridors meet, allowing a route to turn or branch. Vertices correspond to these junctions, and edges represent allowed connections between neighboring junctions. Both edges and vertices have unit capacity: $u_e=1$ for every $e\in E$ and $u_v=1$ for every $v\in V$. Thus, each corridor segment and each junction can be occupied by at most one PPM at a time. The vertex constraint excludes routes that use different corridor segments but intersect at a shared junction.

Each Pauli measurement defines a commodity. For a two-qubit measurement between patches $A$ and $B$, this commodity requires one unit of flow from $A$ to $B$. For example, the simultaneous measurements $P_1=X_1\otimes X_2$ and $P_2=Z_5\otimes Z_7$ require paths connecting patches 1 and 2, and patches 5 and 7, respectively. The paths are selected jointly subject to the corridor and junction capacities, allowing one measurement to be rerouted when the individually preferred routes overlap.

A measurement involving more than two logical qubits generally requires a branching connection. For example, $P=X_1\otimes X_3\otimes X_8\otimes X_{10}$ requires a connected stabilizer region spanning four terminals. We describe such connections using Steiner trees \cite{Dreyfus1971Steiner}. Each measurement is assigned a root patch, and connectivity is enforced from that root to every other participating patch. The resulting multi-commodity Steiner routing problem seeks one tree per measurement, with no corridor edge or junction vertex shared between different trees. Branches of the same tree may share corridor segments and junctions, since capacities count distinct measurements rather than individual root-to-terminal paths.

The routing procedure used throughout this work is therefore:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For each $P_i$, identify the logical patches in its support as terminals and choose one as the root.
    \item Form a rooted Steiner-tree routing request for each measurement.
    \item Assign unit capacity to every corridor edge and junction vertex.
    \item Solve the routing requests jointly to obtain the measurement trees.
\end{enumerate}
Stabilizers along these trees are placed within the green corridor strips, with the boundary-access exceptions described in \cref{sec:Layout}. After placing all boundary and junction stabilizers, we verify that each merged patch is connected and contains no \textit{holes}, defined as unmerged regions completely surrounded by the patch. We also verify that the completed construction implements the requested multi-qubit Pauli measurement.


\subsection{Elongated stabilizers for logical $X_L$ measurements}
\label{subsec:ElongatedXMeas}

We first describe stabilizer placement for the logical $X$ factors. Throughout this work, routing-space data qubits are initialized in $|0\rangle$ before a multi-qubit Pauli measurement, regardless of its Pauli factors. With this convention, logical $X$ factors are coupled to the routing region using standard surface-code stabilizers, while logical $Z$ factors use domain walls, as described in \cref{subsec:ZMeasRules}. Newly measured checks that anticommute with a $Z$ operator stabilizing an initialized routing qubit have random individual outcomes in the first merge round. Nevertheless, the product of the relevant outcomes encodes the parity of the measured Pauli operator \cite{fowler2018low}. White ancillas distinguish checks with random initial outcomes from those shown with grey ancillas; \cref{fig:XX_Meas_dm} illustrates an $X_L\otimes X_L$ measurement.

For the patch orientations used here, minimum-weight representatives of $X_L$ are vertical strings of physical $X$ operators. For each logical $X$ factor, the gauge-fixing step therefore measures $X$-type checks whose product acts as $X$ on every data qubit along the relevant vertical patch boundary. Along the routing tree, stabilizers follow the standard checkerboard pattern, with elongated stabilizers inserted according to the VICL and VCCL rules of \cref{sec:Layout}. Boundary and corner checks are completed using the rules in \cref{sec:MBoundaryStabs}.

Stabilizer placement begins at the root patch selected in \cref{subsec:MCFAlgo}. If the measurement acts as $X$ on this patch, we choose a vertical boundary adjacent to the route and extend standard weight-four stabilizers along the route. If the measurement acts as $Z$, we instead choose a horizontal boundary, add a row of domain walls according to \cref{subsec:ZMeasRules}, and then continue with standard weight-four stabilizers along the route.

Elongated stabilizers can also be required away from checkpoint lines. When connecting a vertical green strip to the vertical boundary of a patch undergoing an $X$ measurement, standard weight-four stabilizers may produce a Pauli-type conflict: two horizontally adjacent stabilizers would have the same type. In this case, the weight-four stabilizers at the patch boundary are promoted to elongated stabilizers of the same type. \Cref{fig:XX_elongated_vs} shows two examples.


