In this construction, logical $Z$ factors couple to horizontal patch boundaries through the \texttt{dw1} and \texttt{dw2} domain-wall stabilizers shown in \cref{fig:Legend}. At a bottom boundary, place weight-four domain walls according to the stabilizer geometry before the merge:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The $Z$ component acts on the bottom-left and bottom-right data qubits of a weight-four $X$-type stabilizer.
    \item The $X$ component acts on the bottom-left and bottom-right data qubits of a weight-four $Z$-type stabilizer.
\end{itemize}
At a top boundary, use the corresponding top-left and top-right data qubits instead.

The $Z$ components of these weight-four domain walls cover all but one boundary data qubit. Denote the remaining qubit by $q_b$. The final domain wall depends on the local geometry:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If $q_b$ is not at a corner where the route changes between horizontal and vertical directions, add a weight-two domain wall whose $Z$ component acts on $q_b$.
    \item If $q_b$ is at such a corner, add a weight-three domain wall whose unique $Z$ component acts on $q_b$.
\end{itemize}
Together, these checks supply the required logical-$Z$ boundary support for the joint Pauli measurement. Examples are shown in \cref{fig:XZZ_Meas_examps}.


\subsection{Boundary stabilizer rules}
\label{sec:MBoundaryStabs}

We complete the merged-patch boundaries by adding or modifying checks around the stabilizers constructed in \cref{subsec:ElongatedXMeas,subsec:ZMeasRules}. The local rules are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Corner $Z$ check.} If a weight-four $Z$-type stabilizer lies at a corner, add a weight-two $X$-type stabilizer on each of its two exposed edges (\cref{fig:Corner_v1EX}).

    \item \textbf{Corner $X$ check.} If a corner weight-four stabilizer is $X$-type, reduce it to weight three by removing from its support the corner data qubit that belongs to no $Z$-type stabilizer (\cref{fig:Corner_v2EX}).

    \item \textbf{Exposed edge of a $Z$ check.} If two data qubits $q_1$ and $q_2$ of a weight-four $Z$-type stabilizer form an exposed boundary edge, add the weight-two check $X_{q_1}X_{q_2}$. \Cref{fig:Corner_v3EX} illustrates the case in which $q_1$ and $q_2$ are the top-right and top-left data qubits.

    \item \textbf{Corner between two $Z$ checks.} If two weight-four $Z$-type stabilizers form a $90^\circ$ corner at the boundary, add a weight-three $X$-type stabilizer on the three boundary data qubits in the union of their supports (\cref{fig:Corner_v4EX,fig:Corner_v5EX,fig:Corner_v6EX}).

    \item \textbf{Corner between a domain wall and a $Z$ check.} Suppose a domain wall and a weight-four $Z$-type stabilizer form a $90^\circ$ boundary corner, with boundary data qubits $q_1$, $q_2$, and $q_3$ in the union of their supports. If $q_1$ lies in the support of the domain wall's $X$ component, add the weight-three domain-wall stabilizer $Z_{q_1}X_{q_2}X_{q_3}$ (\cref{fig:Corner_v7EX,fig:Corner_v8EX}).
\end{itemize}

