In the corner rule for two $Z$-type checks, one check may be an elongated rectangle. The added weight-three $X$ check is then an elongated triangle measured using two ancillas, as illustrated in \cref{fig:Corner_v4EX,fig:Corner_v6EX}.

\textbf{Facing weight-two boundary checks.}
If weight-two $X$ checks on facing horizontal corridor boundaries require the same ancilla, we resolve the conflict by introducing elongated columns at both VCCLs bounding the intervening vertical corridor, within one adjoining horizontal strip. The pair changes the checkerboard pattern between the two lines and restores it beyond the second line. Both columns must be compatible with the existing geometry and fixed checks, and the completed construction is checked again. The same procedure applies to either horizontal traversal direction.

\textbf{Oversized elongated corners.}
The elongated triangles described above remain permitted when their support can be reached by the prescribed measurement ancillas. If boundary completion instead produces a weight-three $X$ check whose support cannot be reached by its two measurement ancillas, as can occur where horizontal and vertical elongated $Z$ checks meet, we retry the construction using compatible alternative VCCL placements along the same routing tree. This additional search is used only when such an oversized corner is present. Every mandatory VICL column is retained in full, and the revised construction must satisfy the stabilizer and measurement-circuit constraints.

\textbf{Weight-four elongated domain wall.}
An additional rule applies where two vertical strips of the merged patch, separated by a single column of data-qubit sites, are joined by a horizontal strip. Let $S_e(Z)$ be a weight-four horizontal elongated $Z$ stabilizer connecting the vertical strips at this junction. Suppose a \texttt{dw1} stabilizer $S_d$ shares exactly one data qubit, $q_{j_1}$, with $S_e(Z)$ and acts as $Z$ on that qubit. Let $q_{j_2}$ be the data qubit in the support of $S_e(Z)$ across the inactive column from $q_{j_1}$ on the same horizontal row. Suppose a standard weight-four $Z$ stabilizer $S_{\text{st}}(Z)$ shares exactly this qubit with $S_e(Z)$.

Add an elongated weight-four domain-wall stabilizer $S_{\text{edw}}$ containing three $X$ factors and one $Z$ factor. Its two horizontally aligned $X$ factors act on $q_{j_1}$ and $q_{j_2}$, and its $Z$ factor acts on another data qubit shared with $S_d$. The remaining $X$ factor completes the elongated rectangle illustrated in \cref{fig:Corner_v9EX}.

\textbf{Weight-four $X$ check at a junction.}
Consider two horizontal strips of a merged patch separated by a single row of \textit{ancilla} sites and joined by a vertical strip. Let $S_1(Z)$ be a standard weight-four $Z$ stabilizer connecting the horizontal strips at the junction. Choose two data qubits $q_1$ and $q_2$ in its support that lie on the same column and form an edge facing the junction boundary. No additional stabilizers extend beyond this edge on its exposed eastward or westward side.

Suppose $q_1$ also belongs to a standard weight-four $Z$ stabilizer $S_2(Z)$, and $q_2$ belongs to a standard weight-four $Z$ stabilizer $S_3(Z)$. Let $q_3$ be the other data qubit of $S_2(Z)$ on the same row as $q_1$, and let $q_4$ be the other data qubit of $S_3(Z)$ on the same row as $q_2$. Complete the junction by adding the weight-four stabilizer $X_{q_1}X_{q_2}X_{q_3}X_{q_4}$, as shown in \cref{fig:Weight_4_X_JunctionEX}.


\section{Fault-tolerant implementation of logical $Y$ measurements}
\label{sec:FaultTolerantY}

We now describe the stabilizer constructions used for logical $Y$ measurements. The objective is to preserve the full effective code distance $d$, so that no set of at most $(d-1)/2$ circuit faults causes a logical failure. We first introduce the underlying geometry; the gate schedules and protruding-lobe refinements used to protect against circuit faults are presented in \cref{subsec:gate_schedule_y_ext,sec:protruding_lobes}. We refer to the standard-corridor stabilizer layout defined in \cref{subsec:standard_corridor_Y} as the ``legacy $Y$-extension patch.'' The numerical evidence for distance preservation and the alternatives for overlapping extensions are discussed below.

\subsection{Standard corridor $Y$ measurements}
\label{subsec:standard_corridor_Y}

For the layout in \cref{fig:Layout_Example}, a logical $Y$ measurement is implemented by extending the patch so that a representative of $Y_L$ lies along a single accessible boundary. We extend the patch horizontally to a width of $2d$, following the approach also considered in Ref.~\cite{Chamberland22b}. \Cref{fig:YX_MeasEX} illustrates a $Y_L\otimes X_L$ measurement: the Pauli operators marked in purple form a minimum-weight representative of the extended logical $Y_L$ operator. The elongated yellow rectangle denotes a weight-five twist stabilizer measuring $X\otimes X\otimes Z\otimes Y\otimes X$, with active data qubits ordered row by row from top to bottom and from left to right within each row. \Cref{fig:LegendEX} illustrates a twist defect and its physical measurement circuit.

We construct the local stabilizer regions for all logical $Y$ factors before completing the routing trees and placing the remaining routing-space stabilizers. We call this preprocessing the \textit{$Y$-extension step}. For each participating patch, the route first leaves horizontally toward an allowed vertical routing strip. Define $s=+1$ for a patch at the top of its unit cell and $s=-1$ for a patch at the bottom. Thus, $s$ specifies the outward vertical direction toward the adjacent horizontal routing strip: north for a top patch and south for a bottom patch.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Horizontal extension.} Extend standard weight-four stabilizers from the logical patch in the direction of the route until the measured region reaches the allowed vertical routing strip. This region is marked by the red box in \cref{fig:YX_MeasEX}.

    \item \textbf{Domain-wall row and vertical extension.} Place a horizontal row of domain walls along the outward-facing horizontal boundary of the original patch, using the logical-$Z$ construction. For $s=+1$, use the top boundary and extend standard weight-four stabilizers northward above the domain walls. For $s=-1$, use the bottom boundary and extend them southward below the domain walls. Continue until the full vertical extent of the allowed horizontal strip is covered. In particular, the northward extension must stop at the strip boundary; no additional rows are added beyond it. This stopping condition produces an asymmetry between extensions from top and bottom patches. The domain-wall region is marked by the purple box in \cref{fig:YX_MeasEX}.

    \item \textbf{Twist and elongated column.} Place a weight-five twist stabilizer adjacent to the domain wall nearest the vertical routing corridor. Immediately beyond the twist in direction $s$, place an elongated $Z$-type stabilizer, followed by an elongated $X$-type stabilizer. Continue the sequence $Z,X,Z,X,\ldots$ northward for $s=+1$ or southward for $s=-1$ until the full vertical extent of the allowed horizontal strip is covered. The yellow rectangle in \cref{fig:YX_MeasEX} denotes the twist defect.

    \item \textbf{Rows adjoining elongated stabilizers.} From each elongated stabilizer introduced in step 3, extend a horizontal row of standard weight-four stabilizers toward the vertical routing corridor, spanning the full width of its allowed strip. The first standard stabilizer has the opposite Pauli type from the adjoining elongated stabilizer; subsequent types alternate along the row. These rows are marked by the yellow box in \cref{fig:YX_MeasEX}.

    \item \textbf{Row adjoining the twist.} Add a horizontal row of standard weight-four stabilizers adjacent to the twist and extending toward the vertical routing corridor. This row begins with a $Z$-type stabilizer and alternates between $Z$ and $X$ until it spans the full width of the allowed vertical strip. The row is marked by the green box in \cref{fig:YX_MeasEX}.

    \item \textbf{Complete the routing construction.} Once all local $Y$ extensions have been generated and combined as described below, continue placing standard weight-four stabilizers along the horizontal and vertical routing corridors. Apply the $X$- and $Z$-measurement routing rules, together with the mandatory VICL rule of \cref{subsec:Intracell} and the VCCL, HICL, and HCCL rules introduced below.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure*}
    \centering
    \subfloat[\label{fig:Update_HCCL_YmeasEX}]{\includegraphics[width=0.6\textwidth]{images/Update_HCCL_Ymeas.pdf}}
    \caption{Elongated checks introduced by the HICL and HCCL look-ahead rules when routing through regions containing fixed $Y$ extensions. The checkpoint rules reconcile the routing stabilizers with the stabilizers already present in these regions.}
    \label{fig:YUpdate_HCCL_Ymeas}
\end{figure*}

When several logical $Y$ factors of the same multi-qubit Pauli measurement have extensions entering a common routing corridor, generate their local constructions independently and then combine the measured regions. Stabilizers with identical support and Pauli action are identified and measured only once. For example, if extensions from opposite sides of a vertical corridor place the same $X$-type stabilizer on the same data qubits, the combined region contains a single $X$ check there. The same rule applies to extensions meeting across a horizontal corridor. \Cref{fig:Y_measure_mergeEX} shows an example.

The data-qubit supports and Pauli assignments of the legacy $Y$-extension stabilizers remain fixed during subsequent routing. This applies to standard weight-four checks, elongated checks, domain walls, and twist defects. If a routing strip overlaps an extension, identical stabilizers are included only once in the merged patch. The remaining routing stabilizers are matched to the fixed extension checks using the checkpoint rules introduced below. Where required, a standard weight-four routing stabilizer is replaced by an elongated stabilizer of the same Pauli type, allowing the checkerboard pattern beyond it to match the extension. Every mandatory VICL column remains fully elongated throughout this process. These rules also apply when a patch undergoing a $Y$ measurement is chosen as the root of the routing tree. The measurement circuits are assigned using the gate-scheduling rules described later.

The additional protruding lobes may be adjusted to prevent holes, as described in \cref{sec:protruding_lobes}. These adjustments leave the legacy $Y$ extension unchanged and retain access to the logical boundaries required by the Pauli measurement. Every connection in the final merged patch must be completed by a compatible set of stabilizers across the entire connecting strip, including its boundaries.

The following look-ahead rule applies initially to both HICLs and HCCLs when routing through regions containing fixed extensions.

\begin{mdframed}[frametitle={Look-ahead HICL and HCCL Rules}]
When extending standard weight-four stabilizers vertically across an HICL or HCCL, inspect the stabilizer location two steps beyond the checkpoint line in the traversal direction. Compare any existing stabilizer there with the newly added standard stabilizer immediately before the crossing.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{The inspected location is empty.} Ignore the checkpoint line and continue the standard checkerboard pattern.
    \item \textbf{The inspected stabilizer has the same type.} Continue the routing construction normally.
    \item \textbf{The inspected stabilizer has the opposite type.} Replace the standard weight-four stabilizer immediately before the checkpoint line by a vertical elongated stabilizer of the same type as the replaced check. Continue the construction using this elongated stabilizer.
\end{itemize}
\end{mdframed}

An example is shown in \cref{fig:YUpdate_HCCL_Ymeas}. For VCCLs, apply the same look-ahead rule in the horizontal traversal direction and use horizontal elongated stabilizers. These VCCL rules apply only outside the fixed $Y$-extension patches, whose stabilizer construction takes precedence over all checkpoint rules. If look-ahead finds a stabilizer at any inspected location, it determines the checkpoint choice for the entire connected crossing, including rows whose inspected locations are empty. When elongation is required, the elongated checks form a complete column spanning the full connected transverse extent of the merged patch, with alternating $X$ and $Z$ types. Only when all inspected locations are empty do we apply the standard VCCL rule of \cref{subsec:Intercell} to the entire crossing. A single connected crossing must not contain a mixture of elongated and standard weight-four checks along the same VCCL.

If the preceding rules leave anticommuting stabilizers at a fixed-extension boundary, or require an oversized weight-three $X$ stabilizer whose data qubits cannot all be reached by its measurement ancillas, we retry the construction using an earlier HICL along the same routing strip. Outside the fixed extensions, we replace a complete row of standard weight-four checks by vertical elongated checks of the same Pauli types, alternating between $X$ and $Z$ across the full connected width. This changes the checkerboard pattern beyond the HICL, allowing it to match the fixed extension. This fallback preserves the routing path, the legacy $Y$-extension checks, and the mandatory VICL columns. The resulting construction is accepted only after checking the complete stabilizer placement and gate schedule.

VICLs always obey the mandatory rule of \cref{subsec:Intracell}: the elongated column spans the full connected transverse extent of the merged patch at the crossin
